export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","name":"BimCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What's new in 0.6","summary":"BimCraft adds grids and columns, wall trim, floors of any shape and ramps, linked models, a project browser with view ranges, ceiling plans, callouts, drafting in a view and your own sheets, pipes and ducts, door, window and furniture families, frame analysis, points for the field, VR review, asset and daylight tabs, and more point cloud formats.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.6","version","new features","coordinates","trim","extend","corner","grids","columns","floor by outline","ramps","site","levels","point cloud","scan","traced drawing","linked models","project browser","view range","view template","sheets","plan scale","crop region","underlay","ceiling plan","callout","hatch","drafting","assistants","pipes","ducts","valves","families","family editor","frame analysis","setting out points","as-built","vr","headset","glb","rendering","assets","maintenance","daylight","4d schedule","stages","ply","pcd","ptx","e57"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-building","text":"Draw a building","level":3},{"id":"doors-windows-and-furniture-in-families","text":"Doors, windows and furniture in families","level":3},{"id":"drawing-comforts","text":"Drawing comforts","level":3},{"id":"views-and-sheets","text":"Views and sheets","level":3},{"id":"analysis-field-and-review","text":"Analysis, field and review","level":3},{"id":"models-you-receive-and-other-programs","text":"Models you receive and other programs","level":3},{"id":"changed","text":"Changed","level":2},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in BimCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Most of it answers what BIM users asked for: grids, trim and corners, views and sheets the BIM way, and pipes and ducts, all saved as plain IFC.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-building\"\u003EDraw a building\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere you are working.\u003C/strong\u003E The pointer&#039;s X, Y and Z (and map eastings and northings when the model has them) show with the level it is on, over the 3D view and the plan. The project origin and the site&#039;s survey point are drawn as markers. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/coordinates\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/coordinates\"\u003ECoordinates in BimCraft\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrim, extend and corner walls.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim / extend\u003C/span\u003E runs a wall to another wall, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner\u003C/span\u003E joins two walls where they meet. Rooms, floors and roofs follow. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/trim-extend-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/trim-extend-walls\"\u003ETrim, extend and join walls\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrids and columns.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E lays out numbered and lettered grid lines by spacing or one by one; they show on every plan and on the drawing set, and the drawing tools snap to them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E places rectangular or round columns, one by one or at every grid crossing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/grids-and-columns\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/grids-and-columns\"\u003EGrids and columns\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFloors of any shape, and ramps.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloor by outline\u003C/span\u003E draws floors and slabs of any shape with openings cut in them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERamp\u003C/span\u003E draws a straight ramp by slope or by length, with landings where the rise needs them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/floors-by-outline-and-ramps\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/floors-by-outline-and-ramps\"\u003EFloors of any shape, openings and ramps\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe site and levels as tools.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite\u003C/span\u003E draws the site&#039;s outline and places and turns the building on it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd levels…\u003C/span\u003E adds levels at given heights or splits the building&#039;s height into equal levels. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/site-building-and-levels\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/site-building-and-levels\"\u003EThe site, the building on it, and its levels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDraw on a point cloud.\u003C/strong\u003E With a scan open, the plan shows its slice at the level&#039;s cut height and every drawing tool snaps to the scanned points. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E menu sets the slice&#039;s thickness, aligns the scan&#039;s floor to the level and turns it square to the building. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-from-a-scan\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-from-a-scan\"\u003EModel from a scan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls from a traced drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E turns a DWG or DXF plan into walls, doors and windows in one step, and keeps the drawing under the plan as a reference. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walls-from-a-drawing\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walls-from-a-drawing\"\u003EWalls from a traced drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe ground from CivilCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain\u003C/span\u003E reads LandXML surfaces, such as CivilCraft&#039;s export, keeping their own points and triangles. With several surfaces in the file, BimCraft asks which one is the ground. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/terrain#bring-the-ground-in\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/terrain\" data-hash=\"bring-the-ground-in\"\u003EThe site&#039;s ground, the building pad and paving\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipes and ducts.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuct\u003C/span\u003E draw runs at a height, and the elbows, tees, reducers and transitions are made for you; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValve\u003C/span\u003E puts a valve on a pipe. Pipe sizes come from public standards, ducts can be sized from their airflow, and drains start at the minimum slope. Each run is saved as one connected system that other programs read, and it appears in the schedules and the takeoff. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pipes-and-ducts\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pipes-and-ducts\"\u003EPipes and ducts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants that can draw.\u003C/strong\u003E An AI assistant or script you connect can now draw walls, doors and windows, floors, levels and grid lines. Each change is one step that \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back. Saving stays off until you turn it on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"doors-windows-and-furniture-in-families\"\u003EDoors, windows and furniture in families\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFamilies and their sizes.\u003C/strong\u003E A family is one kind of door, window or piece of furniture in several sizes; BimCraft shows it once, with its sizes under it. The Door and Window tools pick a family and a size, and a new width and height becomes a new size. In the file each size is a plain IFC type, so other programs read it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlace furniture from a family.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace family\u003C/span\u003E puts furniture on a floor (a table, a shelf unit and a base cabinet to start from); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families#place-furniture\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\" data-hash=\"place-furniture\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA family editor.\u003C/strong\u003E Parameters with formulas, yes or no parameters that show or hide parts, extrusions, revolves, sweeps and Booleans, with handles in a 3D preview. Saving remakes every element of the family. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/family-editor\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/family-editor\"\u003EEdit a family\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFamily files.\u003C/strong\u003E Save a family as its own file or as an IFC library, and open either in another model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families#family-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\" data-hash=\"family-files\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"drawing-comforts\"\u003EDrawing comforts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECurved walls.\u003C/strong\u003E Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShape\u003C/span\u003E to Curved and click where the wall starts, where it ends, then a point on its arc (or type the arc height). A curved wall joins the walls at its ends, holds doors and windows along its arc, makes rooms and is saved as a real arc in IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA wall keeps the line it was drawn on.\u003C/strong\u003E Draw on the outside or inside face and it stays there when the wall gets thicker or its outside is flipped; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation line\u003C/span\u003E in Properties changes which line that is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove and turn library items.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag a cabinet or anything placed from the library in the plan, turn it with its round grip or R, or type X, Y and Rotation in Properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDormers and spiral stairs.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDormer\u003C/span\u003E puts a gable or shed dormer on a roof face and cuts the roof below it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStair\u003C/span\u003E › Spiral draws wedge treads round a center post.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInterior elevations.\u003C/strong\u003E A room&#039;s Properties › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterior elevations…\u003C/span\u003E shows its four walls from inside on one sheet, with the room&#039;s width and height and tags on its doors, windows and cabinets, and downloads as a PDF. Elevations and sections on your sheets also carry level marks, heights and tags.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom a community contributor.\u003C/strong\u003E A wall&#039;s drawing reference (centerline, inside or outside face), alignment guides while drawing walls, and a basement egress window with a well. These are modeling defaults, not a check of egress rules.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"views-and-sheets\"\u003EViews and sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EViews the BIM way.\u003C/strong\u003E A project browser lists every plan, elevation, section, saved 3D view, schedule, sheet, view template and link. Each floor plan has its own view range and can leave out categories or elements, and view templates carry those settings to several plans. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\"\u003EViews, view ranges and your own sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour own sheets.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag plans, elevations, sections and schedules onto a sheet at the scale you pick; the sheet is saved in the model and downloads as a PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets#arrange-a-sheet-of-your-own\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-hash=\"arrange-a-sheet-of-your-own\"\u003EArrange a sheet of your own\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlan scale, crop region and underlay.\u003C/strong\u003E Each floor plan has a scale it prints at, a crop region that cuts it on paper and sheets, and an underlay that draws another level lightly under it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets#crop-a-plan-give-it-a-scale-show-another-level-under-it\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-hash=\"crop-a-plan-give-it-a-scale-show-another-level-under-it\"\u003ECrop a plan, give it a scale, show another level under it\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReflected ceiling plans.\u003C/strong\u003E Every level has a ceiling plan beside its floor plan, with ceilings and their grid, a height tag, lights and beams. Items placed from the library while it is shown hang from the ceiling. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets#a-levels-ceiling-plan\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-hash=\"a-levels-ceiling-plan\"\u003EA level&#039;s ceiling plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECallouts.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E and two corners make a view of part of a floor plan or ceiling plan at its own scale (1:20 to start): a kitchen, a stair, a wall corner. Its rectangle and tag are drawn on the plan; click the tag to open it, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp\u003C/span\u003E goes back. A callout goes on your sheets and prints like a plan. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets#make-a-callout\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-hash=\"make-a-callout\"\u003EMake a callout\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELines and hatches in one view.\u003C/strong\u003E Draw lines, polylines, rectangles and hatch regions (by their corners, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill a room\u003C/span\u003E) in six patterns, in one floor plan, ceiling plan or callout only. They never show in 3D or another view, and they print and go on sheets with their view. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets#draw-lines-and-hatches-in-one-view\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-hash=\"draw-lines-and-hatches-in-one-view\"\u003EDraw lines and hatches in one view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"analysis-field-and-review\"\u003EAnalysis, field and review\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrame analysis.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab works out forces, moments, reactions and deflections for a model&#039;s beams, columns and braces, with load cases and combinations, the deflected shape on the model, and a report as PDF and CSV. It is an analysis aid, not a code check. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/frame-analysis\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/frame-analysis\"\u003EAnalyse the structural frame\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoints for the field.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E tab writes setting-out points (column centers, wall ends, anchor bolts or any elements you pick) for a total station as CSV or TXT, then reads the crew&#039;s as-built points back and shows which are within your tolerance. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/field-points\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/field-points\"\u003EPoints for setting out, and as-built checks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReview in a VR headset.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVR\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar puts you inside the model at full size: jump between floors, measure, cut walls open and place issues with a picture of what you saw. In a live session everyone in VR sees the others. Nothing is uploaded. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/vr-review\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/vr-review\"\u003EReview the model in a VR headset\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssets and maintenance.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssets\u003C/span\u003E tab keeps each piece of equipment&#039;s maker, model, serial number, warranty and service life, and a maintenance record with what is overdue or due soon first. It is all written into the IFC, so other programs read it too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assets-and-maintenance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assets-and-maintenance\"\u003EKeep asset data and a maintenance record in the model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDaylight.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDaylight\u003C/span\u003E tab estimates each room&#039;s average daylight factor from its windows, its inside surfaces and the sky each window sees, measured in the model. It is an early-design estimate, not a simulation or a code check. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/daylight-factor\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/daylight-factor\"\u003EEstimate the daylight in each room\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA model for rendering programs.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor rendering\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar saves what the 3D view shows as a .glb file with its materials, textures and lights, for rendering programs that read glTF, such as Twinmotion. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-for-rendering\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-for-rendering\"\u003ESend the model to a rendering program\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"models-you-receive-and-other-programs\"\u003EModels you receive and other programs\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked models.\u003C/strong\u003E Link other IFC models for coordination: they are shown and cut on the plans but never merged, placed by shared or map coordinates or two picked points, snapped to while drawing, and reloaded when their file changes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/linked-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/linked-models\"\u003ELink other models for coordination\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFour more point cloud formats.\u003C/strong\u003E PLY, PCD, Leica PTX and E57 now open as well as LAS, LAZ, XYZ and PTS, and a card for scanner makers&#039; own formats says how to export E57 or LAS. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/point-cloud-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/point-cloud-formats\"\u003EPoint cloud formats: what opens, and what to export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject bundles.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft opens a project bundle exported from Archicad: the model, its views, its layouts (as PDF and DWG), the picked element&#039;s properties in the project&#039;s own groups, and the project&#039;s standards. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-bundle\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-bundle\"\u003EOpen a project bundle\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBetween two BIM programs.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft writes the files that set Revit and Archicad up for the same properties, and makes a round-trip report of what came back, was changed or was lost when a model went out and came back as IFC. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/two-bim-programs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/two-bim-programs\"\u003EWorking between two BIM programs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject files told apart.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft recognizes an Archicad project file (.pln, .pla) for certain by its first bytes, and says when a file with that name doesn&#039;t start like one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E4D stages and packages.\u003C/strong\u003E The 4D schedule groups tasks into stages and work packages: pick one and it plays on its own dates, with everything else see-through, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect its elements\u003C/span\u003E picks the whole group. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/4d-schedule#stages-and-work-packages\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/4d-schedule\" data-hash=\"stages-and-work-packages\"\u003EPlay a construction schedule on the model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary items in your language.\u003C/strong\u003E An item&#039;s name, description and type show in your language when the item carries them, and it is placed under that name. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library#items-in-your-language\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-hash=\"items-in-your-language\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changed\"\u003EChanged\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA building turned in its site is shown square.\u003C/strong\u003E Fit, Top, Front, Side, the view cube&#039;s faces and every new section follow its walls, and the view cube&#039;s compass still points to north. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA building turned in its site\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs placed in the site\u003C/span\u003E keeps the file&#039;s axes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane#a-building-turned-in-its-site\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\" data-hash=\"a-building-turned-in-its-site\"\u003EA building turned in its site\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors and windows come in families.\u003C/strong\u003E The 18 starter doors and windows are drawn exactly as before. Files from before 0.6 open unchanged; a door or window moves onto a size of its family the first time it is changed. Doors and windows drawn here are now typed in IFC2X3 files too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn automatic roof, foundation or building pad followed every wall. It now follows only the walls that enclose the building, so posts, a garden wall and inside walls no longer add facets to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFixed, hung and picture windows and double doors offered \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHinges: Flip\u003C/span\u003E. They no longer do, and sliding, pocket, barn and bifold doors say \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpens to\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sun&#039;s date and time boxes in Render were hard to read in the model theme while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another date and time\u003C/span\u003E was off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Updating the model…&quot; stayed on screen after the model had reloaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPaint, Views and Render from the plan-only layout now switch to Split, so the 3D view they work in shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA painted wall the drawing tools reshape keeps its color, in other programs too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA section left elements made of parts in different colors (a frame with its glass) looking hollow. Their cut faces are filled now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe section bar hid under the drawing bar. It now sits at the top of the 3D view it cuts and stays above the view cube, and in a narrow window Split puts the plan above the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe toolbar can&#039;t get lost.\u003C/strong\u003E While full screen hides the bars, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top of the window. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\"\u003EWhere did my toolbar go?\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA menu on the toolbar.\u003C/strong\u003E Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#the-toolbars-own-menu\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"the-toolbars-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInterface size.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPen and touch.\u003C/strong\u003E On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen&#039;s side button and eraser end can undo or redo. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/pen-and-touch\" data-topic=\"basics/pen-and-touch\"\u003EDraw with a pen on a tablet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive sessions can grow past 20 people.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E for this session or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent edits\u003C/span\u003E in the Live panel shows who changed what. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/work-together-live#when-many-people-join\" data-topic=\"basics/work-together-live\" data-hash=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWork together live\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E is new in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. \u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish (Mexico)\u003C/strong\u003E is still a draft too. Help topics are in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstallers are still not signed\u003C/strong\u003E (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun anyway\u003C/span\u003E. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixed:\u003C/strong\u003E with the bars hidden, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar…\u003C/span\u003E in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProject bundles and the two-program kit have been tested against stand-ins only, not yet with Archicad or Revit themselves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERendering a recorded walkthrough as a video isn&#039;t built in BimCraft (ModelCraft renders videos from its scenes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EView ranges and view templates are for floor plans only, and sheets are redrawn from the model each time. A set-of-sheets PDF, detail views and legends aren&#039;t built. Ceiling plans cut at one height (2300 mm).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe .glb for rendering has one object per level and material, no saved views or walkthroughs as cameras and no bump maps; there is no FBX, plug-in or live link. It hasn&#039;t been opened in Twinmotion or Enscape themselves yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssets: documents, COBie, sensor feeds and a hosted twin aren&#039;t built; in IFC2X3 files the dates are kept as text. Daylight gives the average daylight factor only, with no solar gain or energy simulation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERamps are straight, drawn floors don&#039;t follow walls, and columns are rectangular or round only (no steel or beams). Assistants can&#039;t delete or move elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipes and ducts: fittings and valves are generic; equipment such as air handlers, fans and pumps, pressure losses, balancing, sprinklers and cable trays aren&#039;t built; drains aren&#039;t sized by fixture units; a run&#039;s corners can&#039;t be dragged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVR has been tested with a headset emulator, not yet with a real headset; inside CadCraft Studio it shows only where the system&#039;s web view offers it. Typing in VR isn&#039;t built: an issue placed in VR gets its words afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EE57 files up to 6 million points and 512 MB; compressed binary PCD files are refused; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E doesn&#039;t list point clouds yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E can place an extra model wrongly after the model is rebuilt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFamilies: one family inside another, arrays of solids, a material as a parameter, sizes from a spreadsheet, detail levels, a family&#039;s own plan lines, families in the library, and editing other programs&#039; types as families. Models with families haven&#039;t been opened in other BIM programs yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELibrary item pages on the site still show English names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything else is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"This is what changed in BimCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Most of it answers what BIM users asked for: grids, trim and corners, views and sheets the BIM way, and pipes and ducts, all saved as plain IFC. New Draw a building Where you are working. The pointer's X, Y and Z (and map eastings and northings when the model has them) show with the level it is on, over the 3D view and the plan. The project origin and the site's survey point are drawn as markers. See Coordinates in BimCraft. Trim, extend and corner walls. Modify › Trim / extend runs a wall to another wall, and Corner joins two walls where they meet. Rooms, floors and roofs follow. See Trim, extend and join walls. Grids and columns. Build › Grid lays out numbered and lettered grid lines by spacing or one by one; they show on every plan and on the drawing set, and the drawing tools snap to them. Build › Column places rectangular or round columns, one by one or at every grid crossing. See Grids and columns. Floors of any shape, and ramps. Build › Floor by outline draws floors and slabs of any shape with openings cut in them. Build › Ramp draws a straight ramp by slope or by length, with landings where the rise needs them. See Floors of any shape, openings and ramps. The site and levels as tools. Build › Site draws the site's outline and places and turns the building on it. Add levels… adds levels at given heights or splits the building's height into equal levels. See The site, the building on it, and its levels. Draw on a point cloud. With a scan open, the plan shows its slice at the level's cut height and every drawing tool snaps to the scanned points. The Scan menu sets the slice's thickness, aligns the scan's floor to the level and turns it square to the building. See Model from a scan. Walls from a traced drawing. Build › From a drawing… turns a DWG or DXF plan into walls, doors and windows in one step, and keeps the drawing under the plan as a reference. See Walls from a traced drawing. The ground from CivilCraft. Build › Terrain reads LandXML surfaces, such as CivilCraft's export, keeping their own points and triangles. With several surfaces in the file, BimCraft asks which one is the ground. See The site's ground, the building pad and paving. Pipes and ducts. Build › Pipe and Duct draw runs at a height, and the elbows, tees, reducers and transitions are made for you; Valve puts a valve on a pipe. Pipe sizes come from public standards, ducts can be sized from their airflow, and drains start at the minimum slope. Each run is saved as one connected system that other programs read, and it appears in the schedules and the takeoff. See Pipes and ducts. Assistants that can draw. An AI assistant or script you connect can now draw walls, doors and windows, floors, levels and grid lines. Each change is one step that Ctrl+Z takes back. Saving stays off until you turn it on in Settings › BimCraft › Assistants. See Let an AI assistant work with your model. Doors, windows and furniture in families Families and their sizes. A family is one kind of door, window or piece of furniture in several sizes; BimCraft shows it once, with its sizes under it. The Door and Window tools pick a family and a size, and a new width and height becomes a new size. In the file each size is a plain IFC type, so other programs read it. See Families and their sizes. Place furniture from a family. Place family puts furniture on a floor (a table, a shelf unit and a base cabinet to start from); R turns it. See Families and their sizes. A family editor. Parameters with formulas, yes or no parameters that show or hide parts, extrusions, revolves, sweeps and Booleans, with handles in a 3D preview. Saving remakes every element of the family. See Edit a family. Family files. Save a family as its own file or as an IFC library, and open either in another model. See Families and their sizes. Drawing comforts Curved walls. Set Wall › Shape to Curved and click where the wall starts, where it ends, then a point on its arc (or type the arc height). A curved wall joins the walls at its ends, holds doors and windows along its arc, makes rooms and is saved as a real arc in IFC. A wall keeps the line it was drawn on. Draw on the outside or inside face and it stays there when the wall gets thicker or its outside is flipped; Location line in Properties changes which line that is. Move and turn library items. Drag a cabinet or anything placed from the library in the plan, turn it with its round grip or R, or type X, Y and Rotation in Properties. Dormers and spiral stairs. Build › Dormer puts a gable or shed dormer on a roof face and cuts the roof below it; Stair › Spiral draws wedge treads round a center post. Interior elevations. A room's Properties › Interior elevations… shows its four walls from inside on one sheet, with the room's width and height and tags on its doors, windows and cabinets, and downloads as a PDF. Elevations and sections on your sheets also carry level marks, heights and tags. From a community contributor. A wall's drawing reference (centerline, inside or outside face), alignment guides while drawing walls, and a basement egress window with a well. These are modeling defaults, not a check of egress rules. Views and sheets Views the BIM way. A project browser lists every plan, elevation, section, saved 3D view, schedule, sheet, view template and link. Each floor plan has its own view range and can leave out categories or elements, and view templates carry those settings to several plans. See Views, view ranges and your own sheets. Your own sheets. Drag plans, elevations, sections and schedules onto a sheet at the scale you pick; the sheet is saved in the model and downloads as a PDF. See Arrange a sheet of your own. Plan scale, crop region and underlay. Each floor plan has a scale it prints at, a crop region that cuts it on paper and sheets, and an underlay that draws another level lightly under it. See Crop a plan, give it a scale, show another level under it. Reflected ceiling plans. Every level has a ceiling plan beside its floor plan, with ceilings and their grid, a height tag, lights and beams. Items placed from the library while it is shown hang from the ceiling. See A level's ceiling plan. Callouts. Annotate › Callout and two corners make a view of part of a floor plan or ceiling plan at its own scale (1:20 to start): a kitchen, a stair, a wall corner. Its rectangle and tag are drawn on the plan; click the tag to open it, and Up goes back. A callout goes on your sheets and prints like a plan. See Make a callout. Lines and hatches in one view. Draw lines, polylines, rectangles and hatch regions (by their corners, or Fill a room) in six patterns, in one floor plan, ceiling plan or callout only. They never show in 3D or another view, and they print and go on sheets with their view. See Draw lines and hatches in one view. Analysis, field and review Frame analysis. The Analysis tab works out forces, moments, reactions and deflections for a model's beams, columns and braces, with load cases and combinations, the deflected shape on the model, and a report as PDF and CSV. It is an analysis aid, not a code check. See Analyse the structural frame. Points for the field. The Points tab writes setting-out points (column centers, wall ends, anchor bolts or any elements you pick) for a total station as CSV or TXT, then reads the crew's as-built points back and shows which are within your tolerance. See Points for setting out, and as-built checks. Review in a VR headset. VR on the toolbar puts you inside the model at full size: jump between floors, measure, cut walls open and place issues with a picture of what you saw. In a live session everyone in VR sees the others. Nothing is uploaded. See Review the model in a VR headset. Assets and maintenance. The Assets tab keeps each piece of equipment's maker, model, serial number, warranty and service life, and a maintenance record with what is overdue or due soon first. It is all written into the IFC, so other programs read it too. See Keep asset data and a maintenance record in the model. Daylight. The Daylight tab estimates each room's average daylight factor from its windows, its inside surfaces and the sky each window sees, measured in the model. It is an early-design estimate, not a simulation or a code check. See Estimate the daylight in each room. A model for rendering programs. For rendering on the toolbar saves what the 3D view shows as a .glb file with its materials, textures and lights, for rendering programs that read glTF, such as Twinmotion. See Send the model to a rendering program. Models you receive and other programs Linked models. Link other IFC models for coordination: they are shown and cut on the plans but never merged, placed by shared or map coordinates or two picked points, snapped to while drawing, and reloaded when their file changes. See Link other models for coordination. Four more point cloud formats. PLY, PCD, Leica PTX and E57 now open as well as LAS, LAZ, XYZ and PTS, and a card for scanner makers' own formats says how to export E57 or LAS. See Point cloud formats: what opens, and what to export. Project bundles. BimCraft opens a project bundle exported from Archicad: the model, its views, its layouts (as PDF and DWG), the picked element's properties in the project's own groups, and the project's standards. See Open a project bundle. Between two BIM programs. BimCraft writes the files that set Revit and Archicad up for the same properties, and makes a round-trip report of what came back, was changed or was lost when a model went out and came back as IFC. See Working between two BIM programs. Project files told apart. BimCraft recognizes an Archicad project file (.pln, .pla) for certain by its first bytes, and says when a file with that name doesn't start like one. See Open .pln and .pla project files. 4D stages and packages. The 4D schedule groups tasks into stages and work packages: pick one and it plays on its own dates, with everything else see-through, and Select its elements picks the whole group. See Play a construction schedule on the model. Library items in your language. An item's name, description and type show in your language when the item carries them, and it is placed under that name. See Place equipment and furniture from the library. Changed A building turned in its site is shown square. Fit, Top, Front, Side, the view cube's faces and every new section follow its walls, and the view cube's compass still points to north. Settings › BimCraft › A building turned in its site › As placed in the site keeps the file's axes. See A building turned in its site. Doors and windows come in families. The 18 starter doors and windows are drawn exactly as before. Files from before 0.6 open unchanged; a door or window moves onto a size of its family the first time it is changed. Doors and windows drawn here are now typed in IFC2X3 files too. Fixed An automatic roof, foundation or building pad followed every wall. It now follows only the walls that enclose the building, so posts, a garden wall and inside walls no longer add facets to it. Fixed, hung and picture windows and double doors offered Hinges: Flip. They no longer do, and sliding, pocket, barn and bifold doors say Opens to. The sun's date and time boxes in Render were hard to read in the model theme while Use another date and time was off. \"Updating the model…\" stayed on screen after the model had reloaded. Paint, Views and Render from the plan-only layout now switch to Split, so the 3D view they work in shows. A painted wall the drawing tools reshape keeps its color, in other programs too. A section left elements made of parts in different colors (a frame with its glass) looking hollow. Their cut faces are filled now. The section bar hid under the drawing bar. It now sits at the top of the 3D view it cuts and stays above the view cube, and in a narrow window Split puts the plan above the 3D view. In every app The toolbar can't get lost. While full screen hides the bars, a small Show bars tab stays at the top of the window. Settings › General › Reset the layout puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own Settings has Reset the layout in every app. See Where did my toolbar go?. A menu on the toolbar. Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See The toolbar's own menu. Interface size. Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See Everything is too big or too small. Pen and touch. On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen's side button and eraser end can undo or redo. Settings › General › Pen and touch. See Draw with a pen on a tablet. Live sessions can grow past 20 people. In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose Allow anyway for this session or Keep it at 20. Recent edits in the Live panel shows who changed what. See Work together live. Bahasa Indonesia is new in Settings › General › Language: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Spanish (Mexico) is still a draft too. Help topics are in English. Installers are still not signed (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose More info, then Run anyway. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose Open. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet. Fixed: with the bars hidden, Arrange the toolbar… in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move. Not in this version yet Project bundles and the two-program kit have been tested against stand-ins only, not yet with Archicad or Revit themselves. Rendering a recorded walkthrough as a video isn't built in BimCraft (ModelCraft renders videos from its scenes). View ranges and view templates are for floor plans only, and sheets are redrawn from the model each time. A set-of-sheets PDF, detail views and legends aren't built. Ceiling plans cut at one height (2300 mm). The .glb for rendering has one object per level and material, no saved views or walkthroughs as cameras and no bump maps; there is no FBX, plug-in or live link. It hasn't been opened in Twinmotion or Enscape themselves yet. Assets: documents, COBie, sensor feeds and a hosted twin aren't built; in IFC2X3 files the dates are kept as text. Daylight gives the average daylight factor only, with no solar gain or energy simulation. Ramps are straight, drawn floors don't follow walls, and columns are rectangular or round only (no steel or beams). Assistants can't delete or move elements. Pipes and ducts: fittings and valves are generic; equipment such as air handlers, fans and pumps, pressure losses, balancing, sprinklers and cable trays aren't built; drains aren't sized by fixture units; a run's corners can't be dragged. VR has been tested with a headset emulator, not yet with a real headset; inside CadCraft Studio it shows only where the system's web view offers it. Typing in VR isn't built: an issue placed in VR gets its words afterwards. E57 files up to 6 million points and 512 MB; compressed binary PCD files are refused; Open a folder doesn't list point clouds yet. Add a model… can place an extra model wrongly after the model is rebuilt. Families: one family inside another, arrays of solids, a material as a parameter, sizes from a spreadsheet, detail levels, a family's own plan lines, families in the library, and editing other programs' types as families. Models with families haven't been opened in other BIM programs yet. Library item pages on the site still show English names. Everything else is in What is not built yet."},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Getting started with BimCraft","summary":"What BimCraft does today, how to open your first model, look at it in 3D, change one value and save a copy.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first run","open","ifc","rvt","dwg","quick start","introduction","viewer","bim"],"headings":[{"id":"open-bimcraft","text":"Open BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"open-your-first-model","text":"Open your first model","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"do-one-real-thing-fix-a-tag","text":"Do one real thing: fix a tag","level":2},{"id":"save-a-copy","text":"Save a copy","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft is the building-model app of the CadCraft family. In this build it \u003Cstrong\u003Eopens, reads and inspects\u003C/strong\u003E building files, and it \u003Cstrong\u003Eedits the data in IFC models\u003C/strong\u003E. It does not draw or model new elements yet. Think of it as a model viewer and checker that also lets you correct names, tags and property values in an IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat it opens:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC (\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe full model in 3D: orbit, storeys, element properties, and editing of names and property values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files (\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA file card: the release it was saved in, project information, the preview picture and linked files. No 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing in 2D: model space and every sheet, layers, linked drawings, print view, and an equipment schedule for tagged foodservice drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA project folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA report of every model in the folder: backups, links, damaged copies, and a suggestion of which file to start with\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything happens in your browser, on your computer. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E for the details.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-bimcraft\"\u003EOpen BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen BimCraft in a current browser. Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari all work; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E lists the differences.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the empty screen that says &quot;Open a building model.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft runs in the browser, and on the desktop inside CadCraft Studio, with the other apps. Cloud saving is not available yet: you open files from your computer and save copies back to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-your-first-model\"\u003EOpen your first model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quickest way to see what BimCraft does is an IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E in the middle of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file in \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line at the bottom of the screen says &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;, then &quot;Building geometry&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see the model in 3D in the middle, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list on the left, and an empty \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. The status line ends with &quot;Opened&quot; and the number of elements.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag a file from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E straight onto the page. The screen shows &quot;Drop to open&quot; while you drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe IFC engine loads from the site the first time you open an IFC model, unless BimCraft was saved for offline use. DWG and DXF drawings, project files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. If the app bar&#039;s button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, everything opens offline. If not, IFC models will not open until you reconnect unless one already opened in this tab. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGoing somewhere without a connection? Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. The saved copy (about 11 MB) includes the IFC engine. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the left mouse button to orbit around the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll the wheel to zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the right mouse button to pan. On a Mac trackpad, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with one finger to orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPinch with two fingers to zoom, and move two fingers together to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"do-one-real-thing-fix-a-tag\"\u003EDo one real thing: fix a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a wall, door or any element in the model. It turns blue, and its properties appear on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, click the value next to \u003Cstrong\u003ETag\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new tag and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section that opened on the left: your change is listed with the old and new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-copy\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the new file in your downloads folder. It is named after the original with &quot; (edited)&quot; added, for example \u003Ccode\u003EClinic (edited).ifc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original file is never changed. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E explains editing in full.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe BimCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E: every panel by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other BIM and drawing programs\u003C/a\u003E: what feels different if you are used to Revit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E: point BimCraft at a project folder and let it sort the files out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E: sheets, layers and print view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"BimCraft is the building-model app of the CadCraft family. In this build it opens, reads and inspects building files, and it edits the data in IFC models. It does not draw or model new elements yet. Think of it as a model viewer and checker that also lets you correct names, tags and property values in an IFC file. What it opens: File What you get IFC (.ifc) The full model in 3D: orbit, storeys, element properties, and editing of names and property values Project and family files (.rvt, .rfa, .rte, .rft) A file card: the release it was saved in, project information, the preview picture and linked files. No 3D. DWG and DXF The drawing in 2D: model space and every sheet, layers, linked drawings, print view, and an equipment schedule for tagged foodservice drawings A project folder A report of every model in the folder: backups, links, damaged copies, and a suggestion of which file to start with Everything happens in your browser, on your computer. Nothing is uploaded. See Files BimCraft opens and saves for the details. Open BimCraft Open BimCraft in a current browser. Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari all work; Browsers, devices and memory lists the differences. Wait for the empty screen that says \"Open a building model.\" BimCraft runs in the browser, and on the desktop inside CadCraft Studio, with the other apps. Cloud saving is not available yet: you open files from your computer and save copies back to it. Open your first model The quickest way to see what BimCraft does is an IFC file. Click Choose a file in the middle of the screen. Pick an .ifc file in Finder / File Explorer / your file manager and confirm. Wait while the status line at the bottom of the screen says \"Starting the IFC engine…\", then \"Building geometry\". You should see the model in 3D in the middle, a File panel and a Storeys list on the left, and an empty Properties panel on the right. The status line ends with \"Opened\" and the number of elements. You can also drag a file from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager straight onto the page. The screen shows \"Drop to open\" while you drag. The IFC engine loads from the site the first time you open an IFC model, unless BimCraft was saved for offline use. DWG and DXF drawings, project files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. If the app bar's button says Offline ready, everything opens offline. If not, IFC models will not open until you reconnect unless one already opened in this tab. See Online and offline. Going somewhere without a connection? Click Save for offline in the app bar and wait for Offline ready. The saved copy (about 11 MB) includes the IFC engine. See Working offline. Look around Drag with the left mouse button to orbit around the model. Scroll the wheel to zoom. Drag with the right mouse button to pan. On a Mac trackpad, hold Shift and drag instead. Press F or click Fit to see the whole model again. Drag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move two fingers together to pan. Tap Fit to see the whole model again. Do one real thing: fix a tag Click a wall, door or any element in the model. It turns blue, and its properties appear on the right. In the Properties panel, click the value next to Tag. Type the new tag and press Enter. Look at the Changes section that opened on the left: your change is listed with the old and new value. Save a copy Click Save as IFC in the Changes section, or press Ctrl+S. Find the new file in your downloads folder. It is named after the original with \" (edited)\" added, for example Clinic (edited).ifc. Your original file is never changed. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy explains editing in full. Where to go next The BimCraft screen: every panel by name. Coming from other BIM and drawing programs: what feels different if you are used to Revit. Open a whole project folder: point BimCraft at a project folder and let it sort the files out. View a DWG or DXF drawing: sheets, layers and print view."},{"slug":"start-a-new-building","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Start a new building","summary":"Make a blank building model with its levels, draw its walls, place items from the library, and save it as IFC.","section":"start","order":15,"keywords":["new","new building","blank","empty","start from scratch","template","levels","storeys","floors","create","model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart a building model from nothing instead of opening one. BimCraft makes a blank IFC model with a project, a site, the building and its levels. You can place equipment and furniture on the levels from the library and save it as an IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWalls can be drawn now: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E. Doors, windows, floors and roofs are being built next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew building\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a file or folder\u003C/strong\u003E, or on the start page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003EBuilding name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose how many \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E it has (1 to 200).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003ELevel height\u003C/strong\u003E, floor to floor: for example \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E3000 mm\u003C/code\u003E. A plain number is feet in the US, millimeters elsewhere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECreate\u003C/span\u003E. The levels are stacked from 0, each one level height above the last, and named Level 1, Level 2 and so on. They show in the model tree, and the plan of Level 1 opens beside the 3D view with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the walls: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E. Rename levels or change their heights in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace items: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E. The placing bar lists every level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E. The file is named after the building, for example \u003Ccode\u003ESmith&#039;s house.ifc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model is saved in millimeters, as IFC4. Other BIM programs open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStoreys\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel lists only levels with something on them; the model tree lists them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntil you save, the new building counts as unsaved work. BimCraft asks before you open another file or leave, and keeps a copy in this browser in case the tab closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Start a building model from nothing instead of opening one. BimCraft makes a blank IFC model with a project, a site, the building and its levels. You can place equipment and furniture on the levels from the library and save it as an IFC file. Walls can be drawn now: see Draw walls. Doors, windows, floors and roofs are being built next. Steps Click New building under Open a file or folder, or on the start page. Type the Building name. Choose how many Levels it has (1 to 200). Type the Level height, floor to floor: for example 10'-0\", 10'6\" or 3000 mm. A plain number is feet in the US, millimeters elsewhere. Click Create. The levels are stacked from 0, each one level height above the last, and named Level 1, Level 2 and so on. They show in the model tree, and the plan of Level 1 opens beside the 3D view with Draw on. Draw the walls: see Draw walls. Rename levels or change their heights in the Levels panel. Place items: see Place equipment and furniture from the library. The placing bar lists every level. Click Save as IFC. The file is named after the building, for example Smith's house.ifc. Good to know The model is saved in millimeters, as IFC4. Other BIM programs open it. Storeys in the left panel lists only levels with something on them; the model tree lists them all. Until you save, the new building counts as unsaved work. BimCraft asks before you open another file or leave, and keeps a copy in this browser in case the tab closes."},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"bimcraft","title":"The BimCraft screen","summary":"A tour of every bar, panel and button, by its on-screen name, and how the screen changes for each kind of file.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","layout","panels","toolbar","interface","ui","tour","properties panel","browser","app bar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-bar","text":"The app bar","level":2},{"id":"the-left-panel","text":"The left panel","level":2},{"id":"the-stage","text":"The stage","level":2},{"id":"the-properties-panel","text":"The Properties panel","level":2},{"id":"on-a-small-screen","text":"On a small screen","level":2},{"id":"screen-modes","text":"Screen modes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe BimCraft screen has a bar across the top and three areas under it: a panel on the left, the stage in the middle and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. What appears in each area depends on what you opened: an IFC model, a project or family file, a drawing or a folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-bar\"\u003EThe app bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin bar across the top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to BimCraft itself. From left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe CadCraft mark: goes to the CadCraft home page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe app name and a version menu. In the staff build it shows which build you are running and lets you switch to a released version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbout BimCraft\u003C/span\u003E: the app&#039;s page on the site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E: BimCraft&#039;s own settings and the ones every app shares. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E: cycles the screen modes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace#screen-modes\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\" data-hash=\"screen-modes\"\u003EScreen modes\u003C/a\u003E below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E: the light and dark themes. The 3D view and the 2D drawings follow the theme you pick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-left-panel\"\u003EThe left panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe left panel always starts with \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a file or folder\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drop area, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file, or drop a file or folder here\u003C/span\u003E. Click it to choose one file, or drop a file or folder on it (or anywhere on the page).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E: choose a whole project folder. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E: appears in Chrome and Edge after you open a folder with the folder picker or by dropping it. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name to open it again, or the small × to forget it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder it, sections appear as they apply:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhen it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour unsaved edits, newest first, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA model or file card is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFacts about the file: size, schema or release, project, author, element count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA DWG or DXF is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormat, units, entity and layer counts, sheets, linked drawings, equipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA folder is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile and model counts, size, problems found\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the folder\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou opened a file from a folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturns to the folder report\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA checkbox per layer, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA checkbox per storey, top storey first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements of each kind the model holds (the 30 most common)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-stage\"\u003EThe stage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe middle of the screen shows the open file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEmpty\u003C/strong\u003E: &quot;Open a building model.&quot; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E, and a short description of each format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC model\u003C/strong\u003E: the 3D view with a toolbar above it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E; the edit commands \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E); then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdates\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECredits\u003C/span\u003E for library items, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E). A view cube sits in the bottom-right corner. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E chooses icons, labels or both on these buttons and their order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject or family file\u003C/strong\u003E: the file card, with the preview picture, project information and linked files. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/read-rvt-file\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/read-rvt-file\"\u003ERead a project or family file\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG or DXF\u003C/strong\u003E: a row of tabs (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, then one per sheet, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E when the drawing has tagged equipment, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E), the drawing, and live X and Y coordinates in a corner. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFolder\u003C/strong\u003E: the folder report, with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box and tables of models, links, IFC models and drawings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Estatus line\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom of the stage tells you what BimCraft is doing (&quot;Reading the model…&quot;, &quot;Building geometry: 1200 of 5400 elements&quot;) and what went wrong, in red, when something fails. Messages about success fade after a few seconds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor an IFC model: the element you clicked, with its name, type, tag, GlobalId and every property set. Values you can change look like buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EProperties panel fields\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a drawing: how to move around, the drawing&#039;s linked drawings and images and whether each was found, and a list of what is \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a file card or folder: a short note on what to open next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-small-screen\"\u003EOn a small screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the window is narrower than a laptop screen, BimCraft stacks its areas: the stage on top, then the panels underneath. Scroll the page to reach the panels. On a phone they form one column; on a tablet, two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-modes\"\u003EScreen modes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on every platform, cycles three modes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENormal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFull screen, with the bars still showing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFull screen with the app bar hidden. Move the pointer to the top edge, or tap there, to bring it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to normal. Browsers without full screen support (such as on an iPhone) switch only between normal and hidden bars.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The BimCraft screen has a bar across the top and three areas under it: a panel on the left, the stage in the middle and the Properties panel on the right. What appears in each area depends on what you opened: an IFC model, a project or family file, a drawing or a folder. The app bar The thin bar across the top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to BimCraft itself. From left to right: The CadCraft mark: goes to the CadCraft home page. The app name and a version menu. In the staff build it shows which build you are running and lets you switch to a released version. About BimCraft: the app's page on the site. Settings: BimCraft's own settings and the ones every app shares. See BimCraft settings. Full screen: cycles the screen modes. See Screen modes below. Paper and Model: the light and dark themes. The 3D view and the 2D drawings follow the theme you pick. The left panel The left panel always starts with Open a file or folder: The drop area, Choose a file, or drop a file or folder here. Click it to choose one file, or drop a file or folder on it (or anywhere on the page). Open a folder: choose a whole project folder. See Open a whole project folder. Recent folders: appears in Chrome and Edge after you open a folder with the folder picker or by dropping it. Click Reopen and the folder name to open it again, or the small × to forget it. Under it, sections appear as they apply: Section When it shows What is in it Changes An IFC model is open Your unsaved edits, newest first, with Undo and Save as IFC File A model or file card is open Facts about the file: size, schema or release, project, author, element count Drawing A DWG or DXF is open Format, units, entity and layer counts, sheets, linked drawings, equipment Folder A folder is open File and model counts, size, problems found Back to the folder You opened a file from a folder Returns to the folder report Layers A drawing is open A checkbox per layer, with All on and All off Storeys An IFC model is open A checkbox per storey, top storey first Elements An IFC model is open How many elements of each kind the model holds (the 30 most common) The stage The middle of the screen shows the open file. Empty: \"Open a building model.\" with Choose a file and Open a folder, and a short description of each format. IFC model: the 3D view with a toolbar above it: Fit, Top, Front, Side, then Edges and Spaces; the edit commands Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and More (see Copy, paste and duplicate library items); then Library, Equipment, Updates and Credits for library items, and Section (see Cut an IFC model with a section plane). A view cube sits in the bottom-right corner. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Settings, General chooses icons, labels or both on these buttons and their order. Project or family file: the file card, with the preview picture, project information and linked files. See Read a project or family file. DWG or DXF: a row of tabs (Model, then one per sheet, then Equipment when the drawing has tagged equipment, 3D, Fit and Print view), the drawing, and live X and Y coordinates in a corner. See View a DWG or DXF drawing and See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D. Folder: the folder report, with the Start here box and tables of models, links, IFC models and drawings. See Open a whole project folder. The status line at the bottom of the stage tells you what BimCraft is doing (\"Reading the model…\", \"Building geometry: 1200 of 5400 elements\") and what went wrong, in red, when something fails. Messages about success fade after a few seconds. The Properties panel The right panel shows: For an IFC model: the element you clicked, with its name, type, tag, GlobalId and every property set. Values you can change look like buttons. See Properties panel fields. For a drawing: how to move around, the drawing's linked drawings and images and whether each was found, and a list of what is Not drawn yet. For a file card or folder: a short note on what to open next. On a small screen When the window is narrower than a laptop screen, BimCraft stacks its areas: the stage on top, then the panels underneath. Scroll the page to reach the panels. On a phone they form one column; on a tablet, two. Screen modes The Full screen button in the app bar, or Ctrl+Shift+F on every platform, cycles three modes: Normal. Full screen, with the bars still showing. Full screen with the app bar hidden. Move the pointer to the top edge, or tap there, to bring it back. Press Esc to go back to normal. Browsers without full screen support (such as on an iPhone) switch only between normal and hidden bars. Related Keys and mouse Files BimCraft opens and saves"},{"slug":"coordinates","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Coordinates in BimCraft","summary":"Where you are working: the pointer's position in the plan and the 3D view, the level you are on, the project origin and the site's survey point.","section":"start","order":22,"keywords":["coordinates","where am I","position","pointer","readout","x y z","level","storey","project origin","origin","base point","survey point","site","map coordinates","eastings","northings","georeference","units","location"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"where-the-numbers-show","text":"Where the numbers show","level":2},{"id":"the-two-markers","text":"The two markers","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKnow where you are in a model at a glance: which level you are drawing on, how far the pointer is from the project origin, and where the site&#039;s survey point is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-numbers-show\"\u003EWhere the numbers show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOver the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E (bottom right, beside the view cube): move the pointer over the model. The box shows the level the point is on and how high above that level&#039;s floor it is, then its \u003Cstrong\u003EX, Y and Z\u003C/strong\u003E. Over empty space it reads the point on the floor of the lowest level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn the plan\u003C/strong\u003E (bottom right, while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E is on): the level the plan shows and the height of its floor, and the pointer&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EX and Y\u003C/strong\u003E while it is over the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the file has a map conversion (it was exported with map coordinates), both boxes add the \u003Cstrong\u003Eeastings and northings\u003C/strong\u003E on the map grid, in the map&#039;s own unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery X, Y and Z is measured from the \u003Cstrong\u003Eproject origin\u003C/strong\u003E, in the units chosen in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E panel (feet and inches, or millimetres). Positive Y points to the model&#039;s north, which is not always true north.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-two-markers\"\u003EThe two markers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject origin\u003C/strong\u003E (a red circle with a cross): the point 0, 0, 0 every coordinate in the model is measured from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurvey point\u003C/strong\u003E (a blue circle with a triangle): the site&#039;s reference point, the one its latitude, longitude and elevation, or its map coordinates, are given for. In the 3D view, point at it to read where it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the site sits on the project origin, as in a new building drawn here, one marker shows both. Click the crosshair button in either box to hide or show the markers; BimCraft remembers your choice in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe numbers change as the pointer moves, and the plan&#039;s box names the level you chose in the level list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe markers stay put while you orbit, pan and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo read a point&#039;s latitude and longitude, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, face areas and locations in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen the 3D box shows nothing until you use a mouse or a pen: a finger moves the view instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"What you'll do Know where you are in a model at a glance: which level you are drawing on, how far the pointer is from the project origin, and where the site's survey point is. Where the numbers show Over the 3D view (bottom right, beside the view cube): move the pointer over the model. The box shows the level the point is on and how high above that level's floor it is, then its X, Y and Z. Over empty space it reads the point on the floor of the lowest level. On the plan (bottom right, while Draw is on): the level the plan shows and the height of its floor, and the pointer's X and Y while it is over the plan. When the file has a map conversion (it was exported with map coordinates), both boxes add the eastings and northings on the map grid, in the map's own unit. Every X, Y and Z is measured from the project origin, in the units chosen in the Levels panel (feet and inches, or millimetres). Positive Y points to the model's north, which is not always true north. The two markers Project origin (a red circle with a cross): the point 0, 0, 0 every coordinate in the model is measured from. Survey point (a blue circle with a triangle): the site's reference point, the one its latitude, longitude and elevation, or its map coordinates, are given for. In the 3D view, point at it to read where it is. When the site sits on the project origin, as in a new building drawn here, one marker shows both. Click the crosshair button in either box to hide or show the markers; BimCraft remembers your choice in this browser. What you should see The numbers change as the pointer moves, and the plan's box names the level you chose in the level list. The markers stay put while you orbit, pan and zoom. To read a point's latitude and longitude, use Measure › Location: see Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D. On a touch screen the 3D box shows nothing until you use a mouse or a pen: a finger moves the view instead."},{"slug":"files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Files BimCraft opens and saves","summary":"Every format BimCraft opens, what it keeps, what it saves, where saved files go, and what changes between browsers and when you are offline.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["formats","open","save","export","download","ifc","rvt","rfa","rte","rft","dwg","dxf","csv","upload","privacy","file size"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-opens","text":"What it opens","level":2},{"id":"not-opened","text":"Not opened","level":3},{"id":"what-it-saves","text":"What it saves","level":2},{"id":"saving-a-second-time","text":"Saving a second time","level":2},{"id":"large-files-and-memory","text":"Large files and memory","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft reads files on your computer, in the browser tab. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded\u003C/strong\u003E to CadCraft or anyone else. When you save, BimCraft hands the browser a new file to download; it never writes over a file you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-opens\"\u003EWhat it opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model in 3D, storeys, element counts and every element&#039;s properties. Names, tags and single property values can be changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA file card: release, build, worksharing, project information, family parameters, linked files and the preview picture. No 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing in 2D: model space, every sheet, layers, linked drawings, images and print view. Model space in 3D too. Formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same as DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eany\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA report of every model and drawing in it. Any IFC, model or drawing in the report opens with a click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E goes into detail.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft decides what a file is by looking at its first bytes as well as its name, so a project file with an unusual extension still opens as a file card.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"not-opened\"\u003ENot opened\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E: BimCraft says &quot;.ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.&quot; Unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E (it holds a plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E) or export again as plain IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDGN drawings are listed in a folder report but do not open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPDF, images and office files do not open on their own. Images are used only as pictures inside a DWG.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-saves\"\u003EWhat it saves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou click\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new IFC file with your edits, named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Eoriginal name\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour browser&#039;s downloads folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E in the equipment schedule\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CSV of the items in the list, named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Edrawing name\u003C/em\u003E equipment.csv&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour browser&#039;s downloads folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThat is all BimCraft writes. It does not save DWG, DXF or project files, and it never changes the files you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge save downloads to the folder set in their settings, usually Downloads. To be asked where to save each time, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; in the browser&#039;s download settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox saves to the Downloads folder unless you set it to &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; in its settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari saves to Downloads unless you choose another folder under \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari &gt; Settings &gt; General &gt; File download location\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-a-second-time\"\u003ESaving a second time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save downloads the whole model with \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery edit since you opened it\u003C/strong\u003E, not only the edits made after the last save. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list starts empty again after each save, so you cannot undo past a save. To start again from the original, open the original file again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"large-files-and-memory\"\u003ELarge files and memory\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E files are read completely into the tab&#039;s memory, and the 3D geometry takes more memory on top. A model of a few hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Above 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off to save memory (&quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject files\u003C/strong\u003E are not read in full. BimCraft reads only the small parts it needs, about 40 KB to 100 KB however large the file is, so a 500 MB central model on a network drive opens in a moment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/strong\u003E files are read completely. A very large drawing shows &quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFolders\u003C/strong\u003E up to 20,000 files open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E for more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening files never needs the internet. One reader loads from the site the first time you need it: the IFC engine, the first time you open an IFC model (&quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;). The DWG and DXF readers are part of the app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the app bar&#039;s button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, every format opens as usual. If not, an IFC model cannot open unless one already opened in this tab: reconnect first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use BimCraft with no connection, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar while you are online and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. The saved copy is about 11 MB because it includes the IFC engine, so IFC models open offline too. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E explains saved copies in general.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. Save your edited copies on your own computer or your office&#039;s file server, as you would any other file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"BimCraft reads files on your computer, in the browser tab. Nothing is uploaded to CadCraft or anyone else. When you save, BimCraft hands the browser a new file to download; it never writes over a file you opened. What it opens Format Extensions What you get IFC .ifc The model in 3D, storeys, element counts and every element's properties. Names, tags and single property values can be changed. Project and family files .rvt .rfa .rte .rft A file card: release, build, worksharing, project information, family parameters, linked files and the preview picture. No 3D. DWG .dwg The drawing in 2D: model space, every sheet, layers, linked drawings, images and print view. Model space in 3D too. Formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked. DXF .dxf The same as DWG. A folder any A report of every model and drawing in it. Any IFC, model or drawing in the report opens with a click. What BimCraft reads from each format goes into detail. BimCraft decides what a file is by looking at its first bytes as well as its name, so a project file with an unusual extension still opens as a file card. Not opened .ifczip and .ifcxml: BimCraft says \".ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.\" Unzip the .ifczip (it holds a plain .ifc) or export again as plain IFC. DGN drawings are listed in a folder report but do not open. PDF, images and office files do not open on their own. Images are used only as pictures inside a DWG. What it saves You click You get Where Save as IFC A new IFC file with your edits, named \"original name (edited).ifc\" Your browser's downloads folder Download CSV in the equipment schedule A CSV of the items in the list, named \"drawing name equipment.csv\" Your browser's downloads folder That is all BimCraft writes. It does not save DWG, DXF or project files, and it never changes the files you opened. Chrome and Edge save downloads to the folder set in their settings, usually Downloads. To be asked where to save each time, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" in the browser's download settings. Firefox saves to the Downloads folder unless you set it to \"Always ask you where to save files\" in its settings. Safari saves to Downloads unless you choose another folder under Safari \u003E Settings \u003E General \u003E File download location. Saving a second time Each save downloads the whole model with every edit since you opened it, not only the edits made after the last save. The Changes list starts empty again after each save, so you cannot undo past a save. To start again from the original, open the original file again. Large files and memory IFC files are read completely into the tab's memory, and the 3D geometry takes more memory on top. A model of a few hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Above 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off to save memory (\"Edges are off for very large models\"). Project files are not read in full. BimCraft reads only the small parts it needs, about 40 KB to 100 KB however large the file is, so a 500 MB central model on a network drive opens in a moment. DWG and DXF files are read completely. A very large drawing shows \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" Folders up to 20,000 files open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read. See Browsers, devices and memory for more. Online and offline Opening files never needs the internet. One reader loads from the site the first time you need it: the IFC engine, the first time you open an IFC model (\"Starting the IFC engine…\"). The DWG and DXF readers are part of the app. You are offline. If the app bar's button says Offline ready, every format opens as usual. If not, an IFC model cannot open unless one already opened in this tab: reconnect first. See Online and offline. To use BimCraft with no connection, click Save for offline in the app bar while you are online and wait for Offline ready. The saved copy is about 11 MB because it includes the IFC engine, so IFC models open offline too. Working offline explains saved copies in general. Cloud saving Cloud saving is not available yet. Save your edited copies on your own computer or your office's file server, as you would any other file. See Your files."},{"slug":"open-ifc-model","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open an IFC model and look around in 3D","summary":"Open an IFC file, orbit, pan and zoom, and use Fit, Top, Front and Side to get your bearings.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["ifc","3d","orbit","pan","zoom","fit","top view","front view","side view","navigate","viewcube","camera"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen an IFC model, move around it in 3D and jump to the standard views. About five minutes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou need an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file. Most BIM programs export one; in Revit it is \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Export &gt; IFC\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E (or the drop area at the top left).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose your \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the status line to finish. It shows &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot; the first time, then &quot;Reading the model…&quot; and &quot;Building geometry: … of … elements&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the model: it opens in a three-quarter view, fitted to the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model to look straight down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E to look at the front, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E to look from the side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to go back to the three-quarter view of the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model in the middle, with thin dark edges for a drawing-like look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left: the file name, size, IFC schema (for example IFC2X3 or IFC4), project name, phase, the program it was exported from, author, export date, and element and triangle counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E lists under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Opened \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E: \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E elements in \u003Cem\u003Es\u003C/em\u003E s&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee everything\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad: drag with one finger pressed to orbit; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag to pan; scroll with two fingers or pinch to zoom. A two-finger click is a right-click, and you can drag with it to pan, but \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag is easier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch with two fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove two fingers together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee everything\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOrbiting always turns around a point in the middle of the model. Panning moves that point, so after a long pan the orbit may feel off-center: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to reset it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting named views.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft has four fixed views (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E), the view cube in the bottom-right corner and free orbit. It does not save views.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for plans and sections.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no 2D plans or section boxes in this build. Cut the model with one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E), or turn storeys off: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a project file and expecting 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file opens as a file card. Export IFC first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Unzip it, or export plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA very large model makes the tab slow or crash.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/inspect-element\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/inspect-element\"\u003EPick an element and read its properties\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open an IFC model, move around it in 3D and jump to the standard views. About five minutes. You need an .ifc file. Most BIM programs export one; in Revit it is File \u003E Export \u003E IFC. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC. Steps Click Choose a file (or the drop area at the top left). Choose your .ifc file and confirm. Wait for the status line to finish. It shows \"Starting the IFC engine…\" the first time, then \"Reading the model…\" and \"Building geometry: … of … elements\". Look at the model: it opens in a three-quarter view, fitted to the screen. Click Top in the toolbar above the model to look straight down. Click Front to look at the front, then Side to look from the side. Click Fit, or press F, to go back to the three-quarter view of the whole model. What you should see The model in the middle, with thin dark edges for a drawing-like look. The File panel on the left: the file name, size, IFC schema (for example IFC2X3 or IFC4), project name, phase, the program it was exported from, author, export date, and element and triangle counts. The Storeys and Elements lists under it. The status line: \"Opened file name: n elements in s s\". Moving around To Do this Orbit Drag with the left button Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift (or Ctrl) and drag with the left button Zoom Scroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button See everything Press F or click Fit On a trackpad: drag with one finger pressed to orbit; hold Shift or Ctrl and drag to pan; scroll with two fingers or pinch to zoom. A two-finger click is a right-click, and you can drag with it to pan, but Shift and drag is easier. To Do this Orbit Drag with one finger Zoom Pinch with two fingers Pan Move two fingers together See everything Tap Fit Orbiting always turns around a point in the middle of the model. Panning moves that point, so after a long pan the orbit may feel off-center: click Fit to reset it. Common mistakes Expecting named views. BimCraft has four fixed views (Fit, Top, Front, Side), the view cube in the bottom-right corner and free orbit. It does not save views. Looking for plans and sections. There are no 2D plans or section boxes in this build. Cut the model with one Section plane (Cut an IFC model with a section plane), or turn storeys off: Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges. Opening a project file and expecting 3D. A project file opens as a file card. Export IFC first. Opening an .ifczip. Unzip it, or export plain .ifc. A very large model makes the tab slow or crash. See Browsers, devices and memory. Next Pick an element and read its properties."},{"slug":"draw-walls","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Draw walls","summary":"Draw walls on the plan of a level by clicking their corners or typing lengths; corners join by themselves, and the walls are saved as IFC.","section":"tasks","order":16,"keywords":["curved","arc","location line","justification","wall","walls","draw","plan","floor plan","room","corner","join","wall type","2x6","2x4","cmu","concrete","height","level","levels","storey","typed length","ortho","snap"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"open-the-plan","text":"Open the plan","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-room","text":"Draw a room","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-drawing-reference","text":"Choose the drawing reference","level":2},{"id":"location-line","text":"Location line","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-curved-wall","text":"Draw a curved wall","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-instead-of-clicking","text":"Type a length instead of clicking","level":2},{"id":"snaps","text":"Snaps","level":2},{"id":"change-undo-delete","text":"Change, undo, delete","level":2},{"id":"levels","text":"Levels","level":2},{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the walls of a building on a plan of each level. Click where a wall starts and then each corner; BimCraft joins the corners for you, gives each wall its layers (siding, sheathing, studs, drywall …) and runs it up to the level above. The 3D view shows the walls as you go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-plan\"\u003EOpen the plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart a new building (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/start-a-new-building\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/start-a-new-building\"\u003EStart a new building\u003C/a\u003E) or open an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. A new building opens with it on already.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan of a level shows on the left and the 3D view on the right. Pick the level in the list at the top of the plan, or with the round button beside it in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E for the plan alone, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit\u003C/span\u003E for both, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E to close the plan. When the window is narrow (or the interface size large), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit\u003C/span\u003E puts the plan above the 3D view instead of beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plan cuts the level 4&#039;-0&quot; (1200 mm) above its floor, as a floor plan does. Scroll to zoom, drag with the middle or right button (or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag) to pan; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, a plain drag pans too. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the pointer over the plan fits it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-room\"\u003EDraw a room\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the wall type in the first list, for example \u003Cstrong\u003EExterior 2×6\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EInterior 2×4\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose its height: \u003Cstrong\u003EUp to the level above\u003C/strong\u003E (the wall follows if that level moves) or \u003Cstrong\u003EFixed height\u003C/strong\u003E and type it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the first wall starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner. Every click makes a wall; the next one starts where it ended. The length and angle show beside the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first point again to close the room. The status line says how many walls the room has, and in centerline mode every wall gets its outside layer on the outside of the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or right-click, or double-click) to stop without closing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corners join by themselves: two walls meet at a mitre, a wall that ends on another one stops at its face (a T), and three or more walls at one point share the corner. Walls that cross are left as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-drawing-reference\"\u003EChoose the drawing reference\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ECenterline\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EInside face\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside face\u003C/strong\u003E before the first click. With a face selected, your clicks and typed lengths describe that face; BimCraft offsets the wall centerline by half the total thickness and joins consecutive corners. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside on left\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside on right\u003C/strong\u003E relative to the direction you draw. For a closed outline drawn counterclockwise, the outside is on the right; clockwise, on the left. Face drawing keeps your chosen outside side when you close the loop. Centerline drawing still sets the outside automatically on closing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging the wall type, reference or outside side ends the current chain. Near a reversing corner, long mitres are capped by leaving square ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing, an endpoint across empty space can align horizontally or vertically with a wall endpoint or an earlier corner. A dashed guide joins that point to the snapped pointer. Shift keeps the drawing direction square; alignment then stops along that direction. Nearby endpoints, midpoints and wall intersections keep priority over distant alignment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"location-line\"\u003ELocation line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003EReference\u003C/strong\u003E on \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside face\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EInside face\u003C/strong\u003E, the wall keeps that face as its location line. Later, if the wall gets thicker or its outside is flipped, that face stays where it is and the wall grows or flips around it. A straight wall that meets it at a corner follows, so the corner still closes. You can change the location line afterwards in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/change-a-wall-or-door\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/change-a-wall-or-door\"\u003EChange a wall, door or window\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-curved-wall\"\u003EDraw a curved wall\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cstrong\u003ECurved\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E list of the options row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the wall starts, then where it ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point on its arc. Or type the arc height and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe chain goes on from the end of the wall. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA curved wall joins the walls at its ends (corners and T joins work), makes rooms, and holds doors and windows along its arc. They stay straight units, square to the wall at their middle, and their distance is measured along the arc. A wall can turn at most three quarters of a circle. It snaps to its ends and to the middle of its arc.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet for curved walls: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim / extend\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner\u003C/span\u003E, framing, automatic plan dimensions and snapping along the arc. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-instead-of-clicking\"\u003EType a length instead of clicking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first click, type and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDraws\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E3810\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea wall that long, in the direction the pointer shows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethat long, at that angle (degrees, counter-clockwise from the right)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@3000,0\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eto a point that far from the last one, across and up\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1200,3400\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eto that point on the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping a number while the Wall tool is on goes straight into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E box. In feet and inches a plain number is inches; in millimeters it is millimeters. You can always type the unit: \u003Ccode\u003E3.5 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snaps\"\u003ESnaps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWall ends, their face corners and wall midpoints snap when the pointer is near them (a square, a triangle).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe pointer snaps to a wall&#039;s center line or either face (a cross).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFrom the last point, the direction holds at every 15° when it is close; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it at 0°, 90°, 180° or 270°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFree directions round the length to 1/2&quot; (or 10 mm).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-undo-delete\"\u003EChange, undo, delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery wall is one step in \u003Cstrong\u003EChanges\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E take walls back and put them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, click a wall in the plan to pick it; the 3D view and \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E follow. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the picked walls, and the walls they joined are cleaned up again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"levels\"\u003ELevels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E panel lists every level with its elevation and height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a new name, elevation or height and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Changing a level&#039;s height (or elevation) moves the levels above it by the same amount, and walls that run up to a level follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a level on top\u003C/span\u003E adds a level above the top one, as tall as it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E chooses feet and inches or millimeters for everything the drawing tools show and read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E. Each wall is an IFC wall with its center line, its joined shape, its type and layers, \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WallCommon\u003C/code\u003E and base quantities (length, height, areas and volumes), so other BIM programs open it and schedule it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls are drawn on their center line. The thickness comes from the wall type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan shows walls drawn here filled, and everything else (library items, models from other programs) cut by the same plane, with what lies below the cut in thin lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls made by other programs show in the plan but can&#039;t be changed with these tools yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Draw the walls of a building on a plan of each level. Click where a wall starts and then each corner; BimCraft joins the corners for you, gives each wall its layers (siding, sheathing, studs, drywall …) and runs it up to the level above. The 3D view shows the walls as you go. Open the plan Start a new building (Start a new building) or open an IFC model. Click Draw in the toolbar. A new building opens with it on already. The plan of a level shows on the left and the 3D view on the right. Pick the level in the list at the top of the plan, or with the round button beside it in the Levels panel. Use Plan for the plan alone, Split for both, 3D to close the plan. When the window is narrow (or the interface size large), Split puts the plan above the 3D view instead of beside it. The plan cuts the level 4'-0\" (1200 mm) above its floor, as a floor plan does. Scroll to zoom, drag with the middle or right button (or hold Space and drag) to pan; with Select on, a plain drag pans too. F with the pointer over the plan fits it. Draw a room Click Wall (or press W). Choose the wall type in the first list, for example Exterior 2×6 or Interior 2×4. Choose its height: Up to the level above (the wall follows if that level moves) or Fixed height and type it. Click where the first wall starts. Click each corner. Every click makes a wall; the next one starts where it ended. The length and angle show beside the pointer. Click the first point again to close the room. The status line says how many walls the room has, and in centerline mode every wall gets its outside layer on the outside of the room. Press Esc (or right-click, or double-click) to stop without closing. The corners join by themselves: two walls meet at a mitre, a wall that ends on another one stops at its face (a T), and three or more walls at one point share the corner. Walls that cross are left as they are. Choose the drawing reference Choose Centerline, Inside face or Outside face before the first click. With a face selected, your clicks and typed lengths describe that face; BimCraft offsets the wall centerline by half the total thickness and joins consecutive corners. Choose Outside on left or Outside on right relative to the direction you draw. For a closed outline drawn counterclockwise, the outside is on the right; clockwise, on the left. Face drawing keeps your chosen outside side when you close the loop. Centerline drawing still sets the outside automatically on closing. Changing the wall type, reference or outside side ends the current chain. Near a reversing corner, long mitres are capped by leaving square ends. While drawing, an endpoint across empty space can align horizontally or vertically with a wall endpoint or an earlier corner. A dashed guide joins that point to the snapped pointer. Shift keeps the drawing direction square; alignment then stops along that direction. Nearby endpoints, midpoints and wall intersections keep priority over distant alignment. Location line With Reference on Outside face or Inside face, the wall keeps that face as its location line. Later, if the wall gets thicker or its outside is flipped, that face stays where it is and the wall grows or flips around it. A straight wall that meets it at a corner follows, so the corner still closes. You can change the location line afterwards in Properties: see Change a wall, door or window. Draw a curved wall Click Wall, then choose Curved in the Shape list of the options row. Click where the wall starts, then where it ends. Click a point on its arc. Or type the arc height and press Enter. The chain goes on from the end of the wall. Press Esc to end it. A curved wall joins the walls at its ends (corners and T joins work), makes rooms, and holds doors and windows along its arc. They stay straight units, square to the wall at their middle, and their distance is measured along the arc. A wall can turn at most three quarters of a circle. It snaps to its ends and to the middle of its arc. Not built yet for curved walls: Trim / extend and Corner, framing, automatic plan dimensions and snapping along the arc. See What is not built yet. Type a length instead of clicking After the first click, type and press Enter: Type Draws 12'6\" or 3810 a wall that long, in the direction the pointer shows 12'6\"\u003C90 that long, at that angle (degrees, counter-clockwise from the right) @3000,0 to a point that far from the last one, across and up 1200,3400 to that point on the plan Typing a number while the Wall tool is on goes straight into the Length box. In feet and inches a plain number is inches; in millimeters it is millimeters. You can always type the unit: 3.5 m, 10 ft, 6 1/2\". Snaps Wall ends, their face corners and wall midpoints snap when the pointer is near them (a square, a triangle). The pointer snaps to a wall's center line or either face (a cross). From the last point, the direction holds at every 15° when it is close; hold Shift to keep it at 0°, 90°, 180° or 270°. Free directions round the length to 1/2\" (or 10 mm). Change, undo, delete Every wall is one step in Changes: Undo and Redo take walls back and put them back. With Select on, click a wall in the plan to pick it; the 3D view and Properties follow. Delete deletes the picked walls, and the walls they joined are cleaned up again. Levels The Levels panel lists every level with its elevation and height. Type a new name, elevation or height and press Enter. Changing a level's height (or elevation) moves the levels above it by the same amount, and walls that run up to a level follow. Add a level on top adds a level above the top one, as tall as it. Units chooses feet and inches or millimeters for everything the drawing tools show and read. Save Click Save as IFC. Each wall is an IFC wall with its center line, its joined shape, its type and layers, Pset_WallCommon and base quantities (length, height, areas and volumes), so other BIM programs open it and schedule it. Good to know Walls are drawn on their center line. The thickness comes from the wall type. The plan shows walls drawn here filled, and everything else (library items, models from other programs) cut by the same plane, with what lies below the cut in thin lines. Walls made by other programs show in the plan but can't be changed with these tools yet."},{"slug":"doors-and-windows","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Doors and windows","summary":"Put doors and windows in drawn walls: point at a wall, see the swing and the distances to the corners, click; the wall opening cuts itself.","section":"tasks","order":17,"keywords":["door","doors","window","windows","opening","swing","hinge","hand","flip","sliding door","pocket door","garage door","double door","casement","single hung","double hung","slider","fixed window","sill","header","bifold","closet door","French doors","barn door","sidelites","entry door","awning","picture window","transom"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"place-a-door","text":"Place a door","level":2},{"id":"place-a-window","text":"Place a window","level":2},{"id":"move-flip-delete","text":"Move, flip, delete","level":2},{"id":"what-gets-refused","text":"What gets refused","level":2},{"id":"what-is-saved","text":"What is saved","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"basement-egress-window-and-well","text":"Basement egress window and well","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut doors and windows in walls you drew (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E). Point at a wall and the door or window shows where it would go, which way it swings and how far it is from each corner. Click, and the opening cuts itself out of the wall in the plan and in 3D.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-door\"\u003EPlace a door\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E on, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDoor\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the type: \u003Cstrong\u003ESingle swing door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDouble swing door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESliding door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPocket door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGarage door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBifold closet door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFrench doors\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBarn door (on the wall)\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EEntry door with sidelites\u003C/strong\u003E. Its usual size fills \u003Cstrong\u003EW\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EH\u003C/strong\u003E; pick another from the size list beside it, or type others, for example \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;-8&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E800\u003C/code\u003E. The list also offers the door families of this model (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at a wall. The door&#039;s symbol follows the pointer along the wall, and the distance from each end of that face shows on the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe door swings to the side of the wall the pointer is on. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E) to put the hinges at the other end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The door is placed and the tool stays on for the next one. Point at the middle of a wall and the door centers itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo place it at an exact distance, point at the wall near the corner you measure from, type the distance (for example \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The distance runs from the nearer end of the wall face to the edge of the door.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-window\"\u003EPlace a window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the type: \u003Cstrong\u003EFixed window\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESingle hung window\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDouble hung window\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ECasement window\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESliding window\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAwning\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPicture window\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ETransom\u003C/strong\u003E, and its \u003Cstrong\u003EW\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EH\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESill\u003C/strong\u003E (the height of the bottom of the window above the floor).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at a wall and click, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as for doors. A window sits on the outside of the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-flip-delete\"\u003EMove, flip, delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, drag a door or window along its wall. Drag it onto another wall to move it there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the picked doors and windows; the wall closes up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMoving or deleting a wall moves or deletes what is in it. When a wall&#039;s start moves along its own line, its doors and windows keep their place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E take each placement, move or delete back and forth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-gets-refused\"\u003EWhat gets refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt doesn&#039;t fit in that wall.\u003C/strong\u003E The door or window is wider than the wall, or would stick out of its end. Try a shorter width or a longer wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt is taller than the wall.\u003C/strong\u003E The sill plus the height is above the top of the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-saved\"\u003EWhat is saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach door or window is an IFC door or window with its size, operation (for example single swing left), its size&#039;s type (one type for each size of its family), \u003Ccode\u003EPset_DoorCommon\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WindowCommon\u003C/code\u003E and base quantities, filling an IFC opening in its wall. The wall&#039;s net side area and volume leave the opening out. Other BIM programs open it as a door in a wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan shows doors open at 90° with their swing, sliding doors as two panels, pocket doors sliding into the wall, garage doors as a dashed line overhead, and windows with their frame and glass lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe width is the overall width of the frame; the door leaf is the width less the two jambs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopying drawn doors and windows is not built yet: place another one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"basement-egress-window-and-well\"\u003EBasement egress window and well\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EBasement egress with well\u003C/strong\u003E in the window type list. It starts as a 36 × 48 inch casement with a sill 36 inches above the level. The well is on the wall&#039;s outside, with at least 48 inches clear width, 36 inches projection, and a bottom 6 inches below the sill. It appears as dashed lines in plan and as three open-topped sides in 3D. The well is part of the window&#039;s IFC body, so saving, moving, resizing or deleting the window carries it along. Resizing keeps at least 6 inches beside each jamb.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are modeling defaults, not a certification of egress compliance. Check the manufacturer&#039;s actual clear opening, local requirements, grade, drainage and any required ladder separately. Well depth and height are preset dimensions; they do not follow terrain.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put doors and windows in walls you drew (Draw walls). Point at a wall and the door or window shows where it would go, which way it swings and how far it is from each corner. Click, and the opening cuts itself out of the wall in the plan and in 3D. Place a door With Draw on, click Door (or press D). Choose the type: Single swing door, Double swing door, Sliding door, Pocket door, Garage door, Bifold closet door, French doors, Barn door (on the wall) or Entry door with sidelites. Its usual size fills W and H; pick another from the size list beside it, or type others, for example 2'-8\" or 800. The list also offers the door families of this model (see Families and their sizes). Point at a wall. The door's symbol follows the pointer along the wall, and the distance from each end of that face shows on the plan. The door swings to the side of the wall the pointer is on. Press H (or click Flip) to put the hinges at the other end. Click. The door is placed and the tool stays on for the next one. Point at the middle of a wall and the door centers itself. To place it at an exact distance, point at the wall near the corner you measure from, type the distance (for example 2' or 600) and press Enter. The distance runs from the nearer end of the wall face to the edge of the door. Place a window Click Window (or press N). Choose the type: Fixed window, Single hung window, Double hung window, Casement window, Sliding window, Awning, Picture window or Transom, and its W, H and Sill (the height of the bottom of the window above the floor). Point at a wall and click, or type a distance and press Enter as for doors. A window sits on the outside of the wall. Move, flip, delete With Select on, drag a door or window along its wall. Drag it onto another wall to move it there. Delete deletes the picked doors and windows; the wall closes up. Moving or deleting a wall moves or deletes what is in it. When a wall's start moves along its own line, its doors and windows keep their place. Undo and Redo take each placement, move or delete back and forth. What gets refused It doesn't fit in that wall. The door or window is wider than the wall, or would stick out of its end. Try a shorter width or a longer wall. It is taller than the wall. The sill plus the height is above the top of the wall. What is saved Each door or window is an IFC door or window with its size, operation (for example single swing left), its size's type (one type for each size of its family), Pset_DoorCommon or Pset_WindowCommon and base quantities, filling an IFC opening in its wall. The wall's net side area and volume leave the opening out. Other BIM programs open it as a door in a wall. Good to know The plan shows doors open at 90° with their swing, sliding doors as two panels, pocket doors sliding into the wall, garage doors as a dashed line overhead, and windows with their frame and glass lines. The width is the overall width of the frame; the door leaf is the width less the two jambs. Copying drawn doors and windows is not built yet: place another one. Basement egress window and well Choose Basement egress with well in the window type list. It starts as a 36 × 48 inch casement with a sill 36 inches above the level. The well is on the wall's outside, with at least 48 inches clear width, 36 inches projection, and a bottom 6 inches below the sill. It appears as dashed lines in plan and as three open-topped sides in 3D. The well is part of the window's IFC body, so saving, moving, resizing or deleting the window carries it along. Resizing keeps at least 6 inches beside each jamb. These are modeling defaults, not a certification of egress compliance. Check the manufacturer's actual clear opening, local requirements, grade, drainage and any required ladder separately. Well depth and height are preset dimensions; they do not follow terrain."},{"slug":"change-a-wall-or-door","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Change a wall, door or window","summary":"Click a drawn wall, door or window and change its type, size, height or place in Properties, or drag it in the plan; each change is one undo step.","section":"tasks","order":18,"keywords":["curved","arc","arc height","radius","location line","justification","change","edit","properties","wall type","thickness","length","height","base offset","top","level","flip","outside","door size","window size","sill","distance","move","grip","drag","change type","several"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"change-a-wall","text":"Change a wall","level":2},{"id":"drag-a-wall-in-the-plan","text":"Drag a wall in the plan","level":2},{"id":"change-a-door-or-window","text":"Change a door or window","level":2},{"id":"change-several-at-once","text":"Change several at once","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange what you drew: pick a wall, door or window, then type a new number in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E or drag it in the plan. The plan and the 3D view follow, the corners join again, and doors and windows stay in their walls.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-wall\"\u003EChange a wall\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E on and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, click the wall in the plan (or in the 3D view).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows a box with the wall&#039;s numbers. Change one and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall type\u003C/strong\u003E: another of the starter types. The thickness and the layers change with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThickness\u003C/strong\u003E: any thickness. The type keeps its other layers and its main layer (the studs or the block) takes the difference, for example &quot;Exterior 2×6, 200 mm&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E: the wall&#039;s end moves along its line. A wall joined at that end comes with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall height\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EUp to the level above\u003C/strong\u003E follows that level (typing a height then sets how far below or above it the top is), \u003Cstrong\u003EFixed height\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the number you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBase offset\u003C/strong\u003E: how far above the level the wall starts. Its top stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOutside\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E puts the outside layers on the other face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELocation line\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003ECenter line\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside face\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EInside face\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft face\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ERight face\u003C/strong\u003E. Changing it moves nothing. Later, when the wall gets thicker (by type or \u003Cstrong\u003EThickness\u003C/strong\u003E) or its outside is flipped, the location line stays put and the wall grows or flips around it. A straight wall meeting it at a corner follows, so the corner still closes. The picked wall&#039;s location line is drawn dashed in the plan. Pick several walls to set it for all of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArc height\u003C/strong\u003E (curved walls): how far the middle bows off a straight line. 0 makes it straight; a negative value bows it to the other side. The radius shows beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach change is one step in \u003Cstrong\u003EChanges\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-a-wall-in-the-plan\"\u003EDrag a wall in the plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen one wall is picked, it shows three grips: a square at each end and a circle in the middle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag an end to make the wall longer, shorter or turn it. It snaps like the Wall tool; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps it square.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the middle to move the whole wall. Walls joined to its ends stretch to follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA picked curved wall shows grips at its ends and a round grip at the middle of its arc. Drag the round grip to bend it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDoors and windows stay in the wall. A wall too short for what it holds is refused with &quot;It doesn&#039;t fit in that wall.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-door-or-window\"\u003EChange a door or window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the door or window in the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E (another door or window of the same kind), \u003Cstrong\u003EWidth\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EHeight\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESill\u003C/strong\u003E (windows), \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom the start\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom the end\u003C/strong\u003E (the distance from each end of the wall face to its edge), \u003Cstrong\u003EHinges\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E (doors with one leaf and casement windows; sliding, pocket, barn and bifold doors say \u003Cstrong\u003EOpens to\u003C/strong\u003E instead; fixed, hung and picture windows and double doors have none), and \u003Cstrong\u003ESwings to\u003C/strong\u003E (doors) or \u003Cstrong\u003EFrame side\u003C/strong\u003E (windows) \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr drag it along its wall, or onto another wall: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/doors-and-windows\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-several-at-once\"\u003EChange several at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick several walls (or several doors, or several windows) with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E offers what they share: \u003Cstrong\u003EChange type…\u003C/strong\u003E, the height, the size. One change sets all of them, and one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E takes all of it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe numbers show in the units chosen in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls, doors and windows from other programs show their IFC data in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E as before, without this box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Change what you drew: pick a wall, door or window, then type a new number in Properties or drag it in the plan. The plan and the 3D view follow, the corners join again, and doors and windows stay in their walls. Change a wall With Draw on and Select on, click the wall in the plan (or in the 3D view). Properties shows a box with the wall's numbers. Change one and press Enter: Wall type: another of the starter types. The thickness and the layers change with it. Thickness: any thickness. The type keeps its other layers and its main layer (the studs or the block) takes the difference, for example \"Exterior 2×6, 200 mm\". Length: the wall's end moves along its line. A wall joined at that end comes with it. Wall height: Up to the level above follows that level (typing a height then sets how far below or above it the top is), Fixed height keeps the number you type. Base offset: how far above the level the wall starts. Its top stays where it was. Outside: Flip puts the outside layers on the other face. Location line: Center line, Outside face, Inside face, Left face or Right face. Changing it moves nothing. Later, when the wall gets thicker (by type or Thickness) or its outside is flipped, the location line stays put and the wall grows or flips around it. A straight wall meeting it at a corner follows, so the corner still closes. The picked wall's location line is drawn dashed in the plan. Pick several walls to set it for all of them. Arc height (curved walls): how far the middle bows off a straight line. 0 makes it straight; a negative value bows it to the other side. The radius shows beside it. Each change is one step in Changes; Undo takes it back. Drag a wall in the plan When one wall is picked, it shows three grips: a square at each end and a circle in the middle. Drag an end to make the wall longer, shorter or turn it. It snaps like the Wall tool; Shift keeps it square. Drag the middle to move the whole wall. Walls joined to its ends stretch to follow. A picked curved wall shows grips at its ends and a round grip at the middle of its arc. Drag the round grip to bend it. Doors and windows stay in the wall. A wall too short for what it holds is refused with \"It doesn't fit in that wall.\" Change a door or window Click the door or window in the plan. In Properties: Type (another door or window of the same kind), Width, Height, Sill (windows), From the start and From the end (the distance from each end of the wall face to its edge), Hinges Flip (doors with one leaf and casement windows; sliding, pocket, barn and bifold doors say Opens to instead; fixed, hung and picture windows and double doors have none), and Swings to (doors) or Frame side (windows) Flip. Or drag it along its wall, or onto another wall: see Doors and windows. Change several at once Pick several walls (or several doors, or several windows) with Shift+click or Ctrl+click. Properties offers what they share: Change type…, the height, the size. One change sets all of them, and one Undo takes all of it back. Good to know The numbers show in the units chosen in the Levels panel. Walls, doors and windows from other programs show their IFC data in Properties as before, without this box."},{"slug":"floors-and-roofs","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs","summary":"Rooms appear as walls close them; give a room or the whole building a floor and a ceiling, and put a hip or gable roof on the walls. They all follow when the walls move.","section":"tasks","order":19,"keywords":["room","rooms","space","area","perimeter","floor","slab","subfloor","ceiling","roof","hip roof","gable roof","pitch","overhang","eave","ridge","valley","6/12"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"rooms","text":"Rooms","level":2},{"id":"floors","text":"Floors","level":2},{"id":"ceilings","text":"Ceilings","level":2},{"id":"a-roof","text":"A roof","level":2},{"id":"change-them","text":"Change them","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFinish the shell of the building. Rooms come by themselves from the walls; floors, ceilings and the roof take one click each. Because they are found from the walls, they follow when the walls move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rooms\"\u003ERooms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery space closed in by drawn walls is a room. It appears as soon as the last wall closes it, with a number (101, 102 … on the first level, 201 … on the next) and a name (\u003Cstrong\u003ERoom 1\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERoom 2\u003C/strong\u003E …), and its area shows in the middle of it on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, click inside a room to pick it. In \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E change its \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003EKitchen\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber\u003C/strong\u003E; its area and perimeter are shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDelete a wall between two rooms and they become one; draw a wall across a room and it becomes two. A room keeps its name and number while the walls round it move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA room can&#039;t be deleted on its own: it is the space the walls make.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAreas are measured inside the wall faces. Rooms are saved as IFC spaces with their floor area, perimeter, height and volume, so the schedules read them (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/quantity-totals\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/quantity-totals\"\u003EAdd up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"floors\"\u003EFloors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloor\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EThis room\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole building\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003EKind\u003C/strong\u003E (wood floor or concrete slab) and its thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside a room (or anywhere inside the building). What the floor would cover is highlighted with its area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The floor&#039;s top is the level&#039;s floor; it goes down by its thickness. A whole-building floor reaches the outside faces of the walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ceilings\"\u003ECeilings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECeiling\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EThis room\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EThe whole building\u003C/strong\u003E, and its \u003Cstrong\u003EHeight\u003C/strong\u003E above the floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo see it from below, with its grid and the lights on it, switch the plan to its ceiling plan: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\"\u003EViews, view ranges and your own sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-roof\"\u003EA roof\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level whose walls carry the roof, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoof\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E as a rise over 12 (\u003Ccode\u003E6/12\u003C/code\u003E) or in degrees (\u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E), and the \u003Cstrong\u003EOverhang\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EEnds\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EHip roof\u003C/strong\u003E (every side slopes), \u003Cstrong\u003EGables on the short sides\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EGables on the long sides\u003C/strong\u003E (a building with four sides), or \u003Cstrong\u003EShed\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside the building: the eave and the ridge show on the plan. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe roof sits on top of the walls, on the level above them, and its underside meets the outside face of the walls. Gable ends are filled with a gable wall as thick as the wall below. An L-, T- or U-shaped building gets a hip roof whose wings meet in valleys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-them\"\u003EChange them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick a floor, ceiling or roof (in the 3D view, the model tree, or a roof by clicking inside its outline on the level above) and change it in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: a floor&#039;s kind, thickness and how far its top is above the level; a ceiling&#039;s height, thickness and \u003Cstrong\u003EPattern\u003C/strong\u003E (the grid a ceiling plan draws on it: none, 600 × 600, 600 × 1200, 2&#039; × 2&#039; or 2&#039; × 4&#039;); a roof&#039;s pitch, overhang, ends and thickness. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMoving, adding or deleting walls makes the rooms, floors, ceilings and the roof over them again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA roof fits any outline. To give each wall its own pitch, gable or shed end, a raised plate, fascia and soffit, or to draw a single roof plane, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/roofs\"\u003ERoofs: a pitch for each wall, gables, sheds and roof planes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes a picked floor, ceiling or roof.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Finish the shell of the building. Rooms come by themselves from the walls; floors, ceilings and the roof take one click each. Because they are found from the walls, they follow when the walls move. Rooms Every space closed in by drawn walls is a room. It appears as soon as the last wall closes it, with a number (101, 102 … on the first level, 201 … on the next) and a name (Room 1, Room 2 …), and its area shows in the middle of it on the plan. With Select on, click inside a room to pick it. In Properties change its Name (for example Kitchen) and Number; its area and perimeter are shown. Delete a wall between two rooms and they become one; draw a wall across a room and it becomes two. A room keeps its name and number while the walls round it move. A room can't be deleted on its own: it is the space the walls make. Areas are measured inside the wall faces. Rooms are saved as IFC spaces with their floor area, perimeter, height and volume, so the schedules read them (see Add up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces). Floors Click Floor. Choose This room or The whole building, the Kind (wood floor or concrete slab) and its thickness. Point inside a room (or anywhere inside the building). What the floor would cover is highlighted with its area. Click. The floor's top is the level's floor; it goes down by its thickness. A whole-building floor reaches the outside faces of the walls. Ceilings Click Ceiling. Choose This room or The whole building, and its Height above the floor. Point inside and click. To see it from below, with its grid and the lights on it, switch the plan to its ceiling plan: see Views, view ranges and your own sheets. A roof On the level whose walls carry the roof, click Roof. Type the Pitch as a rise over 12 (6/12) or in degrees (30), and the Overhang. Choose the Ends: Hip roof (every side slopes), Gables on the short sides or Gables on the long sides (a building with four sides), or Shed. Point inside the building: the eave and the ridge show on the plan. Click. The roof sits on top of the walls, on the level above them, and its underside meets the outside face of the walls. Gable ends are filled with a gable wall as thick as the wall below. An L-, T- or U-shaped building gets a hip roof whose wings meet in valleys. Change them Pick a floor, ceiling or roof (in the 3D view, the model tree, or a roof by clicking inside its outline on the level above) and change it in Properties: a floor's kind, thickness and how far its top is above the level; a ceiling's height, thickness and Pattern (the grid a ceiling plan draws on it: none, 600 × 600, 600 × 1200, 2' × 2' or 2' × 4'); a roof's pitch, overhang, ends and thickness. Each change is one undo step. Good to know Moving, adding or deleting walls makes the rooms, floors, ceilings and the roof over them again. A roof fits any outline. To give each wall its own pitch, gable or shed end, a raised plate, fascia and soffit, or to draw a single roof plane, see Roofs: a pitch for each wall, gables, sheds and roof planes. Delete deletes a picked floor, ceiling or roof."},{"slug":"roofs","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Roofs: a pitch for each wall, gables, sheds and roof planes","summary":"Set each wall under a roof to pitched, gable end or none, use the hip, gable and shed presets, lift the roof with the plate height, add fascia and soffit, and draw single roof planes where the automatic roof doesn't fit.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["roof","pitch","per wall","gable","gable end","shed","lean-to","hip","valley","ridge","plate height","heel","fascia","soffit","eave","rake","overhang","roof plane","porch roof","dormer","gable dormer","shed dormer"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"put-a-roof-on","text":"Put a roof on","level":2},{"id":"one-wall-at-a-time","text":"One wall at a time","level":2},{"id":"plate-height-fascia-and-soffit","text":"Plate height, fascia and soffit","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-roof-plane","text":"Draw a roof plane","level":2},{"id":"add-a-dormer","text":"Add a dormer","level":2},{"id":"what-s-saved","text":"What's saved","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShape the roof from the walls under it. Every wall gets its own setting, so one roof can be hipped at the front, gabled at the ends and stop flat against a taller wall at the back. For a porch or any face the automatic roof can&#039;t make, draw a roof plane by its corners.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-a-roof-on\"\u003EPut a roof on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level whose walls carry the roof, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoof\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E6/12\u003C/code\u003E, or degrees such as \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E) and the \u003Cstrong\u003EOverhang\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a preset: \u003Cstrong\u003EHip roof\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGables on the short sides\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGables on the long sides\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003EShed\u003C/strong\u003E (the side nearest the pointer is the low eave; the other sides are gable ends).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003EFascia and soffit\u003C/strong\u003E ticked for boxed eaves with a fascia board, or untick it for a bare roof edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside the building: the eave, the ridge, the hips and the valleys show on the plan. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAny outline works: L, T, U and plus shapes, angled corners and notches. Hips, ridges and valleys are found where the roof planes meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-wall-at-a-time\"\u003EOne wall at a time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick the roof (on the level above the walls, click inside its outline) and look under \u003Cstrong\u003EEach wall\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E. Pointing at a row marks that wall on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPitched\u003C/strong\u003E: the wall has a sloping roof plane over it. Type its own pitch beside it, for example \u003Ccode\u003E12/12\u003C/code\u003E for steep ends on a \u003Ccode\u003E6/12\u003C/code\u003E roof.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGable end\u003C/strong\u003E: the roof stops in a vertical end over that wall, the wall is filled up to the roof, and the roof runs past it by the \u003Cstrong\u003EGable overhang\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENone (stops flush)\u003C/strong\u003E: the roof stops at that wall with no overhang and no gable wall, for a roof against a taller wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EEnds\u003C/strong\u003E list at the top sets every wall at once; \u003Cstrong\u003ESet per wall\u003C/strong\u003E shows when the walls no longer match a preset.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plate-height-fascia-and-soffit\"\u003EPlate height, fascia and soffit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlate height\u003C/strong\u003E is where the roof planes meet the outside of the walls, measured from the level&#039;s floor. Leave it blank to sit the roof on the top of the walls; type a larger value for a raised heel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFascia height\u003C/strong\u003E is the board along the eaves and up the rakes. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E for none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoxed eaves\u003C/strong\u003E puts a level soffit under each eave, from the wall to the back of the fascia.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery plane meets its wall at the plate height, so walls with different pitches keep one plate line and their eaves end at different heights, as they would be framed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-roof-plane\"\u003EDraw a roof plane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level whose walls the plane sits on, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoof plane\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E and, if it isn&#039;t the top of the walls, the \u003Cstrong\u003EEave height\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners. The first side you draw is the eave; the plane rises from it towards the rest of the shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first corner again, double-click, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops the corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA roof plane doesn&#039;t follow the walls. Pick it on the level above (click inside it) to change its pitch, eave height or thickness, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-dormer\"\u003EAdd a dormer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the level the roof is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDormer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the options row choose \u003Cstrong\u003EDormer kind\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cstrong\u003EGable\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EShed\u003C/strong\u003E) and set \u003Cstrong\u003EWidth\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFront wall\u003C/strong\u003E (the height of the front wall from the roof&#039;s top to the dormer&#039;s eave), \u003Cstrong\u003EPitch\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOverhang\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on a roof face where the middle of the dormer&#039;s front goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft builds the front wall, two cheek walls and the dormer&#039;s roof, and runs it back until it meets the roof. A gable dormer has two planes that meet at a ridge. A shed dormer has one plane, which must be less steep than the roof. The roof below the dormer is cut away. The dormer follows the roof when the walls move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick it to change its kind, width, front wall, pitch or overhang in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it and closes the roof again. It must fit on one roof face, away from hips, valleys and the ridge, and only roofs made with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoof\u003C/span\u003E tool take dormers, not roof planes drawn by hand. Not built yet: a window in the dormer&#039;s front, and dormers on hand-drawn roof planes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-s-saved\"\u003EWhat&#039;s saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERoofs are IFC roofs made of one roof slab per plane, gable walls, one fascia member and one soffit covering. The roof carries its ridge, hip, valley, eave and rake lengths, and its areas, for takeoffs. A roof plane is an IFC roof with one slab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo takes back any roof change in one step, including a preset that changes every wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Shape the roof from the walls under it. Every wall gets its own setting, so one roof can be hipped at the front, gabled at the ends and stop flat against a taller wall at the back. For a porch or any face the automatic roof can't make, draw a roof plane by its corners. Put a roof on On the level whose walls carry the roof, click Roof. Type the Pitch (6/12, or degrees such as 30) and the Overhang. Choose a preset: Hip roof, Gables on the short sides, Gables on the long sides, or Shed (the side nearest the pointer is the low eave; the other sides are gable ends). Leave Fascia and soffit ticked for boxed eaves with a fascia board, or untick it for a bare roof edge. Point inside the building: the eave, the ridge, the hips and the valleys show on the plan. Click. Any outline works: L, T, U and plus shapes, angled corners and notches. Hips, ridges and valleys are found where the roof planes meet. One wall at a time Pick the roof (on the level above the walls, click inside its outline) and look under Each wall in Properties. Pointing at a row marks that wall on the plan. Pitched: the wall has a sloping roof plane over it. Type its own pitch beside it, for example 12/12 for steep ends on a 6/12 roof. Gable end: the roof stops in a vertical end over that wall, the wall is filled up to the roof, and the roof runs past it by the Gable overhang. None (stops flush): the roof stops at that wall with no overhang and no gable wall, for a roof against a taller wall. The Ends list at the top sets every wall at once; Set per wall shows when the walls no longer match a preset. Plate height, fascia and soffit Plate height is where the roof planes meet the outside of the walls, measured from the level's floor. Leave it blank to sit the roof on the top of the walls; type a larger value for a raised heel. Fascia height is the board along the eaves and up the rakes. Type 0 for none. Boxed eaves puts a level soffit under each eave, from the wall to the back of the fascia. Every plane meets its wall at the plate height, so walls with different pitches keep one plate line and their eaves end at different heights, as they would be framed. Draw a roof plane On the level whose walls the plane sits on, open Build and choose Roof plane. Type the Pitch and, if it isn't the top of the walls, the Eave height. Click the corners. The first side you draw is the eave; the plane rises from it towards the rest of the shape. Click the first corner again, double-click, or press Enter to finish. Esc drops the corners. A roof plane doesn't follow the walls. Pick it on the level above (click inside it) to change its pitch, eave height or thickness, or press Delete to remove it. Add a dormer Show the level the roof is on. Open Build and choose Dormer. In the options row choose Dormer kind (Gable or Shed) and set Width, Front wall (the height of the front wall from the roof's top to the dormer's eave), Pitch and Overhang. Click on a roof face where the middle of the dormer's front goes. BimCraft builds the front wall, two cheek walls and the dormer's roof, and runs it back until it meets the roof. A gable dormer has two planes that meet at a ridge. A shed dormer has one plane, which must be less steep than the roof. The roof below the dormer is cut away. The dormer follows the roof when the walls move. Pick it to change its kind, width, front wall, pitch or overhang in Properties. Delete removes it and closes the roof again. It must fit on one roof face, away from hips, valleys and the ridge, and only roofs made with the Roof tool take dormers, not roof planes drawn by hand. Not built yet: a window in the dormer's front, and dormers on hand-drawn roof planes. What's saved Roofs are IFC roofs made of one roof slab per plane, gable walls, one fascia member and one soffit covering. The roof carries its ridge, hip, valley, eave and rake lengths, and its areas, for takeoffs. A roof plane is an IFC roof with one slab. Undo takes back any roof change in one step, including a preset that changes every wall."},{"slug":"plan-schedules-and-pdf","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Schedules, dimensions and a PDF of the plan","summary":"Door, window, wall and room schedules that change as you draw, dimensions on the plan, and the plan of a level saved as a PDF at a standard scale.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["schedule","door schedule","window schedule","wall schedule","room schedule","quantities","takeoff","area","csv","spreadsheet","dimensions","dimension","print","pdf","plot","scale","1/4\"","1:50","sheet"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"schedules","text":"Schedules","level":2},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"save-the-plan-as-a-pdf","text":"Save the plan as a PDF","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee what the building you drew adds up to, while you draw it: how many doors and windows of each size, how much wall of each type, how big each room is. Put dimensions on the plan and save it as a PDF to print or send.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"schedules\"\u003ESchedules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E on, \u003Cstrong\u003ESchedules\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel shows one table at a time:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EWindows\u003C/strong\u003E: each one by its mark (D1, D2 … and W1 …, given as you place them), its type, width, height (and sill), level and the wall it is in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls by type\u003C/strong\u003E: for each wall type, how many walls, their thickness, their total length and their net area (doors and windows left out), with a total.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERooms\u003C/strong\u003E: number, name, level, area and perimeter, with the total area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tables change with every edit. Click a row to pick what it lists. \u003Cstrong\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/strong\u003E saves the table, to open in a spreadsheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarks are the elements&#039; IFC tag: change one in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E (Tag) and the schedule follows. For every IFC model, drawn or not, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/quantity-totals\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/quantity-totals\"\u003EAdd up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces\u003C/a\u003E adds up any property.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plan shows each drawn wall&#039;s length along its outside face (an inside wall: along one face) and, for a wall with doors or windows, a chain from the corners to each opening. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E in the model bar hides them and shows them again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-plan-as-a-pdf\"\u003ESave the plan as a PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the level you want and set the units in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint\u003C/span\u003E in the model bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBimCraft saves &quot;&lt;file&gt; — &lt;level&gt;.pdf&quot;: the plan in black and white at the largest standard scale that fits the page (1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; … on Letter in feet and inches; 1:50, 1:100 … on A4 in millimeters), with the walls filled, doors, windows, room names and areas, dimensions, and a title strip with the level, the file, the cut height, the date and the scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe PDF is drawn as lines and text, so it stays sharp at any zoom and its text can be searched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe schedules list what the drawing tools made. Doors and windows from another program show in Quantity totals instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo price the job, hand the quantities to TakeoffCraft: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/takeoff\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/takeoff\"\u003EQuantities to TakeoffCraft\u003C/a\u003E. The CSV download stays for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See what the building you drew adds up to, while you draw it: how many doors and windows of each size, how much wall of each type, how big each room is. Put dimensions on the plan and save it as a PDF to print or send. Schedules With Draw on, Schedules in the left panel shows one table at a time: Doors and Windows: each one by its mark (D1, D2 … and W1 …, given as you place them), its type, width, height (and sill), level and the wall it is in. Walls by type: for each wall type, how many walls, their thickness, their total length and their net area (doors and windows left out), with a total. Rooms: number, name, level, area and perimeter, with the total area. The tables change with every edit. Click a row to pick what it lists. Download CSV saves the table, to open in a spreadsheet. Marks are the elements' IFC tag: change one in Properties (Tag) and the schedule follows. For every IFC model, drawn or not, Add up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces adds up any property. Dimensions The plan shows each drawn wall's length along its outside face (an inside wall: along one face) and, for a wall with doors or windows, a chain from the corners to each opening. Dimensions in the model bar hides them and shows them again. Save the plan as a PDF Show the level you want and set the units in the Levels panel. Click Print in the model bar. BimCraft saves \"\u003Cfile\u003E — \u003Clevel\u003E.pdf\": the plan in black and white at the largest standard scale that fits the page (1/4\" = 1'-0\", 1/8\" = 1'-0\" … on Letter in feet and inches; 1:50, 1:100 … on A4 in millimeters), with the walls filled, doors, windows, room names and areas, dimensions, and a title strip with the level, the file, the cut height, the date and the scale. The PDF is drawn as lines and text, so it stays sharp at any zoom and its text can be searched. Good to know The schedules list what the drawing tools made. Doors and windows from another program show in Quantity totals instead. To price the job, hand the quantities to TakeoffCraft: see Quantities to TakeoffCraft. The CSV download stays for a spreadsheet."},{"slug":"storeys-and-spaces","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges","summary":"Turn storeys off to see inside a building, show rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines on and off.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["storeys","levels","floors","hide","isolate","rooms","spaces","ifcspace","edges","visibility","section"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-look-at-one-floor","text":"Steps: look at one floor","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"steps-show-rooms-and-spaces","text":"Steps: show rooms and spaces","level":2},{"id":"steps-edges","text":"Steps: edges","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook inside an IFC model by turning off the storeys above the one you care about, then show the rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines off for a cleaner look.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-look-at-one-floor\"\u003ESteps: look at one floor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list on the left. The top storey is first, like reading a section from the roof down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick the roof and every storey above the floor you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E to look down onto that floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick the storeys again to put them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach storey you untick disappears at once, with everything on it. The list only shows storeys that hold something visible. Elements that are not assigned to any storey are grouped as \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on a storey\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom; site elements and some furniture often land there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-show-rooms-and-spaces\"\u003ESteps: show rooms and spaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model. The button stays pressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the model: rooms and spaces appear as pale, see-through volumes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a space to read its properties, such as its name, number and area, on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E again to hide them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpaces are hidden when a model opens because they would otherwise cover the walls and floors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-edges\"\u003ESteps: edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E to turn the thin edge lines off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it again to turn them back on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn very large models (over 1.5 million triangles) the button is greyed out with the tip &quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;. This saves memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnticking a storey and still seeing parts of it.\u003C/strong\u003E An element belongs to the storey the exporting program assigned it to. A tall wall or a stair often belongs only to its base storey, so it stays when the storey above is hidden, or vanishes with the storey below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHiding storeys to see inside.\u003C/strong\u003E A section box is often quicker: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane#a-section-box\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\" data-hash=\"a-section-box\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E. You can also untick rows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking a space through a wall.\u003C/strong\u003E Clicking picks the nearest visible surface. Hide the storey above, or switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, then click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStorey names look odd.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft shows the storey names saved in the file. If they say &quot;Level 1&quot; or a number, that is what the exporter wrote.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look inside an IFC model by turning off the storeys above the one you care about, then show the rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines off for a cleaner look. Steps: look at one floor Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Find the Storeys list on the left. The top storey is first, like reading a section from the roof down. Untick the roof and every storey above the floor you want. Click Top to look down onto that floor. Tick the storeys again to put them back. What you should see Each storey you untick disappears at once, with everything on it. The list only shows storeys that hold something visible. Elements that are not assigned to any storey are grouped as Not on a storey at the bottom; site elements and some furniture often land there. Steps: show rooms and spaces Click Spaces in the toolbar above the model. The button stays pressed. Look at the model: rooms and spaces appear as pale, see-through volumes. Click a space to read its properties, such as its name, number and area, on the right. Click Spaces again to hide them. Spaces are hidden when a model opens because they would otherwise cover the walls and floors. Steps: edges Click Edges to turn the thin edge lines off. Click it again to turn them back on. On very large models (over 1.5 million triangles) the button is greyed out with the tip \"Edges are off for very large models\". This saves memory. Common mistakes Unticking a storey and still seeing parts of it. An element belongs to the storey the exporting program assigned it to. A tall wall or a stair often belongs only to its base storey, so it stays when the storey above is hidden, or vanishes with the storey below. Hiding storeys to see inside. A section box is often quicker: see Cut an IFC model with a section plane. You can also untick rows in the Model tree (see Find your way with the model tree). Clicking a space through a wall. Clicking picks the nearest visible surface. Hide the storey above, or switch to Top, then click. Storey names look odd. BimCraft shows the storey names saved in the file. If they say \"Level 1\" or a number, that is what the exporter wrote."},{"slug":"foundations","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Foundations under the walls","summary":"Put a slab on grade, stem walls with a slab, a crawlspace or a basement under a building with one click; footings are sized from the walls and the number of stories, and the foundation follows when the walls move.","section":"tasks","order":21,"keywords":["foundation","footing","slab on grade","thickened edge","monolithic slab","stem wall","crawlspace","crawl space","basement","basement wall","frost","concrete","footing width"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"put-a-foundation-in","text":"Put a foundation in","level":2},{"id":"change-it","text":"Change it","level":2},{"id":"what-s-saved","text":"What's saved","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the building a foundation that matches its walls. BimCraft follows the outside of the walls, sizes the footings from the wall type and how many stories stand on them, and makes the foundation again whenever the walls change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-a-foundation-in\"\u003EPut a foundation in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level whose walls stand on it (usually the first), open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFoundation\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the kind:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESlab on grade\u003C/strong\u003E: a 4&quot; concrete slab whose top is the floor, with a thickened edge under the walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStem walls and slab\u003C/strong\u003E: foundation walls up to the floor on footings, with a slab inside them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECrawlspace\u003C/strong\u003E: foundation walls that stop under the floor framing, on footings, with no slab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBasement\u003C/strong\u003E: full-height foundation walls on footings with a basement slab, under the floor framing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside the building: the footings show dashed with their size. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking a building that already has a foundation picks it, so you can change it instead of adding a second one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it\"\u003EChange it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick the foundation (click inside the building with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFoundation\u003C/span\u003E, or click any of its parts in the 3D view or the model tree) and change it in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: the kind, the footing width and depth, how far down the footing&#039;s underside is, the wall thickness, how far below the floor the walls stop, the basement height and the slab thickness. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESizes marked \u003Cstrong\u003Eauto\u003C/strong\u003E come from the walls: footings 12&quot;, 15&quot; or 23&quot; wide for one, two or three stories on a slab or stem walls, 18&quot;, 22&quot; or 27&quot; over a crawlspace or basement, and 4&quot; wider under masonry or concrete walls. Type a size to set your own; type \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E to go back to auto.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe automatic sizes are labelled defaults for typical light-frame houses, not engineering. A designer or engineer checks footings, depths and walls for the soil, the frost depth and the loads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-s-saved\"\u003EWhat&#039;s saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA foundation is saved as an IFC element assembly (&quot;Foundation&quot;) made of a footing (or a slab&#039;s thickened edge), a foundation wall and a slab as its kind needs, all concrete, each with its quantities: lengths, widths, heights, areas and volumes, ready for takeoffs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove, add or delete walls and the foundation under them is made again; draw walls on the level above and the footings below widen for the extra story.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes a picked foundation with all its parts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the site&#039;s ground in the model (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/terrain\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/terrain\"\u003EThe site&#039;s ground, the building pad and paving\u003C/a\u003E), stem walls and crawlspace footings step down with it in 8&quot; steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFootings under interior walls are not made yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Give the building a foundation that matches its walls. BimCraft follows the outside of the walls, sizes the footings from the wall type and how many stories stand on them, and makes the foundation again whenever the walls change. Put a foundation in On the level whose walls stand on it (usually the first), open Build and choose Foundation. Choose the kind: Slab on grade: a 4\" concrete slab whose top is the floor, with a thickened edge under the walls. Stem walls and slab: foundation walls up to the floor on footings, with a slab inside them. Crawlspace: foundation walls that stop under the floor framing, on footings, with no slab. Basement: full-height foundation walls on footings with a basement slab, under the floor framing. Point inside the building: the footings show dashed with their size. Click. Clicking a building that already has a foundation picks it, so you can change it instead of adding a second one. Change it Pick the foundation (click inside the building with Foundation, or click any of its parts in the 3D view or the model tree) and change it in Properties: the kind, the footing width and depth, how far down the footing's underside is, the wall thickness, how far below the floor the walls stop, the basement height and the slab thickness. Each change is one undo step. Sizes marked auto come from the walls: footings 12\", 15\" or 23\" wide for one, two or three stories on a slab or stem walls, 18\", 22\" or 27\" over a crawlspace or basement, and 4\" wider under masonry or concrete walls. Type a size to set your own; type 0 to go back to auto. The automatic sizes are labelled defaults for typical light-frame houses, not engineering. A designer or engineer checks footings, depths and walls for the soil, the frost depth and the loads. What's saved A foundation is saved as an IFC element assembly (\"Foundation\") made of a footing (or a slab's thickened edge), a foundation wall and a slab as its kind needs, all concrete, each with its quantities: lengths, widths, heights, areas and volumes, ready for takeoffs. Good to know Move, add or delete walls and the foundation under them is made again; draw walls on the level above and the footings below widen for the extra story. Delete removes a picked foundation with all its parts. With the site's ground in the model (see The site's ground, the building pad and paving), stem walls and crawlspace footings step down with it in 8\" steps. Footings under interior walls are not made yet."},{"slug":"site-building-and-levels","scope":"bimcraft","title":"The site, the building on it, and its levels","summary":"Draw the site's outline, place and turn the building on the site, add levels at given heights or split the building's height, and draw on the level you choose.","section":"tasks","order":21,"keywords":["site","site plan","property line","lot","plot","boundary","building placement","rotate building","place building","levels","storeys","stories","floors","add levels","split height","floor to floor","IfcSite","IfcBuilding","IfcBuildingStorey","spatial structure"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-site","text":"Draw the site","level":2},{"id":"place-the-building-on-the-site","text":"Place the building on the site","level":2},{"id":"add-levels","text":"Add levels","level":2},{"id":"draw-on-a-level","text":"Draw on a level","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet up the parts of an IFC model that hold everything else: the site, the building on it and the building&#039;s levels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA model is organized as a \u003Cstrong\u003Esite\u003C/strong\u003E that holds a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebuilding\u003C/strong\u003E, which holds its \u003Cstrong\u003Elevels\u003C/strong\u003E (storeys); walls, floors and everything else belong to a level. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew building\u003C/span\u003E sets up all three. A model from another program that has a building but no site gets a site the first time you draw one: the building is placed on it where it already was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-site\"\u003EDraw the site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the site (the tool snaps like the Wall tool). Click the first corner again, double-click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back a corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe row shows the site&#039;s area. Draw it again to change it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery level&#039;s plan shows the site outline as a property line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-the-building-on-the-site\"\u003EPlace the building on the site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite\u003C/span\u003E on, type the building&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E on the site and its \u003Cstrong\u003ETurn\u003C/strong\u003E in degrees, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace the building by a click\u003C/span\u003E and click where the building&#039;s origin goes. Everything in the building (levels, walls, grids, all you drew) moves with it; the site and its outline stay. The plans keep showing each level square to the building.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-levels\"\u003EAdd levels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E panel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a level on top\u003C/span\u003E adds one. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd levels…\u003C/span\u003E adds several at once:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAt these heights\u003C/strong\u003E: type heights above the lowest level, separated by commas (for example \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;, 24&#039;, 36&#039;\u003C/code\u003E). Heights that already have a level are skipped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESplit the building&#039;s height\u003C/strong\u003E: type the building&#039;s height and the number of levels. The levels you have move to their new heights from the lowest up, missing ones are added, and any above them stay where they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWalls that run to the level above follow it, as do columns and ramps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-on-a-level\"\u003EDraw on a level\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E tool works on the level chosen in the plan&#039;s level list (or picked in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E panel): what you draw belongs to that level.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach of these changes is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the IFC file: the site (IfcSite) with its outline and area, the building (IfcBuilding) placed on it, and the levels (IfcBuildingStorey) in the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/coordinates\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/coordinates\"\u003ECoordinates in BimCraft\u003C/a\u003E for the project origin and the survey point.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Set up the parts of an IFC model that hold everything else: the site, the building on it and the building's levels. A model is organized as a site that holds a building, which holds its levels (storeys); walls, floors and everything else belong to a level. File › New building sets up all three. A model from another program that has a building but no site gets a site the first time you draw one: the building is placed on it where it already was. Draw the site Turn on Draw, then choose Build › Site. Click the corners of the site (the tool snaps like the Wall tool). Click the first corner again, double-click or press Enter to finish. Backspace takes back a corner. The row shows the site's area. Draw it again to change it. Every level's plan shows the site outline as a property line. Place the building on the site With Site on, type the building's X and Y on the site and its Turn in degrees, or click Place the building by a click and click where the building's origin goes. Everything in the building (levels, walls, grids, all you drew) moves with it; the site and its outline stay. The plans keep showing each level square to the building. Add levels In the Levels panel, Add a level on top adds one. Add levels… adds several at once: At these heights: type heights above the lowest level, separated by commas (for example 12', 24', 36'). Heights that already have a level are skipped. Split the building's height: type the building's height and the number of levels. The levels you have move to their new heights from the lowest up, missing ones are added, and any above them stay where they are. Walls that run to the level above follow it, as do columns and ramps. Draw on a level Every Draw tool works on the level chosen in the plan's level list (or picked in the Levels panel): what you draw belongs to that level. What you should see Each of these changes is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. In the IFC file: the site (IfcSite) with its outline and area, the building (IfcBuilding) placed on it, and the levels (IfcBuildingStorey) in the building. See also Coordinates in BimCraft for the project origin and the survey point."},{"slug":"find","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find elements by class, name or property","summary":"Find every element that matches one or more rules — its class, name, type, storey, material or any property value — pick them all, and keep the rules as a named search set.","section":"tasks","order":22,"keywords":["find","search","filter","query","rule","search set","selection set","saved search","property","value","class","material","pick all"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick every element of one kind or with one value in a single step: every door, every wall on Level 2, every element whose fire rating is EI60, every wall that costs more than a set amount.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the first rule, choose a property (the element&#039;s own ones first, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E, then the model&#039;s property sets), how to compare, and a value. The value box suggests the values the model has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo narrow it down, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a rule\u003C/span\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery rule\u003C/span\u003E to need all of them, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny rule\u003C/span\u003E for one of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in a value box. What matches is picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo keep the rules, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a set\u003C/span\u003E, type a name and click it again. Click a set&#039;s name later to find with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe matches picked in the 3D view and the model tree, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E saying how many by class. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to show only them, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to hide them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWords match without caring about capitals. Numbers compare as numbers: &quot;is more than 1000&quot; means the value, not the text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ehas a value\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ehas no value\u003C/span\u003E need no value; they find what is filled in, or missing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaved sets stay in this browser for every model you open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is found.\u003C/strong\u003E A rule needs a value it can match: look at the suggestions in the value box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA property is missing from the list.\u003C/strong\u003E No element with a shape in this model has it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Pick every element of one kind or with one value in a single step: every door, every wall on Level 2, every element whose fire rating is EI60, every wall that costs more than a set amount. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Find in the left panel. In the first rule, choose a property (the element's own ones first, such as Class and Storey, then the model's property sets), how to compare, and a value. The value box suggests the values the model has. To narrow it down, click Add a rule. Choose Every rule to need all of them, or Any rule for one of them. Click Find, or press Enter in a value box. What matches is picked. To keep the rules, click Save as a set, type a name and click it again. Click a set's name later to find with it. What you should see The matches picked in the 3D view and the model tree, and Properties saying how many by class. Press I to show only them, H to hide them. Words match without caring about capitals. Numbers compare as numbers: \"is more than 1000\" means the value, not the text. has a value and has no value need no value; they find what is filled in, or missing. Saved sets stay in this browser for every model you open. Common mistakes Nothing is found. A rule needs a value it can match: look at the suggestions in the value box. A property is missing from the list. No element with a shape in this model has it. Next Color elements by a property Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"kitchens-and-baths","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Kitchens, baths and furniture from the residential set","summary":"Place cabinets, vanities, appliances, plumbing fixtures and furniture from the library against the walls, then put countertops on the base cabinets that follow them.","section":"tasks","order":22,"keywords":["kitchen","cabinet","base cabinet","wall cabinet","upper cabinet","pantry","sink base","lazy susan","corner cabinet","vanity","countertop","counter","appliance","refrigerator","range","dishwasher","hood","washer","dryer","toilet","sink","bathtub","shower","furniture","sofa","bed","table","residential"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"place-cabinets-and-fixtures","text":"Place cabinets and fixtures","level":2},{"id":"countertops","text":"Countertops","level":2},{"id":"doors-and-windows","text":"Doors and windows","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELay out kitchens, bathrooms and rooms with the residential set: cabinets, vanities, appliances, plumbing fixtures and furniture drawn by CadCraft and free to use (CC0). They come from the library and line up with the walls; countertops then go on the base cabinets in one click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-cabinets-and-fixtures\"\u003EPlace cabinets and fixtures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary\u003C/strong\u003E tab and choose a category: \u003Cstrong\u003ECasework\u003C/strong\u003E (base, wall and tall cabinets, vanities), \u003Cstrong\u003EAppliances\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPlumbing\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFurniture\u003C/strong\u003E. Searching for \u003Ccode\u003Ebase cabinet\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EB24\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW3030\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Etoilet\u003C/code\u003E finds them too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an item and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace\u003C/strong\u003E. Pick the level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint near a wall and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to snap to it: the item&#039;s back goes on the wall face. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place it. Place the next one right beside it; a run of cabinets is just cabinets side by side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWall cabinets, the microwave over the range, range hoods and kitchen sinks carry their own height: place them at the floor like everything else and they hang (or sit) where they belong — wall cabinets 54&quot; up, a sink at a 36&quot; counter. The corner cabinets go in an inside corner with their back on one wall and their side on the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"countertops\"\u003ECountertops\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECountertop\u003C/span\u003E. Set the \u003Cstrong\u003EFront overhang\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003EThickness\u003C/strong\u003E if they aren&#039;t 1&quot; and 1½&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at a run of base cabinets: the countertop it would make shows over all the cabinets that touch, around corners too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe countertop is made from the cabinets under it. When you add or remove a cabinet, the countertop is drawn dashed on the plan until it fits again: click the run with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECountertop\u003C/span\u003E, or pick the countertop and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EFit to the cabinets\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E. Any change to the walls on that level fits countertops by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Door and Window tools have residential types too: \u003Cstrong\u003EBifold closet door\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFrench doors\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBarn door (on the wall)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEntry door with sidelites\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAwning\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPicture window\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETransom\u003C/strong\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/doors-and-windows\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECountertops are saved as furniture (&quot;Countertop&quot;) with their area, perimeter and front edge, ready for a takeoff.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESink cut-outs in countertops and backsplashes are not made yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sizes are common nominal sizes, not any maker&#039;s product; check a real product&#039;s sizes before ordering.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Lay out kitchens, bathrooms and rooms with the residential set: cabinets, vanities, appliances, plumbing fixtures and furniture drawn by CadCraft and free to use (CC0). They come from the library and line up with the walls; countertops then go on the base cabinets in one click. Place cabinets and fixtures Open the Library tab and choose a category: Casework (base, wall and tall cabinets, vanities), Appliances, Plumbing, Furniture. Searching for base cabinet, B24, W3030 or toilet finds them too. Pick an item and choose Place. Pick the level. Point near a wall and press Tab to snap to it: the item's back goes on the wall face. Press R to turn it. Click to place it. Place the next one right beside it; a run of cabinets is just cabinets side by side. Wall cabinets, the microwave over the range, range hoods and kitchen sinks carry their own height: place them at the floor like everything else and they hang (or sit) where they belong — wall cabinets 54\" up, a sink at a 36\" counter. The corner cabinets go in an inside corner with their back on one wall and their side on the other. Countertops On the level, open Build and choose Countertop. Set the Front overhang and the Thickness if they aren't 1\" and 1½\". Point at a run of base cabinets: the countertop it would make shows over all the cabinets that touch, around corners too. Click. The countertop is made from the cabinets under it. When you add or remove a cabinet, the countertop is drawn dashed on the plan until it fits again: click the run with Countertop, or pick the countertop and choose Fit to the cabinets in Properties. Any change to the walls on that level fits countertops by itself. Doors and windows The Door and Window tools have residential types too: Bifold closet door, French doors, Barn door (on the wall), Entry door with sidelites, Awning, Picture window and Transom (see Doors and windows). Good to know Countertops are saved as furniture (\"Countertop\") with their area, perimeter and front edge, ready for a takeoff. Sink cut-outs in countertops and backsplashes are not made yet. The sizes are common nominal sizes, not any maker's product; check a real product's sizes before ordering."},{"slug":"model-tree","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find your way with the model tree","summary":"Browse an IFC model by project, site, building, storey and space, with its elements grouped by kind; tick boxes to show and hide, click a row to pick, double-click to zoom.","section":"tasks","order":23,"keywords":["model tree","tree","structure","spatial structure","browser","outline","navigator","storey","level","floor","space","room","group","class","hide","show","pick","select","zoom"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee how a model is put together and get to any element without hunting for it in 3D: the building, its storeys, the spaces on each storey, and the elements in them, grouped by kind (walls, doors, slabs…).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E on the left opens down to the storeys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the arrow beside a row to open or close it. A storey holds its spaces first, then its elements by kind with how many there are. An element made of parts, such as a stair, holds its parts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a row&#039;s box to hide everything in it; tick it to show it again. A box with a dash means only some of what is in it is shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a row&#039;s name to pick what it stands for: one element, or every element in a group, storey or space. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds them to the pick, or takes them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ei\u003C/span\u003E button beside a storey, space or building to see its own properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a row to zoom to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you pick an element in the 3D view, the tree opens down to it and marks it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden elements are struck through. The tree&#039;s boxes and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) work together: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements the model does not place in any storey are in a group at the end, \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the spatial structure\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA long group shows 200 rows at a time; click \u003Cstrong\u003EShow more\u003C/strong\u003E under it for the rest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys\"\u003EKeys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a row&#039;s name focused: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes or goes up, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ticks the box, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA storey&#039;s box is ticked but nothing shows.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list below the tree turns whole storeys off on its own: tick it there too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA row picks nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Some rows, such as an empty space, have no elements that can be drawn; the status line says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See how a model is put together and get to any element without hunting for it in 3D: the building, its storeys, the spaces on each storey, and the elements in them, grouped by kind (walls, doors, slabs…). Steps Open an IFC model. The Model tree on the left opens down to the storeys. Click the arrow beside a row to open or close it. A storey holds its spaces first, then its elements by kind with how many there are. An element made of parts, such as a stair, holds its parts. Untick a row's box to hide everything in it; tick it to show it again. A box with a dash means only some of what is in it is shown. Click a row's name to pick what it stands for: one element, or every element in a group, storey or space. Ctrl+click adds them to the pick, or takes them out. Click the i button beside a storey, space or building to see its own properties. Double-click a row to zoom to it. What you should see When you pick an element in the 3D view, the tree opens down to it and marks it. Hidden elements are struck through. The tree's boxes and Show (H, I, Shift+H) work together: see Hide, isolate and see through elements. Elements the model does not place in any storey are in a group at the end, Not in the spatial structure. A long group shows 200 rows at a time; click Show more under it for the rest. Keys With a row's name focused: ↑ and ↓ move, → opens, ← closes or goes up, Space ticks the box, Enter picks. Common mistakes A storey's box is ticked but nothing shows. The Storeys list below the tree turns whole storeys off on its own: tick it there too. A row picks nothing. Some rows, such as an empty space, have no elements that can be drawn; the status line says so. Next Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges"},{"slug":"stairs-and-railings","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Stairs and railings","summary":"Draw straight, L, U and winder stairs that size their risers and treads from the floor-to-floor height, with handrails and balusters and the stairwell cut in the floor above; draw guardrails along decks and around openings.","section":"tasks","order":23,"keywords":["spiral","round stair","stair","stairs","staircase","flight","riser","tread","landing","winder","L stair","U stair","switchback","handrail","guardrail","guard","railing","baluster","spindle","newel","post","stairwell","headroom","deck","balcony"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-stair","text":"Draw a stair","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-spiral-stair","text":"Draw a spiral stair","level":2},{"id":"how-the-steps-are-worked-out","text":"How the steps are worked out","level":2},{"id":"change-a-stair","text":"Change a stair","level":2},{"id":"the-stairwell","text":"The stairwell","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-railing","text":"Draw a railing","level":2},{"id":"what-s-saved","text":"What's saved","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd stairs between levels and railings where people need them. A stair works out its own steps from the height between the levels, keeps them within the usual residential limits, puts handrails on its open sides and cuts its stairwell in the floor above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-stair\"\u003EDraw a stair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the level the stair starts from, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStair\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the kind — \u003Cstrong\u003EStraight\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EL with a landing\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EU with a landing\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EL with winders\u003C/strong\u003E — which way it \u003Cstrong\u003Eturns\u003C/strong\u003E, its \u003Cstrong\u003Ewidth\u003C/strong\u003E and where its \u003Cstrong\u003Ehandrails\u003C/strong\u003E go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the stair starts: the middle of the front edge of the bottom step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer the way the stair goes up; the steps follow it, with the number of risers and treads. It snaps to the walls and to 15° steps; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it square.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-spiral-stair\"\u003EDraw a spiral stair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStair\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cstrong\u003ESpiral\u003C/strong\u003E as the kind.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003ETurn\u003C/strong\u003E, which way it winds going up (left is anticlockwise).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center of its post.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the way the bottom tread points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt starts 30&quot; wide (the tread, from the post to the outside), with a 4½&quot; post. The risers are worked out from the floor-to-floor height, at most 9½&quot; by default. Treads are 7½&quot; deep on the walk line, 12&quot; out from the post. The handrail runs on the outside.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E a spiral stair adds \u003Cstrong\u003ECenter post\u003C/strong\u003E, its diameter. The \u003Cstrong\u003ETread\u003C/strong\u003E field is measured on the walk line. Its checks are labelled defaults for spiral stairs: 26&quot; clear width, at least 6¾&quot; of tread on the walk line, risers at most 9½&quot; and handrails 34&quot;–38&quot;. They are not engineering; the building official decides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-steps-are-worked-out\"\u003EHow the steps are worked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe height between the level&#039;s floor and the level above is split into equal risers no higher than 7¾&quot;, and each tread is 10&quot; deep (both can be changed in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E). A landing counts as one step; winders are three steps fanned around the inside corner. Change a level&#039;s height and every stair on it is worked out again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E7¾&quot; risers, 10&quot; treads, 36&quot; width, 6&#039;-8&quot; headroom and 34&quot;–38&quot; handrails are labelled defaults after the usual residential code, not engineering. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E says which are met; your building official decides what applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-stair\"\u003EChange a stair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick it (click it on the plan or in the 3D view) and change in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: its kind and turn, width, highest riser, tread depth, handrails and their height, and \u003Cstrong\u003ECut the floor above\u003C/strong\u003E for the stairwell. The risers and treads it ended up with are shown underneath.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-stairwell\"\u003EThe stairwell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the level above has a floor, the stair cuts it where the steps would have less than 6&#039;-8&quot; of headroom. The cut moves with the stair and goes away when the stair is deleted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-railing\"\u003EDraw a railing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERailing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EGuardrail\u003C/strong\u003E (36&quot; high) or \u003Cstrong\u003EHandrail\u003C/strong\u003E and its height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick along the edge — a deck, a balcony, around a stairwell — and double-click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERailings get posts at their ends and corners and balusters with gaps under 4&quot;. Pick one to change its kind, height or how far above the floor it starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-s-saved\"\u003EWhat&#039;s saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA stair is saved as an IFC stair made of stair flights (with their risers, treads, riser height and tread length), landings, winders and handrails; the stairwell is an opening in the floor slab above. Railings are IFC railings with their length and their number of balusters and posts.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Add stairs between levels and railings where people need them. A stair works out its own steps from the height between the levels, keeps them within the usual residential limits, puts handrails on its open sides and cuts its stairwell in the floor above. Draw a stair On the level the stair starts from, open Build and choose Stair. Choose the kind — Straight, L with a landing, U with a landing or L with winders — which way it turns, its width and where its handrails go. Click where the stair starts: the middle of the front edge of the bottom step. Move the pointer the way the stair goes up; the steps follow it, with the number of risers and treads. It snaps to the walls and to 15° steps; hold Shift to keep it square. Click. Esc starts again. Draw a spiral stair Open Build, choose Stair, then choose Spiral as the kind. Set Turn, which way it winds going up (left is anticlockwise). Click the center of its post. Click the way the bottom tread points. It starts 30\" wide (the tread, from the post to the outside), with a 4½\" post. The risers are worked out from the floor-to-floor height, at most 9½\" by default. Treads are 7½\" deep on the walk line, 12\" out from the post. The handrail runs on the outside. In Properties a spiral stair adds Center post, its diameter. The Tread field is measured on the walk line. Its checks are labelled defaults for spiral stairs: 26\" clear width, at least 6¾\" of tread on the walk line, risers at most 9½\" and handrails 34\"–38\". They are not engineering; the building official decides. How the steps are worked out The height between the level's floor and the level above is split into equal risers no higher than 7¾\", and each tread is 10\" deep (both can be changed in Properties). A landing counts as one step; winders are three steps fanned around the inside corner. Change a level's height and every stair on it is worked out again. 7¾\" risers, 10\" treads, 36\" width, 6'-8\" headroom and 34\"–38\" handrails are labelled defaults after the usual residential code, not engineering. Properties says which are met; your building official decides what applies. Change a stair Pick it (click it on the plan or in the 3D view) and change in Properties: its kind and turn, width, highest riser, tread depth, handrails and their height, and Cut the floor above for the stairwell. The risers and treads it ended up with are shown underneath. The stairwell When the level above has a floor, the stair cuts it where the steps would have less than 6'-8\" of headroom. The cut moves with the stair and goes away when the stair is deleted. Draw a railing Open Build and choose Railing. Choose Guardrail (36\" high) or Handrail and its height. Click along the edge — a deck, a balcony, around a stairwell — and double-click or press Enter to finish. Railings get posts at their ends and corners and balusters with gaps under 4\". Pick one to change its kind, height or how far above the floor it starts. What's saved A stair is saved as an IFC stair made of stair flights (with their risers, treads, riser height and tread length), landings, winders and handrails; the stairwell is an opening in the floor slab above. Railings are IFC railings with their length and their number of balusters and posts."},{"slug":"framing","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Framing walls, floors and roofs","summary":"Generate studs, plates, headers, joists, rafters or trusses from the model and keep them with it; they are made again when the walls, openings, floors or roof change, and the Lumber schedule is the cut list.","section":"tasks","order":24,"keywords":["framing","frame","stud","studs","plate","top plate","header","king stud","jack stud","trimmer","cripple","sill","blocking","corner","California corner","tee","joist","rim joist","band joist","trimmer","rafter","hip rafter","valley rafter","jack rafter","ridge board","ceiling joist","collar tie","truss","lumber","cut list","board feet","2x4","2x6","spacing","on center"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"frame-it","text":"Frame it","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"change-it","text":"Change it","level":2},{"id":"the-cut-list","text":"The cut list","level":2},{"id":"what-s-saved","text":"What's saved","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrame the building from what you drew. BimCraft lays out the studs, plates and headers in the walls, the joists in the floors and the rafters (or trusses) in the roof, sizes them from labelled default tables, keeps them with the model and makes them again whenever what they frame changes. The \u003Cstrong\u003ELumber\u003C/strong\u003E schedule is the cut list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"frame-it\"\u003EFrame it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a level, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFraming\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003Espacing\u003C/strong\u003E (16&quot; or 24&quot; on center; 400 or 600 mm), the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecorners\u003C/strong\u003E (three-stud or California), \u003Cstrong\u003ERafters\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ETrusses\u003C/strong\u003E for roofs, and whether walls get a row of \u003Cstrong\u003Eblocking\u003C/strong\u003E at mid-height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EFrame every wall\u003C/strong\u003E, or click one wall, a floor, or a roof (on the level the roof sits on) to frame just that.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn \u003Cstrong\u003EShow framing\u003C/strong\u003E off to hide every framing piece, like a layer; \u003Cstrong\u003ESee through\u003C/strong\u003E hides the walls, floors and roofs that are framed so you can look at the framing alone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E: a bottom plate (cut at doors), a double top plate, studs on the layout from the wall&#039;s start, king and jack studs and a header at every door and window, cripples above headers and below windows, a rough sill under windows, corners where walls meet (the wall that runs through takes the corner studs), two backer studs where another wall tees in, and blocking between studs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFloors\u003C/strong\u003E: joists across the short way of the floor, rim joists around the edge, and doubled trimmers and headers around a stairwell, with the joists through it cut short (tail joists).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoofs\u003C/strong\u003E: common rafters square to each eave, jack rafters where hips and valleys cut them, hip and valley rafters, ridge boards, ceiling joists on the plates and collar ties — or simple trusses at the spacing, one piece each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it\"\u003EChange it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick any framing piece in the 3D view or the model tree. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows its framing: change the spacing, the corners, the blocking or the roof framing. Moving walls, doors and windows, or changing the floor or the roof, makes the framing again by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo keep a piece you placed on purpose when the framing is made again, pick it and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep this piece when the framing is made again\u003C/strong\u003E. A kept piece stays where it is; when the framing round it changes it is marked so you can check it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-cut-list\"\u003EThe cut list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the Schedules panel choose \u003Cstrong\u003ELumber\u003C/strong\u003E: every framing piece grouped by size and by the stock length you&#039;d buy (whole 2&#039; lengths from 8&#039;; 600 mm steps from 2400 in millimetres), with the number of pieces and the board feet. \u003Cstrong\u003ECSV\u003C/strong\u003E downloads it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHeader, joist, rafter and ceiling joist sizes come from labelled default tables for a typical one- or two-story house, not engineering. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E says when a span or an opening is beyond its table. A designer or engineer checks spans, loads and connections.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-s-saved\"\u003EWhat&#039;s saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach framed wall, floor or roof is an IFC element assembly (&quot;Framing&quot;) of IFC members (studs, plates, rafters, collar ties, blocking, trusses) and beams (headers, joists, rims, ridge boards), each with its size, length and board feet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Frame the building from what you drew. BimCraft lays out the studs, plates and headers in the walls, the joists in the floors and the rafters (or trusses) in the roof, sizes them from labelled default tables, keeps them with the model and makes them again whenever what they frame changes. The Lumber schedule is the cut list. Frame it On a level, open Build and choose Framing. Choose the spacing (16\" or 24\" on center; 400 or 600 mm), the corners (three-stud or California), Rafters or Trusses for roofs, and whether walls get a row of blocking at mid-height. Click Frame every wall, or click one wall, a floor, or a roof (on the level the roof sits on) to frame just that. Turn Show framing off to hide every framing piece, like a layer; See through hides the walls, floors and roofs that are framed so you can look at the framing alone. What you get Walls: a bottom plate (cut at doors), a double top plate, studs on the layout from the wall's start, king and jack studs and a header at every door and window, cripples above headers and below windows, a rough sill under windows, corners where walls meet (the wall that runs through takes the corner studs), two backer studs where another wall tees in, and blocking between studs. Floors: joists across the short way of the floor, rim joists around the edge, and doubled trimmers and headers around a stairwell, with the joists through it cut short (tail joists). Roofs: common rafters square to each eave, jack rafters where hips and valleys cut them, hip and valley rafters, ridge boards, ceiling joists on the plates and collar ties — or simple trusses at the spacing, one piece each. Change it Pick any framing piece in the 3D view or the model tree. Properties shows its framing: change the spacing, the corners, the blocking or the roof framing. Moving walls, doors and windows, or changing the floor or the roof, makes the framing again by itself. To keep a piece you placed on purpose when the framing is made again, pick it and choose Keep this piece when the framing is made again. A kept piece stays where it is; when the framing round it changes it is marked so you can check it. The cut list In the Schedules panel choose Lumber: every framing piece grouped by size and by the stock length you'd buy (whole 2' lengths from 8'; 600 mm steps from 2400 in millimetres), with the number of pieces and the board feet. CSV downloads it. Header, joist, rafter and ceiling joist sizes come from labelled default tables for a typical one- or two-story house, not engineering. Properties says when a span or an opening is beyond its table. A designer or engineer checks spans, loads and connections. What's saved Each framed wall, floor or roof is an IFC element assembly (\"Framing\") of IFC members (studs, plates, rafters, collar ties, blocking, trusses) and beams (headers, joists, rims, ridge boards), each with its size, length and board feet."},{"slug":"hide-and-isolate","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Hide, isolate and see through elements","summary":"Pick several elements, hide them, show only them, or show them with everything else see-through, then bring everything back.","section":"tasks","order":24,"keywords":["hide","isolate","see-through","transparent","ghost","x-ray","show all","unhide","select several","multiple selection","ctrl click","shift click","visibility"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClear the view down to what you are looking at: hide elements that are in the way, show only the ones you picked, or keep everything else on screen as a faint outline so you still know where you are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element to pick it. To pick more, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click each one. Clicking a picked element that way takes it out again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar and choose what to do, or press its key:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) hides the picked elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIsolate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows only the picked elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESee-through\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows the picked elements as they are and everything else see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to show every element again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo cut away everything round the picked elements instead, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection box round the pick\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift X\"\u003E⇧X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith several elements picked, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E says how many and lists them by class. Click one on its own to see its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden elements cannot be picked: a click lands on whatever is behind them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESee-through elements cannot be picked either, so you can click straight through walls to what you isolated.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdges hide with their element, and turn faint with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHiding, isolating and see-through stay as they are while you edit, place library items or cut the model with a section plane. Opening another model shows everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe menu items are greyed out.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing is picked. Click an element first; point at an item to read why it cannot act.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIsolate showed a wall I had hidden.\u003C/strong\u003E Isolating shows exactly what you picked, hidden or not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole storey is missing.\u003C/strong\u003E That is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list, not \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E: tick the storey again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Clear the view down to what you are looking at: hide elements that are in the way, show only the ones you picked, or keep everything else on screen as a faint outline so you still know where you are. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Click an element to pick it. To pick more, hold Ctrl or Shift and click each one. Clicking a picked element that way takes it out again. Open Show in the toolbar and choose what to do, or press its key: Hide (H) hides the picked elements. Isolate (I) shows only the picked elements. See-through (T) shows the picked elements as they are and everything else see-through. Click Show all in the same menu, or press Shift+H, to show every element again. To cut away everything round the picked elements instead, click Section box round the pick in the same menu, or press Shift+X. See Cut an IFC model with a section plane. What you should see With several elements picked, Properties says how many and lists them by class. Click one on its own to see its properties. Hidden elements cannot be picked: a click lands on whatever is behind them. See-through elements cannot be picked either, so you can click straight through walls to what you isolated. Edges hide with their element, and turn faint with it. Hiding, isolating and see-through stay as they are while you edit, place library items or cut the model with a section plane. Opening another model shows everything again. Common mistakes The menu items are greyed out. Nothing is picked. Click an element first; point at an item to read why it cannot act. Isolate showed a wall I had hidden. Isolating shows exactly what you picked, hidden or not. A whole storey is missing. That is the Storeys list, not Show all: tick the storey again. Next Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"section-plane","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Cut an IFC model with a section plane","summary":"Slice the 3D model from the top, front or side, or keep only what is inside a section box or a volume of several boxes with tilted faces, to see inside; move the cut by dragging or typing, flip it, and pick what the cut shows.","section":"tasks","order":25,"keywords":["section","section plane","section box","grips","drag","margin","selection box","clip box","cut","clipping","slice","plan cut","floor plan","elevation","inside","interior","flip","offset","section volume","stepped section","L-shaped","tilt","sloping cut","oblique plane","extra plane"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"a-section-box","text":"A section box","level":2},{"id":"a-volume-of-several-boxes-tilted-faces-and-extra-planes","text":"A volume of several boxes, tilted faces and extra planes","level":2},{"id":"a-building-turned-in-its-site","text":"A building turned in its site","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECut through an IFC model with one plane to see inside it: from the top for a plan, or from the front or side for a section through the building.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The model is cut halfway up, and a bar appears under the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose where to cut from: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E. Front and Side also turn the view to look at the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the cut: drag the \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E slider, or type a distance in metres in the box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You can type millimetres too, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1200 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E to keep the other side of the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element on the cut to see its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOff\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E again, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take the cut away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-section-box\"\u003EA section box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E in the same bar to keep only what is inside a box. Each of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E has two sliders: where the box starts and where it ends, in metres from the model&#039;s edge. The values show beside them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox round the pick\u003C/span\u003E fits the box round the elements you picked and zooms to them. It leaves 300 mm to spare on every side; change that in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom round the pick\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith elements picked, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection box round the pick\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift X\"\u003E⇧X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same in one step, even when no cut is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrips\u003C/strong\u003E on the box move it with the pointer: drag a square in the middle of a face to move that face, one in the middle of an edge to move the two faces that meet there, or one at a corner to move the three. The sliders follow. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E a corner moves the box in plan, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E in elevation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole model\u003C/span\u003E opens the box to the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E go back to one plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-volume-of-several-boxes-tilted-faces-and-extra-planes\"\u003EA volume of several boxes, tilted faces and extra planes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA box is not always the shape you need: a stepped section, an L-shaped wing, or a cut along a wall that is not square to the grid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a box\u003C/span\u003E. The cut now keeps what is inside \u003Cstrong\u003Eany\u003C/strong\u003E of the boxes, so two boxes make a stepped or L-shaped volume. The list beside it says which box the sliders change (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox 1\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox 2\u003C/span\u003E…); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove this box\u003C/span\u003E takes the chosen one away. A volume holds up to 8 boxes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt and planes\u003C/span\u003E to shape the chosen box:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace\u003C/span\u003E and type how far it leans each way, in degrees (up to 80). The face turns about its middle; the other faces stay where the sliders put them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESquare\u003C/span\u003E puts it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a plane\u003C/span\u003E cuts the box with one more plane. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E is the way the cut-away side faces, in degrees from X towards Y; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E is how far that turns upward (0 for an upright plane, 90 for a level one); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E is how far the plane is from the middle of the box, in metres. × removes it. A box takes up to 6 extra planes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the volume cuts through solids, the cut faces are filled as before, also where two boxes meet in a step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the outline disappears and nothing is left, the tilts or planes leave nothing inside that box; the bar says so. Tilt a face back or change a plane&#039;s offset.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-building-turned-in-its-site\"\u003EA building turned in its site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMany models put the building at an angle to the file&#039;s axes (true north, shared coordinates). When the walls run at an angle, a new section starts square to the building: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E and the box run along its own walls, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESquare to the building\u003C/span\u003E in the section bar is on. Click it for the model&#039;s axes, and again to go back (the status line says by how much the building is turned). With \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings › BimCraft › A building turned in its site › As placed in the site\u003C/strong\u003E, sections and views start on the model&#039;s axes instead. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is below the cut, so you look down into the rooms. The offset is measured up from the bottom of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFront\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is behind the cut, seen from the front; \u003Cstrong\u003ESide\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is beyond it, seen from the side. The offset is measured from the front or the side of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA thin colored outline shows where the plane is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the plane cuts through a wall, slab or other solid, the cut face is filled in a dark color, like a drawn section. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E in the bar turns this off and on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view cube in the bottom-right corner turns the model to look at the cut from any side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClicking picks what you can see: anything cut away cannot be picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing changed.\u003C/strong\u003E The cut may be above the roof or outside the model. Drag the slider, or type a smaller offset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offset jumped back.\u003C/strong\u003E A distance beyond the model is moved to its end, and the status line says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn issue shows only part of my volume.\u003C/strong\u003E An issue (BCF) keeps one box with its tilted faces and planes: the first box of a volume. The status line says so when you make the issue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cut is hollow.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E in the bar is off: click it to fill the cut faces of walls, slabs and other solids.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA filled cut looks speckled or misses a piece.\u003C/strong\u003E Filling needs closed solids; an element whose shape in the file is not closed (a single sheet, tree foliage) is left unfilled rather than painted over. An element made of parts in different colours (a frame and its glass) is filled when its parts close together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere is the section bar?\u003C/strong\u003E It sits at the top of the 3D view it cuts: on the 3D half in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit\u003C/span\u003E, and under the drawing bar in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E alone. In a short view it scrolls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E — drawings in 3D have the same section plane\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Cut through an IFC model with one plane to see inside it: from the top for a plan, or from the front or side for a section through the building. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Click Section in the toolbar, or press X. The model is cut halfway up, and a bar appears under the toolbar. Choose where to cut from: Top, Front or Side. Front and Side also turn the view to look at the cut. Move the cut: drag the Offset slider, or type a distance in metres in the box and press Enter. You can type millimetres too, for example 1200 mm. Click Flip to keep the other side of the cut. Click an element on the cut to see its properties. Click Off, click Section again, or press X to take the cut away. A section box Click Box in the same bar to keep only what is inside a box. Each of X, Y and Z has two sliders: where the box starts and where it ends, in metres from the model's edge. The values show beside them. Box round the pick fits the box round the elements you picked and zooms to them. It leaves 300 mm to spare on every side; change that in Settings › BimCraft › Room round the pick. With elements picked, Show › Section box round the pick or Shift+X does the same in one step, even when no cut is on. Grips on the box move it with the pointer: drag a square in the middle of a face to move that face, one in the middle of an edge to move the two faces that meet there, or one at a corner to move the three. The sliders follow. In Top a corner moves the box in plan, in Front or Side in elevation. Whole model opens the box to the whole model again. Top, Front or Side go back to one plane. A volume of several boxes, tilted faces and extra planes A box is not always the shape you need: a stepped section, an L-shaped wing, or a cut along a wall that is not square to the grid. In Box, click Add a box. The cut now keeps what is inside any of the boxes, so two boxes make a stepped or L-shaped volume. The list beside it says which box the sliders change (Box 1, Box 2…); Remove this box takes the chosen one away. A volume holds up to 8 boxes. Open Tilt and planes to shape the chosen box: Choose a Face and type how far it leans each way, in degrees (up to 80). The face turns about its middle; the other faces stay where the sliders put them. Square puts it back. Add a plane cuts the box with one more plane. Faces is the way the cut-away side faces, in degrees from X towards Y; Slope is how far that turns upward (0 for an upright plane, 90 for a level one); Offset is how far the plane is from the middle of the box, in metres. × removes it. A box takes up to 6 extra planes. Where the volume cuts through solids, the cut faces are filled as before, also where two boxes meet in a step. If the outline disappears and nothing is left, the tilts or planes leave nothing inside that box; the bar says so. Tilt a face back or change a plane's offset. A building turned in its site Many models put the building at an angle to the file's axes (true north, shared coordinates). When the walls run at an angle, a new section starts square to the building: Front, Side and the box run along its own walls, and Square to the building in the section bar is on. Click it for the model's axes, and again to go back (the status line says by how much the building is turned). With Settings › BimCraft › A building turned in its site › As placed in the site, sections and views start on the model's axes instead. See Settings. What you should see Top keeps what is below the cut, so you look down into the rooms. The offset is measured up from the bottom of the model. Front keeps what is behind the cut, seen from the front; Side keeps what is beyond it, seen from the side. The offset is measured from the front or the side of the model. A thin colored outline shows where the plane is. Where the plane cuts through a wall, slab or other solid, the cut face is filled in a dark color, like a drawn section. Fill in the bar turns this off and on. The view cube in the bottom-right corner turns the model to look at the cut from any side. Clicking picks what you can see: anything cut away cannot be picked. Common mistakes Nothing changed. The cut may be above the roof or outside the model. Drag the slider, or type a smaller offset. The offset jumped back. A distance beyond the model is moved to its end, and the status line says so. An issue shows only part of my volume. An issue (BCF) keeps one box with its tilted faces and planes: the first box of a volume. The status line says so when you make the issue. The cut is hollow. Fill in the bar is off: click it to fill the cut faces of walls, slabs and other solids. A filled cut looks speckled or misses a piece. Filling needs closed solids; an element whose shape in the file is not closed (a single sheet, tree foliage) is left unfilled rather than painted over. An element made of parts in different colours (a frame and its glass) is filled when its parts close together. Where is the section bar? It sits at the top of the 3D view it cuts: on the 3D half in Split, and under the drawing bar in Plan alone. In a short view it scrolls. Next Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D — drawings in 3D have the same section plane"},{"slug":"terrain","scope":"bimcraft","title":"The site's ground, the building pad and paving","summary":"Bring the ground into the model from survey points, contours, a LAS / LAZ scan, a GeoTIFF, a LandXML surface (CivilCraft's) or a few spot elevations; cut the house's building pad in with its slopes and cut / fill; drape driveways, walks and patios on it; hand it to CivilCraft as LandXML.","section":"tasks","order":25,"keywords":["terrain","ground","site","topography","survey","points","PNEZD","contours","LAS","LAZ","LiDAR","GeoTIFF","DEM","elevation","spot elevation","grade","grading","pad","building pad","cut","fill","earthwork","daylight","slope","driveway","walk","patio","paving","LandXML","CivilCraft"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"bring-the-ground-in","text":"Bring the ground in","level":2},{"id":"the-building-pad","text":"The building pad","level":2},{"id":"driveways-walks-and-patios","text":"Driveways, walks and patios","level":2},{"id":"footings-step-to-the-grade","text":"Footings step to the grade","level":2},{"id":"hand-it-to-civilcraft","text":"Hand it to CivilCraft","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut the house on its site. The ground becomes part of the model: shown in 3D, cut in sections and elevations, with contours on the plan. A building pad is cut into it under the house, driveways and walks follow it, and footings step down with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-the-ground-in\"\u003EBring the ground in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEither:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a survey file…\u003C/strong\u003E: survey points (PNEZD, PENZD or plain easting, northing, elevation; comma, tab or space separated), contours in a DXF or DWG (polylines at their elevation, 3D polylines), a LAS or LAZ scan (ground points kept, thinned to about one per square meter), a GeoTIFF or ASCII elevation grid, or a \u003Cstrong\u003ELandXML\u003C/strong\u003E surface such as CivilCraft exports (its own points and triangles are kept; when the file holds several surfaces, BimCraft asks which one is the ground; its units come from the file). Choose the file&#039;s units first (LandXML says its own).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr type a \u003Cstrong\u003ESpot elevation\u003C/strong\u003E (from this level&#039;s floor, so \u003Ccode\u003E-1&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is a foot below it) and click spots on the plan, change the elevation and click more, then \u003Cstrong\u003EMake the ground\u003C/strong\u003E. Three spots are enough to start.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESurvey coordinates far from the model (state plane, UTM) are moved onto the house, and the elevations are set so the ground at the house is about 8&quot; under the first floor. \u003Cstrong\u003ERaise the ground\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E corrects that by any amount.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-building-pad\"\u003EThe building pad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuilding pad\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cstrong\u003EMargin\u003C/strong\u003E around the house, how far the \u003Cstrong\u003Epad sits below the floor\u003C/strong\u003E (8&quot; under a slab, more under a crawlspace) and the \u003Cstrong\u003ESlopes\u003C/strong\u003E out to the existing ground (\u003Ccode\u003E2:1\u003C/code\u003E = two feet out for each foot up or down).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint inside the house and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ground is flattened under the house and out to the margin, then slopes up into the hillside (cut) and down to the low side (fill) until it meets the existing ground. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E of the ground and of the pad give the cut and fill volumes. Move the walls and the pad follows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"driveways-walks-and-patios\"\u003EDriveways, walks and patios\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaving\u003C/span\u003E, pick \u003Cstrong\u003EWalk\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDriveway\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EPatio\u003C/strong\u003E, and click the corners. Click the first corner again, double-click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. The paving follows the finished grade; change its kind or thickness in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"footings-step-to-the-grade\"\u003EFootings step to the grade\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the ground in the model, stem walls and crawlspace footings follow it: along each wall the footing stays its depth under the ground outside, stepping in whole 8&quot; steps where the ground falls (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/foundations\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/foundations\"\u003EFoundations under the walls\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hand-it-to-civilcraft\"\u003EHand it to CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELandXML for CivilCraft\u003C/strong\u003E (in the Terrain row, or the ground&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E) downloads the existing ground and the finished grade as a LandXML file. Open it in CivilCraft for real grading, drainage and earthwork.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ground needs an IFC4 model, like every new building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EContours show on the plan of the ground level at the interval set in the ground&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E; every fifth is heavier.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ground is saved as an IFC terrain element (a closed triangulated surface, so sections cut it); the pad and paving are saved with it. Delete the ground to start again — its pad and paving go with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put the house on its site. The ground becomes part of the model: shown in 3D, cut in sections and elevations, with contours on the plan. A building pad is cut into it under the house, driveways and walks follow it, and footings step down with it. Bring the ground in Open Build and choose Terrain. Either: Open a survey file…: survey points (PNEZD, PENZD or plain easting, northing, elevation; comma, tab or space separated), contours in a DXF or DWG (polylines at their elevation, 3D polylines), a LAS or LAZ scan (ground points kept, thinned to about one per square meter), a GeoTIFF or ASCII elevation grid, or a LandXML surface such as CivilCraft exports (its own points and triangles are kept; when the file holds several surfaces, BimCraft asks which one is the ground; its units come from the file). Choose the file's units first (LandXML says its own). Or type a Spot elevation (from this level's floor, so -1' is a foot below it) and click spots on the plan, change the elevation and click more, then Make the ground. Three spots are enough to start. Survey coordinates far from the model (state plane, UTM) are moved onto the house, and the elevations are set so the ground at the house is about 8\" under the first floor. Raise the ground in Properties corrects that by any amount. The building pad Choose Building pad. Set the Margin around the house, how far the pad sits below the floor (8\" under a slab, more under a crawlspace) and the Slopes out to the existing ground (2:1 = two feet out for each foot up or down). Point inside the house and click. The ground is flattened under the house and out to the margin, then slopes up into the hillside (cut) and down to the low side (fill) until it meets the existing ground. Properties of the ground and of the pad give the cut and fill volumes. Move the walls and the pad follows. Driveways, walks and patios Choose Paving, pick Walk, Driveway or Patio, and click the corners. Click the first corner again, double-click or press Enter to finish. The paving follows the finished grade; change its kind or thickness in Properties. Footings step to the grade With the ground in the model, stem walls and crawlspace footings follow it: along each wall the footing stays its depth under the ground outside, stepping in whole 8\" steps where the ground falls (see Foundations under the walls). Hand it to CivilCraft LandXML for CivilCraft (in the Terrain row, or the ground's Properties) downloads the existing ground and the finished grade as a LandXML file. Open it in CivilCraft for real grading, drainage and earthwork. Good to know The ground needs an IFC4 model, like every new building. Contours show on the plan of the ground level at the interval set in the ground's Properties; every fifth is heavier. The ground is saved as an IFC terrain element (a closed triangulated surface, so sections cut it); the pad and paving are saved with it. Delete the ground to start again — its pad and paving go with it."},{"slug":"color-by-property","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Color elements by a property","summary":"Paint every element by the value of one property, such as its class, type, storey, material, fire rating or cost, read the legend, click a value to show only those elements, and save the colors as a scheme to use on other models.","section":"tasks","order":26,"keywords":["color","colour","color by","legend","smart view","appearance","property","type","material","fire rating","filter","isolate","theme","values","scheme","color scheme","save colors","reuse","team","json","swatch"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"change-colors-and-keep-them-as-a-scheme","text":"Change colors and keep them as a scheme","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee at a glance how a property is spread over the model: which walls are external, which materials are used where, which elements have no fire rating. Every element takes the color of its value, and the legend says what each color means and how many elements have it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a property in the list. The first ones belong to every element (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E…); the rest come from the model&#039;s property sets and quantity sets, under the set&#039;s name. The number after each one is how many elements have it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the legend: a color, the value and how many elements have it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value to show only those elements. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to add more values. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to show everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOff\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the model&#039;s own colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUp to seven values each get their own color, the most common value first. More values than that share one gray, listed as \u003Cstrong\u003Eother values\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number with many different values is split into six equal ranges, from light (low) to dark (high).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements without the property are a pale gray, listed as \u003Cstrong\u003ENo value\u003C/strong\u003E, so the colored ones stand out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe colors change with Paper and Model, and stay while you edit the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA type&#039;s property counts for every element of that type; when an element has its own value for the same property, its own value wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-colors-and-keep-them-as-a-scheme\"\u003EChange colors and keep them as a scheme\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value&#039;s swatch in the legend to choose another color for it. The model is repainted at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave scheme\u003C/span\u003E downloads the colors as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file named after the property, for example \u003Ccode\u003EClass colors.json\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn another model, or on a teammate&#039;s computer, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen scheme…\u003C/span\u003E and choose that file. Every value in the scheme gets its color, so the same fire rating or system is the same color in every model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA value the scheme does not have is painted gray and listed as &quot;not in the scheme&quot;; values in the scheme that this model does not have stay listed with 0, so you can see the whole scheme.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA scheme keeps its colors in both themes. Choosing a property in the list starts from the automatic colors again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This model has no … values&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The scheme was saved for a property this model does not have (another property set name, for example). Open a scheme made for this kind of model, or choose a property in the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA property is missing from the list.\u003C/strong\u003E No element with a shape in the model has it. Properties of storeys and spaces themselves are not listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is the same color.\u003C/strong\u003E Every element has the same value, or none: look at the legend&#039;s counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See at a glance how a property is spread over the model: which walls are external, which materials are used where, which elements have no fire rating. Every element takes the color of its value, and the legend says what each color means and how many elements have it. Steps Open an IFC model. Click the Color tab on the right. Choose a property in the list. The first ones belong to every element (Class, Type, Storey, Material, Name…); the rest come from the model's property sets and quantity sets, under the set's name. The number after each one is how many elements have it. Read the legend: a color, the value and how many elements have it. Click a value to show only those elements. Hold Ctrl and click to add more values. Press Shift+H to show everything again. Click Off to go back to the model's own colors. What you should see Up to seven values each get their own color, the most common value first. More values than that share one gray, listed as other values. A number with many different values is split into six equal ranges, from light (low) to dark (high). Elements without the property are a pale gray, listed as No value, so the colored ones stand out. The colors change with Paper and Model, and stay while you edit the model. A type's property counts for every element of that type; when an element has its own value for the same property, its own value wins. Change colors and keep them as a scheme Click a value's swatch in the legend to choose another color for it. The model is repainted at once. Save scheme downloads the colors as a small .json file named after the property, for example Class colors.json. On another model, or on a teammate's computer, click Open scheme… and choose that file. Every value in the scheme gets its color, so the same fire rating or system is the same color in every model. A value the scheme does not have is painted gray and listed as \"not in the scheme\"; values in the scheme that this model does not have stay listed with 0, so you can see the whole scheme. A scheme keeps its colors in both themes. Choosing a property in the list starts from the automatic colors again. Common mistakes \"This model has no … values\". The scheme was saved for a property this model does not have (another property set name, for example). Open a scheme made for this kind of model, or choose a property in the list. A property is missing from the list. No element with a shape in the model has it. Properties of storeys and spaces themselves are not listed. Everything is the same color. Every element has the same value, or none: look at the legend's counts. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Find your way with the model tree"},{"slug":"sheets","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Elevations, sections and sheets","summary":"Draw section lines on the plan, then open Sheets for a print-ready set made from the model: plans of each level, the four elevations, building sections, schedules and wall framing, with title blocks and scales, as one PDF.","section":"tasks","order":26,"keywords":["sheets","drawing set","construction documents","print","plot","pdf","elevation","elevations","north elevation","section","building section","section line","cut","hidden lines","title block","sheet number","scale","1/4\" = 1'-0\"","1:50","1:100","paper","11x17","24x36","A3","A1","permit set"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-section-line","text":"Draw a section line","level":2},{"id":"open-the-sheets","text":"Open the sheets","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-the-sheets","text":"What is on the sheets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMake a drawing set from the model: the plan of each level, the north, east, south and west elevations, a building section for every section line, the door, window, room and lumber schedules, and an elevation of each framed wall. The set is drawn again from the model each time you open it, so it always matches what you built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-section-line\"\u003EDraw a section line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a level, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection line\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the line starts and where it ends. The short line at its middle shows the side the section looks toward: the left of the line, going from the first click to the second.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line shows on the plan with its heads and its letter (A, then B, and so on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick the line to change it in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E: its letter, \u003Cstrong\u003EFlip the view\u003C/strong\u003E to look the other way, and \u003Cstrong\u003ELook as far as\u003C/strong\u003E to stop the view a set distance behind the cut (0 shows everything behind it). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-sheets\"\u003EOpen the sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E in the model bar (beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sheets are listed on the left: \u003Cstrong\u003EA-101\u003C/strong\u003E and on for plans, \u003Cstrong\u003EA-201\u003C/strong\u003E for elevations, \u003Cstrong\u003EA-301\u003C/strong\u003E for sections, \u003Cstrong\u003EA-401\u003C/strong\u003E for schedules and \u003Cstrong\u003ES-101\u003C/strong\u003E for wall framing. Click one to see it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E: Letter, 11 × 17, 24 × 36, A3 or A1.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the set as PDF\u003C/span\u003E: one page per sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-the-sheets\"\u003EWhat is on the sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlans\u003C/strong\u003E show walls, doors, windows, rooms with their areas, dimensions and section lines, at the largest standard scale that fits: 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; or smaller (1:50 or smaller in millimetres).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevations\u003C/strong\u003E are drawn from the 3D model with hidden lines removed, two to a sheet, all at the same scale, with a ground line under them. The roof, the foundations and the ground show as they are modeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESections\u003C/strong\u003E fill what the cut passes through (walls, floors, the roof) and draw what is seen beyond it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevations and sections carry their notes:\u003C/strong\u003E a mark for each level with its name and height on the right, the height between levels on the left, and on an elevation the building&#039;s width under it and a tag (D1, W1) on each door and window in a wall facing you. For a room&#039;s own walls see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/interior-elevations\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/interior-elevations\"\u003EInterior elevations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchedules\u003C/strong\u003E are the same tables as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedules\u003C/span\u003E in the model bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFraming\u003C/strong\u003E shows each framed wall&#039;s studs, plates, headers and sills as seen from its outside, so a crew can lay it out. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/framing\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/framing\"\u003EFraming walls, floors and roofs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sheet has a title block with its number, its title, the file&#039;s name and the date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets come from the model: to change a drawing, change the model and open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElevations and sections show lines and fills only: dimensions, level marks and notes on them are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Make a drawing set from the model: the plan of each level, the north, east, south and west elevations, a building section for every section line, the door, window, room and lumber schedules, and an elevation of each framed wall. The set is drawn again from the model each time you open it, so it always matches what you built. Draw a section line On a level, open Build and choose Section line. Click where the line starts and where it ends. The short line at its middle shows the side the section looks toward: the left of the line, going from the first click to the second. The line shows on the plan with its heads and its letter (A, then B, and so on). Pick the line to change it in Properties: its letter, Flip the view to look the other way, and Look as far as to stop the view a set distance behind the cut (0 shows everything behind it). Press Delete to remove it. Open the sheets Click Sheets in the model bar (beside Print). The sheets are listed on the left: A-101 and on for plans, A-201 for elevations, A-301 for sections, A-401 for schedules and S-101 for wall framing. Click one to see it. Choose the Paper: Letter, 11 × 17, 24 × 36, A3 or A1. Click Download the set as PDF: one page per sheet. What is on the sheets Plans show walls, doors, windows, rooms with their areas, dimensions and section lines, at the largest standard scale that fits: 1/4\" = 1'-0\" or smaller (1:50 or smaller in millimetres). Elevations are drawn from the 3D model with hidden lines removed, two to a sheet, all at the same scale, with a ground line under them. The roof, the foundations and the ground show as they are modeled. Sections fill what the cut passes through (walls, floors, the roof) and draw what is seen beyond it. Elevations and sections carry their notes: a mark for each level with its name and height on the right, the height between levels on the left, and on an elevation the building's width under it and a tag (D1, W1) on each door and window in a wall facing you. For a room's own walls see Interior elevations. Schedules are the same tables as Schedules in the model bar. Framing shows each framed wall's studs, plates, headers and sills as seen from its outside, so a crew can lay it out. See Framing walls, floors and roofs. Each sheet has a title block with its number, its title, the file's name and the date. Sheets come from the model: to change a drawing, change the model and open Sheets again. Elevations and sections show lines and fills only: dimensions, level marks and notes on them are not built yet."},{"slug":"several-models","scope":"bimcraft","title":"See several IFC models together","summary":"Add more IFC files to the scene, such as architecture, structure and services, see them in the same coordinates, move one, hide one, and choose which one you edit.","section":"tasks","order":27,"keywords":["federate","federation","federated model","several models","multiple files","combine","overlay","coordination","discipline","structure","mep","services","offset","move model","active model","add a model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at models from different disciplines in one place: the architect&#039;s model with the structural and services models in it. Each stays its own file; BimCraft only shows them together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the first IFC model as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E on the left, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E and choose one or more IFC files. Each appears in the list and in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a model to hide it; tick it to show it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a model comes in at the wrong place (a different survey point), click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E under it, type how far to move it along X, Y and Z in metres, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model with the dot is the one you edit. Click another model&#039;s dot to edit that one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick × beside a model to take it out of the scene.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery model in the first model&#039;s coordinates, as its file places it. Models exported from the same project line up without moving anything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPicking an element of a model you are not editing shows its properties read only, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit that model\u003C/span\u003E to switch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list, placing library items and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E all work on the model you edit. Hide, isolate, see-through and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E work across every model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E downloads the model you edit, with its changes only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA model is far away or missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Its file uses other coordinates. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to find it, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E it into place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete or Copy is greyed out on a picked element.\u003C/strong\u003E It belongs to another model: switch to it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a file with Choose a file replaced everything.\u003C/strong\u003E That starts a new scene. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E to add files to the one you have.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look at models from different disciplines in one place: the architect's model with the structural and services models in it. Each stays its own file; BimCraft only shows them together. Steps Open the first IFC model as usual. In Models on the left, click Add a model… and choose one or more IFC files. Each appears in the list and in the 3D view. Untick a model to hide it; tick it to show it again. If a model comes in at the wrong place (a different survey point), click Move under it, type how far to move it along X, Y and Z in metres, and click Apply. The model with the dot is the one you edit. Click another model's dot to edit that one. Click × beside a model to take it out of the scene. What you should see Every model in the first model's coordinates, as its file places it. Models exported from the same project line up without moving anything. Picking an element of a model you are not editing shows its properties read only, with Edit that model to switch. The Model tree, Storeys, Color, the Changes list, placing library items and Save as IFC all work on the model you edit. Hide, isolate, see-through and Select all work across every model. Save as IFC downloads the model you edit, with its changes only. Common mistakes A model is far away or missing. Its file uses other coordinates. Use Fit to find it, then Move it into place. Delete or Copy is greyed out on a picked element. It belongs to another model: switch to it first. Opening a file with Choose a file replaced everything. That starts a new scene. Use Add a model… to add files to the one you have. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"point-clouds","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Show a point cloud beside the model","summary":"Open a laser scan (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, PTS or XYZ) in the 3D view on its own or with an IFC model, color it, cut a thin slice through it, measure to its points, and hand it to the drafting app to trace as-built lines.","section":"tasks","order":27,"keywords":["point cloud","laser scan","scan","lidar","las","laz","e57","ply","pcd","ptx","xyz","pts","as-built","as built","survey","registration","slice","plan cut","snap","measure","trace","scan to bim"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"where-the-cloud-goes","text":"Where the cloud goes","level":2},{"id":"trace-the-as-built-lines","text":"Trace the as-built lines","level":2},{"id":"how-many-points","text":"How many points","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at a laser scan of a building next to its model: check that what was built matches the design, measure between scanned points, and cut a thin slice to read walls, doors and columns from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the cloud: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E a \u003Ccode\u003E.las\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.laz\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.e57\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ply\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pcd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ptx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pts\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.xyz\u003C/code\u003E file, or drop it on the page (see the topic on point cloud formats for what each one brings, and what to export from a scanner&#039;s own software). With a model open, the cloud joins the model in the 3D view; opening a cloud never closes the model or its unsaved changes. Without a model, the cloud opens on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cloud bar appears under the toolbar. It says how many points show and how many the file has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor by\u003C/span\u003E: the file&#039;s own colors (when the scanner stored them), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntensity\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E (LiDAR classes such as ground, vegetation and building) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne color\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E makes the points bigger or smaller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlice\u003C/span\u003E to keep only a thin band 1.2 m above the lowest point, seen from the top: walls, door openings and columns read like a floor plan. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E cut upright bands for elevations and sections. Move the band with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAt\u003C/span\u003E, and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness\u003C/span\u003E (meters). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOff\u003C/span\u003E shows the whole cloud again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The pointer snaps to the cloud&#039;s points (the label says &quot;point cloud&quot;), so you can measure from wall to wall in the slice, or from a model element to the scan. A model corner or edge within reach still wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E, a cloud point gives its latitude and longitude from the cloud&#039;s own coordinate system, when the file names one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E hides the cloud for a moment; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes it out. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint cloud\u003C/span\u003E hides or shows the bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-cloud-goes\"\u003EWhere the cloud goes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E in the bar says how the cloud lines up with the model:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E: the cloud&#039;s numbers are the model&#039;s own (a scan registered to the design&#039;s coordinates).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMap coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E: the model has a map conversion (IfcMapConversion) and the cloud is in the same projected system: the cloud is placed through it. If the two name different systems, the status line says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECentered on the model\u003C/strong\u003E: when neither puts the cloud near the model, its middle goes on the model&#039;s middle and its lowest point on the model&#039;s floor. Pick another choice to override it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the file does not say its units, BimCraft reads meters and offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in the bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trace-the-as-built-lines\"\u003ETrace the as-built lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrace in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E to hand the cloud to the drafting app in a new tab. It opens there under the drawing, where a plan slice and object snap let you draw the walls over the scan. See the CadCraft topic on point clouds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-many-points\"\u003EHow many points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scan can hold hundreds of millions of points. BimCraft reads every point once and keeps an even share of them to draw: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E in the bar sets how many (1, 2, 5 or 10 million). More points need more memory and a faster graphics card. Counts per LiDAR class are of every point in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFiles are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. LAS 1.0–1.4 and LAZ are read (LAZ files up to 1 GB, held in memory while they are read); E57 files up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now (larger scans: export LAS or LAZ); PLY, PCD and PTX in full; text clouds are \u003Ccode\u003Ex y z\u003C/code\u003E, with color (\u003Ccode\u003Er g b\u003C/code\u003E) and intensity columns when they are there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look at a laser scan of a building next to its model: check that what was built matches the design, measure between scanned points, and cut a thin slice to read walls, doors and columns from. Steps Open the cloud: Open a .las, .laz, .e57, .ply, .pcd, .ptx, .pts or .xyz file, or drop it on the page (see the topic on point cloud formats for what each one brings, and what to export from a scanner's own software). With a model open, the cloud joins the model in the 3D view; opening a cloud never closes the model or its unsaved changes. Without a model, the cloud opens on its own. The cloud bar appears under the toolbar. It says how many points show and how many the file has. Choose Color by: the file's own colors (when the scanner stored them), Height, Intensity, Class (LiDAR classes such as ground, vegetation and building) or One color. Size makes the points bigger or smaller. Click Plan under Slice to keep only a thin band 1.2 m above the lowest point, seen from the top: walls, door openings and columns read like a floor plan. Front and Side cut upright bands for elevations and sections. Move the band with At, and change Thickness (meters). Off shows the whole cloud again. Click Measure in the toolbar. The pointer snaps to the cloud's points (the label says \"point cloud\"), so you can measure from wall to wall in the slice, or from a model element to the scan. A model corner or edge within reach still wins. With Measure › Location, a cloud point gives its latitude and longitude from the cloud's own coordinate system, when the file names one. Hide hides the cloud for a moment; Remove takes it out. Show › Point cloud hides or shows the bar. Where the cloud goes Place in the bar says how the cloud lines up with the model: Model coordinates: the cloud's numbers are the model's own (a scan registered to the design's coordinates). Map coordinates: the model has a map conversion (IfcMapConversion) and the cloud is in the same projected system: the cloud is placed through it. If the two name different systems, the status line says so. Centered on the model: when neither puts the cloud near the model, its middle goes on the model's middle and its lowest point on the model's floor. Pick another choice to override it. When the file does not say its units, BimCraft reads meters and offers Units in the bar. Trace the as-built lines Click Trace in CadCraft to hand the cloud to the drafting app in a new tab. It opens there under the drawing, where a plan slice and object snap let you draw the walls over the scan. See the CadCraft topic on point clouds. How many points A scan can hold hundreds of millions of points. BimCraft reads every point once and keeps an even share of them to draw: Show in the bar sets how many (1, 2, 5 or 10 million). More points need more memory and a faster graphics card. Counts per LiDAR class are of every point in the file. Files are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. LAS 1.0–1.4 and LAZ are read (LAZ files up to 1 GB, held in memory while they are read); E57 files up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now (larger scans: export LAS or LAZ); PLY, PCD and PTX in full; text clouds are x y z, with color (r g b) and intensity columns when they are there."},{"slug":"electrical-plan","scope":"bimcraft","title":"The electrical plan","summary":"Place outlets, switches, lights, sensors, junction boxes and panels on the plan with the same symbols as CadCraft's Electrical add-on, link switches to the lights they turn on, and list devices and circuits in the schedules.","section":"tasks","order":27,"keywords":["electrical","electrical plan","outlet","receptacle","duplex","switch","three-way","dimmer","light","ceiling light","recessed light","downlight","motion sensor","junction box","panel","distribution board","circuit","load","watts","amps","switch leg","device schedule","panel schedule"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"place-devices","text":"Place devices","level":2},{"id":"link-switches-to-lights","text":"Link switches to lights","level":2},{"id":"change-and-list-them","text":"Change and list them","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the house&#039;s electrical plan in the model: outlets, switches and panels on the walls, lights and sensors on the ceiling. Each device is a real electrical element in the IFC file, with its tag, circuit and load, and switches remember the lights they turn on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-devices\"\u003EPlace devices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a level, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the device: outlets, switches and sensors, lights, a junction box or a panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a \u003Cstrong\u003ECircuit\u003C/strong\u003E if you know it (any name: 1, 2, A-3). Leave \u003Cstrong\u003EMounting height\u003C/strong\u003E blank for the usual height of that device.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on the plan. Outlets, switches and panels go on the face of the nearest wall, facing into the room; lights, sensors and junction boxes go on the ceiling where you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDevices are tagged as you place them: \u003Cstrong\u003ER\u003C/strong\u003E for outlets, \u003Cstrong\u003ES\u003C/strong\u003E for switches, \u003Cstrong\u003EL\u003C/strong\u003E for lights, \u003Cstrong\u003EM\u003C/strong\u003E for motion sensors, \u003Cstrong\u003EJ\u003C/strong\u003E for junction boxes, \u003Cstrong\u003EP\u003C/strong\u003E for panels. Devices on a wall move with it when you move the wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-switches-to-lights\"\u003ELink switches to lights\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a switch (or a motion sensor) and click \u003Cstrong\u003ELink lights\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each light it turns on. Click a linked light again to unlink it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cstrong\u003EDone\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plan draws a dashed curve from the switch to each of its lights. A light&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows which switches turn it on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-and-list-them\"\u003EChange and list them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick a device to change its tag, circuit, load (watts) and mounting height. Pick several to put them all on one circuit at once. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove one; a deleted light is taken off its switches.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedules\u003C/span\u003E choose \u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical devices\u003C/strong\u003E for every device with its level, circuit, load, height and the lights each switch turns on, or \u003Cstrong\u003ECircuits\u003C/strong\u003E for each circuit&#039;s devices, total load and current. Both are on the drawing set&#039;s schedule sheet too: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/sheets\"\u003EElevations, sections and sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMounting heights and loads are planning defaults (an outlet 16&quot; up and 200 W, a switch at 48&quot;, a ceiling light 60 W), not a design: the electrician or engineer sizes the circuits and checks the code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe symbols are the ones CadCraft&#039;s Electrical add-on draws on installation plans, so a plan from either app reads the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Draw the house's electrical plan in the model: outlets, switches and panels on the walls, lights and sensors on the ceiling. Each device is a real electrical element in the IFC file, with its tag, circuit and load, and switches remember the lights they turn on. Place devices On a level, open Build and choose Electrical. Choose the device: outlets, switches and sensors, lights, a junction box or a panel. Type a Circuit if you know it (any name: 1, 2, A-3). Leave Mounting height blank for the usual height of that device. Click on the plan. Outlets, switches and panels go on the face of the nearest wall, facing into the room; lights, sensors and junction boxes go on the ceiling where you click. Devices are tagged as you place them: R for outlets, S for switches, L for lights, M for motion sensors, J for junction boxes, P for panels. Devices on a wall move with it when you move the wall. Link switches to lights Pick a switch (or a motion sensor) and click Link lights in Properties. Click each light it turns on. Click a linked light again to unlink it. Press Enter or click Done. The plan draws a dashed curve from the switch to each of its lights. A light's Properties shows which switches turn it on. Change and list them Pick a device to change its tag, circuit, load (watts) and mounting height. Pick several to put them all on one circuit at once. Press Delete to remove one; a deleted light is taken off its switches. In Schedules choose Electrical devices for every device with its level, circuit, load, height and the lights each switch turns on, or Circuits for each circuit's devices, total load and current. Both are on the drawing set's schedule sheet too: see Elevations, sections and sheets. Mounting heights and loads are planning defaults (an outlet 16\" up and 200 W, a switch at 48\", a ceiling light 60 W), not a design: the electrician or engineer sizes the circuits and checks the code. The symbols are the ones CadCraft's Electrical add-on draws on installation plans, so a plan from either app reads the same."},{"slug":"map-layers","scope":"bimcraft","title":"A map on the ground under the model","summary":"Lay a street map or aerial picture on the ground under a georeferenced IFC model, from map tiles, map services or a picture with a world file.","section":"tasks","order":28,"keywords":["map","map layers","basemap","aerial","satellite","site","ground","tiles","xyz","wms","wmts","map service","world file","georeference","ifcmapconversion","projected crs","epsg","site context"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee the model in its place: the site&#039;s street map or aerial picture on the ground under it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model that carries its place on the map (a map conversion, IfcMapConversion, with a projected coordinate system). Exporters write it when the project&#039;s coordinates (survey point, shared coordinates) are set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap layers…\u003C/span\u003E. The dialog says the model&#039;s system, for example EPSG:28356. A model without a map conversion cannot have map layers: the dialog says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/span\u003E. Until then nothing is asked of any server; when it is on, the servers you add see the area round the model and your internet address, never your model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a layer\u003C/span\u003E choose tiles (an address with \u003Ccode\u003E{z}\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E{x}\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E{y}\u003C/code\u003E), a WMS, WMTS or ArcGIS service (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet layers\u003C/span\u003E, then choose one), or a picture with its world file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the dialog. The map lies on the ground just under the model&#039;s lowest point, over the model&#039;s footprint and as far again round it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe map is on screen only; it is not saved in the IFC file. The list of layers is kept in this browser. Use only map services whose terms allow it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do See the model in its place: the site's street map or aerial picture on the ground under it. Steps Open an IFC model that carries its place on the map (a map conversion, IfcMapConversion, with a projected coordinate system). Exporters write it when the project's coordinates (survey point, shared coordinates) are set. Choose Show › Map layers…. The dialog says the model's system, for example EPSG:28356. A model without a map conversion cannot have map layers: the dialog says so. Turn on Map layers from the internet. Until then nothing is asked of any server; when it is on, the servers you add see the area round the model and your internet address, never your model. In Add a layer choose tiles (an address with {z}, {x} and {y}), a WMS, WMTS or ArcGIS service (Get layers, then choose one), or a picture with its world file. Close the dialog. The map lies on the ground just under the model's lowest point, over the model's footprint and as far again round it. The map is on screen only; it is not saved in the IFC file. The list of layers is kept in this browser. Use only map services whose terms allow it."},{"slug":"measure","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D","summary":"Measure between two points of an IFC model with snapping to corners and edges, read the difference along X, Y and Z, and measure the area of a flat face.","section":"tasks","order":28,"keywords":["measure","measurement","distance","length","dimension","area","face area","snap","corner","edge","tape","ruler","clearance","location","latitude","longitude","lat long","coordinates","georeference","map conversion","EPSG","survey","eastings","northings","WGS 84","datum"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"where-a-point-is-on-the-earth","text":"Where a point is on the earth","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck a clearance, a wall length or the size of a face straight from the model, without opening the authoring program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A bar opens at the bottom of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA distance:\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E chosen, click the first point, then the second. Near a corner the pointer snaps to it, near an edge onto the edge; the small label at the pointer says which. Each pair of clicks makes one measurement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA face&#039;s area:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace area\u003C/span\u003E and click a face. The whole flat face lights up and its area shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E to remove the measurements, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to stop measuring.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line between the two points with its length on it, and in the bar the length with how far it goes along X, Y and Z (in the model&#039;s axes, Z up), in metres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA face&#039;s area in square metres, on the face and in the bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELabels stay on their measurements while you orbit, pan and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you measure, clicks do not pick elements. A section plane or box still cuts the model, and you can measure what it shows, cut faces included.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-a-point-is-on-the-earth\"\u003EWhere a point is on the earth\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E in the measure bar, then click a point on the model (it snaps to corners and edges as distances do). The bar shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Elatitude and longitude\u003C/strong\u003E, in decimal degrees and in degrees, minutes and seconds, in WGS 84 to start;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Emap coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E (eastings and northings in metres) in the model&#039;s own map system, for example \u003Ccode\u003EEPSG:28356\u003C/code\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eheight\u003C/strong\u003E and the vertical datum the file names, and the file&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eown datum\u003C/strong\u003E (for example GDA94).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E puts it all on the clipboard in one line. Change the system the latitude and longitude are in under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E: WGS 84 (as GPS and web maps), the file&#039;s own datum, or another common one such as ETRS89 or NAD83.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis needs a georeferenced model: one exported with map coordinates (a map conversion to a projected system with an EPSG code). A model that only has a site latitude and longitude gets an approximate position, worked out from the site&#039;s point and its true north, and the bar says so. With several models open, each point uses the georeference of its own model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving between datums (for example GDA94 to WGS 84) is done without grid files, so it is good to a few metres; the status line says how good. The map coordinates themselves are exact.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;…has no georeference&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The file has no map conversion and no site latitude and longitude. Export it again with its coordinate system set (shared coordinates / survey point in the authoring tool).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMap coordinates but no latitude and longitude.\u003C/strong\u003E The file names its map system without an EPSG code, or with one that is not in the list; the status line says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe point lands on the face, not the corner.\u003C/strong\u003E Snapping looks at the element under the pointer. Move closer to the corner, or zoom in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe area is smaller than expected.\u003C/strong\u003E Openings are left out, and a face that bends is several faces: click each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stopped measuring.\u003C/strong\u003E The first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops a half-made distance; the second stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Check a clearance, a wall length or the size of a face straight from the model, without opening the authoring program. Steps Open an IFC model. Click Measure in the toolbar, or press M. A bar opens at the bottom of the view. A distance: with Distance chosen, click the first point, then the second. Near a corner the pointer snaps to it, near an edge onto the edge; the small label at the pointer says which. Each pair of clicks makes one measurement. A face's area: choose Face area and click a face. The whole flat face lights up and its area shows. Click Clear to remove the measurements, or Done (or Esc) to stop measuring. What you should see A line between the two points with its length on it, and in the bar the length with how far it goes along X, Y and Z (in the model's axes, Z up), in metres. A face's area in square metres, on the face and in the bar. Labels stay on their measurements while you orbit, pan and zoom. While you measure, clicks do not pick elements. A section plane or box still cuts the model, and you can measure what it shows, cut faces included. Where a point is on the earth Click Location in the measure bar, then click a point on the model (it snaps to corners and edges as distances do). The bar shows: the latitude and longitude, in decimal degrees and in degrees, minutes and seconds, in WGS 84 to start; the map coordinates (eastings and northings in metres) in the model's own map system, for example EPSG:28356; the height and the vertical datum the file names, and the file's own datum (for example GDA94). Copy puts it all on the clipboard in one line. Change the system the latitude and longitude are in under Settings › BimCraft › Location: WGS 84 (as GPS and web maps), the file's own datum, or another common one such as ETRS89 or NAD83. This needs a georeferenced model: one exported with map coordinates (a map conversion to a projected system with an EPSG code). A model that only has a site latitude and longitude gets an approximate position, worked out from the site's point and its true north, and the bar says so. With several models open, each point uses the georeference of its own model. Moving between datums (for example GDA94 to WGS 84) is done without grid files, so it is good to a few metres; the status line says how good. The map coordinates themselves are exact. Common mistakes \"…has no georeference\". The file has no map conversion and no site latitude and longitude. Export it again with its coordinate system set (shared coordinates / survey point in the authoring tool). Map coordinates but no latitude and longitude. The file names its map system without an EPSG code, or with one that is not in the list; the status line says which. The point lands on the face, not the corner. Snapping looks at the element under the pointer. Move closer to the corner, or zoom in. The area is smaller than expected. Openings are left out, and a face that bends is several faces: click each. Esc stopped measuring. The first Esc drops a half-made distance; the second stops. Next Cut an IFC model with a section plane Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"takeoff","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Quantities to TakeoffCraft","summary":"One click sends the model's quantities to TakeoffCraft — walls by type, roofs by sloped area, ridges, hips and valleys, foundations, doors and windows, cabinets, electrical devices, floor area and the lumber cut list — marked on a plan of each level, with the trade packs' assemblies on the items.","section":"tasks","order":28,"keywords":["takeoff","quantity takeoff","estimate","estimating","materials list","bill of materials","takeoffcraft","quantities","wall area","roof area","squares","footing","concrete","cubic yards","cut list","lumber","trade pack","assembly"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"send-it","text":"Send it","level":2},{"id":"what-is-measured","text":"What is measured","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn the model into a materials list and estimate in TakeoffCraft. BimCraft draws a plan of each level at a standard scale, marks every quantity on it, and puts the takeoff inside the PDF. TakeoffCraft measures the marks itself, so every number can be checked on the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-it\"\u003ESend it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E in the model bar (beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETakeoffCraft opens in a new tab with the plans. When it asks, choose to open the takeoff it found in the PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd your prices in TakeoffCraft&#039;s quantities table; the assemblies already list the materials.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen TakeoffCraft isn&#039;t available here, the button downloads the PDF instead. Open it in TakeoffCraft later: it finds the takeoff inside.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-measured\"\u003EWhat is measured\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E by type and height: their centrelines as runs, with the wall height on the item, so TakeoffCraft gives the length and the wall area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoofs\u003C/strong\u003E: each roof plane as an area with its pitch (TakeoffCraft gives the sloped area), ridges, hips and valleys (hips and valleys along their slope), eaves and rakes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFoundations\u003C/strong\u003E: footings and stem walls as runs round their middles, slabs as areas with their thickness, so the concrete comes out by volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/strong\u003E counted by type, with window trim.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBase cabinets\u003C/strong\u003E by the length of their fronts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical devices\u003C/strong\u003E counted by kind.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFloor area\u003C/strong\u003E of each level, from its rooms.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe lumber cut list\u003C/strong\u003E from the framing, counted on a sheet of its own (one dot a piece).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere a trade pack has a matching assembly — exterior and interior walls, slabs, footings, stem walls, shingles, ridge and hip cap, valleys, eaves, interior doors, window trim, base cabinets, outlets, switches, lights, junction boxes and the panel — the item comes with its materials and formulas. The packs come without prices and have not been reviewed by an estimator yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plans are made from the model each time you click, so after a change just click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Turn the model into a materials list and estimate in TakeoffCraft. BimCraft draws a plan of each level at a standard scale, marks every quantity on it, and puts the takeoff inside the PDF. TakeoffCraft measures the marks itself, so every number can be checked on the sheet. Send it Click Takeoff in the model bar (beside Sheets). TakeoffCraft opens in a new tab with the plans. When it asks, choose to open the takeoff it found in the PDF. Add your prices in TakeoffCraft's quantities table; the assemblies already list the materials. When TakeoffCraft isn't available here, the button downloads the PDF instead. Open it in TakeoffCraft later: it finds the takeoff inside. What is measured Walls by type and height: their centrelines as runs, with the wall height on the item, so TakeoffCraft gives the length and the wall area. Roofs: each roof plane as an area with its pitch (TakeoffCraft gives the sloped area), ridges, hips and valleys (hips and valleys along their slope), eaves and rakes. Foundations: footings and stem walls as runs round their middles, slabs as areas with their thickness, so the concrete comes out by volume. Doors and windows counted by type, with window trim. Base cabinets by the length of their fronts. Electrical devices counted by kind. Floor area of each level, from its rooms. The lumber cut list from the framing, counted on a sheet of its own (one dot a piece). Where a trade pack has a matching assembly — exterior and interior walls, slabs, footings, stem walls, shingles, ridge and hip cap, valleys, eaves, interior doors, window trim, base cabinets, outlets, switches, lights, junction boxes and the panel — the item comes with its materials and formulas. The packs come without prices and have not been reviewed by an estimator yet. The plans are made from the model each time you click, so after a change just click Takeoff again."},{"slug":"issues-bcf","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Issues and BCF files","summary":"Save a view of the model as an issue — camera, picked elements, hidden ones, section — share it as a BCF file with other coordination tools, and open their BCF files to see their issues.","section":"tasks","order":29,"keywords":["bcf","bcfzip","issue","issues","topic","viewpoint","clash report","coordination","comment","snapshot","open bcf","save bcf","export","import"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoint at a problem in the model and keep the view that shows it, then hand it on in a BCF file, the format other coordination tools read and write.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and set up the view: orbit to it, pick the elements involved, hide what is in the way, cut it with a section plane or box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew issue from this view\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a title, and a comment if you like; choose a status; give your name or e-mail (signed in, your account&#039;s name is filled in; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings#your-name-on-issues\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"your-name-on-issues\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E). Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECreate issue\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E on an issue to put its view back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave BCF\u003C/span\u003E to download every issue as one \u003Ccode\u003E.bcf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo see issues someone else made, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen BCF…\u003C/span\u003E and choose their \u003Ccode\u003E.bcf\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.bcfzip\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach issue in the list with a picture of its view, its title, status, author and date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E puts back the camera, the picked elements, what was hidden (or the only things shown) and the section. Elements are found by their GlobalId in every open model; the status line says if some are not there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA section plane or box from another tool is shown when it lines up with the model&#039;s axes; tilted or extra planes come back as a section volume (up to 6 planes). An issue keeps the first box of a volume of several boxes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file is BCF 2.1 and keeps the view in the model&#039;s own coordinates, so other tools put the camera in the same place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIssues stay in this tab until you save them. Opening another model keeps the list; open the model the issues are about before you show them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA parallel (orthogonal) camera from another tool is shown with this viewer&#039;s perspective camera, backed off so the same part of the model fills the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow moved the camera somewhere odd.\u003C/strong\u003E The issue was made on another model or with other coordinates. Open the right model first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file will not open.\u003C/strong\u003E It is not a BCF zip, or it is damaged; the status line says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Point at a problem in the model and keep the view that shows it, then hand it on in a BCF file, the format other coordination tools read and write. Steps Open an IFC model and set up the view: orbit to it, pick the elements involved, hide what is in the way, cut it with a section plane or box. Click the Issues tab on the right, then New issue from this view. Type a title, and a comment if you like; choose a status; give your name or e-mail (signed in, your account's name is filled in; see BimCraft settings). Click Create issue. Click Show on an issue to put its view back. Click Save BCF to download every issue as one .bcf file. To see issues someone else made, click Open BCF… and choose their .bcf or .bcfzip file. What you should see Each issue in the list with a picture of its view, its title, status, author and date. Show puts back the camera, the picked elements, what was hidden (or the only things shown) and the section. Elements are found by their GlobalId in every open model; the status line says if some are not there. A section plane or box from another tool is shown when it lines up with the model's axes; tilted or extra planes come back as a section volume (up to 6 planes). An issue keeps the first box of a volume of several boxes. Good to know The file is BCF 2.1 and keeps the view in the model's own coordinates, so other tools put the camera in the same place. Issues stay in this tab until you save them. Opening another model keeps the list; open the model the issues are about before you show them. A parallel (orthogonal) camera from another tool is shown with this viewer's perspective camera, backed off so the same part of the model fills the view. Common mistakes Show moved the camera somewhere odd. The issue was made on another model or with other coordinates. Open the right model first. The file will not open. It is not a BCF zip, or it is damaged; the status line says why. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"model-from-a-scan","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Model from a scan","summary":"A short walkthrough from a laser scan to walls: open the point cloud beside a new building, line it up with a level, and trace its walls on the plan.","section":"tasks","order":29,"keywords":["scan","laser scan","point cloud","as-built","existing building","survey","trace","scan to BIM","model from scan","slice","align","square","LAS","LAZ","E57","PLY"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw an existing building from its laser scan: walls where the scan shows them, on the right level, square to each other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA building to draw in.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew building\u003C/span\u003E, with as many levels as the building has (you can add levels later, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/site-building-and-levels\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/site-building-and-levels\"\u003EThe site, the building on it, and its levels\u003C/a\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E turns on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe scan.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint cloud\u003C/span\u003E and choose the file (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX or XYZ: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/point-clouds\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/point-clouds\"\u003EShow a point cloud beside the model\u003C/a\u003E). The cloud shows in the 3D view, and the plan shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Eslice\u003C/strong\u003E: the scanned points 1200 mm (4 feet) above the level, as dots. That is the height where walls, doors and windows read best.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELine it up with the level.\u003C/strong\u003E Open the model bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E menu and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign its floor to this level\u003C/span\u003E: the cloud moves up or down until the floor it scanned sits on the level shown. Check the slice now cuts through the walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESquare to the building.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn it square to the building\u003C/span\u003E turns the cloud so the walls in its slice run along the plan&#039;s axes, so walls you draw with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held line up with the scan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrace the walls.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E and click along the dots: the tool snaps to scanned points (the snap mark is a small diamond) as well as to the walls you have drawn. A wall is drawn on its center line, so click along the middle of the two rows of dots a wall leaves; type its thickness by choosing the wall type first. Then add the doors and windows where the slice shows the gaps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe next level.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose the level above in the plan&#039;s level list: the slice follows the level&#039;s cut height. Align the floor to that level again only if the scan&#039;s floors are not where your levels are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe slice changes as you change level, and a thicker slice (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlice thickness\u003C/span\u003E) shows more points where a scan is thin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo the alignment\u003C/span\u003E puts the cloud back where it was placed when it opened. Aligning moves the cloud in the view only: it is not saved in the IFC file, and walls you have drawn stay put.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the slice on the plan\u003C/span\u003E hides or shows the dots.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBig scans: the plan draws up to 60,000 points of the slice and the 3D view a few million of the whole cloud, both picked evenly through the scan, so a large file stays quick. To trace in the drafting app instead, use the cloud bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrace in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Draw an existing building from its laser scan: walls where the scan shows them, on the right level, square to each other. Steps A building to draw in. File › New building, with as many levels as the building has (you can add levels later, see The site, the building on it, and its levels). Draw turns on. The scan. Show › Point cloud and choose the file (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX or XYZ: see Show a point cloud beside the model). The cloud shows in the 3D view, and the plan shows its slice: the scanned points 1200 mm (4 feet) above the level, as dots. That is the height where walls, doors and windows read best. Line it up with the level. Open the model bar's Scan menu and click Align its floor to this level: the cloud moves up or down until the floor it scanned sits on the level shown. Check the slice now cuts through the walls. Square to the building. Scan › Turn it square to the building turns the cloud so the walls in its slice run along the plan's axes, so walls you draw with Shift held line up with the scan. Trace the walls. Choose Wall and click along the dots: the tool snaps to scanned points (the snap mark is a small diamond) as well as to the walls you have drawn. A wall is drawn on its center line, so click along the middle of the two rows of dots a wall leaves; type its thickness by choosing the wall type first. Then add the doors and windows where the slice shows the gaps. The next level. Choose the level above in the plan's level list: the slice follows the level's cut height. Align the floor to that level again only if the scan's floors are not where your levels are. What you should see The slice changes as you change level, and a thicker slice (Scan › Slice thickness) shows more points where a scan is thin. Scan › Undo the alignment puts the cloud back where it was placed when it opened. Aligning moves the cloud in the view only: it is not saved in the IFC file, and walls you have drawn stay put. Scan › Show the slice on the plan hides or shows the dots. Big scans: the plan draws up to 60,000 points of the slice and the 3D view a few million of the whole cloud, both picked evenly through the scan, so a large file stays quick. To trace in the drafting app instead, use the cloud bar's Trace in CadCraft."},{"slug":"clash-check","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check for clashes","summary":"Find where elements of one set run into elements of another — ducts through beams, walls into slabs — in one model or across several, with a tolerance, and turn a clash into an issue.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["clash","clash detection","clash check","hard clash","interference","collision","coordination","overlap","tolerance","mep","structure"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind the places where two sets of elements run into each other, for example everything in the services model against the structure, then look at each one and keep the ones that matter as issues.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model, and add the others with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E if you are checking across models. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgainst\u003C/span\u003E, choose two sets: the elements you picked, a class (over every open model), a saved search set (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E), or everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet how much overlap to ignore, in metres. 0.01 ignores elements that only just meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The check runs in the background; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a result to see it: the two elements are picked and shown, everything else see-through, and the view zooms to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake an issue of it\u003C/span\u003E under a result to write it up in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe clashes listed with both elements&#039; names (and their files, when several models are open) and how deep they overlap, the deepest first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces that only touch, such as a wall standing on a slab or a door in its opening, are not clashes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis is a basic hard-clash check: elements whose surfaces cross. An element hidden wholly inside another (a pipe buried in a wall) is not found yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe depth is how far the two elements&#039; bounds overlap at the least: a guide, not an exact measurement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, face areas and locations in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Find the places where two sets of elements run into each other, for example everything in the services model against the structure, then look at each one and keep the ones that matter as issues. Steps Open an IFC model, and add the others with Add a model… if you are checking across models. See See several IFC models together. Open Clash check in the left panel. In Check and Against, choose two sets: the elements you picked, a class (over every open model), a saved search set (see Find elements by class, name or property), or everything. Set how much overlap to ignore, in metres. 0.01 ignores elements that only just meet. Click Run. The check runs in the background; Cancel stops it. Click a result to see it: the two elements are picked and shown, everything else see-through, and the view zooms to them. Click Make an issue of it under a result to write it up in the Issues tab. See Issues and BCF files. What you should see The clashes listed with both elements' names (and their files, when several models are open) and how deep they overlap, the deepest first. Faces that only touch, such as a wall standing on a slab or a door in its opening, are not clashes. Good to know This is a basic hard-clash check: elements whose surfaces cross. An element hidden wholly inside another (a pipe buried in a wall) is not found yet. The depth is how far the two elements' bounds overlap at the least: a guide, not an exact measurement. Next Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"floors-by-outline-and-ramps","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Floors of any shape, openings and ramps","summary":"Draw a floor or slab by its outline (a balcony, a cantilever, a landing), cut openings in it, and draw ramps with their landings.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["floor","slab","outline","balcony","cantilever","landing","deck","floor opening","hole","shaft","void","ramp","slope","accessible ramp","wheelchair ramp","1:12","IfcRamp","IfcSlab"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-floor-by-its-outline","text":"Draw a floor by its outline","level":2},{"id":"cut-an-opening","text":"Cut an opening","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-ramp","text":"Draw a ramp","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMake floors that do not follow rooms, such as a balcony beyond the walls, and draw ramps between levels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-floor-by-its-outline\"\u003EDraw a floor by its outline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, choose the level, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloor by outline\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the row under the tools, choose the kind (concrete slab or wood floor), its use (floor or landing), its thickness and where its top is above the level (0 = level with the floor of the level; a negative value sets it lower).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners. The tool snaps to walls, grid lines and their crossings; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep an edge square. The length of each edge and the area show as you go. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the last corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first corner again, double-click, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-an-opening\"\u003ECut an opening\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the floor (in the 3D view, or its outline on the plan), then in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd an opening\u003C/span\u003E and click the corners of the opening inside the floor, finishing as above. The floor&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E list its openings, each with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, and its area without them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-ramp\"\u003EDraw a ramp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERamp\u003C/span\u003E. Set its width, and whether it goes \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the level above\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Eup a rise\u003C/strong\u003E you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose how it is set: \u003Cstrong\u003Eby slope\u003C/strong\u003E (1:12 to start; the ramp is as long as the rise needs) or \u003Cstrong\u003Eby length\u003C/strong\u003E (your second click sets the length, and the slope follows).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the foot of the ramp (the middle of its bottom edge), then click the way up. The preview shows its length, its flights and its slope.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rise of more than 30 inches (760 mm) is split into flights with a 5-foot (1525 mm) landing between them. These are common planning values, not your code: check the rules where you build. A ramp steeper than 1:12 is flagged in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach floor, opening and ramp is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the plan, a drawn floor&#039;s outline (an opening is drawn crossed), and a ramp&#039;s outline, flights, landings and an arrow up with its slope.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProperties for a drawn floor (kind, use, thickness, top, area, openings) and for a ramp (width, top, rise, slope or length); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA ramp to the level above follows that level when its height changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the IFC file: a drawn floor is a slab (IfcSlab, a floor or a landing) with its openings cut out (IfcOpeningElement); a ramp is IfcRamp made of sloped flights (IfcRampFlight) and landing slabs, with its slope and width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFloors drawn this way stay where you drew them. Floors made with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloor\u003C/span\u003E follow the rooms when the walls move: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\"\u003ERooms, floors, ceilings and roofs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Make floors that do not follow rooms, such as a balcony beyond the walls, and draw ramps between levels. Draw a floor by its outline Turn on Draw, choose the level, then Build › Floor by outline. In the row under the tools, choose the kind (concrete slab or wood floor), its use (floor or landing), its thickness and where its top is above the level (0 = level with the floor of the level; a negative value sets it lower). Click the corners. The tool snaps to walls, grid lines and their crossings; hold Shift to keep an edge square. The length of each edge and the area show as you go. Backspace takes back the last corner. Click the first corner again, double-click, or press Enter to finish. Cut an opening Click the floor (in the 3D view, or its outline on the plan), then in Properties click Add an opening and click the corners of the opening inside the floor, finishing as above. The floor's Properties list its openings, each with Delete, and its area without them. Draw a ramp Choose Build › Ramp. Set its width, and whether it goes to the level above or up a rise you type. Choose how it is set: by slope (1:12 to start; the ramp is as long as the rise needs) or by length (your second click sets the length, and the slope follows). Click the foot of the ramp (the middle of its bottom edge), then click the way up. The preview shows its length, its flights and its slope. A rise of more than 30 inches (760 mm) is split into flights with a 5-foot (1525 mm) landing between them. These are common planning values, not your code: check the rules where you build. A ramp steeper than 1:12 is flagged in its Properties. What you should see Each floor, opening and ramp is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. On the plan, a drawn floor's outline (an opening is drawn crossed), and a ramp's outline, flights, landings and an arrow up with its slope. Properties for a drawn floor (kind, use, thickness, top, area, openings) and for a ramp (width, top, rise, slope or length); Delete removes either. A ramp to the level above follows that level when its height changes. In the IFC file: a drawn floor is a slab (IfcSlab, a floor or a landing) with its openings cut out (IfcOpeningElement); a ramp is IfcRamp made of sloped flights (IfcRampFlight) and landing slabs, with its slope and width. Floors drawn this way stay where you drew them. Floors made with Floor follow the rooms when the walls move: see Rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs."},{"slug":"inspect-element","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Pick an element and read its properties","summary":"Click any element in the 3D model to highlight it and read its name, type, tag, GlobalId, property sets and quantities.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["properties","pick","select","click","element","parameters","property sets","pset","quantities","globalid","guid","type","inspect"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"getting-at-hidden-elements","text":"Getting at hidden elements","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick elements in an IFC model and read everything the file says about them: attributes, property sets, type properties and quantities.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element, for example a door. Click without dragging: a drag of more than a few pixels orbits instead of picking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the element: it is highlighted in blue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. At the top are the element&#039;s kind (such as &quot;Door&quot; or &quot;WallStandardCase&quot;) and its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the first block: \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EObject type\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETag\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDescription\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGlobalId\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELine\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down to the property sets. The first three are open; click the name of any other set to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another element to switch to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click empty background, to clear the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe highlighted element and its data on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProperty sets in alphabetical order. Sets that come from the element&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Etype\u003C/strong\u003E have &quot; (type)&quot; after their name and the note &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuantity sets (such as lengths, areas and volumes) listed with the other sets. Quantities are read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EValues you can change look like buttons. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E shows how.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EProperties panel fields\u003C/a\u003E explains each field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"getting-at-hidden-elements\"\u003EGetting at hidden elements\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo reach something inside the building, untick the storeys above it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo reach a wall behind a room volume, turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDragging a little while clicking.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft treats that as an orbit and does not pick. Hold the mouse still.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting units next to numbers.\u003C/strong\u003E Values are shown as stored in the file, rounded to four decimals, without a unit symbol. Lengths in a metric export are usually millimeters or meters; check the exporter&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for a parameter by its familiar name.\u003C/strong\u003E Exporters rename and regroup parameters into IFC property sets such as \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WallCommon\u003C/code\u003E. Look through each set; custom parameters often sit in a set named after the exporting program or your company.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting a search box.\u003C/strong\u003E Click to pick an element; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click picks more, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks every element shown. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E list on the left only counts elements by kind; it is not clickable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking on a project file card.\u003C/strong\u003E A file card has no elements to pick: &quot;Element properties need the 3D model. Open an IFC export of this file to see them.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Pick elements in an IFC model and read everything the file says about them: attributes, property sets, type properties and quantities. Steps Open an IFC model. Click an element, for example a door. Click without dragging: a drag of more than a few pixels orbits instead of picking. Look at the element: it is highlighted in blue. Look at the Properties panel on the right. At the top are the element's kind (such as \"Door\" or \"WallStandardCase\") and its name. Read the first block: Name, Type, Object type, Tag, Description, GlobalId and Line. Scroll down to the property sets. The first three are open; click the name of any other set to open it. Click another element to switch to it. Press Esc, or click empty background, to clear the selection. What you should see The highlighted element and its data on the right. Property sets in alphabetical order. Sets that come from the element's type have \" (type)\" after their name and the note \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Quantity sets (such as lengths, areas and volumes) listed with the other sets. Quantities are read-only. Values you can change look like buttons. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy shows how. Properties panel fields explains each field. Getting at hidden elements To reach something inside the building, untick the storeys above it in the Storeys list. To reach a wall behind a room volume, turn Spaces off. Click the element. Common mistakes Dragging a little while clicking. BimCraft treats that as an orbit and does not pick. Hold the mouse still. Expecting units next to numbers. Values are shown as stored in the file, rounded to four decimals, without a unit symbol. Lengths in a metric export are usually millimeters or meters; check the exporter's settings. Looking for a parameter by its familiar name. Exporters rename and regroup parameters into IFC property sets such as Pset_WallCommon. Look through each set; custom parameters often sit in a set named after the exporting program or your company. Expecting a search box. Click to pick an element; Ctrl+click or Shift+click picks more, and Ctrl+A picks every element shown. The Elements list on the left only counts elements by kind; it is not clickable. Picking on a project file card. A file card has no elements to pick: \"Element properties need the 3D model. Open an IFC export of this file to see them.\""},{"slug":"grids-and-columns","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Grids and columns","summary":"Lay out grid lines with numbered and lettered bubbles, by spacing or one by one, draw walls and columns along them, and see them on plans, elevations and sections.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["grid","grids","grid line","column grid","axis","axes","bubble","gridline","structural grid","1 2 3","A B C","column","columns","pillar","post","snap","IfcGrid","IfcColumn"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"place-a-whole-grid","text":"Place a whole grid","level":2},{"id":"draw-grid-lines-one-by-one","text":"Draw grid lines one by one","level":2},{"id":"change-a-grid-line","text":"Change a grid line","level":2},{"id":"draw-along-the-grid","text":"Draw along the grid","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet out a building on a grid, then draw walls and place columns along its lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-whole-grid\"\u003EPlace a whole grid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace a grid…\u003C/span\u003E. Say how many \u003Cstrong\u003Enumbered lines\u003C/strong\u003E (1, 2, 3…) and \u003Cstrong\u003Elettered lines\u003C/strong\u003E (A, B, C…) you want and how far apart. Type one spacing, or several separated by commas for uneven bays (for example \u003Ccode\u003E20&#039;, 24&#039;, 20&#039;\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPast the last lines\u003C/span\u003E is how far each line runs beyond the outer lines of the other kind, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurned (degrees)\u003C/span\u003E turns the whole grid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace it\u003C/span\u003E, then click where the first crossing (1 / A) goes. The preview follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbered lines run up the plan and are spaced across it; lettered lines run across the plan and are spaced up it. Letters skip I and O, as on drawings, so they are never read as 1 and 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-grid-lines-one-by-one\"\u003EDraw grid lines one by one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E on, click where a line starts and where it ends. It gets the next label of the lines it runs along (after 3 comes 4; after B comes C). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-grid-line\"\u003EChange a grid line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E on, click a line to pick it. Type a new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabel\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this line\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBubbles\u003C/span\u003E puts the bubbles at both ends, at the start or end of each line, or nowhere. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-along-the-grid\"\u003EDraw along the grid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E tool, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E tool and the other drawing tools snap to grid lines and to where they cross (the snap mark is a small circle with a cross).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColumns:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E, pick the shape (rectangular or round), the size, and whether the column runs \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the level above\u003C/strong\u003E or to a fixed height, then click. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAt every grid crossing\u003C/span\u003E places a column at each crossing that has none yet. Click a column to change its shape, size or height in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it. Columns that run to the level above follow it when its height changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grid on the plan of every level, with a bubble and its label at the ends of each line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGrid lines on the drawing set: on the plans, and on elevations and sections where a line is seen end on, with its bubble above the building. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/sheets\"\u003EElevations, sections and sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the IFC file: one grid for the building (IfcGrid, its numbered lines in one list and its lettered lines in the other), and columns as IfcColumn with their size, height and quantities. Other BIM programs show both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA grid that came in a file from another program is shown, and the tools snap to it, but its lines cannot be changed here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Set out a building on a grid, then draw walls and place columns along its lines. Place a whole grid Turn on Draw, then choose Build › Grid. Click Place a grid…. Say how many numbered lines (1, 2, 3…) and lettered lines (A, B, C…) you want and how far apart. Type one spacing, or several separated by commas for uneven bays (for example 20', 24', 20'). Past the last lines is how far each line runs beyond the outer lines of the other kind, and Turned (degrees) turns the whole grid. Click Place it, then click where the first crossing (1 / A) goes. The preview follows the pointer. Numbered lines run up the plan and are spaced across it; lettered lines run across the plan and are spaced up it. Letters skip I and O, as on drawings, so they are never read as 1 and 0. Draw grid lines one by one With Grid on, click where a line starts and where it ends. It gets the next label of the lines it runs along (after 3 comes 4; after B comes C). Press Esc to stop. Change a grid line With Grid on, click a line to pick it. Type a new Label and press Enter, or click Delete this line (or press Delete). Bubbles puts the bubbles at both ends, at the start or end of each line, or nowhere. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. Draw along the grid The Wall tool, the Column tool and the other drawing tools snap to grid lines and to where they cross (the snap mark is a small circle with a cross). Columns: choose Build › Column, pick the shape (rectangular or round), the size, and whether the column runs to the level above or to a fixed height, then click. At every grid crossing places a column at each crossing that has none yet. Click a column to change its shape, size or height in Properties; Delete removes it. Columns that run to the level above follow it when its height changes. What you should see The grid on the plan of every level, with a bubble and its label at the ends of each line. Grid lines on the drawing set: on the plans, and on elevations and sections where a line is seen end on, with its bubble above the building. See Elevations, sections and sheets. In the IFC file: one grid for the building (IfcGrid, its numbered lines in one list and its lettered lines in the other), and columns as IfcColumn with their size, height and quantities. Other BIM programs show both. A grid that came in a file from another program is shown, and the tools snap to it, but its lines cannot be changed here."},{"slug":"walk","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Walk through a model","summary":"Walk through an IFC model at eye height with the keyboard, with gravity keeping you on the floor and walls stopping you.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["walk","walkthrough","first person","fly","navigate","eye height","gravity","collision","wasd","inside","tour"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee a model as someone standing in it: walk down a corridor, into a room, up a stair.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the floor where you want to stand. You stand there with your eyes at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEye height\u003C/span\u003E (1.6 m to start), looking the way the view looked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the arrow keys. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go faster. Drag in the view to look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E to stop. The view stays where you were, ready to orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGravity\u003C/strong\u003E keeps you on the floor below you and takes you up steps and stairs. Turn it off to float: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rises, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sinks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStop at walls\u003C/strong\u003E stops you just short of walls, doors and anything else in the way, and lets you slide along them. Turn it off to walk through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden and see-through elements, and what a section cuts away, do not stop you and are not stood on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happens when I press W.\u003C/strong\u003E The keys go to a box you are typing in: click in the view first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI fell a long way.\u003C/strong\u003E There was no floor under the spot (an opening, or the floor is hidden). Turn gravity off, or start on a floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See a model as someone standing in it: walk down a corridor, into a room, up a stair. Steps Open an IFC model and click Walk in the toolbar. Click the floor where you want to stand. You stand there with your eyes at Eye height (1.6 m to start), looking the way the view looked. Move with W A S D or the arrow keys. Hold Shift to go faster. Drag in the view to look round. Press Esc or click Done to stop. The view stays where you were, ready to orbit. What you should see Gravity keeps you on the floor below you and takes you up steps and stairs. Turn it off to float: E or Page Up rises, Q or Page Down sinks. Stop at walls stops you just short of walls, doors and anything else in the way, and lets you slide along them. Turn it off to walk through. Hidden and see-through elements, and what a section cuts away, do not stop you and are not stood on. Common mistakes Nothing happens when I press W. The keys go to a box you are typing in: click in the view first. I fell a long way. There was no floor under the spot (an opening, or the floor is hidden). Turn gravity off, or start on a floor. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"model-checks","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a model for common problems","summary":"Find elements missing a property you need, GlobalIds used twice, elements on no storey and copies stacked on top of each other.","section":"tasks","order":32,"keywords":["check","model check","validation","qa","quality","missing property","required","duplicate","globalid","guid","storey","stacked","overlap"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook over a model before you hand it on: the problems that break schedules, quantities and other people&#039;s software.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel checks\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERequired properties\u003C/span\u003E, choose a class (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny class\u003C/span\u003E) and a property every element of it must have. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a property\u003C/span\u003E for more. They stay in this browser for every model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun checks\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a result to pick those elements and zoom to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMissing a required property\u003C/strong\u003E: each rule with the elements that have no value for it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGlobalId used twice\u003C/strong\u003E: elements sharing one GlobalId. Other software treats them as one element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn no storey\u003C/strong\u003E: elements with a shape that no storey contains.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopies on top of each other\u003C/strong\u003E: elements of the same class and type with the same shape in the same place, which count twice in quantities.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChecks look at the active model when several are open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter an edit, run the checks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look over a model before you hand it on: the problems that break schedules, quantities and other people's software. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Model checks in the left panel. Under Required properties, choose a class (or Any class) and a property every element of it must have. Click Add a property for more. They stay in this browser for every model. Click Run checks. Click a result to pick those elements and zoom to them. What you should see Missing a required property: each rule with the elements that have no value for it. GlobalId used twice: elements sharing one GlobalId. Other software treats them as one element. On no storey: elements with a shape that no storey contains. Copies on top of each other: elements of the same class and type with the same shape in the same place, which count twice in quantities. Good to know Checks look at the active model when several are open. See See several IFC models together. After an edit, run the checks again. Next Find elements by class, name or property Delete, cut, copy and paste elements"},{"slug":"render","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Render a picture of the model","summary":"Make a daylight picture of the 3D view: sky, the sun at your site's latitude, soft shadows, glass windows, then save it as a PNG.","section":"tasks","order":32,"keywords":["render","rendering","path tracing","path traced","ray tracing","global illumination","reflections","refraction","high quality","photo","photoreal","realistic","picture","image","png","still","sky","sun","daylight","shadows","soft shadows","ambient occlusion","glass","windows","metal","materials","exposure","brightness","overcast","dusk","studio","presentation","visualization"],"headings":[{"id":"render-the-view","text":"Render the view","level":2},{"id":"what-shows-in-the-picture","text":"What shows in the picture","level":2},{"id":"settings","text":"Settings","level":2},{"id":"lamps","text":"Lamps","level":2},{"id":"glass-and-other-materials","text":"Glass and other materials","level":2},{"id":"high-quality","text":"High quality","level":2},{"id":"how-long-it-takes","text":"How long it takes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar makes a picture of the 3D view that looks like daylight: a sky, the sun where the site&#039;s latitude puts it, soft shadows, light from the whole sky that darkens corners, and colors that can look like glass or metal. The model and the file are not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"render-the-view\"\u003ERender the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the 3D view the way you want the picture: orbit and zoom, hide storeys or elements you don&#039;t want, and set a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003Esection\u003C/a\u003E if the picture should show a cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality\u003C/span\u003E, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture fills in as it renders and gets smoother with each pass. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ends it early and keeps the picture so far.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave PNG\u003C/span\u003E to download it. It is named after the model&#039;s file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe view&#039;s size\u003C/span\u003E to check the camera, then render \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E at a bigger size for the final picture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-shows-in-the-picture\"\u003EWhat shows in the picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat the 3D view shows solid: hidden and see-through elements, hidden storeys and hidden models are left out, and rooms show only when \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E is on. A section plane or section box cuts the picture as it cuts the view. Elements show in their own colors: coloring by property (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E tab) is not used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view&#039;s size, twice the view&#039;s size, Full HD, 4K or a square\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMode\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E (below) with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime limit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuality\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many passes (or, in high quality, samples) are averaged: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E 16, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood\u003C/span\u003E 64, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E 256. More passes give smoother shadows and edges and take longer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESky\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear sky\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOvercast\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDusk\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhite studio\u003C/span\u003E (a plain white background)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJune 21 at 15:00 at the latitude the file gives for its site (IfcSite), turned to the model&#039;s true north. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another date and time for this picture\u003C/span\u003E for another time\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrightness\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELighter or darker, in steps (stops)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGround under the model\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ground at the model&#039;s lowest point that takes the shadows and fades into the horizon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw edges\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraws the elements&#039; edges thinly over the picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the sun is below the horizon at the chosen time it is night: the sky goes dark and the model&#039;s lamps light it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lamps\"\u003ELamps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery light fixture in the model is a lamp in the picture: the fixtures in an IFC file (IfcLightFixture, or an element typed as a light fixture), including ones you place from the library. Each shines down from just under the fixture. When the file gives a fixture&#039;s wattage, its brightness follows from it; otherwise it is 800 lumens, a warm white.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog lists the lamps under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELamps\u003C/span\u003E: untick one to switch it off, or type its brightness in lumens. These changes are saved in the IFC (the fixture&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Light\u003C/code\u003E property set, where you can also change them in Properties), and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a file says nothing about where its site is, the sun is placed at 40° north, and the dialog says so. The time is the site&#039;s clock time, worked out from its longitude, so it can be an hour off where clocks are set differently.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings are kept in this browser for the next picture. They are not saved in the IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"glass-and-other-materials\"\u003EGlass and other materials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterials\u003C/span\u003E, every color on screen has a look: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatte\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESatin\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlossy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolished metal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlass\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrosted glass\u003C/span\u003E. Colors the IFC file makes see-through (window glazing, for example) start as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlass\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E mode, glass and metal reflect the sky, not the rest of the model, and light does not bend through glass. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E mode below does both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"high-quality\"\u003EHigh quality\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMode\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHigh quality (path traced)\u003C/span\u003E for a picture that follows light the way a camera sees it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Elight bounces between surfaces, so a room lit through a window, or by a lamp, is lit all round and colors bleed onto their neighbors;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eglass is real glass: light bends through it and it reflects the model;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Emetal and polished surfaces reflect the rest of the model;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Elight panels light as panels, with soft shadows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is much slower than \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFast\u003C/span\u003E: each pass traces every pixel&#039;s light again. The picture starts grainy and clears as samples add up, so start small (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe view&#039;s size\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E) and choose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime limit\u003C/span\u003E: when it runs out, the picture so far is kept. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E keeps it too. The first pass waits while the browser prepares the path tracer, a few seconds on a graphics card, longer without one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn this mode edges are not drawn, textures show their color and roughness but not their bumps, and a section cut is made on the model before the picture starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-long-it-takes\"\u003EHow long it takes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is made on your own computer, by its graphics card; nothing is uploaded. Time grows with the passes and the pixels: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBest\u003C/span\u003E has 16 times the passes of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraft\u003C/span\u003E, and a 4K picture has about four times the pixels of Full HD. Large models take longer per pass.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Render on the toolbar makes a picture of the 3D view that looks like daylight: a sky, the sun where the site's latitude puts it, soft shadows, light from the whole sky that darkens corners, and colors that can look like glass or metal. The model and the file are not changed. Render the view Set up the 3D view the way you want the picture: orbit and zoom, hide storeys or elements you don't want, and set a section if the picture should show a cut. Click Render on the toolbar. Choose the Size and the Quality, then click Render in the dialog. The picture fills in as it renders and gets smoother with each pass. Stop (or Esc) ends it early and keeps the picture so far. Click Save PNG to download it. It is named after the model's file. Start with Draft at The view's size to check the camera, then render Good or Best at a bigger size for the final picture. What shows in the picture What the 3D view shows solid: hidden and see-through elements, hidden storeys and hidden models are left out, and rooms show only when Spaces is on. A section plane or section box cuts the picture as it cuts the view. Elements show in their own colors: coloring by property (the Color tab) is not used. Settings Setting What it does Size The view's size, twice the view's size, Full HD, 4K or a square Mode Fast, or High quality (path traced) (below) with its Time limit Quality How many passes (or, in high quality, samples) are averaged: Draft 16, Good 64, Best 256. More passes give smoother shadows and edges and take longer Sky Clear sky, Overcast, Dusk or White studio (a plain white background) Sun June 21 at 15:00 at the latitude the file gives for its site (IfcSite), turned to the model's true north. Tick Use another date and time for this picture for another time Brightness Lighter or darker, in steps (stops) Ground under the model A ground at the model's lowest point that takes the shadows and fades into the horizon Draw edges Draws the elements' edges thinly over the picture When the sun is below the horizon at the chosen time it is night: the sky goes dark and the model's lamps light it. Lamps Every light fixture in the model is a lamp in the picture: the fixtures in an IFC file (IfcLightFixture, or an element typed as a light fixture), including ones you place from the library. Each shines down from just under the fixture. When the file gives a fixture's wattage, its brightness follows from it; otherwise it is 800 lumens, a warm white. The dialog lists the lamps under Lamps: untick one to switch it off, or type its brightness in lumens. These changes are saved in the IFC (the fixture's CadCraft_Light property set, where you can also change them in Properties), and Ctrl+Z undoes them. When a file says nothing about where its site is, the sun is placed at 40° north, and the dialog says so. The time is the site's clock time, worked out from its longitude, so it can be an hour off where clocks are set differently. These settings are kept in this browser for the next picture. They are not saved in the IFC file. Glass and other materials Under Materials, every color on screen has a look: Matte, Satin, Glossy, Metal, Polished metal, Glass or Frosted glass. Colors the IFC file makes see-through (window glazing, for example) start as Glass. In the Fast mode, glass and metal reflect the sky, not the rest of the model, and light does not bend through glass. The High quality (path traced) mode below does both. High quality Choose Mode › High quality (path traced) for a picture that follows light the way a camera sees it: light bounces between surfaces, so a room lit through a window, or by a lamp, is lit all round and colors bleed onto their neighbors; glass is real glass: light bends through it and it reflects the model; metal and polished surfaces reflect the rest of the model; light panels light as panels, with soft shadows. It is much slower than Fast: each pass traces every pixel's light again. The picture starts grainy and clears as samples add up, so start small (The view's size, Draft) and choose a Time limit: when it runs out, the picture so far is kept. Stop keeps it too. The first pass waits while the browser prepares the path tracer, a few seconds on a graphics card, longer without one. In this mode edges are not drawn, textures show their color and roughness but not their bumps, and a section cut is made on the model before the picture starts. How long it takes The picture is made on your own computer, by its graphics card; nothing is uploaded. Time grows with the passes and the pixels: Best has 16 times the passes of Draft, and a 4K picture has about four times the pixels of Full HD. Large models take longer per pass."},{"slug":"walls-from-a-drawing","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Walls from a traced drawing","summary":"Turn a DWG or DXF plan into walls, doors and windows on a level, and keep the drawing under the plan as a reference.","section":"tasks","order":32,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","trace","traced","import plan","convert","cad to bim","2d to 3d","walls from lines","layers","blocks","doors from blocks","underlay","reference drawing","background","xref","drafting app"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse a plan you (or someone else) drew in the drafting app as the start of the model: its wall lines become walls, its door and window blocks become doors and windows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E and choose the level the drawing shows in the plan&#039;s level list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the DWG or DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the dialog:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing units\u003C/strong\u003E come from the drawing; choose others if they are wrong (a plan drawn in feet read as inches is twelve times too small).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayers with the walls\u003C/strong\u003E: tick the layers the wall lines are on. Layers whose names say &quot;wall&quot; are ticked for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe lines are\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003Eboth faces of each wall\u003C/strong\u003E (the usual way plans are drawn: each pair of parallel lines up to 600 mm apart becomes one wall as thick as the gap), \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe walls&#039; center lines\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Eone face\u003C/strong\u003E with the wall to its left or right. With center lines or one face, the wall type sets the thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWall type\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EWall height\u003C/strong\u003E, as for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlocks\u003C/strong\u003E: choose a door or window type for each block that is a door or a window, or leave it out. Blocks whose names say &quot;door&quot; or &quot;window&quot; are chosen for you. Each one is put in the wall it sits on, as wide as the block along the wall; a door opens to the side its swing is drawn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere it goes\u003C/strong\u003E: at the drawing&#039;s own coordinates, or with its lower-left corner on the plan&#039;s origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep the drawing under the plan of this level\u003C/strong\u003E: leave it ticked to see the drawing as thin lines under what you make.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line under the choices says how many walls, doors and windows it will make. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake them\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe walls, doors and windows on the level, joined where they meet, with the rooms they close; all of it is one step, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drawing as thin lines under the plan. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E lists the drawings kept on the level, each with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E; removing one leaves the walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the IFC file the kept drawing is an annotation on the level (its lines), so other BIM programs show it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArcs are left out (BimCraft draws straight walls), and so are lines inside blocks. Lines with no partner in &quot;both faces&quot; are counted in the dialog and left out: draw those walls with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E tool, or tidy them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim / extend\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/trim-extend-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/trim-extend-walls\"\u003ETrim, extend and join walls\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETraced over a scan in the drafting app? Open the scan here too and check the walls against it: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-from-a-scan\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-from-a-scan\"\u003EModel from a scan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Use a plan you (or someone else) drew in the drafting app as the start of the model: its wall lines become walls, its door and window blocks become doors and windows. Steps Turn on Draw and choose the level the drawing shows in the plan's level list. Choose Build › From a drawing…, then Choose a drawing… and pick the DWG or DXF. In the dialog: Drawing units come from the drawing; choose others if they are wrong (a plan drawn in feet read as inches is twelve times too small). Layers with the walls: tick the layers the wall lines are on. Layers whose names say \"wall\" are ticked for you. The lines are: both faces of each wall (the usual way plans are drawn: each pair of parallel lines up to 600 mm apart becomes one wall as thick as the gap), the walls' center lines, or one face with the wall to its left or right. With center lines or one face, the wall type sets the thickness. Wall type and Wall height, as for the Wall tool. Blocks: choose a door or window type for each block that is a door or a window, or leave it out. Blocks whose names say \"door\" or \"window\" are chosen for you. Each one is put in the wall it sits on, as wide as the block along the wall; a door opens to the side its swing is drawn on. Where it goes: at the drawing's own coordinates, or with its lower-left corner on the plan's origin. Keep the drawing under the plan of this level: leave it ticked to see the drawing as thin lines under what you make. The line under the choices says how many walls, doors and windows it will make. Click Make them. What you should see The walls, doors and windows on the level, joined where they meet, with the rooms they close; all of it is one step, so Ctrl+Z takes it back. The drawing as thin lines under the plan. Build › From a drawing… lists the drawings kept on the level, each with Remove; removing one leaves the walls. In the IFC file the kept drawing is an annotation on the level (its lines), so other BIM programs show it too. Arcs are left out (BimCraft draws straight walls), and so are lines inside blocks. Lines with no partner in \"both faces\" are counted in the dialog and left out: draw those walls with the Wall tool, or tidy them with Modify › Trim / extend (see Trim, extend and join walls). Traced over a scan in the drafting app? Open the scan here too and check the walls against it: see Model from a scan."},{"slug":"quantity-totals","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Add up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces","summary":"Count elements and add up their lengths, areas, volumes or any number property, grouped by any property; list one row per element; or schedule the spaces with their areas and volumes. Each downloads as a CSV.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["quantity","quantities","takeoff","totals","sum","schedule","bill of quantities","boq","qto","csv","group by","count","length","area","volume","spaces","rooms","room schedule","area schedule","element list"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"one-row-per-element-or-the-spaces","text":"One row per element, or the spaces","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGet totals out of a model: wall lengths per storey, door counts per type, slab volume per material, the cost of every zone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery element\u003C/span\u003E or only the picked ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup by\u003C/span\u003E, choose a property, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a group\u003C/span\u003E to split further, up to three, for example by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd up\u003C/span\u003E, tick the numbers to total. The model&#039;s quantity sets are ticked to start with.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E to open the table in a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA row per group with how many elements it has and each total, and a total row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements without a value are counted but add nothing. When that happens, the CSV adds a &quot;left out&quot; column next to the total so you can see how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers are in the file&#039;s own units, as the file stores them, often millimetres for lengths.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-row-per-element-or-the-spaces\"\u003EOne row per element, or the spaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne row per element\u003C/strong\u003E: each element on its own row, with the properties under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumns\u003C/span\u003E and the numbers under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd up\u003C/span\u003E. The CSV starts with each element&#039;s GlobalId. Click the first value in a row to pick that element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpaces\u003C/strong\u003E: each room with its number, name, storey, area and volume, and their totals. Areas and volumes come from the space&#039;s quantities as the file states them. When the file has none, they are measured from the space&#039;s shape in m² and m³ and shown in italics; the CSV says which is which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Get totals out of a model: wall lengths per storey, door counts per type, slab volume per material, the cost of every zone. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Quantity totals in the left panel. In Count, choose Every element or only the picked ones. Under Group by, choose a property, such as Class. Click Add a group to split further, up to three, for example by Storey. Under Add up, tick the numbers to total. The model's quantity sets are ticked to start with. Click Download CSV to open the table in a spreadsheet. What you should see A row per group with how many elements it has and each total, and a total row. Elements without a value are counted but add nothing. When that happens, the CSV adds a \"left out\" column next to the total so you can see how many. Numbers are in the file's own units, as the file stores them, often millimetres for lengths. One row per element, or the spaces Change Show: One row per element: each element on its own row, with the properties under Columns and the numbers under Add up. The CSV starts with each element's GlobalId. Click the first value in a row to pick that element. Spaces: each room with its number, name, storey, area and volume, and their totals. Areas and volumes come from the space's quantities as the file states them. When the file has none, they are measured from the space's shape in m² and m³ and shown in italics; the CSV says which is which. Next Find elements by class, name or property Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"paint-materials","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Paint materials onto walls, floors and roofs","summary":"Give elements a material such as brick, siding, shingles, wood or tile, on the whole element or on one side, saved in the IFC and shown in rendered pictures.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["paint","material","materials","finish","finishes","texture","brick","siding","shingles","roofing","wood","stone","tile","concrete","stucco","grass","gravel","glass","metal","wall side","inside","outside","floor finish","room","surface style"],"headings":[{"id":"paint-elements","text":"Paint elements","level":2},{"id":"paint-one-side","text":"Paint one side","level":2},{"id":"take-a-material-off","text":"Take a material off","level":2},{"id":"in-the-file","text":"In the file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar opens the material library over the 3D view: brick and block, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, ground, glass and metal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint-elements\"\u003EPaint elements\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a material.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick elements in the 3D view. Each click paints the element you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo paint many at once, select them first (click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click, or a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003Esearch\u003C/a\u003E), then click the material: every selected element takes it in one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe element shows the material&#039;s color in the 3D view at once. Its texture shows in a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/render\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/render\"\u003Erendered picture\u003C/a\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E ends painting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint-one-side\"\u003EPaint one side\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the side clicked\u003C/span\u003E, then click the side you want: the outside of a wall in siding and its inside in another finish, a floor&#039;s top in wood with its underside left as it is. The rest of the element keeps its material.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA side is found from the element&#039;s own directions, so it stays the same side when you move or turn the element.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo paint floors room by room, give each room its own floor (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloor\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EThis room\u003C/strong\u003E, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\"\u003ERooms, floors, ceilings and roofs\u003C/a\u003E), then paint each floor&#039;s top.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-a-material-off\"\u003ETake a material off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the striped swatch (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENo material\u003C/span\u003E), then click the element. Every material on it, sides included, goes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-file\"\u003EIn the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPainting changes the IFC (one step each, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it) and stays plain IFC:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eeach painted element gets the property set \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Render\u003C/code\u003E naming its material, and one property per painted side;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eits body gets a surface style with the material&#039;s color, so other BIM programs show the color. In IFC4 the style also names the texture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen BimCraft reshapes a wall you painted (you move its end, say), the wall keeps its material and its color, in BimCraft, in pictures and in other programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe textures are drawn by BimCraft itself: they work offline and are free to use for anything (CC0). Your own image textures are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Paint on the toolbar opens the material library over the 3D view: brick and block, siding, roofing, wood, stone, tile, concrete, stucco, ground, glass and metal. Paint elements Click Paint. Click a material. Click elements in the 3D view. Each click paints the element you click. To paint many at once, select them first (click, Ctrl+click, or a search), then click the material: every selected element takes it in one step. The element shows the material's color in the 3D view at once. Its texture shows in a rendered picture. Esc or Close ends painting. Paint one side Choose Only the side clicked, then click the side you want: the outside of a wall in siding and its inside in another finish, a floor's top in wood with its underside left as it is. The rest of the element keeps its material. A side is found from the element's own directions, so it stays the same side when you move or turn the element. To paint floors room by room, give each room its own floor (Draw › Floor › This room, see Rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs), then paint each floor's top. Take a material off Click the striped swatch (No material), then click the element. Every material on it, sides included, goes. In the file Painting changes the IFC (one step each, Ctrl+Z undoes it) and stays plain IFC: each painted element gets the property set CadCraft_Render naming its material, and one property per painted side; its body gets a surface style with the material's color, so other BIM programs show the color. In IFC4 the style also names the texture. When BimCraft reshapes a wall you painted (you move its end, say), the wall keeps its material and its color, in BimCraft, in pictures and in other programs. The textures are drawn by BimCraft itself: they work offline and are free to use for anything (CC0). Your own image textures are not built yet."},{"slug":"vr-review","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Review the model in a VR headset","summary":"Walk the IFC model at full size in a VR headset: jump around, turn, measure, cut it open, place BCF issues with a picture, see it as a table-top model, and meet the others of a live session.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["vr","virtual reality","headset","webxr","xr","immersive","quest","oculus","vive","index","pico","walk through","walkthrough","teleport","design review","coordination","review","measure","section","cut","issue","issues","bcf","table top","scale model","presence","avatars","live","together"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"start","text":"Start","level":2},{"id":"move-around","text":"Move around","level":2},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure","level":2},{"id":"cut-it-open","text":"Cut it open","level":2},{"id":"place-an-issue","text":"Place an issue","level":2},{"id":"see-it-as-a-table-top-model","text":"See it as a table-top model","level":2},{"id":"with-others-live","text":"With others, live","level":2},{"id":"leave","text":"Leave","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVR\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar puts you inside the model with a VR headset, at full size. You stand where the 3D view&#039;s camera is, on the floor below it, facing the same way. The headset shows what the 3D view shows: hidden storeys, elements and models stay hidden, and painted materials come along. Nothing is uploaded: the browser draws it on this computer (or in the headset&#039;s own browser).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA headset the browser can drive (WebXR): a standalone headset&#039;s own browser, or Chrome or Edge on a PC with a headset connected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn IFC model open, on a secure page (https), as it is on this site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen there is no headset, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVR\u003C/span\u003E is greyed out and says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start\"\u003EStart\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the 3D view where you want to start: in a room, or outside looking at the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVR\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPut on the headset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe menu is on the back of your left hand. Point at it with the right controller and pull the trigger to pick a button. (With one hand only, the menu floats in front of where you started.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-around\"\u003EMove around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJump:\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, point at a floor and pull the trigger; you land there. Or push the stick forward to aim, and let go to jump. You can only land on floors, slabs and ground (surfaces facing up), never on a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETurn:\u003C/strong\u003E push the stick left or right: you turn 30° each time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalk:\u003C/strong\u003E in your play area, simply walk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E, then pull the trigger on two points. The distance shows between them, in metres. A point near a corner snaps to the corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-it-open\"\u003ECut it open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E and pull the trigger on a surface:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a wall (or anything not a floor): everything between you and that surface is cut away, so you see in. This works best from outside, or across a room;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a floor, from above: a plan cut 1.2 m over that floor, so the walls show their lower part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E takes the cut and the measurements away. If a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003Esection plane\u003C/a\u003E cuts the model when you start, the headset starts with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-an-issue\"\u003EPlace an issue\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssue\u003C/span\u003E and pull the trigger where the problem is. A numbered pin marks it, and the issue goes into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab as a BCF topic, with a picture of what you saw and the camera where you stood, in the model&#039;s own coordinates. Save them with the rest of your \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003Eissues\u003C/a\u003E as a BCF file; other coordination tools open them at the same view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd a comment to an issue after you leave VR, in the Issues tab: typing in the headset is not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-it-as-a-table-top-model\"\u003ESee it as a table-top model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETable top\u003C/span\u003E shrinks the model to fit a table in front of you; walk round it, or turn it with the stick. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull size\u003C/span\u003E puts you back where you were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-others-live\"\u003EWith others, live\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/work-live\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/work-live\"\u003Elive session\u003C/a\u003E, everyone in VR sees the others as a head and two hands in their own color, with their name, and a dot where each one points. Edits anyone makes reach your headset within a moment. People at the desk are not shown in the headset yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"leave\"\u003ELeave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave VR\u003C/span\u003E (or take the headset off and end it there). The 3D view on the screen moves to where your head was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETested with a headset emulator, not yet on every headset. A large model can make the headset stutter: hide storeys or models you don&#039;t need first (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003Ehide and isolate\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"VR on the toolbar puts you inside the model with a VR headset, at full size. You stand where the 3D view's camera is, on the floor below it, facing the same way. The headset shows what the 3D view shows: hidden storeys, elements and models stay hidden, and painted materials come along. Nothing is uploaded: the browser draws it on this computer (or in the headset's own browser). What you need A headset the browser can drive (WebXR): a standalone headset's own browser, or Chrome or Edge on a PC with a headset connected. An IFC model open, on a secure page (https), as it is on this site. When there is no headset, VR is greyed out and says why. Start Set up the 3D view where you want to start: in a room, or outside looking at the building. Click VR on the toolbar. Put on the headset. The menu is on the back of your left hand. Point at it with the right controller and pull the trigger to pick a button. (With one hand only, the menu floats in front of where you started.) Move around Jump: in Move, point at a floor and pull the trigger; you land there. Or push the stick forward to aim, and let go to jump. You can only land on floors, slabs and ground (surfaces facing up), never on a wall. Turn: push the stick left or right: you turn 30° each time. Walk: in your play area, simply walk. Measure Pick Measure, then pull the trigger on two points. The distance shows between them, in metres. A point near a corner snaps to the corner. Cut it open Pick Section and pull the trigger on a surface: on a wall (or anything not a floor): everything between you and that surface is cut away, so you see in. This works best from outside, or across a room; on a floor, from above: a plan cut 1.2 m over that floor, so the walls show their lower part. Clear takes the cut and the measurements away. If a section plane cuts the model when you start, the headset starts with it. Place an issue Pick Issue and pull the trigger where the problem is. A numbered pin marks it, and the issue goes into the Issues tab as a BCF topic, with a picture of what you saw and the camera where you stood, in the model's own coordinates. Save them with the rest of your issues as a BCF file; other coordination tools open them at the same view. Add a comment to an issue after you leave VR, in the Issues tab: typing in the headset is not built yet. See it as a table-top model Table top shrinks the model to fit a table in front of you; walk round it, or turn it with the stick. Full size puts you back where you were. With others, live In a live session, everyone in VR sees the others as a head and two hands in their own color, with their name, and a dot where each one points. Edits anyone makes reach your headset within a moment. People at the desk are not shown in the headset yet. Leave Pick Leave VR (or take the headset off and end it there). The 3D view on the screen moves to where your head was. Tested with a headset emulator, not yet on every headset. A large model can make the headset stutter: hide storeys or models you don't need first (hide and isolate)."},{"slug":"trim-extend-walls","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Trim, extend and join walls","summary":"Trim or extend walls to another wall, and join two walls at the point where they meet, each as one step you can undo.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["trim","extend","corner","join","meet","clean up","wall end","wall corner","cut back","lengthen","shorten","modify","TR"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETidy the walls you drew: cut back a wall that runs past another, lengthen one that stops short, or close an open corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E and choose the level in the plan&#039;s level list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrim or extend to a wall:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim / extend\u003C/span\u003E. Click the wall to trim or extend to (the edge), then click each wall you want to change. A wall that runs past the edge is cut back to it, and the part you click is the part that stays; a wall that stops short is lengthened until it meets the edge. The edge stays chosen, so you can click several walls one after the other. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to choose another edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJoin two walls at a corner:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner\u003C/span\u003E, click the first wall, then the second. Both run to the point where they meet, and BimCraft cleans the corner. When a wall crosses the other one, the part you clicked stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start again, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you point at a wall, the plan shows where it would go, already joined to the walls it meets, with its new length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERooms, floors, ceilings, roofs and what follows the walls are made again, as after any change to the walls. A room closed by a corner appears with its number and area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA door or window on a part that is cut off is deleted with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls are drawn on their center line, so they are trimmed and extended along it: a wall meets the other wall&#039;s center line, and the joint is cleaned into a T or an L.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBimCraft says why when it cannot do it: the two walls are parallel, the wall&#039;s line passes beside the other wall, a door or window is in the way (move or delete it first), or what is left would be shorter than 50 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly walls drawn in BimCraft can be trimmed or extended.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/change-a-wall-or-door\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/change-a-wall-or-door\"\u003EChange a wall, door or window\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Tidy the walls you drew: cut back a wall that runs past another, lengthen one that stops short, or close an open corner. Steps Turn on Draw and choose the level in the plan's level list. Trim or extend to a wall: choose Modify › Trim / extend. Click the wall to trim or extend to (the edge), then click each wall you want to change. A wall that runs past the edge is cut back to it, and the part you click is the part that stays; a wall that stops short is lengthened until it meets the edge. The edge stays chosen, so you can click several walls one after the other. Press Esc to choose another edge. Join two walls at a corner: choose Modify › Corner, click the first wall, then the second. Both run to the point where they meet, and BimCraft cleans the corner. When a wall crosses the other one, the part you clicked stays. Press Esc to start again, or V to go back to Select. What you should see While you point at a wall, the plan shows where it would go, already joined to the walls it meets, with its new length. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. Rooms, floors, ceilings, roofs and what follows the walls are made again, as after any change to the walls. A room closed by a corner appears with its number and area. A door or window on a part that is cut off is deleted with it. Good to know Walls are drawn on their center line, so they are trimmed and extended along it: a wall meets the other wall's center line, and the joint is cleaned into a T or an L. BimCraft says why when it cannot do it: the two walls are parallel, the wall's line passes beside the other wall, a door or window is in the way (move or delete it first), or what is left would be shorter than 50 mm. Only walls drawn in BimCraft can be trimmed or extended. See also Draw walls and Change a wall, door or window."},{"slug":"compare-versions","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Compare two versions of a model","summary":"Open two versions of the same IFC model and see which elements were added, removed and changed, matched by their GlobalIds, with a CSV of every change.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["compare","diff","difference","versions","revision","changes","changed","added","removed","moved","globalid","guid","what changed"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee what changed between the model you had and the one you were sent: which elements are new, which are gone, which moved or got new property values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the newer version, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the older one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare versions\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOlder version\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENewer version\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element in a list to pick it and see its properties. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E for every change, property by property.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the normal view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdded\u003C/strong\u003E elements in blue, from the newer version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemoved\u003C/strong\u003E elements in red, from the older version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanged\u003C/strong\u003E elements in ochre, from the newer version, each saying what changed: properties, a move with how far, or a new shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything that is the same, see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements are matched by their GlobalId. A program that gives elements new GlobalIds on every export makes every element look removed and added again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPositions are compared where the models sit in the scene. If one version was moved on purpose, line them up first with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA GlobalId used twice in one model is left out of the comparison. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-checks\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-checks\"\u003ECheck a model for common problems\u003C/a\u003E finds those.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/quantity-totals\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/quantity-totals\"\u003EAdd up quantities by property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See what changed between the model you had and the one you were sent: which elements are new, which are gone, which moved or got new property values. Steps Open the newer version, then click Add a model… and choose the older one. See See several IFC models together. In Models, open Compare versions. Choose the Older version and the Newer version. Click Compare. Click an element in a list to pick it and see its properties. Click Download CSV for every change, property by property. Click Clear to go back to the normal view. What you should see Added elements in blue, from the newer version. Removed elements in red, from the older version. Changed elements in ochre, from the newer version, each saying what changed: properties, a move with how far, or a new shape. Everything that is the same, see-through. Good to know Elements are matched by their GlobalId. A program that gives elements new GlobalIds on every export makes every element look removed and added again. Positions are compared where the models sit in the scene. If one version was moved on purpose, line them up first with Move in Models. A GlobalId used twice in one model is left out of the comparison. Check a model for common problems finds those. Next Issues and BCF files Add up quantities by property"},{"slug":"linked-models","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Link other models for coordination","summary":"Show another IFC model beside yours by reference (never merged), place it by shared coordinates, map coordinates or two points, snap to it while drawing, and reload it when its file changes.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["link","linked model","reference","xref","coordination","federation","structure","MEP","consultant","shared coordinates","survey","map coordinates","reload","unload","document reference","IfcDocumentReference"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"link-a-model","text":"Link a model","level":2},{"id":"work-with-it","text":"Work with it","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw against a consultant&#039;s model (the structure, the services, the existing building) without copying anything into your own file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elinked model\u003C/strong\u003E is shown in the 3D view and cut on your plans, but it is never changed and never saved into your model. Your model keeps only the link: the file&#039;s name and where it goes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-a-model\"\u003ELink a model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the left panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinked models\u003C/span\u003E section, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink a model…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the IFC file. It shows at once, and its row says \u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose where it goes in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlacement\u003C/span\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShared coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E (to start): its coordinates are yours, as when both models were set out from the same origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMap coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E: both files&#039; map coordinates line them up (both need a map conversion; BimCraft says so when one has none).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy two points…\u003C/strong\u003E: click a point on the linked model, then where it goes in yours; then a second pair. The link is moved and turned (about the vertical) to fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach link and placement change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-with-it\"\u003EWork with it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plans show the linked model cut at the level&#039;s cut height as thin lines, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E tool and the other drawing tools snap to its corners and edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick its elements in the 3D view to read their properties; a linked model is never the one you edit, so its radio button in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E is off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file changed?\u003C/strong\u003E When the browser can watch the file (a file opened through its file picker, or in Studio), BimCraft reloads the link by itself a few seconds after the file is saved again, and says so. Otherwise click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReload\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnload\u003C/span\u003E hides it and frees the memory; the link stays in your model. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad…\u003C/span\u003E shows it again (you choose the file: a browser cannot open a file by its name on its own).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe × removes the link from your model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your IFC file: an IfcDocumentReference for each link (the file&#039;s name), related to the project, and where it goes in the project&#039;s property set CadCraft_Links. Opening your model again lists its links as \u003Cstrong\u003ENot loaded\u003C/strong\u003E until you load them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClash checks, Compare and Find can use a loaded link like any other open model: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Draw against a consultant's model (the structure, the services, the existing building) without copying anything into your own file. A linked model is shown in the 3D view and cut on your plans, but it is never changed and never saved into your model. Your model keeps only the link: the file's name and where it goes. Link a model In the left panel's Linked models section, click Link a model… and choose the IFC file. It shows at once, and its row says Shown. Choose where it goes in Placement: Shared coordinates (to start): its coordinates are yours, as when both models were set out from the same origin. Map coordinates: both files' map coordinates line them up (both need a map conversion; BimCraft says so when one has none). By two points…: click a point on the linked model, then where it goes in yours; then a second pair. The link is moved and turned (about the vertical) to fit. Each link and placement change is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. Work with it The plans show the linked model cut at the level's cut height as thin lines, and the Wall tool and the other drawing tools snap to its corners and edges. Pick its elements in the 3D view to read their properties; a linked model is never the one you edit, so its radio button in Models is off. The file changed? When the browser can watch the file (a file opened through its file picker, or in Studio), BimCraft reloads the link by itself a few seconds after the file is saved again, and says so. Otherwise click Reload. Unload hides it and frees the memory; the link stays in your model. Load… shows it again (you choose the file: a browser cannot open a file by its name on its own). The × removes the link from your model. What you should see In your IFC file: an IfcDocumentReference for each link (the file's name), related to the project, and where it goes in the project's property set CadCraft_Links. Opening your model again lists its links as Not loaded until you load them. Clash checks, Compare and Find can use a loaded link like any other open model: see See several IFC models together."},{"slug":"views-and-walkthroughs","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Save views and walkthroughs","summary":"Keep camera views of the model by name, go back to them, play them one after another, and record a walk through the building to play back.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["views","saved views","camera","cameras","viewpoint","scene","scenes","tour","presentation","walkthrough","walk through","fly through","record","play","path","animation"],"headings":[{"id":"save-a-view","text":"Save a view","level":2},{"id":"play-the-views","text":"Play the views","level":2},{"id":"record-a-walkthrough","text":"Record a walkthrough","level":2},{"id":"in-the-file","text":"In the file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar keeps cameras of the 3D view in the model&#039;s file, so you and anyone you send it to can go straight back to them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-view\"\u003ESave a view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the 3D view: orbit, zoom, hide what you don&#039;t want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, type a name (or keep the one offered) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave view\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving with a name that is already in the list updates that view. Click a view&#039;s name to go back to it; the camera moves there smoothly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe arrows change the order, the pencil renames, the bin deletes. Each change is one step, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"play-the-views\"\u003EPlay the views\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay the views\u003C/span\u003E moves from one view to the next in the list&#039;s order, stopping a moment at each. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"record-a-walkthrough\"\u003ERecord a walkthrough\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walk\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walk\"\u003EWalk\u003C/a\u003E and start walking inside the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecord a walk\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalk with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and look round with the mouse.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop recording\u003C/span\u003E. The walk is saved as &quot;Walk 1&quot;, with its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a walk to play it back as you walked it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-file\"\u003EIn the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EViews and walks are kept in the IFC as the property set \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Views\u003C/code\u003E on the project: plain IFC that other programs ignore. They are in the first model&#039;s coordinates, so they still fit after the model is reloaded or another model is added.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo render a picture from a saved view, go to the view, then click \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/render\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/render\"\u003ERender\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Views on the toolbar keeps cameras of the 3D view in the model's file, so you and anyone you send it to can go straight back to them. Save a view Set up the 3D view: orbit, zoom, hide what you don't want. Click Views, type a name (or keep the one offered) and click Save view or press Enter. Saving with a name that is already in the list updates that view. Click a view's name to go back to it; the camera moves there smoothly. The arrows change the order, the pencil renames, the bin deletes. Each change is one step, so Ctrl+Z undoes it. Play the views Play the views moves from one view to the next in the list's order, stopping a moment at each. Esc or Stop ends it. Record a walkthrough Turn on Walk and start walking inside the building. In Views, click Record a walk. Walk with W A S D and look round with the mouse. Click Stop recording. The walk is saved as \"Walk 1\", with its length. Click a walk to play it back as you walked it. Esc stops it. In the file Views and walks are kept in the IFC as the property set CadCraft_Views on the project: plain IFC that other programs ignore. They are in the first model's coordinates, so they still fit after the model is reloaded or another model is added. To render a picture from a saved view, go to the view, then click Render."},{"slug":"model-for-rendering","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Send the model to a rendering program","summary":"Save what the 3D view shows as a glTF model (.glb) with its materials, textures and lamps, for rendering and visualization programs that read glTF.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["glb","gltf","gl transmission format","export","rendering","render","visualization","real time","archviz","materials","textures","lamps","lights","asset","custom asset"],"headings":[{"id":"save-it","text":"Save it","level":2},{"id":"what-the-file-carries","text":"What the file carries","level":2},{"id":"what-it-leaves-out","text":"What it leaves out","level":2},{"id":"enscape-and-other-plug-in-renderers","text":"Enscape and other plug-in renderers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor rendering\u003C/span\u003E on the toolbar saves what the 3D view shows as one \u003Cstrong\u003E.glb\u003C/strong\u003E file (glTF 2.0, the open format from the Khronos Group). Rendering programs that read glTF open it with the model&#039;s materials and lamps; Twinmotion, for example, opens .glb files and keeps their lights. Nothing is uploaded: the file is made on this computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-it\"\u003ESave it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the IFC model and set up the 3D view: hide the storeys, elements or linked models you don&#039;t want, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/paint-materials\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/paint-materials\"\u003Epaint the materials\u003C/a\u003E you want to see.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFor rendering\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode\u003EGLB\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file downloads as &quot;&lt;model name&gt;.glb&quot;. The status bar says how many triangles, materials and lamps it holds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-file-carries\"\u003EWhat the file carries\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGeometry\u003C/strong\u003E of every element the 3D view shows, in meters, with Y up (glTF&#039;s own axes). One object per storey and material, named &quot;Level 1 — Brick, red&quot;, so you can pick a material in the rendering program and swap it for its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMaterials:\u003C/strong\u003E each color with the look you chose for it in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/render\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/render\"\u003ERender\u003C/a\u003E (matte, satin, glossy, metal, glass…), and each painted library material with its color, roughness and \u003Cstrong\u003Etexture\u003C/strong\u003E (its picture is in the file, scaled to its real size: a brick is a brick&#039;s size). Glass is see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELamps:\u003C/strong\u003E every light fixture that is switched on in Render › Lamps, as a point or spot light with its brightness (lumens turned into candela), color and beam angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere the site is:\u003C/strong\u003E the site&#039;s latitude and longitude are written in the file for reference. Rendering programs have their own sun: type the place in there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-leaves-out\"\u003EWhat it leaves out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe section cut (whole elements go in) and elements shown see-through (they are left out).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElement by element objects (one object per storey and material instead), IFC properties, bump maps, saved views and recorded walks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdges and the background.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"enscape-and-other-plug-in-renderers\"\u003EEnscape and other plug-in renderers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEnscape runs inside another design program and has no file to open a whole project. Two ways:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the model&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E (File › Save) in the program Enscape runs in; most of them read IFC. Colors come along; the library materials come as their colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBring a \u003Cstrong\u003Esmall part\u003C/strong\u003E (furniture, a fixture) in as a custom asset from a .glb: isolate it first, then save. Enscape advises small assets (a few tens of thousands of triangles).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug-ins and a live link to these programs need their makers&#039; own kits and are not built. The FBX and Datasmith formats have no published specification, so BimCraft does not write them; glTF is the open route.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"For rendering on the toolbar saves what the 3D view shows as one .glb file (glTF 2.0, the open format from the Khronos Group). Rendering programs that read glTF open it with the model's materials and lamps; Twinmotion, for example, opens .glb files and keeps their lights. Nothing is uploaded: the file is made on this computer. Save it Open the IFC model and set up the 3D view: hide the storeys, elements or linked models you don't want, and paint the materials you want to see. Click For rendering (or type GLB). The file downloads as \"\u003Cmodel name\u003E.glb\". The status bar says how many triangles, materials and lamps it holds. What the file carries Geometry of every element the 3D view shows, in meters, with Y up (glTF's own axes). One object per storey and material, named \"Level 1 — Brick, red\", so you can pick a material in the rendering program and swap it for its own. Materials: each color with the look you chose for it in Render (matte, satin, glossy, metal, glass…), and each painted library material with its color, roughness and texture (its picture is in the file, scaled to its real size: a brick is a brick's size). Glass is see-through. Lamps: every light fixture that is switched on in Render › Lamps, as a point or spot light with its brightness (lumens turned into candela), color and beam angle. Where the site is: the site's latitude and longitude are written in the file for reference. Rendering programs have their own sun: type the place in there. What it leaves out The section cut (whole elements go in) and elements shown see-through (they are left out). Element by element objects (one object per storey and material instead), IFC properties, bump maps, saved views and recorded walks. Edges and the background. Enscape and other plug-in renderers Enscape runs inside another design program and has no file to open a whole project. Two ways: Open the model's IFC (File › Save) in the program Enscape runs in; most of them read IFC. Colors come along; the library materials come as their colors. Bring a small part (furniture, a fixture) in as a custom asset from a .glb: isolate it first, then save. Enscape advises small assets (a few tens of thousands of triangles). Plug-ins and a live link to these programs need their makers' own kits and are not built. The FBX and Datasmith formats have no published specification, so BimCraft does not write them; glTF is the open route."},{"slug":"views-and-sheets","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Views, view ranges and your own sheets","summary":"Find every plan, elevation, section, 3D view, schedule and sheet in the project browser, set how each plan is cut and what it shows, make callouts, draw lines and hatches in one view, keep settings in view templates, and arrange sheets of your own.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["callout","callout view","detail view","enlarged plan","drafting","detail lines","drafting lines","hatch","hatch region","filled region","fill pattern","reflected ceiling plan","rcp","ceiling plan","ceiling grid","light fixtures","project browser","views","view range","crop","crop region","crop view","view scale","underlay","show another level","halftone","cut plane","cut height","top","bottom","view depth","visibility","categories","hide in view","view template","templates","sheet","sheets","title block","viewport","scale","arrange sheets","layout","pdf","plan view settings","browser"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"the-project-browser","text":"The project browser","level":2},{"id":"set-a-plan-s-view-range","text":"Set a plan's view range","level":2},{"id":"crop-a-plan-give-it-a-scale-show-another-level-under-it","text":"Crop a plan, give it a scale, show another level under it","level":2},{"id":"a-level-s-ceiling-plan","text":"A level's ceiling plan","level":2},{"id":"make-a-callout","text":"Make a callout","level":2},{"id":"draw-lines-and-hatches-in-one-view","text":"Draw lines and hatches in one view","level":2},{"id":"keep-the-settings-in-a-view-template","text":"Keep the settings in a view template","level":2},{"id":"arrange-a-sheet-of-your-own","text":"Arrange a sheet of your own","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWork the way BIM projects are organized: every view in one list, each plan cut where you want it, the same settings for several plans through a template, and sheets you lay out yourself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-project-browser\"\u003EThe project browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E. The left panel has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProject browser\u003C/span\u003E under the models.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt lists:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFloor plans\u003C/strong\u003E: each level. Click one to see its plan. The slider button beside it sets its view range (below). A plan that follows a template shows the template&#039;s name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECeiling plans\u003C/strong\u003E: each level&#039;s reflected ceiling plan (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECallouts\u003C/strong\u003E: each callout with the view it is drawn on, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew callout\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EMissing\u003C/strong\u003E lists callouts whose view is gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevations\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESections\u003C/strong\u003E: the four sides and every section line. Click one to open the drawing set they are drawn in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D views\u003C/strong\u003E: the views you saved with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchedules\u003C/strong\u003E: click one to show it under the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheets\u003C/strong\u003E: the sheets you made, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing set…\u003C/span\u003E for the whole set made from the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView templates\u003C/strong\u003E, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew template\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cstrong\u003ELinks\u003C/strong\u003E (the linked models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-a-plan-s-view-range\"\u003ESet a plan&#039;s view range\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the slider button beside a level in \u003Cstrong\u003EFloor plans\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the heights, above that level:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut at\u003C/strong\u003E: where the plan cuts through walls, doors and windows (1200 mm, about 4 ft, unless you change it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E: things above the cut and under the top are drawn dashed (a high cabinet, a beam). Leave it empty for the level&#039;s height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBottom\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDepth below the bottom\u003C/strong\u003E: things lower than the cut are drawn in thin lines down to the bottom, and that far under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EShown in this plan\u003C/strong\u003E, clear what this plan leaves out: furniture, rooms, columns and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo leave out single elements: pick them in the plan or the 3D view, open the dialog and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide the selected here\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow them all\u003C/span\u003E brings them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse these settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plan is cut again at once, and the drawing set and your sheets follow it. A point cloud&#039;s slice follows the cut height too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"crop-a-plan-give-it-a-scale-show-another-level-under-it\"\u003ECrop a plan, give it a scale, show another level under it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three are in the same dialog (the slider button beside a level):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E: the scale the plan prints at (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint\u003C/span\u003E on the plan&#039;s bar) and comes onto a sheet at. \u003Cstrong\u003EFit\u003C/strong\u003E picks the largest standard scale that fits.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECrop region\u003C/strong\u003E: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw on the plan…\u003C/span\u003E, then click one corner and the opposite corner on the plan (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops). The plan outside the region is veiled on screen, and you can still work there; printed, on sheets and in the drawing set, the plan shows only what is inside. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E takes the crop away. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E fits the crop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnderlay\u003C/strong\u003E: choose another level. Its walls, cut elements, doors and windows are drawn light under this plan, to line things up with the level below or above. You cannot pick them here, and they print light grey.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA view template carries the scale with the heights and categories. The crop region and the underlay always belong to the one plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-level-s-ceiling-plan\"\u003EA level&#039;s ceiling plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery level has a reflected ceiling plan beside its floor plan: the plan seen from below, for laying out ceilings and what hangs from them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the level under \u003Cstrong\u003ECeiling plans\u003C/strong\u003E in the browser, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECeiling plan\u003C/span\u003E beside the level on the plan&#039;s bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe walls are cut at 2300 mm (about 7&#039;-6&quot;). What lies above that cut and under the level&#039;s height is drawn: ceilings with their grid and a height tag (\u003Ccode\u003ECLG 2438\u003C/code\u003E), lights, beams. Doors, windows and what lies below the cut are left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo make a ceiling, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECeiling\u003C/span\u003E as on the floor plan, then pick it and choose its \u003Cstrong\u003EPattern\u003C/strong\u003E in Properties. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\"\u003ERooms, floors, ceilings and roofs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo hang a light from it, place the item from the library while the ceiling plan is shown: it goes in \u003Cstrong\u003EAt the ceiling\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe slider button beside a ceiling plan sets its cut and top, scale, categories, crop region and underlay, as for a floor plan. Ceiling plans take no view template.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is drawn the same way round as the floor plan (a reflected plan, not a mirror image). A ceiling lower than the cut is not drawn: lower the cut for that level&#039;s ceiling plan. Ceiling plans go on your own sheets like plans; the drawing set does not add them by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-callout\"\u003EMake a callout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA callout shows part of a plan larger, at its own scale, as a view of its own: a kitchen, a stair, a wall corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the plan, ceiling plan or callout it belongs to.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E on the plan&#039;s bar (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew callout\u003C/span\u003E in the browser), then click one corner of the area and the opposite corner. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe callout is made at 1:20 (1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; in feet and inches). Its rectangle and a round tag are drawn on the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the tag with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, or the callout in the browser, to open it: the plan shows only that area, at the callout&#039;s scale. The bar says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout:\u003C/span\u003E and its name; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp\u003C/span\u003E goes back to the view it is drawn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe slider button beside it (in the browser or on the bar) renames it, changes its scale, draws its rectangle again or deletes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA callout shows what its view shows: the same cut, the same categories, a ceiling plan&#039;s ceilings. A callout of a callout works the same way. Put a callout on a sheet like a plan; its tag then says where it is (\u003Ccode\u003E1 / A-501\u003C/code\u003E: the first view on sheet A-501). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint\u003C/span\u003E in a callout prints just that area at its scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeleting a callout also deletes the callouts drawn inside it, and the lines and hatches drawn in them (you are asked first). If the level a callout is drawn on is deleted, the callout stays under \u003Cstrong\u003EMissing\u003C/strong\u003E until you delete it, and the sheets it is on report it as missing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-lines-and-hatches-in-one-view\"\u003EDraw lines and hatches in one view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrafting is 2D work that belongs to one view only: notes in lines, a hatched area of concrete or earth, a filled region. It is never in the 3D view and never in another plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the floor plan, ceiling plan or callout to draw in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E on the plan&#039;s bar:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E: click where it starts and where it ends. The next line starts where the last one ended; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts afresh.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E: click its points; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a double-click ends it, a click on its first point closes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E: two opposite corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch region\u003C/span\u003E: choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EPattern\u003C/strong\u003E (Solid, Diagonal, Cross-hatch, Concrete, Insulation, Earth) and its scale on the bar, then click the corners; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, a double-click or a click on the first corner closes it. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill a room\u003C/span\u003E ticked, one click in a room hatches its outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints snap as walls do; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps a line square, and you can type a length as for walls. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick a line or a hatch with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on the plan. Properties changes a hatch&#039;s pattern and scale and has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove…\u003C/span\u003E (click the point to move from, then where it goes). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes it. Each line, hatch and change is one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines and hatches print with the plan, go onto your sheets with it and are in the drawing set. Clear \u003Cstrong\u003EAnnotations\u003C/strong\u003E in a view range to leave them out of that view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-the-settings-in-a-view-template\"\u003EKeep the settings in a view template\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew template\u003C/span\u003E in the browser, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a template…\u003C/span\u003E in a plan&#039;s view range (that plan then uses it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name, set the heights and categories, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the template\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn any plan&#039;s view range, choose the template. The plan takes its heights and categories; change the template to change every plan that uses it. Elements you hid one by one stay hidden in their own plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a template in the browser to change, rename or delete it. Deleting one leaves its plans with its settings as their own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrange-a-sheet-of-your-own\"\u003EArrange a sheet of your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E. Type its number and title and choose the paper.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E beside a plan, ceiling plan, callout, elevation, section or schedule. It lands in the middle of the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag it where it goes. With it chosen, pick a \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E, or leave it on the plan&#039;s own scale (when the plan has one), else the scale that fits a third of the sheet. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake off the sheet\u003C/span\u003E removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the sheet\u003C/span\u003E. It is listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESheets\u003C/strong\u003E; click it to change it later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload as PDF\u003C/span\u003E saves that sheet as one page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlans cut at your heights, with what is above the cut dashed and what you cleared left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA cropped plan veiled outside its region on screen and cut to it on paper, at the scale you gave it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA ceiling plan with the ceilings&#039; grids and height tags, and the lights at the ceiling.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECallouts with their rectangle and tag on the plan, each opening at its own scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines and hatches only in the view you drew them in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour sheets drawn again from the model every time you open them, so they always match it. A view whose level or section line was deleted is reported, not drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery change here is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll of this is kept in the IFC file as property sets other programs leave alone: each level&#039;s view range and ceiling plan on the level, the templates, callouts and your sheets on the project. Lines and hatches are IFC annotations in their level, so other programs see them as 2D curves and hatched areas (their own drawing of each pattern), though not which view they belong to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther BIM programs call the cut a view range or cut plane, templates view templates or view settings, drafting lines detail lines, and hatch regions filled regions. The ideas are the same; the view settings do not travel to them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Work the way BIM projects are organized: every view in one list, each plan cut where you want it, the same settings for several plans through a template, and sheets you lay out yourself. The project browser Turn on Draw. The left panel has a Project browser under the models. It lists: Floor plans: each level. Click one to see its plan. The slider button beside it sets its view range (below). A plan that follows a template shows the template's name. Ceiling plans: each level's reflected ceiling plan (below). Callouts: each callout with the view it is drawn on, and New callout. Missing lists callouts whose view is gone. Elevations and Sections: the four sides and every section line. Click one to open the drawing set they are drawn in. 3D views: the views you saved with Views. Schedules: click one to show it under the plan. Sheets: the sheets you made, New sheet, and Drawing set… for the whole set made from the model. View templates, with New template, and Links (the linked models). Set a plan's view range Click the slider button beside a level in Floor plans. Set the heights, above that level: Cut at: where the plan cuts through walls, doors and windows (1200 mm, about 4 ft, unless you change it). Top: things above the cut and under the top are drawn dashed (a high cabinet, a beam). Leave it empty for the level's height. Bottom and Depth below the bottom: things lower than the cut are drawn in thin lines down to the bottom, and that far under it. Under Shown in this plan, clear what this plan leaves out: furniture, rooms, columns and so on. To leave out single elements: pick them in the plan or the 3D view, open the dialog and click Hide the selected here. Show them all brings them back. Click Use these settings. The plan is cut again at once, and the drawing set and your sheets follow it. A point cloud's slice follows the cut height too. Crop a plan, give it a scale, show another level under it All three are in the same dialog (the slider button beside a level): Scale: the scale the plan prints at (Print on the plan's bar) and comes onto a sheet at. Fit picks the largest standard scale that fits. Crop region: click Draw on the plan…, then click one corner and the opposite corner on the plan (Esc stops). The plan outside the region is veiled on screen, and you can still work there; printed, on sheets and in the drawing set, the plan shows only what is inside. Clear takes the crop away. Fit fits the crop. Underlay: choose another level. Its walls, cut elements, doors and windows are drawn light under this plan, to line things up with the level below or above. You cannot pick them here, and they print light grey. A view template carries the scale with the heights and categories. The crop region and the underlay always belong to the one plan. A level's ceiling plan Every level has a reflected ceiling plan beside its floor plan: the plan seen from below, for laying out ceilings and what hangs from them. Click the level under Ceiling plans in the browser, or choose Ceiling plan beside the level on the plan's bar. The walls are cut at 2300 mm (about 7'-6\"). What lies above that cut and under the level's height is drawn: ceilings with their grid and a height tag (CLG 2438), lights, beams. Doors, windows and what lies below the cut are left out. To make a ceiling, use Ceiling as on the floor plan, then pick it and choose its Pattern in Properties. See Rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs. To hang a light from it, place the item from the library while the ceiling plan is shown: it goes in At the ceiling. See Place equipment and furniture from the library. The slider button beside a ceiling plan sets its cut and top, scale, categories, crop region and underlay, as for a floor plan. Ceiling plans take no view template. It is drawn the same way round as the floor plan (a reflected plan, not a mirror image). A ceiling lower than the cut is not drawn: lower the cut for that level's ceiling plan. Ceiling plans go on your own sheets like plans; the drawing set does not add them by itself. Make a callout A callout shows part of a plan larger, at its own scale, as a view of its own: a kitchen, a stair, a wall corner. Show the plan, ceiling plan or callout it belongs to. Choose Annotate › Callout on the plan's bar (or New callout in the browser), then click one corner of the area and the opposite corner. Esc stops. The callout is made at 1:20 (1/2\" = 1'-0\" in feet and inches). Its rectangle and a round tag are drawn on the plan. Click the tag with Select, or the callout in the browser, to open it: the plan shows only that area, at the callout's scale. The bar says Callout: and its name; Up goes back to the view it is drawn on. The slider button beside it (in the browser or on the bar) renames it, changes its scale, draws its rectangle again or deletes it. A callout shows what its view shows: the same cut, the same categories, a ceiling plan's ceilings. A callout of a callout works the same way. Put a callout on a sheet like a plan; its tag then says where it is (1 / A-501: the first view on sheet A-501). Print in a callout prints just that area at its scale. Deleting a callout also deletes the callouts drawn inside it, and the lines and hatches drawn in them (you are asked first). If the level a callout is drawn on is deleted, the callout stays under Missing until you delete it, and the sheets it is on report it as missing. Draw lines and hatches in one view Drafting is 2D work that belongs to one view only: notes in lines, a hatched area of concrete or earth, a filled region. It is never in the 3D view and never in another plan. Show the floor plan, ceiling plan or callout to draw in. Choose from Annotate on the plan's bar: Line: click where it starts and where it ends. The next line starts where the last one ended; Enter starts afresh. Polyline: click its points; Enter or a double-click ends it, a click on its first point closes it. Rectangle: two opposite corners. Hatch region: choose the Pattern (Solid, Diagonal, Cross-hatch, Concrete, Insulation, Earth) and its scale on the bar, then click the corners; Enter, a double-click or a click on the first corner closes it. With Fill a room ticked, one click in a room hatches its outline. Points snap as walls do; Shift keeps a line square, and you can type a length as for walls. Backspace takes back the last point. Pick a line or a hatch with Select on the plan. Properties changes a hatch's pattern and scale and has Move… (click the point to move from, then where it goes). Delete deletes it. Each line, hatch and change is one step. Lines and hatches print with the plan, go onto your sheets with it and are in the drawing set. Clear Annotations in a view range to leave them out of that view. Keep the settings in a view template Click New template in the browser, or Save as a template… in a plan's view range (that plan then uses it). Give it a name, set the heights and categories, and click Save the template. In any plan's view range, choose the template. The plan takes its heights and categories; change the template to change every plan that uses it. Elements you hid one by one stay hidden in their own plan. Click a template in the browser to change, rename or delete it. Deleting one leaves its plans with its settings as their own. Arrange a sheet of your own Click New sheet. Type its number and title and choose the paper. Click Add beside a plan, ceiling plan, callout, elevation, section or schedule. It lands in the middle of the sheet. Drag it where it goes. With it chosen, pick a Scale, or leave it on the plan's own scale (when the plan has one), else the scale that fits a third of the sheet. Take off the sheet removes it. Click Save the sheet. It is listed under Sheets; click it to change it later. Download as PDF saves that sheet as one page. What you should see Plans cut at your heights, with what is above the cut dashed and what you cleared left out. A cropped plan veiled outside its region on screen and cut to it on paper, at the scale you gave it. A ceiling plan with the ceilings' grids and height tags, and the lights at the ceiling. Callouts with their rectangle and tag on the plan, each opening at its own scale. Lines and hatches only in the view you drew them in. Your sheets drawn again from the model every time you open them, so they always match it. A view whose level or section line was deleted is reported, not drawn. Every change here is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. All of this is kept in the IFC file as property sets other programs leave alone: each level's view range and ceiling plan on the level, the templates, callouts and your sheets on the project. Lines and hatches are IFC annotations in their level, so other programs see them as 2D curves and hatched areas (their own drawing of each pattern), though not which view they belong to. Other BIM programs call the cut a view range or cut plane, templates view templates or view settings, drafting lines detail lines, and hatch regions filled regions. The ideas are the same; the view settings do not travel to them."},{"slug":"interior-elevations","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Interior elevations","summary":"Make one sheet that shows the four walls of a room from inside, with dimensions and tags, and save it as a PDF.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["interior elevation","room","wall","cabinet","kitchen","elevation","sheet","PDF","dimension","tag","space","A-501"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"make-the-sheet","text":"Make the sheet","level":2},{"id":"what-the-sheet-shows","text":"What the sheet shows","level":2},{"id":"on-your-own-sheets","text":"On your own sheets","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick a room and get one sheet that shows its four walls as you would see them standing inside. It is made for kitchens, baths and any room where you need to see the cabinets, doors and windows on each wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-sheet\"\u003EMake the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the room. In the plan with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E on, click inside the room. In the 3D view or the model tree, pick the space (an IfcSpace).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterior elevations…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe dialog opens with the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E to save it. The file is named &quot;&lt;file&gt; — Interior elevations — &lt;room&gt;.pdf&quot; and is a vector PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E when you are done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-sheet-shows\"\u003EWhat the sheet shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet has four views, two by two, at one scale: the largest standard scale that fits. They are \u003Cstrong\u003ENorth wall\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEast wall\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESouth wall\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EWest wall\u003C/strong\u003E. A title block carries the sheet number A-501, the title &quot;Interior elevations — &quot; with the room number and name, the project and the date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach view is cut through the middle of the room and looks at the wall on that side. The side walls, and anything cut in them such as a window, show as grey cut poché round the view. The view stops just past the face of the wall, so nothing behind the wall shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach view carries:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe room&#039;s width under it, from inside face to inside face;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe room&#039;s height beside it, from the floor to the level above;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea square tag on each door and a hexagon tag on each window in that wall, with its mark (D1, W1 and so on);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea circle tag with the item number on each library item placed in front of that wall: cabinets, fixtures and equipment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe views are made from the model each time you open the dialog, so they always match it. Move a cabinet, then open the dialog again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-your-own-sheets\"\u003EOn your own sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach wall of each room is also a view you can put on a sheet you make: in the project browser click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cstrong\u003EInterior elevations\u003C/strong\u003E add &quot;101 Kitchen — North wall&quot; and the others. It comes with the same dimensions and tags.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInterior elevations in the generated drawing set (they come from the room&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E or your own sheets), rooms that are not mostly rectangular (the view looks square at each side of the room&#039;s box), drafting, notes or your own dimensions on them, and choosing which walls to show. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Pick a room and get one sheet that shows its four walls as you would see them standing inside. It is made for kitchens, baths and any room where you need to see the cabinets, doors and windows on each wall. Make the sheet Pick the room. In the plan with Draw on, click inside the room. In the 3D view or the model tree, pick the space (an IfcSpace). In Properties, click Interior elevations…. The dialog opens with the sheet. Click Download PDF to save it. The file is named \"\u003Cfile\u003E — Interior elevations — \u003Croom\u003E.pdf\" and is a vector PDF. Click Close when you are done. What the sheet shows The sheet has four views, two by two, at one scale: the largest standard scale that fits. They are North wall, East wall, South wall and West wall. A title block carries the sheet number A-501, the title \"Interior elevations — \" with the room number and name, the project and the date. Each view is cut through the middle of the room and looks at the wall on that side. The side walls, and anything cut in them such as a window, show as grey cut poché round the view. The view stops just past the face of the wall, so nothing behind the wall shows. Each view carries: the room's width under it, from inside face to inside face; the room's height beside it, from the floor to the level above; a square tag on each door and a hexagon tag on each window in that wall, with its mark (D1, W1 and so on); a circle tag with the item number on each library item placed in front of that wall: cabinets, fixtures and equipment. The views are made from the model each time you open the dialog, so they always match it. Move a cabinet, then open the dialog again. On your own sheets Each wall of each room is also a view you can put on a sheet you make: in the project browser click New sheet, then under Interior elevations add \"101 Kitchen — North wall\" and the others. It comes with the same dimensions and tags. Not built yet Interior elevations in the generated drawing set (they come from the room's Properties or your own sheets), rooms that are not mostly rectangular (the view looks square at each side of the room's box), drafting, notes or your own dimensions on them, and choosing which walls to show. See What is not built yet."},{"slug":"open-in-another-app","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open a file in another app","summary":"Hand the drawing or model you have open to another app in the lineup in a new tab — a drawing to the drafting app, a model to the detailing app — without uploading anything.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["handoff","hand off","open in","edit in","another app","new tab","send to","detailing","drafting","cadcraft","detailcraft"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECarry on with a file in the app made for the next job, without saving it and opening it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing to edit it in the drafting app, or an IFC model to detail it in the detailing app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E on the 2D view of a drawing, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in DetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar of a model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe other app opens in a new tab with the file already open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file in the new tab. BimCraft keeps it open too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA model with unsaved changes goes over with those changes in it. Your own copy is not saved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing goes over without its linked drawings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file passes between the two tabs inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA button only shows when the other app is open where you are and can take a file this way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf nothing opens, the browser blocked the new tab: allow pop-ups for this site and click again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Carry on with a file in the app made for the next job, without saving it and opening it again. Steps Open a DWG or DXF drawing to edit it in the drafting app, or an IFC model to detail it in the detailing app. Click Edit in CadCraft on the 2D view of a drawing, or Open in DetailCraft in the toolbar of a model. The other app opens in a new tab with the file already open. What you should see The file in the new tab. BimCraft keeps it open too. A model with unsaved changes goes over with those changes in it. Your own copy is not saved. A drawing goes over without its linked drawings. Good to know The file passes between the two tabs inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded. A button only shows when the other app is open where you are and can take a file this way. If nothing opens, the browser blocked the new tab: allow pop-ups for this site and click again. Next View a DWG or DXF drawing Files BimCraft opens and saves"},{"slug":"pipes-and-ducts","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Pipes and ducts","summary":"Draw pipe and duct runs on the plan by their centre lines; the elbows, tees, reducers and transitions are made for you, the runs belong to systems with connected ports in the IFC file, and they show in plans, schedules and the takeoff.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["pipe","pipes","piping","plumbing","duct","ducts","ductwork","hvac","mep","mechanical","elbow","tee","reducer","transition","fitting","fittings","valve","valves","gate valve","ball valve","globe valve","check valve","riser","slope","drain","sanitary","storm","vent","cold water","hot water","chilled water","heating","gas","fire protection","supply air","return air","exhaust","system","systems","port","ports","airflow","cfm","friction","equal friction","sizing","schedule","takeoff"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-pipe","text":"Draw a pipe","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-duct","text":"Draw a duct","level":2},{"id":"the-fittings-made-for-you","text":"The fittings, made for you","level":2},{"id":"risers","text":"Risers","level":2},{"id":"drains-and-their-slope","text":"Drains and their slope","level":2},{"id":"valves","text":"Valves","level":2},{"id":"change-a-run","text":"Change a run","level":2},{"id":"plans","text":"Plans","level":2},{"id":"schedules-and-the-takeoff","text":"Schedules and the takeoff","level":2},{"id":"in-the-ifc-file","text":"In the IFC file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the pipes and ducts of a building the way you draw walls: click the corners of a run&#039;s centre line at the height you set. BimCraft makes the fittings, puts each part in its system and connects them, so other programs read the run as one connected system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-pipe\"\u003EDraw a pipe\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the bar, pick the \u003Cstrong\u003Esystem\u003C/strong\u003E (cold water, hot water, sanitary drain, gas…). It sets a usual material: copper for water, PVC for drains and vents, steel for heating, chilled water, gas and fire protection. Change the \u003Cstrong\u003Ematerial\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Eclass\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Esize\u003C/strong\u003E if you need to. Sizes and walls come from public standards: steel pipe ASME B36.10M, copper tube ASTM B88, PVC ASTM D1785 and PEX ASTM F876.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EAt\u003C/strong\u003E: the height of the pipe&#039;s centre line above the level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the pipe starts, then each corner. Double-click, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, at the last point. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the last corner; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops the run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pipe snaps like walls do, and to other runs: start on a pipe&#039;s end to carry on from it, or end on a pipe&#039;s line to branch off it. A new run takes the height of the pipe it snaps to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-duct\"\u003EDraw a duct\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuct\u003C/span\u003E. Pick the system (supply, return, exhaust or outside air) and the shape: round, rectangular or flat oval.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the width and height (a round duct&#039;s width is its diameter), and \u003Cstrong\u003EAt\u003C/strong\u003E for the centre line&#039;s height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo size it from the airflow, type the \u003Cstrong\u003EAirflow\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003EFriction\u003C/strong\u003E rate and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize it\u003C/span\u003E. The duct is sized by equal friction, the method in the duct design chapter of the ASHRAE Handbook, and rounded up to a stocked size, so the friction comes out at or under the rate you typed. With no rate typed it uses 0.08 in. of water per 100 ft (0.8 Pa/m). A rectangular duct keeps the height you typed and gets a width; BimCraft says so when its sides are more than 4 to 1.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners as for a pipe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-fittings-made-for-you\"\u003EThe fittings, made for you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat BimCraft makes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA run turns\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn elbow. Steel pipe at the long radius of ASME B16.9 (1.5 × the size), other pipe at one outside diameter, round duct at 1.5 diameters, rectangular duct at one width (or one height for a turn up or down)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA run ends on another run&#039;s line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA tee; the other run is split there\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo runs meet end to end at different sizes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA reducer (pipes) or a transition (ducts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo runs of one size meet end to end at an angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn elbow\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you change, move or delete a run, every fitting on that level is made again. If a leg is too short for the fittings at its ends, BimCraft says so and changes nothing: move the corner further away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe fittings and valves are generic: drawn from the pipe or duct size, not from a maker&#039;s catalogue. Socket, press and grooved fittings, valve bodies and equipment have makers&#039; own sizes; check them with your supplier before you order.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"risers\"\u003ERisers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing, type a new height in \u003Cstrong\u003EAt\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the run goes straight up or down at the last corner, with an elbow at each end. A riser can go up into the level above. Each level shows the parts of a run its view range reaches, with \u003Cstrong\u003EUP\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EDN\u003C/strong\u003E at the riser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drains-and-their-slope\"\u003EDrains and their slope\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESanitary and storm drains start at the smallest slope the International Plumbing Code allows for their size: 1/4 in. per foot up to 2-1/2 in., 1/8 in. per foot from 3 to 6 in., 1/16 in. per foot from 8 in. Type another slope in the bar or in Properties: \u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot;/ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 %\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E. The run falls from its first point, so draw drains in the direction they flow. The plan shows the fall and an arrow. Properties warns when a drain is flatter than the code&#039;s minimum.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"valves\"\u003EValves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValve\u003C/span\u003E and the kind: gate, ball, globe or check.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on a pipe where it goes. The pipe is cut round it and the valve is connected to both sides.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProperties of the run lists its valves, each with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-run\"\u003EChange a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick any part of a run, in the plan or the 3D view. In Properties:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESystem\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMaterial\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EClass\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E (pipes), or \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWidth\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EHeight\u003C/strong\u003E (ducts).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHeight above the level\u003C/strong\u003E moves the whole run up or down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E (pipes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlow\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize it\u003C/span\u003E: for water, the smallest size that keeps the water at 8 ft/s (2.4 m/s), or 5 ft/s (1.5 m/s) for hot water. For ducts, the velocity and friction rate of the duct as drawn. Drains are sized by fixture units, which BimCraft does not do yet: pick their size yourself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one step of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the whole run.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plans\"\u003EPlans\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipes smaller than 150 mm (about 6 in.) are drawn as one line on their centre line, larger pipes and all ducts as two lines at their width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach system has its own colour; the plan PDF prints them in black.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach run is labelled with its size and system (for example \u003Ccode\u003E2&quot; CW\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E16×10 SA\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plan&#039;s view range decides which runs it shows, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShown in this plan\u003C/span\u003E can leave out \u003Cstrong\u003EPipes\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EDucts\u003C/strong\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\"\u003EViews, view ranges and your own sheets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"schedules-and-the-takeoff\"\u003ESchedules and the takeoff\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedules\u003C/span\u003E panel adds \u003Cstrong\u003EPipes\u003C/strong\u003E (by system, material and size, with their lengths), \u003Cstrong\u003EDucts\u003C/strong\u003E (by system, shape and size, with lengths and the outside surface area) and \u003Cstrong\u003EFittings and valves\u003C/strong\u003E (by kind and size, counted). Click a row to pick what it lists; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E downloads it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETakeoff\u003C/span\u003E takes the runs along to TakeoffCraft: pipe and duct by size as runs drawn on the plan (sloped drains measured along their slope), fittings and valves counted where they are: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/takeoff\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/takeoff\"\u003ESend the model&#039;s quantities to TakeoffCraft\u003C/a\u003E. Risers are left out of that sheet, because a plan cannot measure them; the Pipes schedule has their length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-ifc-file\"\u003EIn the IFC file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach run is an assembly holding its parts: pipe and duct segments, fittings and valves, each with its type, material, a colour for its system and its ports. Touching ports are connected, and every part belongs to its distribution system (IFC2X3 files get the same as flow segments, fittings and controllers in a system). Other programs that read IFC systems see one connected run per system. The run&#039;s own definition is kept with it, so BimCraft makes it again the same way when you change it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Draw the pipes and ducts of a building the way you draw walls: click the corners of a run's centre line at the height you set. BimCraft makes the fittings, puts each part in its system and connects them, so other programs read the run as one connected system. Draw a pipe Turn on Draw, open Build and choose Pipe. In the bar, pick the system (cold water, hot water, sanitary drain, gas…). It sets a usual material: copper for water, PVC for drains and vents, steel for heating, chilled water, gas and fire protection. Change the material, class and size if you need to. Sizes and walls come from public standards: steel pipe ASME B36.10M, copper tube ASTM B88, PVC ASTM D1785 and PEX ASTM F876. Set At: the height of the pipe's centre line above the level. Click where the pipe starts, then each corner. Double-click, or press Enter, at the last point. Backspace takes back the last corner; Esc drops the run. The pipe snaps like walls do, and to other runs: start on a pipe's end to carry on from it, or end on a pipe's line to branch off it. A new run takes the height of the pipe it snaps to. Draw a duct Choose Duct. Pick the system (supply, return, exhaust or outside air) and the shape: round, rectangular or flat oval. Type the width and height (a round duct's width is its diameter), and At for the centre line's height. To size it from the airflow, type the Airflow and the Friction rate and click Size it. The duct is sized by equal friction, the method in the duct design chapter of the ASHRAE Handbook, and rounded up to a stocked size, so the friction comes out at or under the rate you typed. With no rate typed it uses 0.08 in. of water per 100 ft (0.8 Pa/m). A rectangular duct keeps the height you typed and gets a width; BimCraft says so when its sides are more than 4 to 1. Click the corners as for a pipe. The fittings, made for you Where What BimCraft makes A run turns An elbow. Steel pipe at the long radius of ASME B16.9 (1.5 × the size), other pipe at one outside diameter, round duct at 1.5 diameters, rectangular duct at one width (or one height for a turn up or down) A run ends on another run's line A tee; the other run is split there Two runs meet end to end at different sizes A reducer (pipes) or a transition (ducts) Two runs of one size meet end to end at an angle An elbow When you change, move or delete a run, every fitting on that level is made again. If a leg is too short for the fittings at its ends, BimCraft says so and changes nothing: move the corner further away. The fittings and valves are generic: drawn from the pipe or duct size, not from a maker's catalogue. Socket, press and grooved fittings, valve bodies and equipment have makers' own sizes; check them with your supplier before you order. Risers While drawing, type a new height in At and press Enter: the run goes straight up or down at the last corner, with an elbow at each end. A riser can go up into the level above. Each level shows the parts of a run its view range reaches, with UP or DN at the riser. Drains and their slope Sanitary and storm drains start at the smallest slope the International Plumbing Code allows for their size: 1/4 in. per foot up to 2-1/2 in., 1/8 in. per foot from 3 to 6 in., 1/16 in. per foot from 8 in. Type another slope in the bar or in Properties: 1/4\"/ft, 2 % or 1:50. The run falls from its first point, so draw drains in the direction they flow. The plan shows the fall and an arrow. Properties warns when a drain is flatter than the code's minimum. Valves Choose Valve and the kind: gate, ball, globe or check. Click on a pipe where it goes. The pipe is cut round it and the valve is connected to both sides. Properties of the run lists its valves, each with Remove. Change a run Pick any part of a run, in the plan or the 3D view. In Properties: System, Material, Class and Size (pipes), or Shape, Width, Height (ducts). Height above the level moves the whole run up or down. Slope (pipes). Flow with Size it: for water, the smallest size that keeps the water at 8 ft/s (2.4 m/s), or 5 ft/s (1.5 m/s) for hot water. For ducts, the velocity and friction rate of the duct as drawn. Drains are sized by fixture units, which BimCraft does not do yet: pick their size yourself. Each change is one step of Undo. Delete removes the whole run. Plans Pipes smaller than 150 mm (about 6 in.) are drawn as one line on their centre line, larger pipes and all ducts as two lines at their width. Each system has its own colour; the plan PDF prints them in black. Each run is labelled with its size and system (for example 2\" CW or 16×10 SA). A plan's view range decides which runs it shows, and Shown in this plan can leave out Pipes or Ducts: see Views, view ranges and your own sheets. Schedules and the takeoff The Schedules panel adds Pipes (by system, material and size, with their lengths), Ducts (by system, shape and size, with lengths and the outside surface area) and Fittings and valves (by kind and size, counted). Click a row to pick what it lists; Download CSV downloads it. Takeoff takes the runs along to TakeoffCraft: pipe and duct by size as runs drawn on the plan (sloped drains measured along their slope), fittings and valves counted where they are: see Send the model's quantities to TakeoffCraft. Risers are left out of that sheet, because a plan cannot measure them; the Pipes schedule has their length. In the IFC file Each run is an assembly holding its parts: pipe and duct segments, fittings and valves, each with its type, material, a colour for its system and its ports. Touching ports are connected, and every part belongs to its distribution system (IFC2X3 files get the same as flow segments, fittings and controllers in a system). Other programs that read IFC systems see one connected run per system. The run's own definition is kept with it, so BimCraft makes it again the same way when you change it."},{"slug":"4d-schedule","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Play a construction schedule on the model","summary":"Open a task list as a CSV, link each task to elements by a property, and move through the dates to see what is built, in progress and not started yet.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["4d","schedule","construction sequence","timeline","phasing","programme","gantt","tasks","dates","simulation","progress","built","csv","stage","phase","work package","wbs","group"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"the-task-list","text":"The task list","level":2},{"id":"stages-and-work-packages","text":"Stages and work packages","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWatch the building go up: each task in your schedule linked to the elements it builds, shown day by day.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E4D schedule\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload a template\u003C/span\u003E for a task list with a week per storey, and change it in a spreadsheet. Or write your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a task list…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the CSV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the date slider, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-task-list\"\u003EThe task list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV with a header row and the columns \u003Cstrong\u003ETask\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EStart\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EField\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E. Commas or semicolons both work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETask\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinish\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-02\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-13\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcRoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPset_WallCommon.IsExternal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite dates as YYYY-MM-DD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA row links the task to every element whose \u003Cstrong\u003EField\u003C/strong\u003E has exactly that \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E. The field is a property such as Storey, Class, Name, Type, Material, Tag or GlobalId, or any property written as its set and name: \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WallCommon.FireRating\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat a task on more rows, with the same dates, to link more elements to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stages-and-work-packages\"\u003EStages and work packages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd a \u003Cstrong\u003EStage\u003C/strong\u003E column, a \u003Cstrong\u003EPackage\u003C/strong\u003E column, or both, to gather tasks into stages and work packages. Phase and WBS work as column names too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETask\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinish\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPackage\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1 slab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-02\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-04\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcSlab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructure\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1 walls\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-05\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-13\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructure\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoors\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-30\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-12-04\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcDoor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit-out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoinery\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA task belongs to one stage and one package. If a later row of the same task names another, that row is listed with its line number and left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe same package name in two stages is two packages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the list has groups, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E list appears above the date. Choose a stage or a package and it plays as one:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe slider and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E run from the group&#039;s first start to its last finish,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eonly the group&#039;s tasks are listed and painted, and everything outside the group is see-through,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe line under the list says how many of its tasks are done, under way and to come on that date,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect its elements\u003C/span\u003E selects every element of the group, for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E or a schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery task\u003C/strong\u003E to see the whole schedule again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuilt\u003C/strong\u003E: elements whose tasks are all finished, in their own colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn progress\u003C/strong\u003E: elements with a task under way that day, in ochre. A task is under way from its start date through its finish date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot started\u003C/strong\u003E: see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements in no task stay as they are. The list shows how many elements each task found. Rows that could not be read, or found nothing, are listed with their line numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Watch the building go up: each task in your schedule linked to the elements it builds, shown day by day. Steps Open an IFC model and open 4D schedule in the left panel. Click Download a template for a task list with a week per storey, and change it in a spreadsheet. Or write your own. Click Open a task list… and choose the CSV. Drag the date slider, or click Play. The task list A CSV with a header row and the columns Task, Start, Finish, Field and Value. Commas or semicolons both work. Task Start Finish Field Value Level 1 2026-11-02 2026-11-13 Storey Level 1 Roof 2026-11-16 2026-11-20 Class IfcRoof Roof 2026-11-16 2026-11-20 Pset_WallCommon.IsExternal Yes Write dates as YYYY-MM-DD. A row links the task to every element whose Field has exactly that Value. The field is a property such as Storey, Class, Name, Type, Material, Tag or GlobalId, or any property written as its set and name: Pset_WallCommon.FireRating. Repeat a task on more rows, with the same dates, to link more elements to it. Stages and work packages Add a Stage column, a Package column, or both, to gather tasks into stages and work packages. Phase and WBS work as column names too. Task Start Finish Field Value Stage Package Level 1 slab 2026-11-02 2026-11-04 Class IfcSlab Structure Frame Level 1 walls 2026-11-05 2026-11-13 Storey Level 1 Structure Frame Doors 2026-11-30 2026-12-04 Class IfcDoor Fit-out Joinery A task belongs to one stage and one package. If a later row of the same task names another, that row is listed with its line number and left out. The same package name in two stages is two packages. When the list has groups, a Show list appears above the date. Choose a stage or a package and it plays as one: the slider and Play run from the group's first start to its last finish, only the group's tasks are listed and painted, and everything outside the group is see-through, the line under the list says how many of its tasks are done, under way and to come on that date, Select its elements selects every element of the group, for Show, Section or a schedule. Choose Every task to see the whole schedule again. What you should see Built: elements whose tasks are all finished, in their own colors. In progress: elements with a task under way that day, in ochre. A task is under way from its start date through its finish date. Not started: see-through. Elements in no task stay as they are. The list shows how many elements each task found. Rows that could not be read, or found nothing, are listed with their line numbers. Next Find elements by class, name or property Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"families","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Families and their sizes","summary":"Doors, windows and furniture come in families. Each size of a family is one type in the IFC file, so the type list stays short in BimCraft while other programs read plain types.","section":"tasks","order":37,"keywords":["family","families","type","types","size","sizes","door type","window type","furniture","table","shelf","cabinet","new size","duplicate","place family","family file","ccfamily","ifc library","project library","group","visibility","IfcDoorType","IfcWindowType","IfcGroup"],"headings":[{"id":"what-a-family-is","text":"What a family is","level":2},{"id":"where-to-find-them","text":"Where to find them","level":2},{"id":"doors-and-windows","text":"Doors and windows","level":2},{"id":"new-size-from-this-family","text":"New size from this family","level":2},{"id":"place-furniture","text":"Place furniture","level":2},{"id":"duplicate-a-family","text":"Duplicate a family","level":2},{"id":"family-files","text":"Family files","level":2},{"id":"other-programs-types","text":"Other programs' types","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-family-is\"\u003EWhat a family is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA family is one kind of thing in several sizes: a single swing door that comes 32, 36 and 42 inches wide, or a table that comes long and short. In BimCraft you see the family once, with its sizes under it, instead of a long list of types.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIFC has no families. In the file each size is an ordinary type (a door type, a window type, a furniture type…), and the family is a group that lists its sizes. Other BIM programs read the types and the group as they are; BimCraft reads the family back from them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-find-them\"\u003EWhere to find them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe project browser&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EFamilies\u003C/strong\u003E group lists the families in this model, with how many sizes each has and how many are placed. Below them are the \u003Cstrong\u003EStarter families\u003C/strong\u003E: the doors and windows of the Door and Window tools, and a table, a shelf unit and a base cabinet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFamilies…\u003C/span\u003E shows the same list in a window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a family to open it: its sizes, what each size&#039;s values are, and how many elements use each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Door and Window tools list the families of that kind, then a \u003Cstrong\u003Esize list\u003C/strong\u003E beside them. Pick a size, or type \u003Cstrong\u003EW\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EH\u003C/strong\u003E: a width and height no size has yet becomes a new size of the family when you place the door. Doors of the same size share one type.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, a picked door or window shows its \u003Cstrong\u003EFamily\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESize\u003C/strong\u003E. Choose another size from the list, or type a new width or height. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the family\u003C/span\u003E opens the family it belongs to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach door&#039;s or window&#039;s frame follows its own wall&#039;s thickness, as before families. The size&#039;s type also keeps the door drawn in a 140 mm wall, for programs that read types.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new-size-from-this-family\"\u003ENew size from this family\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the family.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ENew size from this family\u003C/strong\u003E, type its values (Width, Height and the family&#039;s other parameters) and, if you like, a name. Without a name it is named from its values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake the size\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo sizes cannot have the same values. \u003Cstrong\u003ERename\u003C/strong\u003E changes a size&#039;s name; \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes away a size nobody uses; \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E picks the elements of that size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-furniture\"\u003EPlace furniture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace family\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E in a family&#039;s window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the bar, choose the family and its size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on the plan to place it on the level. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90° before you click. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA placed piece&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E show its size, where it is and how far it is turned; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it. Every piece of one size shares that size&#039;s shape, so changing the family changes them all.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate-a-family\"\u003EDuplicate a family\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not have to start from nothing. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E copies a family with its parameters, solids and sizes under a new name. Change the copy in the editor; the original stays as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"family-files\"\u003EFamily files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the family file\u003C/span\u003E saves a \u003Ccode\u003E.ccfamily.json\u003C/code\u003E: the family&#039;s parameters, solids and sizes. Open it in another model with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a family file…\u003C/span\u003E in the Families group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E saves the family as a small IFC file: its sizes&#039; types and its group, declared by an IFC project library. Other programs open it as a set of types; BimCraft opens it as the family again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA family file opened here is read as data and checked before anything is written. A family whose key is already in the model comes in as a separate family.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"other-programs-types\"\u003EOther programs&#039; types\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETypes made by other programs show in the Families group too, grouped by a guess: by the part of their elements&#039; names before the first colon, else by type names that differ only in their numbers. The family window says which guess it used. They are listed and you can select their elements, but they are not edited here: they have no family recipe.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe starter doors and windows are drawn exactly as they were before families. Files made before families open unchanged: their doors and windows show under their family with the sizes in use, and move onto a size of their own the first time you change them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/family-editor\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/family-editor\"\u003EEdit a family\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/doors-and-windows\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What a family is A family is one kind of thing in several sizes: a single swing door that comes 32, 36 and 42 inches wide, or a table that comes long and short. In BimCraft you see the family once, with its sizes under it, instead of a long list of types. IFC has no families. In the file each size is an ordinary type (a door type, a window type, a furniture type…), and the family is a group that lists its sizes. Other BIM programs read the types and the group as they are; BimCraft reads the family back from them. Where to find them The project browser's Families group lists the families in this model, with how many sizes each has and how many are placed. Below them are the Starter families: the doors and windows of the Door and Window tools, and a table, a shelf unit and a base cabinet. Build › Families… shows the same list in a window. Click a family to open it: its sizes, what each size's values are, and how many elements use each. Doors and windows The Door and Window tools list the families of that kind, then a size list beside them. Pick a size, or type W and H: a width and height no size has yet becomes a new size of the family when you place the door. Doors of the same size share one type. In Properties, a picked door or window shows its Family and Size. Choose another size from the list, or type a new width or height. Open the family opens the family it belongs to. Each door's or window's frame follows its own wall's thickness, as before families. The size's type also keeps the door drawn in a 140 mm wall, for programs that read types. New size from this family Open the family. Under New size from this family, type its values (Width, Height and the family's other parameters) and, if you like, a name. Without a name it is named from its values. Click Make the size. Two sizes cannot have the same values. Rename changes a size's name; Remove takes away a size nobody uses; Select picks the elements of that size. Place furniture Build › Place family, or Place in a family's window. In the bar, choose the family and its size. Click on the plan to place it on the level. Press R to turn it 90° before you click. Esc stops. A placed piece's Properties show its size, where it is and how far it is turned; Delete removes it. Every piece of one size shares that size's shape, so changing the family changes them all. Duplicate a family You do not have to start from nothing. Duplicate copies a family with its parameters, solids and sizes under a new name. Change the copy in the editor; the original stays as it was. Family files Save the family file saves a .ccfamily.json: the family's parameters, solids and sizes. Open it in another model with Open a family file… in the Families group. Save as IFC saves the family as a small IFC file: its sizes' types and its group, declared by an IFC project library. Other programs open it as a set of types; BimCraft opens it as the family again. A family file opened here is read as data and checked before anything is written. A family whose key is already in the model comes in as a separate family. Other programs' types Types made by other programs show in the Families group too, grouped by a guess: by the part of their elements' names before the first colon, else by type names that differ only in their numbers. The family window says which guess it used. They are listed and you can select their elements, but they are not edited here: they have no family recipe. The starter doors and windows are drawn exactly as they were before families. Files made before families open unchanged: their doors and windows show under their family with the sizes in use, and move onto a size of their own the first time you change them. See also Edit a family and Doors and windows."},{"slug":"family-editor","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Edit a family","summary":"Change a family's parameters, formulas and solids, show parts by a Yes / No parameter, cut and join solids, and see the result drawn from the IFC it will save.","section":"tasks","order":38,"keywords":["family editor","edit family","parameter","parameters","formula","formulas","visibility","yes no","solid","solids","box","extrusion","extrude","revolve","sweep","profile","arc","boolean","booleans","cut","join","difference","union","handle","handles","work plane","sketch","new family"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-editor","text":"Open the editor","level":2},{"id":"parameters","text":"Parameters","level":2},{"id":"solids","text":"Solids","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-profile","text":"Draw a profile","level":3},{"id":"booleans","text":"Booleans","level":2},{"id":"the-preview-and-its-handles","text":"The preview and its handles","level":2},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-editor\"\u003EOpen the editor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a family (the project browser&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EFamilies\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFamilies…\u003C/span\u003E) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit the family\u003C/span\u003E. To start from nothing, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew family\u003C/span\u003E: give it a name, say what it is (a door, a window, furniture, a fixture or something else) and, for a door or window, which starter its plan symbol and hand come from. The editor opens on a family with a few parameters and one solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEditing a starter family keeps your changes in this model only. Other models keep the starter as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing in the model changes until you click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the family\u003C/span\u003E. The editor has its own undo and redo (the arrow buttons beside the name, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E); saving is one step in the model&#039;s undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parameters\"\u003EParameters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E tab lists the family&#039;s parameters: a name, a kind (length, angle, number, or Yes / No) and a default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eformula\u003C/strong\u003E makes a parameter follow others, for example \u003Ccode\u003EWidth / 30\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eif(Width &gt; 1200, 2, 1)\u003C/code\u003E. Lengths in formulas are millimeters, or write a unit: \u003Ccode\u003E3&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6in\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E50cm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERenaming a parameter renames it in every formula and field that uses it. A parameter still in use cannot be removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA door or window family also gets two names from its wall: \u003Ccode\u003EWallThickness\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EFace\u003C/code\u003E (1 or −1, the side the door swings to).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solids\"\u003ESolids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E tab lists the solids. Add a box, an extrusion, a revolve or a sweep:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESolid\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you set\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eits two corners: x0 to x1, y0 to y1, z0 to z1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrusion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea profile on a work plane (plan, front or side), where the plane is, and a depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevolve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea profile, an axis on the plane, and an angle up to 360°\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESweep\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea path of points and a radius (a rod or a rail)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery number can be a typed number or an expression of the parameters. Each solid has a color and \u003Cstrong\u003EShown when\u003C/strong\u003E: leave it empty to show it always, or name a Yes / No parameter (or write a comparison such as \u003Ccode\u003EShelves &gt;= 3\u003C/code\u003E) to show it only then. That is a visibility parameter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-profile\"\u003EDraw a profile\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn extrusion&#039;s or a revolve&#039;s profile is drawn on its work plane under the preview:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on the paper to add a point after the picked one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a point to move it. Points snap to the grid; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to place them freely.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a small square on a segment to add a point there. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click it to make that segment an arc (click again for the other way, then straight).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a point to remove it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHollow points are set by parameters: change them in the table, not by dragging.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"booleans\"\u003EBooleans\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBooleans\u003C/span\u003E tab cuts the second solid out of the first, or joins them. A Boolean&#039;s result can be cut or joined again. A solid used in a Boolean is drawn only as part of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the second solid is hidden by its Yes / No parameter, the first is kept as it is; when the first is hidden, the result is hidden.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-preview-and-its-handles\"\u003EThe preview and its handles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe preview is the family written as IFC, by the same code that saves it into the model, and drawn back. What you see is what other programs get. Under it, \u003Cstrong\u003ESize shown\u003C/strong\u003E sets the parameter values the preview uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHandles\u003C/strong\u003E are dots on the preview: \u003Cstrong\u003EThe size\u003C/strong\u003E shows a handle for Width, Depth and Height; \u003Cstrong\u003EThe solid picked\u003C/strong\u003E shows one on each face of the solid you picked. Drag a handle:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a face set by a parameter, the size shown changes (the family&#039;s formulas are kept);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a face set by a typed number, the number changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the family\u003C/span\u003E checks the family, then draws every size of it again and makes every element of it again, as one step you can undo. If a size cannot be drawn with the change (for example a solid would have no width at that size), nothing is saved and the editor says which size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe geometry is saved as IFC&#039;s own solids: extrusions, revolves, swept disks, and Boolean results. Nothing is turned into a list of triangles, so files stay small and other programs can read the shapes as shapes. Profiles with arcs are saved with arcs in IFC4; IFC2X3 has no arc segments in a profile, so there the arcs are saved as short straight pieces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open the editor Open a family (the project browser's Families, or Build › Families…) and click Edit the family. To start from nothing, click New family: give it a name, say what it is (a door, a window, furniture, a fixture or something else) and, for a door or window, which starter its plan symbol and hand come from. The editor opens on a family with a few parameters and one solid. Editing a starter family keeps your changes in this model only. Other models keep the starter as it is. Nothing in the model changes until you click Save the family. The editor has its own undo and redo (the arrow buttons beside the name, or Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y); saving is one step in the model's undo. Parameters The Parameters tab lists the family's parameters: a name, a kind (length, angle, number, or Yes / No) and a default. A formula makes a parameter follow others, for example Width / 30 or if(Width \u003E 1200, 2, 1). Lengths in formulas are millimeters, or write a unit: 3', 6in, 50cm. Renaming a parameter renames it in every formula and field that uses it. A parameter still in use cannot be removed. A door or window family also gets two names from its wall: WallThickness and Face (1 or −1, the side the door swings to). Solids The Solids tab lists the solids. Add a box, an extrusion, a revolve or a sweep: Solid What you set Box its two corners: x0 to x1, y0 to y1, z0 to z1 Extrusion a profile on a work plane (plan, front or side), where the plane is, and a depth Revolve a profile, an axis on the plane, and an angle up to 360° Sweep a path of points and a radius (a rod or a rail) Every number can be a typed number or an expression of the parameters. Each solid has a color and Shown when: leave it empty to show it always, or name a Yes / No parameter (or write a comparison such as Shelves \u003E= 3) to show it only then. That is a visibility parameter. Draw a profile An extrusion's or a revolve's profile is drawn on its work plane under the preview: Click on the paper to add a point after the picked one. Drag a point to move it. Points snap to the grid; hold Shift to place them freely. Click a small square on a segment to add a point there. Shift+click it to make that segment an arc (click again for the other way, then straight). Double-click a point to remove it. Hollow points are set by parameters: change them in the table, not by dragging. Booleans The Booleans tab cuts the second solid out of the first, or joins them. A Boolean's result can be cut or joined again. A solid used in a Boolean is drawn only as part of it. When the second solid is hidden by its Yes / No parameter, the first is kept as it is; when the first is hidden, the result is hidden. The preview and its handles The preview is the family written as IFC, by the same code that saves it into the model, and drawn back. What you see is what other programs get. Under it, Size shown sets the parameter values the preview uses. Handles are dots on the preview: The size shows a handle for Width, Depth and Height; The solid picked shows one on each face of the solid you picked. Drag a handle: on a face set by a parameter, the size shown changes (the family's formulas are kept); on a face set by a typed number, the number changes. Saving Save the family checks the family, then draws every size of it again and makes every element of it again, as one step you can undo. If a size cannot be drawn with the change (for example a solid would have no width at that size), nothing is saved and the editor says which size. The geometry is saved as IFC's own solids: extrusions, revolves, swept disks, and Boolean results. Nothing is turned into a list of triangles, so files stay small and other programs can read the shapes as shapes. Profiles with arcs are saved with arcs in IFC4; IFC2X3 has no arc segments in a profile, so there the arcs are saved as short straight pieces. See also Families and their sizes."},{"slug":"edit-ifc-properties","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Change IFC properties and save an edited copy","summary":"Change an element's name, tag, description or a property value, undo, and download a new IFC file while the original stays as it was.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["edit","change","properties","parameters","tag","name","rename","mark","save as","download","undo","copy","ifc","write"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-you-can-change","text":"What you can change","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"kinds-of-values","text":"Kinds of values","level":2},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo","level":2},{"id":"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element","text":"Why your edit changes only one element","level":2},{"id":"warnings-before-you-lose-work","text":"Warnings before you lose work","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECorrect data in an IFC model: rename an element, fix its tag, change a property value. Then save a new IFC file with your changes. The file you opened is never touched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-can-change\"\u003EWhat you can change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEditable\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, Object type, Tag, Description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESingle property values (text, numbers, Yes/No)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType properties (sets marked &quot;(type)&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, and the change applies to every element of that type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType, GlobalId, Line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities (lengths, areas, volumes, counts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELists, enumerations and other multi-part values\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometry, position, adding or deleting elements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click the element you want to change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, click the value you want to change. It turns into a text box. An empty value shows as \u003Cem\u003Eempty\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click somewhere else, to keep it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the value: it is now bold with a blue bar beside it, which marks an edited value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section on the left: the change is listed as \u003Cem\u003Eelement\u003C/em\u003E: \u003Cem\u003Efield\u003C/em\u003E, old value to new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat for other values or other elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E (it shows how many changes are waiting, for example &quot;Save as IFC (3)&quot;), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind &quot;\u003Cem\u003Eoriginal name\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc&quot; in your downloads folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status line says &quot;Saved \u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc with \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E changes (… bytes). Your original file is unchanged.&quot; The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list empties, ready for the next round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"kinds-of-values\"\u003EKinds of values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E: anything you type is kept as is. Clearing Name, Object type, Tag or Description removes the value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumbers\u003C/strong\u003E: type a plain number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2.4\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E, in the file&#039;s own units. No unit symbols. Anything else shows &quot;Use a number.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhole numbers\u003C/strong\u003E (counts, integers): &quot;Use a whole number.&quot; if you type a decimal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYes/No\u003C/strong\u003E: the value becomes a menu with Yes and No.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach value keeps its IFC type, so a number stays a number and other programs read it correctly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo\"\u003EUndo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you are not typing in a field.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat to undo more. Each undo removes the newest change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot undo past a save: after saving, the list starts again. The saved file keeps everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\"\u003EWhy your edit changes only one element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExporters save space by sharing data. One property set may be shared by twenty doors, and one value (&quot;Fire rating: 60 min&quot;) may be listed in ninety sets. If BimCraft simply changed the shared value, every element that shares it would change too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESo BimCraft copies before it writes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the property set you edit is shared with other elements, your element gets its own copy of the set, and only the copy changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the value is shared with other sets, your set gets its own copy of the value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe neighbors keep their old data. This is called \u003Cem\u003Ecopy-on-write\u003C/em\u003E. It adds a few small entries to the file, which every IFC program reads normally.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType properties are the exception on purpose.\u003C/strong\u003E A set marked &quot;(type)&quot; belongs to the element&#039;s type, and in IFC every element of that type reads it from there. Changing it changes the type, so every element of the type shows the new value. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list marks such edits with &quot;(type)&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"warnings-before-you-lose-work\"\u003EWarnings before you lose work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening another file while you have unsaved changes asks &quot;You have \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E unsaved changes to \u003Cem\u003Efile\u003C/em\u003E. Discard them?&quot; Click Cancel to keep working, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the tab with unsaved changes makes the browser ask whether to leave.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping units.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E2400 mm\u003C/code\u003E is refused. Type \u003Ccode\u003E2400\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanging a type property when you meant one element.\u003C/strong\u003E Look for the &quot;(type)&quot; note before you type. If you want one element to differ, change an instance property or its Tag or Name instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting the original file to change.\u003C/strong\u003E Saving always downloads a new file. Replace the original yourself if that is what you want, and keep a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with no changes.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft only saves when there is something to save; otherwise the browser&#039;s own &quot;Save page&quot; may open. Close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving twice and expecting two small files.\u003C/strong\u003E Each save holds every edit since you opened the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting edits to go back into a project file.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft writes IFC only. Bring the edited IFC into your authoring program the way you import any IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Correct data in an IFC model: rename an element, fix its tag, change a property value. Then save a new IFC file with your changes. The file you opened is never touched. What you can change Field Editable Name, Object type, Tag, Description Yes Single property values (text, numbers, Yes/No) Yes Type properties (sets marked \"(type)\") Yes, and the change applies to every element of that type Type, GlobalId, Line No Quantities (lengths, areas, volumes, counts) No Lists, enumerations and other multi-part values No Geometry, position, adding or deleting elements No Steps Open an IFC model and click the element you want to change. In the Properties panel, click the value you want to change. It turns into a text box. An empty value shows as empty. Type the new value. Press Enter, or click somewhere else, to keep it. Press Esc to cancel. Look at the value: it is now bold with a blue bar beside it, which marks an edited value. Look at the Changes section on the left: the change is listed as element: field, old value to new value. Repeat for other values or other elements. Click Save as IFC (it shows how many changes are waiting, for example \"Save as IFC (3)\"), or press Ctrl+S. Find \"original name (edited).ifc\" in your downloads folder. What you should see The status line says \"Saved name (edited).ifc with n changes (… bytes). Your original file is unchanged.\" The Changes list empties, ready for the next round. Kinds of values Text: anything you type is kept as is. Clearing Name, Object type, Tag or Description removes the value. Numbers: type a plain number, such as 2.4 or 1200, in the file's own units. No unit symbols. Anything else shows \"Use a number.\" Whole numbers (counts, integers): \"Use a whole number.\" if you type a decimal. Yes/No: the value becomes a menu with Yes and No. Each value keeps its IFC type, so a number stays a number and other programs read it correctly. Undo Click Undo in the Changes section, or press Ctrl+Z while you are not typing in a field. Repeat to undo more. Each undo removes the newest change. You cannot undo past a save: after saving, the list starts again. The saved file keeps everything. Why your edit changes only one element Exporters save space by sharing data. One property set may be shared by twenty doors, and one value (\"Fire rating: 60 min\") may be listed in ninety sets. If BimCraft simply changed the shared value, every element that shares it would change too. So BimCraft copies before it writes: If the property set you edit is shared with other elements, your element gets its own copy of the set, and only the copy changes. If the value is shared with other sets, your set gets its own copy of the value. The neighbors keep their old data. This is called copy-on-write. It adds a few small entries to the file, which every IFC program reads normally. Type properties are the exception on purpose. A set marked \"(type)\" belongs to the element's type, and in IFC every element of that type reads it from there. Changing it changes the type, so every element of the type shows the new value. The Changes list marks such edits with \"(type)\". See Change a type property for every element of a type. Warnings before you lose work Opening another file while you have unsaved changes asks \"You have n unsaved changes to file. Discard them?\" Click Cancel to keep working, then save. Closing or reloading the tab with unsaved changes makes the browser ask whether to leave. Common mistakes Typing units. 2400 mm is refused. Type 2400. Changing a type property when you meant one element. Look for the \"(type)\" note before you type. If you want one element to differ, change an instance property or its Tag or Name instead. Expecting the original file to change. Saving always downloads a new file. Replace the original yourself if that is what you want, and keep a copy. Pressing Ctrl+S with no changes. BimCraft only saves when there is something to save; otherwise the browser's own \"Save page\" may open. Close it. Saving twice and expecting two small files. Each save holds every edit since you opened the model. Expecting edits to go back into a project file. BimCraft writes IFC only. Bring the edited IFC into your authoring program the way you import any IFC."},{"slug":"assets-and-maintenance","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Keep asset data and a maintenance record in the model","summary":"Use the handed-over IFC model for running the building: maker, model, serial number, warranty and service life for equipment and fixtures, and a record of the maintenance done, all written into the IFC.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["assets","asset register","facility management","fm","operations","maintenance","service","warranty","serial number","barcode","maker","manufacturer","model","service life","handover","cobie","digital twin","overdue","due","work order"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-listed","text":"What is listed","level":2},{"id":"asset-data","text":"Asset data","level":2},{"id":"the-maintenance-record","text":"The maintenance record","level":2},{"id":"how-it-is-kept-in-the-ifc","text":"How it is kept in the IFC","level":2},{"id":"csv","text":"CSV","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssets\u003C/span\u003E tab in the right panel turns the open IFC model into a record of the building&#039;s equipment. Everything is written into the IFC itself, in IFC&#039;s own property sets and tasks, so the next program that opens the file reads it too. Nothing is uploaded and no server is needed: save the IFC to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-listed\"\u003EWhat is listed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEquipment and fixtures: furniture, sanitary fixtures, light fixtures, appliances, air terminals, valves, pumps, fans, boilers, tanks, outlets, switchboards, lifts and other such elements, and library items with equipment data.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAny other element you \u003Cstrong\u003Etrack\u003C/strong\u003E: pick it in the model (a door, a wall, a pump drawn as a plain object) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack picked\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnything that already has asset data or a maintenance task, written here or by another program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is overdue comes first, then what is due within 30 days. Filter by name, tag, maker, model or serial number, by level, or tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly overdue or due within 30 days\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"asset-data\"\u003EAsset data\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an asset in the list (or pick it in the model) to open its data: maker, model, model reference, serial number, barcode, the day it was installed, the warranty number and its start and end, and its service life in years. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the data\u003C/span\u003E writes them in one step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it). Leave a box empty to remove that value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-maintenance-record\"\u003EThe maintenance record\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the data, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecord work done\u003C/span\u003E: the day it was done, the work, who did it, when it is next due and notes. Each entry is one step; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E change it later. The newest entry&#039;s next due date decides whether the asset is overdue or due soon.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-is-kept-in-the-ifc\"\u003EHow it is kept in the IFC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMaker and model in \u003Ccode\u003EPset_ManufacturerTypeInformation\u003C/code\u003E, serial number, barcode and the installed day in \u003Ccode\u003EPset_ManufacturerOccurrence\u003C/code\u003E, the warranty in \u003Ccode\u003EPset_Warranty\u003C/code\u003E, the service life in \u003Ccode\u003EPset_ServiceLife\u003C/code\u003E, on the element itself. When the element&#039;s type already carries a maker or model, it shows until you type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach maintenance entry is an IFC \u003Cstrong\u003Etask\u003C/strong\u003E of the maintenance kind, tied to the element, with its dates (in IFC4 also as the task&#039;s own times) and a \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Maintenance\u003C/code\u003E set with the day, who did it and the next due day.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EValues another element shares are copied first: changing one asset never changes its neighbours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn older IFC2X3 files the dates are written as text (YYYY-MM-DD); that version wants a separate date record there, which BimCraft does not write.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"csv\"\u003ECSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E saves every asset with its data and every maintenance entry, one row each, for a spreadsheet or another maintenance program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESensor readings, live feeds, documents attached to an asset and a COBie spreadsheet are not built yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The Assets tab in the right panel turns the open IFC model into a record of the building's equipment. Everything is written into the IFC itself, in IFC's own property sets and tasks, so the next program that opens the file reads it too. Nothing is uploaded and no server is needed: save the IFC to keep it. What is listed Equipment and fixtures: furniture, sanitary fixtures, light fixtures, appliances, air terminals, valves, pumps, fans, boilers, tanks, outlets, switchboards, lifts and other such elements, and library items with equipment data. Any other element you track: pick it in the model (a door, a wall, a pump drawn as a plain object) and click Track picked. Anything that already has asset data or a maintenance task, written here or by another program. What is overdue comes first, then what is due within 30 days. Filter by name, tag, maker, model or serial number, by level, or tick Only overdue or due within 30 days. Asset data Click an asset in the list (or pick it in the model) to open its data: maker, model, model reference, serial number, barcode, the day it was installed, the warranty number and its start and end, and its service life in years. Save the data writes them in one step (Ctrl+Z undoes it). Leave a box empty to remove that value. The maintenance record Under the data, Record work done: the day it was done, the work, who did it, when it is next due and notes. Each entry is one step; Edit and Delete change it later. The newest entry's next due date decides whether the asset is overdue or due soon. How it is kept in the IFC Maker and model in Pset_ManufacturerTypeInformation, serial number, barcode and the installed day in Pset_ManufacturerOccurrence, the warranty in Pset_Warranty, the service life in Pset_ServiceLife, on the element itself. When the element's type already carries a maker or model, it shows until you type your own. Each maintenance entry is an IFC task of the maintenance kind, tied to the element, with its dates (in IFC4 also as the task's own times) and a CadCraft_Maintenance set with the day, who did it and the next due day. Values another element shares are copied first: changing one asset never changes its neighbours. In older IFC2X3 files the dates are written as text (YYYY-MM-DD); that version wants a separate date record there, which BimCraft does not write. CSV CSV saves every asset with its data and every maintenance entry, one row each, for a spreadsheet or another maintenance program. Sensor readings, live feeds, documents attached to an asset and a COBie spreadsheet are not built yet. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"daylight-factor","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Estimate the daylight in each room","summary":"The average daylight factor of every room from its windows, its inside surfaces and the sky each window sees, with the sky angle measured in the model. An early-design estimate, not a simulation or a code check.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["daylight","daylight factor","average daylight factor","adf","natural light","window","glazing","sky angle","obstruction","overshadowing","balcony","overhang","reflectance","transmittance","early design","analysis","rooms","spaces"],"headings":[{"id":"work-it-out","text":"Work it out","level":2},{"id":"the-formula","text":"The formula","level":2},{"id":"how-the-sky-angle-is-measured","text":"How the sky angle is measured","level":2},{"id":"what-it-leaves-out","text":"What it leaves out","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDaylight\u003C/span\u003E tab in the right panel works out each room&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eaverage daylight factor\u003C/strong\u003E: how much of the light of an overcast sky outside reaches the room, on average, as a percentage. It reads the open IFC model&#039;s rooms (spaces) and windows. Nothing is changed in the model and nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is an early-design estimate by a published formula, \u003Cstrong\u003Enot a simulation\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Enot a check against a building code or a standard\u003C/strong\u003E. BS EN 17037, LEED, BREEAM and energy codes ask for a daylight simulation with the climate of the place; use a daylight simulation program for those.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-it-out\"\u003EWork it out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model that has rooms (spaces) and windows. Models drawn here have a room inside every closed set of walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDaylight\u003C/span\u003E tab and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalculate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach room shows its daylight factor and a band: under 2 %, 2–5 %, 5 % or more. Click a room to see its surfaces and each window&#039;s area and sky angle; the room and its windows are picked in the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange an input and the numbers follow at once; after changing the model, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalculate again\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E saves every room with its numbers and the inputs used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-formula\"\u003EThe formula\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAverage daylight factor (%) = T × Ag × θ × M ÷ (A × (1 − R²)), added up over the room&#039;s windows (Littlefair&#039;s formula, used by BRE 209, BS 8206-2 and CIBSE LG10):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ET\u003C/strong\u003E, the glass&#039;s light transmittance (0.7 to start: use the maker&#039;s figure).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAg\u003C/strong\u003E, the glazed area: each window&#039;s area (its overall width × height, else its size in the model) × the \u003Cstrong\u003Eglazed share\u003C/strong\u003E (0.75 to start: the rest is frame).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eθ\u003C/strong\u003E, the angle of sky the window sees, in degrees (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EM\u003C/strong\u003E, the maintenance factor for dirt on the glass (1 to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA\u003C/strong\u003E, every inside surface of the room: floor, walls, ceiling and windows, taken from the room&#039;s own shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ER\u003C/strong\u003E, their mean reflectance, weighted by area: floor 0.2, walls 0.5, ceiling 0.7 to start; glass counts as 0.1.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-sky-angle-is-measured\"\u003EHow the sky angle is measured\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each window BimCraft cuts the model with an upright plane square to the window, from the outside face of the glass, and looks from the horizon straight up: everything the cut meets in front of the window (a building opposite, a balcony or an overhang above, the eaves) hides part of that 90°. θ is what is left. The window itself and the wall it sits in are left out. It does this at five places across the window&#039;s width and takes the mean, so a thin post in front of the middle takes its share, not the whole sky.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA building 8.5 m above the window&#039;s centre and 9.9 m away hides 40.65° (the angle whose tangent is 8.5 ÷ 9.9), leaving θ = 49.35°.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-leaves-out\"\u003EWhat it leaves out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERoof windows (glass lying flat or sloping) are listed but not counted: the formula here is for windows in walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWindows with no room behind them (a hall without a space, for example) are listed apart.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDirect sunlight, glare, the daylight at a given point, the hours of sun and any energy figure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe bands are from older British guidance (BS 8206-2), shown as a guide, never as a pass or fail.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Daylight tab in the right panel works out each room's average daylight factor: how much of the light of an overcast sky outside reaches the room, on average, as a percentage. It reads the open IFC model's rooms (spaces) and windows. Nothing is changed in the model and nothing is uploaded. This is an early-design estimate by a published formula, not a simulation and not a check against a building code or a standard. BS EN 17037, LEED, BREEAM and energy codes ask for a daylight simulation with the climate of the place; use a daylight simulation program for those. Work it out Open an IFC model that has rooms (spaces) and windows. Models drawn here have a room inside every closed set of walls. Open the Daylight tab and click Calculate. Each room shows its daylight factor and a band: under 2 %, 2–5 %, 5 % or more. Click a room to see its surfaces and each window's area and sky angle; the room and its windows are picked in the model. Change an input and the numbers follow at once; after changing the model, Calculate again. CSV saves every room with its numbers and the inputs used. The formula Average daylight factor (%) = T × Ag × θ × M ÷ (A × (1 − R²)), added up over the room's windows (Littlefair's formula, used by BRE 209, BS 8206-2 and CIBSE LG10): T, the glass's light transmittance (0.7 to start: use the maker's figure). Ag, the glazed area: each window's area (its overall width × height, else its size in the model) × the glazed share (0.75 to start: the rest is frame). θ, the angle of sky the window sees, in degrees (below). M, the maintenance factor for dirt on the glass (1 to start). A, every inside surface of the room: floor, walls, ceiling and windows, taken from the room's own shape. R, their mean reflectance, weighted by area: floor 0.2, walls 0.5, ceiling 0.7 to start; glass counts as 0.1. How the sky angle is measured For each window BimCraft cuts the model with an upright plane square to the window, from the outside face of the glass, and looks from the horizon straight up: everything the cut meets in front of the window (a building opposite, a balcony or an overhang above, the eaves) hides part of that 90°. θ is what is left. The window itself and the wall it sits in are left out. It does this at five places across the window's width and takes the mean, so a thin post in front of the middle takes its share, not the whole sky. A building 8.5 m above the window's centre and 9.9 m away hides 40.65° (the angle whose tangent is 8.5 ÷ 9.9), leaving θ = 49.35°. What it leaves out Roof windows (glass lying flat or sloping) are listed but not counted: the formula here is for windows in walls. Windows with no room behind them (a hall without a space, for example) are listed apart. Direct sunlight, glare, the daylight at a given point, the hours of sun and any energy figure. The bands are from older British guidance (BS 8206-2), shown as a guide, never as a pass or fail."},{"slug":"type-vs-instance","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Change a type property for every element of a type","summary":"Tell type properties from instance properties, change a value on the type so every element follows, and check that one element's own edit stays its own.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["type","instance","type properties","family type","shared","copy on write","all elements","bulk edit","manufacturer","model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"type-or-instance","text":"Type or instance?","level":2},{"id":"steps-change-the-type","text":"Steps: change the type","level":2},{"id":"steps-change-one-element-only","text":"Steps: change one element only","level":2},{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet the manufacturer on a door type so every door of that type shows it, then give one door its own fire rating without touching the others. This shows the difference between type and instance properties in BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-or-instance\"\u003EType or instance?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Einstance\u003C/strong\u003E property belongs to one element: its tag, its mark, its room, its own fire rating.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etype\u003C/strong\u003E property belongs to the type that many elements share: manufacturer, model, width of a door type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, sets that come from the type have &quot; (type)&quot; after their name and the line &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot; Every other set belongs to the element itself. \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E in the first block shows the type&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-change-the-type\"\u003ESteps: change the type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click a door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENote its \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E in the first block of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, for example &quot;Single-Flush 900 x 2100&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to a set whose name ends in &quot;(type)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the value you want to change, for example \u003Cstrong\u003EManufacturer\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list: the entry ends with &quot;(type)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another door of the same type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the same value: it shows the new value too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-change-one-element-only\"\u003ESteps: change one element only\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a set without &quot;(type)&quot; in its name, for example \u003Ccode\u003EPset_DoorCommon\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EFireRating\u003C/strong\u003E, type a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the door next to it, of the same type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the same value: it still shows the old value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEven if the exporter shared that property set between both doors, BimCraft gave the first door its own copy before changing it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties#why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-hash=\"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\"\u003EWhy your edit changes only one element\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the saved copy in BimCraft, or in any IFC viewer, to check both changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot every model has types.\u003C/strong\u003E Some exporters write no type objects, or put everything on the instance. Then there are no &quot;(type)&quot; sets, and each element must be changed on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe same value appears in a type set and an instance set.\u003C/strong\u003E Some exporters write a parameter in both places. Other programs may read either one; change both if you need them to agree.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to pick &quot;all doors of this type&quot; and edit them together.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no multi-select in this build. A type property is the way to change many elements at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Set the manufacturer on a door type so every door of that type shows it, then give one door its own fire rating without touching the others. This shows the difference between type and instance properties in BimCraft. Type or instance? An instance property belongs to one element: its tag, its mark, its room, its own fire rating. A type property belongs to the type that many elements share: manufacturer, model, width of a door type. In the Properties panel, sets that come from the type have \" (type)\" after their name and the line \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Every other set belongs to the element itself. Type in the first block shows the type's name. Steps: change the type Open an IFC model and click a door. Note its Type in the first block of the Properties panel, for example \"Single-Flush 900 x 2100\". Scroll to a set whose name ends in \"(type)\". Click the value you want to change, for example Manufacturer. Type the new value and press Enter. Look at the Changes list: the entry ends with \"(type)\". Click another door of the same type. Check the same value: it shows the new value too. Steps: change one element only Click one door. Open a set without \"(type)\" in its name, for example Pset_DoorCommon. Click a value, such as FireRating, type a new value and press Enter. Click the door next to it, of the same type. Check the same value: it still shows the old value. Even if the exporter shared that property set between both doors, BimCraft gave the first door its own copy before changing it. See Why your edit changes only one element. Save Click Save as IFC or press Ctrl+S. Open the saved copy in BimCraft, or in any IFC viewer, to check both changes. Common mistakes Not every model has types. Some exporters write no type objects, or put everything on the instance. Then there are no \"(type)\" sets, and each element must be changed on its own. The same value appears in a type set and an instance set. Some exporters write a parameter in both places. Other programs may read either one; change both if you need them to agree. Expecting to pick \"all doors of this type\" and edit them together. There is no multi-select in this build. A type property is the way to change many elements at once."},{"slug":"frame-analysis","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Analyse the structural frame","summary":"Forces, reactions and deflections of the model's beams, columns and braces under load cases and combinations you set, kept in the IFC. An analysis aid, not a code check.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["analysis","structural analysis","frame analysis","analytical model","stiffness method","loads","load case","combination","dead load","live load","self weight","own weight","reactions","bending moment","shear","axial force","deflection","deflected shape","supports","pinned","fixed","release","report","pdf","csv","craftscript","frame3d","beams","columns","members"],"headings":[{"id":"run-it","text":"Run it","level":2},{"id":"how-the-analytical-model-is-made","text":"How the analytical model is made","level":2},{"id":"cases-combinations-signs-and-the-report","text":"Cases, combinations, signs and the report","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right finds the forces in the open model&#039;s beams, columns and braces, the reactions at the column feet and how far the members bend, for the loads you give it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an \u003Cstrong\u003Eanalysis aid, not a code check\u003C/strong\u003E. It applies no design code: it does not size members or check capacities, and it does not pick load factors. An engineer checks the analytical model, its loads and supports, and decides what the numbers mean.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-it\"\u003ERun it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick one or more beams, columns or braces in the view, choose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECase\u003C/span\u003E, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad\u003C/span\u003E and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDirection\u003C/span\u003E, type the value and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd load\u003C/span\u003E: kN/m for a load spread along a member, kN for a point load.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun analysis\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table lists each member&#039;s largest axial force, shears, bending moments, torsion and deflection; click a member&#039;s name to pick it in the view. Below are the reactions. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOn the model\u003C/span\u003E draws the deflected shape, or one action as a diagram beside each member. The results go out of date when the model changes: run it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach load and setting is one change in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E takes it back, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E keeps them in the file (the project&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Analysis\u003C/code\u003E property set), so the next time the model opens here its loads are still there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-analytical-model-is-made\"\u003EHow the analytical model is made\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery IfcBeam, IfcColumn and IfcMember whose shape is one straight extrusion becomes a member on its section&#039;s centroid line. Its section properties come from the profile: I, U, L and T shapes, rectangles and tubes, circles and round tubes, or any closed outline (then the torsion constant is an estimate, said in the report). Anything else is left out and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a model, beams stop at a column&#039;s face and sit below the floor. Each member end is carried along its own line onto the axis of the member it frames into, when that axis is close (within about the size of the sections), and that member is split there. The report says how many ends moved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColumn feet\u003C/strong\u003E nothing goes below are supports: fixed (the start) or pinned (held in place and against twisting, free to turn).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeam ends\u003C/strong\u003E are rigid or pinned by the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam ends with no connection\u003C/span\u003E rule; \u003Cstrong\u003Ebraces\u003C/strong\u003E (IfcMember) are pinned at both ends. Pick one member to give its ends, or a column&#039;s foot, a rule of their own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMaterials by name: concrete (E = 30 GPa), timber (E = 11 GPa), otherwise steel (E = 200 GPa).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the frame can move without bending anything — a mechanism — the run stops and says where and which way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cases-combinations-signs-and-the-report\"\u003ECases, combinations, signs and the report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe analysis starts with \u003Cstrong\u003EDead\u003C/strong\u003E (own weight), \u003Cstrong\u003ELive\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDead + live\u003C/strong\u003E; add cases and combinations with the factors your design code asks for. N &gt; 0 is tension; My &gt; 0 puts the member&#039;s local −z side in tension (a beam sagging under gravity has My &gt; 0). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E hold the assumptions, sections, cases, reactions with their sums, member forces, displacements and an envelope. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as CraftScript\u003C/span\u003E writes the frame for PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace, where the library function \u003Ccode\u003Eframe3d\u003C/code\u003E solves it in readable code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe method is the direct stiffness method, first order and linear, checked against textbook cases. Not built yet: P-Δ, buckling, dynamics, area loads, member offsets, tension-only braces, walls and slabs in the frame, design code checks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Analysis tab on the right finds the forces in the open model's beams, columns and braces, the reactions at the column feet and how far the members bend, for the loads you give it. It is an analysis aid, not a code check. It applies no design code: it does not size members or check capacities, and it does not pick load factors. An engineer checks the analytical model, its loads and supports, and decides what the numbers mean. Run it Open an IFC model and the Analysis tab. Pick one or more beams, columns or braces in the view, choose a Case, a Load and a Direction, type the value and press Add load: kN/m for a load spread along a member, kN for a point load. Press Run analysis. The table lists each member's largest axial force, shears, bending moments, torsion and deflection; click a member's name to pick it in the view. Below are the reactions. On the model draws the deflected shape, or one action as a diagram beside each member. The results go out of date when the model changes: run it again. Each load and setting is one change in the Changes list: Undo takes it back, and Save as IFC keeps them in the file (the project's CadCraft_Analysis property set), so the next time the model opens here its loads are still there. How the analytical model is made Every IfcBeam, IfcColumn and IfcMember whose shape is one straight extrusion becomes a member on its section's centroid line. Its section properties come from the profile: I, U, L and T shapes, rectangles and tubes, circles and round tubes, or any closed outline (then the torsion constant is an estimate, said in the report). Anything else is left out and counted. In a model, beams stop at a column's face and sit below the floor. Each member end is carried along its own line onto the axis of the member it frames into, when that axis is close (within about the size of the sections), and that member is split there. The report says how many ends moved. Column feet nothing goes below are supports: fixed (the start) or pinned (held in place and against twisting, free to turn). Beam ends are rigid or pinned by the Beam ends with no connection rule; braces (IfcMember) are pinned at both ends. Pick one member to give its ends, or a column's foot, a rule of their own. Materials by name: concrete (E = 30 GPa), timber (E = 11 GPa), otherwise steel (E = 200 GPa). If the frame can move without bending anything — a mechanism — the run stops and says where and which way. Cases, combinations, signs and the report The analysis starts with Dead (own weight), Live and Dead + live; add cases and combinations with the factors your design code asks for. N \u003E 0 is tension; My \u003E 0 puts the member's local −z side in tension (a beam sagging under gravity has My \u003E 0). Report (PDF) and Report (CSV) hold the assumptions, sections, cases, reactions with their sums, member forces, displacements and an envelope. Export as CraftScript writes the frame for PartCraft's Script workspace, where the library function frame3d solves it in readable code. The method is the direct stiffness method, first order and linear, checked against textbook cases. Not built yet: P-Δ, buckling, dynamics, area loads, member offsets, tension-only braces, walls and slabs in the frame, design code checks. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"assistants","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Let an AI assistant work with your model","summary":"Turn on assistants in Settings so an assistant on your computer can find elements, read their properties, isolate them in the view and draw walls, doors, windows, floors, levels and grids, through CadCraft Studio. Off until you turn it on.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","script","automation","studio","find elements","isolate","properties","permissions","disconnect","activity","draw walls","add doors","add windows","levels","grids","floors","undo","save","command line","cli","headless","use with ai assistants"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-bimcraft","text":"What an assistant can do in BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"while-an-assistant-is-connected","text":"While an assistant is connected","level":2},{"id":"without-a-window-scripts-and-the-command-line","text":"Without a window: scripts and the command line","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-off","text":"Turn it off","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (any assistant that speaks MCP) look into the IFC model you have open and draw in it, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-bimcraft\"\u003EWhat an assistant can do in BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt asks to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind elements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt gets a list of elements by class, storey, name or property value, as \u003Cstrong\u003EFind\u003C/strong\u003E does\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead properties\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt gets one element&#039;s attributes, type, property sets and quantities, as \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescribe the model, list walls\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt gets the levels, walls, doors, windows, rooms, floors, grids and columns, and each wall&#039;s ends, type and height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIsolate, see-through, hide or show\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 3D view changes as if you had used the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the view and draw\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw walls, doors, windows, floors, levels or grid lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model changes as if you had used the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E tools: walls join, rooms, floors and roofs follow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the view and draw\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect elements, undo its own steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElements are picked in the view; its last step is taken back\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the view and draw\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new IFC file with every change, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E makes it (the original is never changed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change an assistant makes is one step in \u003Cstrong\u003EChanges\u003C/strong\u003E, named &quot;Assistant: …&quot; (for example &quot;Assistant: add 4 walls&quot;), so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back its whole action, and you can see whose step it was. It cannot delete or move what is there, and it cannot open files: you open the model; it works with the one that is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants reach BimCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E and choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked for you. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EChange the view and draw\u003C/strong\u003E too if the assistant may isolate and hide elements and draw, and \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E if it may save the model as a new file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-an-assistant-is-connected\"\u003EWhile an assistant is connected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisconnect\u003C/span\u003E to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E, under \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAsk in plain words, for example &quot;isolate the external walls on Level 1&quot;, &quot;which doors have no fire rating?&quot; or &quot;draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a door in the south wall&quot;. The assistant finds the elements and shows them in your view, or draws them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-a-window-scripts-and-the-command-line\"\u003EWithout a window: scripts and the command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same drawing tools work on IFC files in folders you name, with no window open: CadCraft&#039;s MCP server and command line can start a new building or open an IFC file, describe it, add walls, doors, windows, floors, levels and grids, undo and save it as a new \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file. You decide which folders it may use and whether it may change or save anything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-off\"\u003ETurn it off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUntick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (any assistant that speaks MCP) look into the IFC model you have open and draw in it, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it. What an assistant can do in BimCraft It asks to What happens Needs Find elements It gets a list of elements by class, storey, name or property value, as Find does Read Read properties It gets one element's attributes, type, property sets and quantities, as Properties shows them Read Describe the model, list walls It gets the levels, walls, doors, windows, rooms, floors, grids and columns, and each wall's ends, type and height Read Isolate, see-through, hide or show The 3D view changes as if you had used the Show menu Change the view and draw Draw walls, doors, windows, floors, levels or grid lines The model changes as if you had used the Draw tools: walls join, rooms, floors and roofs follow Change the view and draw Select elements, undo its own steps Elements are picked in the view; its last step is taken back Change the view and draw Save A new IFC file with every change, as Save as IFC makes it (the original is never changed) Save Each change an assistant makes is one step in Changes, named \"Assistant: …\" (for example \"Assistant: add 4 walls\"), so Ctrl+Z takes back its whole action, and you can see whose step it was. It cannot delete or move what is there, and it cannot open files: you open the model; it works with the one that is open. Turn it on Assistants reach BimCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in. Open Settings and choose the BimCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft. Read is ticked for you. Tick Change the view and draw too if the assistant may isolate and hide elements and draw, and Save if it may save the model as a new file. Connect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it). While an assistant is connected A note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click Disconnect to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue. Activity, under Assistants in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked. Ask in plain words, for example \"isolate the external walls on Level 1\", \"which doors have no fire rating?\" or \"draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a door in the south wall\". The assistant finds the elements and shows them in your view, or draws them. Without a window: scripts and the command line The same drawing tools work on IFC files in folders you name, with no window open: CadCraft's MCP server and command line can start a new building or open an IFC file, describe it, add walls, doors, windows, floors, levels and grids, undo and save it as a new .ifc file. You decide which folders it may use and whether it may change or save anything. Turn it off Untick Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time. See also Find elements by class, name or property Hide, isolate and see through elements BimCraft settings"},{"slug":"read-rvt-file","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Read a project or family file","summary":"Open an .rvt, .rfa, .rte or .rft file and read its release, worksharing, project information, family parameters, links and preview picture, without the program that made it.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["rvt","rfa","rte","rft","project file","family","release","version","which version","build","worksharing","central","project information","preview","thumbnail","links"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"why-there-is-no-3d","text":"Why there is no 3D","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind out what is in a project or family file without opening it in Revit: which release saved it, whether it is workshared, the project information, and what it links to. Useful when a file arrives from someone else and you need to know what release to open it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait a moment. The status line says &quot;Reading the file…&quot; and then &quot;Opened \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the top of the card: the preview picture saved in the file, the kind of file and the release it was saved in, and the project name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down to read the sections of the card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card in the middle, with these sections when the file holds them:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it tells you\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile name, size, kind (project, project template, family or family template), the release and build it was saved in, worksharing, the central model&#039;s path, where it was last saved, who saved it, locale and document GUID\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s own fields, such as name, number, client, address and status. Fields still at their new-project defaults are left out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily, Family: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor a family: its name, category, release, last update and its parameters, group by group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery linked model, CAD link, image and keynote table the file points to, with its saved path\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the left, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel repeats the key facts and adds \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E: how much of the file BimCraft actually read, usually about 40 KB of a file many megabytes in size. On the right, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel says element properties need the 3D model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-there-is-no-3d\"\u003EWhy there is no 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D model and the elements inside a project file are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only the parts of the file that are documented or plainly readable: the file information, project information, family data, the link list and the preview picture. It does not guess at the rest. To see the model in 3D and read element properties, export IFC from the program that made the file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E See above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinks show only their saved path.\u003C/strong\u003E A file opened on its own cannot check whether its links exist. Open the folder it lives in instead, and BimCraft marks each link as found or not: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This file is incomplete…&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still &quot;online only&quot; does this too. BimCraft still opens it and shows only what was recovered: an amber notice at the top of the card, a \u003Cstrong\u003ERecovered from this file\u003C/strong\u003E section listing the parts whose every byte is in the file, and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the file\u003C/strong\u003E for the rest. Nothing is filled in for the missing part, so the release, preview picture or project information can be missing when they were stored past the cut. If even the file&#039;s list of contents was cut off, nothing can be opened. Copy the original again and reopen it for the full file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No preview picture in this file&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E Some files are saved without one. That is normal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a backup by mistake.\u003C/strong\u003E Files named like \u003Ccode\u003ETower.0004.rvt\u003C/code\u003E are numbered backups. Opening the folder groups them with their model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Find out what is in a project or family file without opening it in Revit: which release saved it, whether it is workshared, the project information, and what it links to. Useful when a file arrives from someone else and you need to know what release to open it in. Steps Click Choose a file. Choose an .rvt, .rfa, .rte or .rft file. Wait a moment. The status line says \"Reading the file…\" and then \"Opened file name\". Look at the top of the card: the preview picture saved in the file, the kind of file and the release it was saved in, and the project name. Scroll down to read the sections of the card. What you should see The card in the middle, with these sections when the file holds them: Section What it tells you File File name, size, kind (project, project template, family or family template), the release and build it was saved in, worksharing, the central model's path, where it was last saved, who saved it, locale and document GUID Project information The project's own fields, such as name, number, client, address and status. Fields still at their new-project defaults are left out. Family, Family: … For a family: its name, category, release, last update and its parameters, group by group Linked and referenced files Every linked model, CAD link, image and keynote table the file points to, with its saved path On the left, the File panel repeats the key facts and adds Read: how much of the file BimCraft actually read, usually about 40 KB of a file many megabytes in size. On the right, the Properties panel says element properties need the 3D model. Why there is no 3D The 3D model and the elements inside a project file are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only the parts of the file that are documented or plainly readable: the file information, project information, family data, the link list and the preview picture. It does not guess at the rest. To see the model in 3D and read element properties, export IFC from the program that made the file. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC. Common mistakes Expecting 3D. See above. Links show only their saved path. A file opened on its own cannot check whether its links exist. Open the folder it lives in instead, and BimCraft marks each link as found or not: Open a whole project folder. \"This file is incomplete…\" The file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still \"online only\" does this too. BimCraft still opens it and shows only what was recovered: an amber notice at the top of the card, a Recovered from this file section listing the parts whose every byte is in the file, and Not in the file for the rest. Nothing is filled in for the missing part, so the release, preview picture or project information can be missing when they were stored past the cut. If even the file's list of contents was cut off, nothing can be opened. Copy the original again and reopen it for the full file. \"No preview picture in this file\". Some files are saved without one. That is normal. Opening a backup by mistake. Files named like Tower.0004.rvt are numbered backups. Opening the folder groups them with their model."},{"slug":"field-points","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Points for setting out, and as-built checks","summary":"Number the places a crew sets out from the model — column centres, wall ends, anchor bolts, sleeves, any element you pick — write them for a total station, then read the crew's as-built points back and see what is out.","section":"tasks","order":61,"keywords":["layout points","setting out","set out","stake out","staking","total station","survey","point file","csv","txt","pnezd","penzd","xyz","northing","easting","elevation","as built","as-built","check","tolerance","deviation","anchor bolts","column centres","wall ends","sleeves","survey coordinates","map conversion"],"headings":[{"id":"make-the-points","text":"Make the points","level":2},{"id":"check-the-as-built-points","text":"Check the as-built points","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right makes the points a crew sets out on site from the open model, and checks the points they measure afterwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-points\"\u003EMake the points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick what to set out: \u003Cstrong\u003Ecolumn centres\u003C/strong\u003E (the foot of each column&#039;s centre line), \u003Cstrong\u003Ewall ends\u003C/strong\u003E (both ends of each wall&#039;s axis, at its base), \u003Cstrong\u003Eanchor bolts\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Esleeves\u003C/strong\u003E (elements whose name or type says sleeve), and the \u003Cstrong\u003Epicked elements\u003C/strong\u003E (each at its placement point — pick them in the view first).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the first number and a prefix, the coordinates, the units, the columns and the file:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E: the file&#039;s own coordinates. \u003Cstrong\u003ESurvey grid\u003C/strong\u003E: the map grid of the model&#039;s georeference (shown only when the IFC has a map conversion), in metres or the unit you choose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EColumns \u003Cstrong\u003EPNEZD\u003C/strong\u003E (point, northing, easting, elevation, description), \u003Cstrong\u003EPENZD\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EXYZ\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003ECSV\u003C/strong\u003E (commas) or \u003Cstrong\u003ETXT\u003C/strong\u003E (tabs).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake points\u003C/span\u003E: the list shows them and the view marks them in blue. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the points\u003C/span\u003E writes the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints come in a fixed order — by kind, then in rows across the plan — so making them again from the same model gives the same numbers. The file is the one CivilCraft opens and CadCraft&#039;s EXPORTPOINTS writes: one point per line, three decimals, a description in quotes when it holds a comma.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-as-built-points\"\u003ECheck the as-built points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the crew sends back what they measured, choose a \u003Cstrong\u003Etolerance\u003C/strong\u003E in millimetres and how to pair the points — by \u003Cstrong\u003Epoint number\u003C/strong\u003E (they kept your numbers) or the \u003Cstrong\u003Enearest shot\u003C/strong\u003E — and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead as-built points…\u003C/span\u003E. Read the file in the same units and coordinates you wrote.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach design point is then \u003Cstrong\u003Ewithin\u003C/strong\u003E the tolerance, \u003Cstrong\u003Eoutside\u003C/strong\u003E it, \u003Cstrong\u003Enot measured\u003C/strong\u003E, and any shot nobody asked for is \u003Cstrong\u003Eextra\u003C/strong\u003E. The table gives the differences in millimetres (as built minus design: east, north, height, and the distance in plan); the view marks each point green or red with a line to the shot, and grey when it was not measured. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E writes the whole comparison.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing here changes the model. Not built yet: setting out from slab edges, grid intersections or openings made for you, and sending points to an instrument directly — use the file.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Points tab on the right makes the points a crew sets out on site from the open model, and checks the points they measure afterwards. Make the points Open an IFC model and the Points tab. Tick what to set out: column centres (the foot of each column's centre line), wall ends (both ends of each wall's axis, at its base), anchor bolts, sleeves (elements whose name or type says sleeve), and the picked elements (each at its placement point — pick them in the view first). Choose the first number and a prefix, the coordinates, the units, the columns and the file: Model: the file's own coordinates. Survey grid: the map grid of the model's georeference (shown only when the IFC has a map conversion), in metres or the unit you choose. Columns PNEZD (point, northing, easting, elevation, description), PENZD or XYZ; CSV (commas) or TXT (tabs). Press Make points: the list shows them and the view marks them in blue. Download the points writes the file. Points come in a fixed order — by kind, then in rows across the plan — so making them again from the same model gives the same numbers. The file is the one CivilCraft opens and CadCraft's EXPORTPOINTS writes: one point per line, three decimals, a description in quotes when it holds a comma. Check the as-built points When the crew sends back what they measured, choose a tolerance in millimetres and how to pair the points — by point number (they kept your numbers) or the nearest shot — and press Read as-built points…. Read the file in the same units and coordinates you wrote. Each design point is then within the tolerance, outside it, not measured, and any shot nobody asked for is extra. The table gives the differences in millimetres (as built minus design: east, north, height, and the distance in plan); the view marks each point green or red with a line to the shot, and grey when it was not measured. Report (CSV) writes the whole comparison. Nothing here changes the model. Not built yet: setting out from slab edges, grid intersections or openings made for you, and sending points to an instrument directly — use the file."},{"slug":"rvt-to-ifc","scope":"bimcraft","title":"From a project file to BimCraft through IFC","summary":"The IFC export settings that open best in BimCraft (version, property sets, base quantities, rooms, linked models, coordinates), how to open the result, and what the export keeps.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["export","ifc export","rvt to ifc","convert","3d","geometry","ifc2x3","ifc4","coordination view","reference view","design transfer view","property sets","base quantities","rooms","spaces","linked files","shared coordinates","survey point","project base point","settings"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"the-settings-that-open-best-here","text":"The settings that open best here","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"what-the-export-keeps-and-what-it-does-not","text":"What the export keeps, and what it does not","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn a project file into an IFC file you can orbit, inspect, check and edit in BimCraft. BimCraft does not read the 3D model inside an \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E file yet, so the export happens in the program that made the file (Revit); BimCraft opens the result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project in the program that made it. BimCraft&#039;s card names the release it was saved in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a 3D view that shows what you want to export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Export &gt; IFC\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the export dialog choose a setup (or \u003Cstrong\u003EModify setup…\u003C/strong\u003E) with the settings below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a folder and file name, and export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn BimCraft, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E and choose the new \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file. Linked models exported as their own files open into the same scene with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-settings-that-open-best-here\"\u003EThe settings that open best here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting in the exporter\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoose\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC version\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC4 Reference View\u003C/strong\u003E for looking, checking and taking off; \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0\u003C/strong\u003E when others need IFC2X3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth open fully. \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC4 Design Transfer View\u003C/strong\u003E opens too, with more exact shapes, but the file is larger and slower.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E (plain)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft does not open \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhase / visible elements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe phase you mean, and \u003Cstrong\u003EExport only elements visible in view\u003C/strong\u003E when you set up the 3D view for it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeaves out what you hid on purpose.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProperty sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport Revit property sets\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EExport IFC common property sets\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey become the property sets BimCraft shows in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, and that \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel checks\u003C/span\u003E use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase quantities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport base quantities\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E and schedules use them (lengths, areas, volumes) instead of guessing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERooms and areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport rooms in 3D views\u003C/strong\u003E (or the Rooms / Spaces option)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERooms arrive as spaces: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E to see them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport linked files as separate IFCs\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach link becomes its own file; open them together with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model\u003C/span\u003E and they line up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoordinate base\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShared coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E (or the survey point)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file then carries its place on the map (IfcMapConversion), so Measure › Location, map layers on the ground and point clouds placed by map coordinates work. The project base point keeps the model near the origin but without its map place.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit walls and columns by level\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn, when you take off quantities per storey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach storey&#039;s part is its own element.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model in 3D, with storeys taken from the project&#039;s levels and the project information in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Cstrong\u003EExported from\u003C/strong\u003E names the exporter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-export-keeps-and-what-it-does-not\"\u003EWhat the export keeps, and what it does not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKept\u003C/strong\u003E: geometry, storeys, element kinds, names, types, most parameters (as property sets), base quantities when asked for, rooms as spaces, and the map place with shared coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot kept\u003C/strong\u003E: views, sheets, annotation, families as editable families, worksets and design options. An IFC file is a snapshot of the model&#039;s elements, not the project. For the sheets, export DWG and open it in the 2D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParameters missing in BimCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E Turn on the property set options above and export again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERooms missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Turn on the rooms option, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving as \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose plain IFC, or unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo map place.\u003C/strong\u003E Export with shared coordinates; check that the project&#039;s survey point is set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting edits to flow back.\u003C/strong\u003E Edits you save in BimCraft go into a new IFC file, not into the project file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Turn a project file into an IFC file you can orbit, inspect, check and edit in BimCraft. BimCraft does not read the 3D model inside an .rvt file yet, so the export happens in the program that made the file (Revit); BimCraft opens the result. Steps Open the project in the program that made it. BimCraft's card names the release it was saved in. Open a 3D view that shows what you want to export. Choose File \u003E Export \u003E IFC. In the export dialog choose a setup (or Modify setup…) with the settings below. Choose a folder and file name, and export. In BimCraft, click Choose a file and choose the new .ifc file. Linked models exported as their own files open into the same scene with Add a model. The settings that open best here Setting in the exporter Choose Why IFC version IFC4 Reference View for looking, checking and taking off; IFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0 when others need IFC2X3 Both open fully. IFC4 Design Transfer View opens too, with more exact shapes, but the file is larger and slower. File type IFC (plain) BimCraft does not open .ifczip or .ifcxml. Phase / visible elements The phase you mean, and Export only elements visible in view when you set up the 3D view for it Leaves out what you hid on purpose. Property sets Export Revit property sets and Export IFC common property sets They become the property sets BimCraft shows in Properties, and that Find, Color and Model checks use. Base quantities Export base quantities Quantity totals and schedules use them (lengths, areas, volumes) instead of guessing. Rooms and areas Export rooms in 3D views (or the Rooms / Spaces option) Rooms arrive as spaces: click Spaces to see them. Linked files Export linked files as separate IFCs Each link becomes its own file; open them together with Add a model and they line up. Coordinate base Shared coordinates (or the survey point) The file then carries its place on the map (IfcMapConversion), so Measure › Location, map layers on the ground and point clouds placed by map coordinates work. The project base point keeps the model near the origin but without its map place. Split walls and columns by level On, when you take off quantities per storey Each storey's part is its own element. What you should see The model in 3D, with storeys taken from the project's levels and the project information in the File panel. Exported from names the exporter. What the export keeps, and what it does not Kept: geometry, storeys, element kinds, names, types, most parameters (as property sets), base quantities when asked for, rooms as spaces, and the map place with shared coordinates. Not kept: views, sheets, annotation, families as editable families, worksets and design options. An IFC file is a snapshot of the model's elements, not the project. For the sheets, export DWG and open it in the 2D view. Common mistakes Parameters missing in BimCraft. Turn on the property set options above and export again. Rooms missing. Turn on the rooms option, then click Spaces. Saving as .ifczip. Choose plain IFC, or unzip the .ifczip. No map place. Export with shared coordinates; check that the project's survey point is set. Expecting edits to flow back. Edits you save in BimCraft go into a new IFC file, not into the project file."},{"slug":"pln-project-files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open .pln and .pla project files","summary":"What BimCraft does with .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn files today, and how to export IFC and DWG from them so the model and drawings open here.","section":"tasks","order":72,"keywords":["pln","pla","tpl","bpn","project file","archive","template","backup","ifc export","dwg export","translator","zones","layouts"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-opens-today","text":"What opens today","level":2},{"id":"export-ifc-the-model","text":"Export IFC (the model)","level":2},{"id":"export-dwg-the-drawings","text":"Export DWG (the drawings)","level":2},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGet a project saved as \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E (project), \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E (archive with its library), \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E (template) or \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E (backup) into BimCraft. These are Archicad&#039;s own files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-opens-today\"\u003EWhat opens today\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe project files themselves do not open yet.\u003C/strong\u003E Their format is not published, and BimCraft does not read it yet. When you open one, BimCraft says so and points here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft checks the file&#039;s first bytes.\u003C/strong\u003E Archicad projects from version 17 on start with the same short signature, so BimCraft can tell you for certain that a file is an Archicad project. A file with an Archicad name that does not start that way has probably been renamed, is damaged, or comes from an older version. The signature does not tell a project, an archive and a template apart.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn IFC export opens fully\u003C/strong\u003E: the model in 3D, storeys, zones as spaces, element data, property sets and classifications, with every BimCraft tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA DWG export opens\u003C/strong\u003E in the 2D view, with its layers and layouts, and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E. To edit it, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr bring the whole project as one zip, with its views, layouts and property groups: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-bundle\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-bundle\"\u003EOpen a project bundle\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-ifc-the-model\"\u003EExport IFC (the model)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project in Archicad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Save As\u003C/strong\u003E, and pick \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E as the file type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an IFC translator (Archicad&#039;s name for a set of IFC export settings). One for \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC4 Reference View\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0\u003C/strong\u003E suits BimCraft. IFC4X3 opens too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what to export: the entire project, or only the elements visible on the stories you are looking at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E in BimCraft and choose the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck these in the translator&#039;s settings before you export:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZones\u003C/strong\u003E: export them, so rooms arrive as spaces. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E in BimCraft to see them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperties and classifications\u003C/strong\u003E: export them, so they show in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E and can be used in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, not \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E. BimCraft opens plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-dwg-the-drawings\"\u003EExport DWG (the drawings)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the plan, section or layout you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Save As\u003C/strong\u003E, and pick \u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E as the file type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a DXF-DWG translator, and save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a whole set of sheets, save the layouts from the Layout Book, or publish them as DWG.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E in BimCraft. Drawings linked by the export open with it when they are in the same folder; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003ELinked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is an Archicad project: its first bytes say so.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Export IFC or DWG as above, and open that file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is named like an Archicad file but does not start like one.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Check where the file came from: it may be another kind of file with a new name (try opening it under its real extension), a copy that did not finish, or a project from a version before 17. Open it in Archicad if you can, then export IFC or DWG.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe model opens without rooms.\u003C/strong\u003E Zones were not exported. Turn them on in the IFC translator and export again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings are not cut.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft says so under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. Export again; if it stays, open the model in the program that made it and check the openings there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText on the DWG looks different.\u003C/strong\u003E The export may keep fonts BimCraft does not have; BimCraft draws text in a plain sans-serif font.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Get a project saved as .pln (project), .pla (archive with its library), .tpl (template) or .bpn (backup) into BimCraft. These are Archicad's own files. What opens today The project files themselves do not open yet. Their format is not published, and BimCraft does not read it yet. When you open one, BimCraft says so and points here. BimCraft checks the file's first bytes. Archicad projects from version 17 on start with the same short signature, so BimCraft can tell you for certain that a file is an Archicad project. A file with an Archicad name that does not start that way has probably been renamed, is damaged, or comes from an older version. The signature does not tell a project, an archive and a template apart. An IFC export opens fully: the model in 3D, storeys, zones as spaces, element data, property sets and classifications, with every BimCraft tool. A DWG export opens in the 2D view, with its layers and layouts, and in 3D. To edit it, use Edit in CadCraft. Or bring the whole project as one zip, with its views, layouts and property groups: see Open a project bundle. Export IFC (the model) Open the project in Archicad. Choose File \u003E Save As, and pick IFC as the file type. Pick an IFC translator (Archicad's name for a set of IFC export settings). One for IFC4 Reference View or IFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0 suits BimCraft. IFC4X3 opens too. Choose what to export: the entire project, or only the elements visible on the stories you are looking at. Save, then click Choose a file in BimCraft and choose the .ifc file. Check these in the translator's settings before you export: Zones: export them, so rooms arrive as spaces. Click Spaces in BimCraft to see them. Properties and classifications: export them, so they show in Properties and can be used in Find, Color and Quantity totals. Plain .ifc, not .ifczip or .ifcxml. BimCraft opens plain .ifc only. Export DWG (the drawings) Open the plan, section or layout you want. Choose File \u003E Save As, and pick DWG as the file type. Pick a DXF-DWG translator, and save. For a whole set of sheets, save the layouts from the Layout Book, or publish them as DWG. Open the .dwg in BimCraft. Drawings linked by the export open with it when they are in the same folder; see Linked drawings and pictures. Troubleshooting \"… is an Archicad project: its first bytes say so.\" Export IFC or DWG as above, and open that file. \"… is named like an Archicad file but does not start like one.\" Check where the file came from: it may be another kind of file with a new name (try opening it under its real extension), a copy that did not finish, or a project from a version before 17. Open it in Archicad if you can, then export IFC or DWG. The model opens without rooms. Zones were not exported. Turn them on in the IFC translator and export again. Openings are not cut. BimCraft says so under Not drawn yet in the File panel. Export again; if it stays, open the model in the program that made it and check the openings there. Text on the DWG looks different. The export may keep fonts BimCraft does not have; BimCraft draws text in a plain sans-serif font. See also Get a project file into 3D through IFC and What BimCraft reads from each format."},{"slug":"open-project-bundle","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open a project bundle","summary":"A project bundle is one zip with a model, its views, its layouts and its property groups. Open it, show a view with its storey and layers, open a layout, and see properties in the project's own groups.","section":"tasks","order":73,"keywords":["bundle","project bundle","ccbundle","zip","views","view map","layouts","layout book","layer combination","property groups","standards","pln","template"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"open-a-bundle","text":"Open a bundle","level":2},{"id":"show-a-view","text":"Show a view","level":2},{"id":"open-a-layout","text":"Open a layout","level":2},{"id":"see-properties-in-the-project-s-groups","text":"See properties in the project's groups","level":2},{"id":"standards-and-notes","text":"Standards and notes","level":2},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a \u003Cstrong\u003Eproject bundle\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E.ccbundle.zip\u003C/code\u003E) and work with the project the way it was organised: its views, its layouts and its property groups, on top of the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bundle holds:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe model as IFC,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe layouts as PDF and DWG,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea list of what IFC leaves out: the views (each with its storey, layer combination and scale), the layouts and the drawings placed on them, property definitions in their groups, classifications, and standards such as layers, pen sets, line types, fills, surfaces and composites.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBundles are written by an open-source script that runs inside Archicad and asks Archicad itself for these lists. The script is being tested and is not published yet. BimCraft never reads \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E files directly; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-bundle\"\u003EOpen a bundle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E, or drop the \u003Ccode\u003E.ccbundle.zip\u003C/code\u003E on BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model opens as any IFC does, and the \u003Cstrong\u003EProject bundle\u003C/strong\u003E section appears on the left, under the file&#039;s facts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing is uploaded: the bundle is read on your computer, one file at a time, so a large model is not copied twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-a-view\"\u003EShow a view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EViews\u003C/strong\u003E, views are grouped by the folders they had in the project. Click a view&#039;s name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA floor plan\u003C/strong\u003E shows only its storey, seen from the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIts layer combination\u003C/strong\u003E hides the elements on the layers that combination hides. The status line says how many elements that hid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIts scale\u003C/strong\u003E is shown beside the name and in the status line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E shows every storey.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo show everything again, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E and tick every storey.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft finds the layers through the IFC file&#039;s layer assignments. If the IFC was exported without them, a layer combination hides nothing, and the status line says 0 elements hidden.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-layout\"\u003EOpen a layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ELayouts\u003C/strong\u003E, each layout shows its number, name, paper size and the views placed on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDF\u003C/strong\u003E saves the layout&#039;s PDF from the bundle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E opens the layout&#039;s drawing in the 2D view. Click any view to come back to the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA layout with neither says \u003Cstrong\u003ENo file in the bundle\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-properties-in-the-project-s-groups\"\u003ESee properties in the project&#039;s groups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick an element. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperty groups\u003C/strong\u003E shows its properties under the groups the project defined, matched through the property set and name the definition gives for IFC, or else by its name. The rest stay in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E as usual. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperty definitions\u003C/strong\u003E lists every definition with its group, type and IFC name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"standards-and-notes\"\u003EStandards and notes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStandards\u003C/strong\u003E counts the layers, layer combinations, pen sets, line types, fills, surfaces, composites and materials in the bundle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENotes\u003C/strong\u003E lists what the script could not get from that version of Archicad, any field BimCraft could not read, and any file the bundle names but does not hold.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is a zip but not a project bundle.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The zip has no \u003Ccode\u003Emanifest.json\u003C/code\u003E. Unzip it and open the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E inside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is a newer project bundle than this BimCraft reads.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Update BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… does not hold the model its manifest names.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The zip was cut short or edited. Make the bundle again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA view shows every storey.\u003C/strong\u003E Its storey is not in the model&#039;s IFC (the status line says so). Check that the IFC export included that storey.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/two-bim-programs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/two-bim-programs\"\u003EWorking between two BIM programs\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open a project bundle (.ccbundle.zip) and work with the project the way it was organised: its views, its layouts and its property groups, on top of the model. A bundle holds: the model as IFC, the layouts as PDF and DWG, a list of what IFC leaves out: the views (each with its storey, layer combination and scale), the layouts and the drawings placed on them, property definitions in their groups, classifications, and standards such as layers, pen sets, line types, fills, surfaces and composites. Bundles are written by an open-source script that runs inside Archicad and asks Archicad itself for these lists. The script is being tested and is not published yet. BimCraft never reads .pln or .tpl files directly; see Open .pln and .pla project files. Open a bundle Click Choose a file, or drop the .ccbundle.zip on BimCraft. The model opens as any IFC does, and the Project bundle section appears on the left, under the file's facts. Nothing is uploaded: the bundle is read on your computer, one file at a time, so a large model is not copied twice. Show a view Under Views, views are grouped by the folders they had in the project. Click a view's name: A floor plan shows only its storey, seen from the top. Its layer combination hides the elements on the layers that combination hides. The status line says how many elements that hid. Its scale is shown beside the name and in the status line. A 3D view shows every storey. To show everything again, use Show all in Show and tick every storey. BimCraft finds the layers through the IFC file's layer assignments. If the IFC was exported without them, a layer combination hides nothing, and the status line says 0 elements hidden. Open a layout Under Layouts, each layout shows its number, name, paper size and the views placed on it. PDF saves the layout's PDF from the bundle. DWG opens the layout's drawing in the 2D view. Click any view to come back to the model. A layout with neither says No file in the bundle. See properties in the project's groups Pick an element. Property groups shows its properties under the groups the project defined, matched through the property set and name the definition gives for IFC, or else by its name. The rest stay in Properties as usual. Property definitions lists every definition with its group, type and IFC name. Standards and notes Standards counts the layers, layer combinations, pen sets, line types, fills, surfaces, composites and materials in the bundle. Notes lists what the script could not get from that version of Archicad, any field BimCraft could not read, and any file the bundle names but does not hold. Troubleshooting \"… is a zip but not a project bundle.\" The zip has no manifest.json. Unzip it and open the .ifc inside. \"… is a newer project bundle than this BimCraft reads.\" Update BimCraft. \"… does not hold the model its manifest names.\" The zip was cut short or edited. Make the bundle again. A view shows every storey. Its storey is not in the model's IFC (the status line says so). Check that the IFC export included that storey. See also Working between two BIM programs and What BimCraft reads from each format."},{"slug":"open-project-folder","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open a whole project folder","summary":"Point BimCraft at a project folder to see every model, its backups and links at once, and let the Start here box suggest which file to work on.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["folder","directory","project folder","network drive","server","start here","which file","backups","links","xrefs","recent folders","drag folder"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"use-the-start-here-box","text":"Use the Start here box","level":2},{"id":"open-files-from-the-report","text":"Open files from the report","level":2},{"id":"reopen-a-folder-later","text":"Reopen a folder later","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a whole project folder, such as the one you were handed for a job, and let BimCraft sort it out: which models are there, which copies are backups, what links to what, which files are damaged, and which file you most likely want to open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the few kilobytes each model card needs are read, so a folder on a network drive or a synced cloud folder is fine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and click \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen\u003C/strong\u003E, depending on your system).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EView files\u003C/strong\u003E when the browser asks &quot;Let site view files?&quot;. This only lets the tab read the folder; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line counts: &quot;Listing the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and confirm. Firefox may ask you to confirm that the page can read the folder&#039;s files; they still stay on your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and click \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E. Despite the word, nothing leaves your Mac: the files are only handed to the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also \u003Cstrong\u003Edrag the folder\u003C/strong\u003E from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E onto the page. A dragged folder opens without any permission prompt in every browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe folder report fills the middle:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe folder name, counts of models, CAD drawings, IFC models and files, and the total size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERed or amber notes if any files are incomplete or any linked files are missing from the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box: the file BimCraft suggests you open, with the reasons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E: one row per model, with its backups grouped under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC models\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, when the folder has any.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the left, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E panel sums it up; \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E says &quot;None&quot; when every copy is sound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-start-here-box\"\u003EUse the Start here box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box names one file and says why, for example:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Newest saved work in this folder (Mar 3, 2025).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003EConsultant.rvt\u003C/em\u003E is linked into other models, so it is background, not the file to edit.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003ETower.rvt\u003C/em\u003E looks like the obvious choice, but it is damaged; its last good copy is from …&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is a central model: in Revit, open it with &quot;Create New Local&quot; and work in the local copy.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the reasons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen its card\u003C/span\u003E to read that file&#039;s card, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow it in the list\u003C/span\u003E to find its row in \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck with whoever last worked on the project before you rely on it. The box says so too: it is a best guess from names, dates and links.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-files-from-the-report\"\u003EOpen files from the report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a model&#039;s name to open its card. The card checks each link against the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name at the top of the card (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left) to return.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any IFC file under \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC models\u003C/strong\u003E to open it in 3D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any DWG or DXF under \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E to open it in 2D. Its linked drawings and pictures are found in the folder automatically.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reopen-a-folder-later\"\u003EReopen a folder later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFolders you opened with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E or by dragging are listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E on the left. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name to open it again without choosing it. After a browser restart, Chrome or Edge asks once more; choose &quot;Allow on every visit&quot; to stop it asking. The list is kept in this browser only, never sent anywhere. Click × to forget a folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis browser cannot remember folders between visits. Choose the folder again with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E, or drag it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking a folder that is too big.\u003C/strong\u003E Over 20,000 files: &quot;This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.&quot; Pick the project folder, not the whole drive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking the parent of several projects.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box then chooses across projects. Pick one project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevit&#039;s own folders look ignored.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E folders are housekeeping and are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting a model to open in 3D from its card.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file is a card only. Open its IFC export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPhones and tablets.\u003C/strong\u003E Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders at all. Use a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/check-folder-health\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/check-folder-health\"\u003ECheck a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/folder-roles\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/folder-roles\"\u003EFolder report: roles, statuses and links\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open a whole project folder, such as the one you were handed for a job, and let BimCraft sort it out: which models are there, which copies are backups, what links to what, which files are damaged, and which file you most likely want to open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read, so a folder on a network drive or a synced cloud folder is fine. Steps Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and click Select (or Upload or Open, depending on your system). Click View files when the browser asks \"Let site view files?\". This only lets the tab read the folder; nothing is uploaded. Wait while the status line counts: \"Listing the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and confirm. Firefox may ask you to confirm that the page can read the folder's files; they still stay on your computer. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and click Upload. Despite the word, nothing leaves your Mac: the files are only handed to the tab. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". You can also drag the folder from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager onto the page. A dragged folder opens without any permission prompt in every browser. What you should see The folder report fills the middle: The folder name, counts of models, CAD drawings, IFC models and files, and the total size. Red or amber notes if any files are incomplete or any linked files are missing from the folder. The Start here box: the file BimCraft suggests you open, with the reasons. Models: one row per model, with its backups grouped under it. Linked and referenced files, IFC models, CAD drawings and Set aside, when the folder has any. On the left, the Folder panel sums it up; Problems says \"None\" when every copy is sound. Use the Start here box The box names one file and says why, for example: \"Newest saved work in this folder (Mar 3, 2025).\" \"Consultant.rvt is linked into other models, so it is background, not the file to edit.\" \"Tower.rvt looks like the obvious choice, but it is damaged; its last good copy is from …\" \"This is a central model: in Revit, open it with \"Create New Local\" and work in the local copy.\" Read the reasons. Click Open its card to read that file's card, or Show it in the list to find its row in Models. Check with whoever last worked on the project before you rely on it. The box says so too: it is a best guess from names, dates and links. Open files from the report Click a model's name to open its card. The card checks each link against the folder. Click Back to and the folder name at the top of the card (or Back to the folder on the left) to return. Click any IFC file under IFC models to open it in 3D. Click any DWG or DXF under CAD drawings to open it in 2D. Its linked drawings and pictures are found in the folder automatically. Reopen a folder later Folders you opened with Open a folder or by dragging are listed under Recent folders on the left. Click Reopen and the folder name to open it again without choosing it. After a browser restart, Chrome or Edge asks once more; choose \"Allow on every visit\" to stop it asking. The list is kept in this browser only, never sent anywhere. Click × to forget a folder. This browser cannot remember folders between visits. Choose the folder again with Open a folder, or drag it in. Common mistakes Picking a folder that is too big. Over 20,000 files: \"This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.\" Pick the project folder, not the whole drive. Picking the parent of several projects. The Start here box then chooses across projects. Pick one project. Revit's own folders look ignored. *_backup, *_log and Revit_temp folders are housekeeping and are listed under Set aside, not read. Expecting a model to open in 3D from its card. A project file is a card only. Open its IFC export. Phones and tablets. Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders at all. Use a computer. See Check a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers and Folder report: roles, statuses and links."},{"slug":"check-folder-health","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers","summary":"Use the folder report to find cut-off model files, pick the newest complete backup, see which links are missing, and spot lock and temp files.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["health check","damaged","corrupt","incomplete","truncated","backup","missing links","xref missing","audit","leftovers","lock files","dwl","bak","handover"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-damaged-copies","text":"Steps: damaged copies","level":2},{"id":"what-the-health-check-does","text":"What the health check does","level":2},{"id":"steps-missing-links","text":"Steps: missing links","level":2},{"id":"steps-leftovers","text":"Steps: leftovers","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck a project folder before you hand it over or start work on it: is every model complete, which backup is the last good one, and are all the linked files there?\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-damaged-copies\"\u003ESteps: damaged copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project folder. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the notes under the folder name. &quot;2 files are incomplete or unreadable. See the models below.&quot; means there is something to look at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E, look at the \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E column. \u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E is fine. An amber &quot;1 incomplete&quot; means one copy is bad but another is sound. A red \u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E means no sound copy was found.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EAll … copies\u003C/strong\u003E under a model with a problem. It lists the main file and every numbered backup, newest first, with size, date and status.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the note above the list, for example &quot;Tower.rvt is incomplete (183.2 MB, should be at least 214.0 MB). The card shows the newest complete copy, Tower.0012.rvt (Mar 3, 2025).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the copy you want to open. Only complete copies can be clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-health-check-does\"\u003EWhat the health check does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery copy of every model gets a quick check of its header, 512 bytes each, so even a folder of large models is checked in seconds. A file whose own index points past its end was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still &quot;online only&quot; looks the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card for each model is read from the main file when it is sound, and from the newest sound backup when it is not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-missing-links\"\u003ESteps: missing links\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for the note &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E linked files are not in this folder.&quot; under the folder name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/strong\u003E. Missing links are listed first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E column for each link:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E: found at the path the model saved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E: a file with the same name is elsewhere in the folder. Check it is the right one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E: a keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer, so they are not expected in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E: not found anywhere in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead \u003Cstrong\u003EUsed by\u003C/strong\u003E to see which models link each file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft looks for each link first by its relative path, then by the end of its saved path (so \u003Ccode\u003E\\\\server\\jobs\\Tower\\Cad\\X-Refs\\site.dwg\u003C/code\u003E is found at \u003Ccode\u003EX-Refs/site.dwg\u003C/code\u003E when you picked the \u003Ccode\u003ECad\u003C/code\u003E folder), then by file name anywhere in the folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-leftovers\"\u003ESteps: leftovers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E at the end of the report.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the three lines that may appear:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHousekeeping folders (\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E): not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELock, log and backup leftovers: files such as \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E. These are left by programs that crashed or were open when the folder was copied. They are usually safe to delete once nobody has the files open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther files: everything else BimCraft does not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDeleting the damaged main file before saving a good backup.\u003C/strong\u003E Copy the newest complete backup first, then deal with the damaged file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrusting &quot;Same name found&quot; blindly.\u003C/strong\u003E Two different drawings can share a name. Open both if in doubt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThinking a missing library file is a problem.\u003C/strong\u003E Keynote and assembly code tables live with the program on each computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file count differs between browsers.\u003C/strong\u003E In Firefox and Safari, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E hands over every file, including those inside housekeeping folders, so the count (and the 20,000-file limit) includes them. Dragging the folder in skips them, as Chrome and Edge do. The report is otherwise the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Check a project folder before you hand it over or start work on it: is every model complete, which backup is the last good one, and are all the linked files there? Steps: damaged copies Open the project folder. See Open a whole project folder. Read the notes under the folder name. \"2 files are incomplete or unreadable. See the models below.\" means there is something to look at. In Models, look at the Status column. Complete is fine. An amber \"1 incomplete\" means one copy is bad but another is sound. A red Incomplete means no sound copy was found. Open All … copies under a model with a problem. It lists the main file and every numbered backup, newest first, with size, date and status. Read the note above the list, for example \"Tower.rvt is incomplete (183.2 MB, should be at least 214.0 MB). The card shows the newest complete copy, Tower.0012.rvt (Mar 3, 2025).\" Click the copy you want to open. Only complete copies can be clicked. What the health check does Every copy of every model gets a quick check of its header, 512 bytes each, so even a folder of large models is checked in seconds. A file whose own index points past its end was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still \"online only\" looks the same. The card for each model is read from the main file when it is sound, and from the newest sound backup when it is not. Steps: missing links Look for the note \"n linked files are not in this folder.\" under the folder name. Scroll to Linked and referenced files. Missing links are listed first. Read the Where column for each link: In this folder: found at the path the model saved. Same name found: a file with the same name is elsewhere in the folder. Check it is the right one. Library file: a keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer, so they are not expected in the folder. Not in this folder: not found anywhere in the folder. Read Used by to see which models link each file. BimCraft looks for each link first by its relative path, then by the end of its saved path (so \\\\server\\jobs\\Tower\\Cad\\X-Refs\\site.dwg is found at X-Refs/site.dwg when you picked the Cad folder), then by file name anywhere in the folder. Steps: leftovers Scroll to Set aside at the end of the report. Read the three lines that may appear: Housekeeping folders (*_backup, *_log, Revit_temp): not read. Lock, log and backup leftovers: files such as .dwl, .dwl2, .bak, .err, .log, .tmp, .sv$. These are left by programs that crashed or were open when the folder was copied. They are usually safe to delete once nobody has the files open. Other files: everything else BimCraft does not read. Common mistakes Deleting the damaged main file before saving a good backup. Copy the newest complete backup first, then deal with the damaged file. Trusting \"Same name found\" blindly. Two different drawings can share a name. Open both if in doubt. Thinking a missing library file is a problem. Keynote and assembly code tables live with the program on each computer. The file count differs between browsers. In Firefox and Safari, Open a folder hands over every file, including those inside housekeeping folders, so the count (and the 20,000-file limit) includes them. Dragging the folder in skips them, as Chrome and Edge do. The report is otherwise the same."},{"slug":"view-dwg","scope":"bimcraft","title":"View a DWG or DXF drawing","summary":"Open a DWG or DXF, move around model space and sheets, read coordinates, and see what is not drawn yet.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["dynamic blocks","visibility states","dwg","dxf","cad","drawing","2d","view","viewer","pan","zoom","model space","paper space","layout","coordinates","open dwg"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"dynamic-blocks","text":"Dynamic blocks","level":2},{"id":"not-drawn-yet","text":"Not drawn yet","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a CAD drawing in 2D, move around it, switch between model space and its sheets, and read coordinates. BimCraft shows DWG and DXF drawings; it does not edit or save them. To change one, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E and the drawing opens in the CadCraft drafting app in a new tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E, or drop the drawing onto the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the drawing…&quot;. Large drawings take a few seconds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the tabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E first, then one tab per sheet, in the drawing&#039;s own order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab to see that sheet: the paper, with the model shown through its viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E to go back to model space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the drawing and read the X and Y coordinates in the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drawing, fitted to the window. Colors follow the drawing; white lines (color 7) draw in the theme&#039;s ink, and pale colors are darkened on the light theme so they stay readable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left: file, size, format (for example &quot;AutoCAD 2018 (AC1032)&quot;), units, entity and layer counts, the number of sheets, and linked drawings and equipment when there are any.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list on the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right: how to move around, linked drawings and images, and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any mouse button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel; the point under the pointer stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, scrolling with two fingers \u003Cstrong\u003Ezooms\u003C/strong\u003E, it does not pan. Pinch zooms too. To pan, click and drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger to pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings: tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole sheet, and use a mouse or trackpad to zoom in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dynamic-blocks\"\u003EDynamic blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dynamic block is drawn as the drawing shows it: each placed block in the visibility state the drawing was saved with (&quot;Plan&quot; or &quot;Front&quot;, &quot;Single bowl&quot; or &quot;Double bowl&quot;). On the right, \u003Cstrong\u003EDynamic blocks\u003C/strong\u003E lists every one placed in model space: how many are placed, the states they show, and every state the block has. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, click a placed block to see \u003Cstrong\u003EDynamic block\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EState shown\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOther states\u003C/strong\u003E in Properties. BimCraft does not switch states.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-drawn-yet\"\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome kinds of objects are not drawn in this build. They are counted under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E on the right (&quot;These parts of the drawing are skipped for now, not guessed at.&quot;), so you know something is missing. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solid fills, text and multiline text, blocks with attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, images and viewports are drawn. 3D faces, polyface meshes and polygon meshes are drawn as their edges seen from above; switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E to see them as surfaces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA binary DXF does not open: BimCraft says so. Save it again as an ASCII DXF, or as a DWG, from the program that made it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing looks empty.\u003C/strong\u003E Some layers may be off or frozen in the drawing itself; they start unticked in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E. If the status says &quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.&quot;, there is nothing in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA sheet shows an empty frame.\u003C/strong\u003E The sheet may rely on linked drawings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003EFind linked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText looks a little different.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft draws text in a narrow sans-serif font, not the drawing&#039;s own fonts, so text can be a little wider or narrower than in the program that made it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The bracket at the end names the file&#039;s format code. Save it from the program that made it as an older DWG or as DXF, and try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to measure, draw or edit.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft only views drawings. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E to open the same file in the CadCraft drafting app, which edits it and saves a copy as DWG or DXF. Your original file is not changed, and linked drawings stay in BimCraft. The button shows only where CadCraft is open to you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\"\u003EWork with layers and sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/print-view\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/print-view\"\u003ECheck a drawing in print view\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open a CAD drawing in 2D, move around it, switch between model space and its sheets, and read coordinates. BimCraft shows DWG and DXF drawings; it does not edit or save them. To change one, click Edit in CadCraft and the drawing opens in the CadCraft drafting app in a new tab. Steps Click Choose a file, or drop the drawing onto the page. Choose a .dwg or .dxf file. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the drawing…\". Large drawings take a few seconds. Look at the tabs above the drawing: Model first, then one tab per sheet, in the drawing's own order. Click a sheet tab to see that sheet: the paper, with the model shown through its viewports. Click Model to go back to model space. Move the pointer over the drawing and read the X and Y coordinates in the corner. What you should see The drawing, fitted to the window. Colors follow the drawing; white lines (color 7) draw in the theme's ink, and pale colors are darkened on the light theme so they stay readable. The Drawing panel on the left: file, size, format (for example \"AutoCAD 2018 (AC1032)\"), units, entity and layer counts, the number of sheets, and linked drawings and equipment when there are any. The Layers list on the left. On the right: how to move around, linked drawings and images, and Not drawn yet. Moving around To Do this Pan Drag with any mouse button Zoom Scroll the wheel; the point under the pointer stays put Fit Double-click, press F, or click Fit On a trackpad, scrolling with two fingers zooms, it does not pan. Pinch zooms too. To pan, click and drag. Drag with one finger to pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings: tap Fit to see the whole sheet, and use a mouse or trackpad to zoom in. Dynamic blocks A dynamic block is drawn as the drawing shows it: each placed block in the visibility state the drawing was saved with (\"Plan\" or \"Front\", \"Single bowl\" or \"Double bowl\"). On the right, Dynamic blocks lists every one placed in model space: how many are placed, the states they show, and every state the block has. In 3D, click a placed block to see Dynamic block, State shown and Other states in Properties. BimCraft does not switch states. Not drawn yet Some kinds of objects are not drawn in this build. They are counted under Not drawn yet on the right (\"These parts of the drawing are skipped for now, not guessed at.\"), so you know something is missing. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solid fills, text and multiline text, blocks with attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, images and viewports are drawn. 3D faces, polyface meshes and polygon meshes are drawn as their edges seen from above; switch to 3D to see them as surfaces. A binary DXF does not open: BimCraft says so. Save it again as an ASCII DXF, or as a DWG, from the program that made it. Common mistakes The drawing looks empty. Some layers may be off or frozen in the drawing itself; they start unticked in Layers. Click All on. If the status says \"name is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.\", there is nothing in it. A sheet shows an empty frame. The sheet may rely on linked drawings. See Find linked drawings and pictures. Text looks a little different. BimCraft draws text in a narrow sans-serif font, not the drawing's own fonts, so text can be a little wider or narrower than in the program that made it. \"name could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet.\" The bracket at the end names the file's format code. Save it from the program that made it as an older DWG or as DXF, and try again. Expecting to measure, draw or edit. BimCraft only views drawings. Click Edit in CadCraft to open the same file in the CadCraft drafting app, which edits it and saves a copy as DWG or DXF. Your original file is not changed, and linked drawings stay in BimCraft. The button shows only where CadCraft is open to you. Next See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D Work with layers and sheets Check a drawing in print view"},{"slug":"dwg-in-3d","scope":"bimcraft","title":"See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D","summary":"Switch a drawing's model space to 3D to see elevations, thickness, 3D faces, meshes and blocks, orbit round it, click an object to see its properties, and see what is not drawn yet.","section":"tasks","order":105,"keywords":["3d","dwg","dxf","drawing","model space","orbit","section","cut","clipping","pick","select","properties","attributes","handle","elevation","thickness","extrusion","3dface","polyface","mesh","3d polyline","blocks","views","perspective","parallel","extents","acis","solids"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"cut-it-with-a-section-plane","text":"Cut it with a section plane","level":2},{"id":"see-an-object-s-properties","text":"See an object's properties","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"what-is-drawn-in-3d","text":"What is drawn in 3D","level":2},{"id":"in-the-2d-view-only","text":"In the 2D view only","level":2},{"id":"not-drawn-yet","text":"Not drawn yet","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at a drawing&#039;s model space in 3D. Lines and polylines sit at their real heights, objects with a thickness stand up as walls, and 3D faces, meshes and 3D blocks show as surfaces you can orbit round. The sheets stay 2D.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E in the bar above the drawing, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait a moment the first time: the status line says &quot;Building the drawing in 3D…&quot;, then how many lines and faces it shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom (see the table below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a view in the second bar: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E to switch between a perspective and a parallel view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to see the whole drawing again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E again, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E or a sheet tab to go back to 2D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EModel space in 3D, Z up, in the drawing&#039;s own colors. Faces are a shade lighter than their edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers you turn off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E disappear from the 3D view too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right: the number of lines and faces, the size of the drawing in X, Y and Z, \u003Cstrong\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-it-with-a-section-plane\"\u003ECut it with a section plane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The bar under it works as it does for IFC models (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E): cut from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E, drag or type the offset — in the drawing&#039;s own units here — and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E to keep the other side. What the plane cuts away cannot be picked. Going back to 2D takes the cut away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-an-object-s-properties\"\u003ESee an object&#039;s properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an object in the 3D view. It turns the pick color and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows what the file says about it: its type, layer, color, linetype and handle, then its own data, such as a line&#039;s start, end and length, a circle&#039;s center and radius, a 3D face&#039;s corners, or a block reference&#039;s block, insertion point, scale, rotation and attribute values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA block reference picks as a whole, the blocks nested in it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA click a few pixels from a line picks the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to pick more objects, or to take one out; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E then counts them by type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or a click on empty space, clears the pick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects on layers you turned off cannot be picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button, or with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel; it zooms toward what is under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee it all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELook from a side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face, edge or corner of the view cube in the bottom-right corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube can be swapped for an axis icon, or hidden, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move them together to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-drawn-in-3d\"\u003EWhat is drawn in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the drawing\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, 3D polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt their real heights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines, circles, arcs, ellipses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn their own plane, at their elevation, tilted when the drawing tilts them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThickness on lines, polylines, circles and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWalls of that height; circles are closed top and bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D faces, polyface meshes, polygon meshes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShaded surfaces with their visible edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlocks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlaced, turned and scaled in 3D, nested blocks too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir outlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlain 2D linework\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat, where it was drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-2d-view-only\"\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText, multiline text and the visible attributes of blocks are drawn in 3D, in their own plane at their own height, and a click on them picks them. Wide polylines show their width as a flat band. Multileaders, pictures and hatch patterns are counted under \u003Cstrong\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/strong\u003E: see them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab or the sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-drawn-yet\"\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolids and surfaces stored as ACIS bodies (3D solids, bodies, regions and surfaces) and subdivision meshes are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E with how many there are. They are skipped, not guessed at.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything looks flat.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing is 2D: its lines have no height. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E to check; a flat drawing shows as a line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA solid model shows nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Solids made with a solid modeler are ACIS bodies, listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E. Export the model as IFC or as 3D faces from the program that made it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing is a tiny cluster in the middle.\u003C/strong\u003E Something far away in model space, such as an old copy of a plan, makes the extents large. Zoom in with the wheel where you want to look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\"\u003EWork with layers and sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look at a drawing's model space in 3D. Lines and polylines sit at their real heights, objects with a thickness stand up as walls, and 3D faces, meshes and 3D blocks show as surfaces you can orbit round. The sheets stay 2D. Steps Open a DWG or DXF. See View a DWG or DXF drawing. Click 3D in the bar above the drawing, or press 3. Wait a moment the first time: the status line says \"Building the drawing in 3D…\", then how many lines and faces it shows. Orbit, pan and zoom (see the table below). Click a view in the second bar: Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left or Right. Click Perspective to switch between a perspective and a parallel view. Click Extents or press F to see the whole drawing again. Click 3D again, press 3, or click Model or a sheet tab to go back to 2D. What you should see Model space in 3D, Z up, in the drawing's own colors. Faces are a shade lighter than their edges. Layers you turn off in Layers disappear from the 3D view too. On the right: the number of lines and faces, the size of the drawing in X, Y and Z, In the 2D view only and Not drawn yet. Cut it with a section plane Click Section on the 3D bar, or press X. The bar under it works as it does for IFC models (see Cut an IFC model with a section plane): cut from the Top, Front or Side, drag or type the offset — in the drawing's own units here — and Flip to keep the other side. What the plane cuts away cannot be picked. Going back to 2D takes the cut away. See an object's properties Click an object in the 3D view. It turns the pick color and Properties shows what the file says about it: its type, layer, color, linetype and handle, then its own data, such as a line's start, end and length, a circle's center and radius, a 3D face's corners, or a block reference's block, insertion point, scale, rotation and attribute values. A block reference picks as a whole, the blocks nested in it too. A click a few pixels from a line picks the line. Hold Ctrl and click to pick more objects, or to take one out; Properties then counts them by type. Esc, or a click on empty space, clears the pick. Objects on layers you turned off cannot be picked. Moving around To Do this Orbit Drag with the middle button, or with the left button Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift and drag Zoom Scroll the wheel; it zooms toward what is under the pointer See it all Extents or F Look from a side Click a face, edge or corner of the view cube in the bottom-right corner The view cube can be swapped for an axis icon, or hidden, in Settings. See BimCraft settings. On a trackpad, swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom. Drag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move them together to pan. What is drawn in 3D In the drawing In 3D Lines, 3D polylines At their real heights Polylines, circles, arcs, ellipses In their own plane, at their elevation, tilted when the drawing tilts them Thickness on lines, polylines, circles and arcs Walls of that height; circles are closed top and bottom 3D faces, polyface meshes, polygon meshes Shaded surfaces with their visible edges Blocks Placed, turned and scaled in 3D, nested blocks too Hatches Their outlines Plain 2D linework Flat, where it was drawn In the 2D view only Text, multiline text and the visible attributes of blocks are drawn in 3D, in their own plane at their own height, and a click on them picks them. Wide polylines show their width as a flat band. Multileaders, pictures and hatch patterns are counted under In the 2D view only: see them on the Model tab or the sheets. Not drawn yet Solids and surfaces stored as ACIS bodies (3D solids, bodies, regions and surfaces) and subdivision meshes are listed under Not drawn yet with how many there are. They are skipped, not guessed at. Common mistakes Everything looks flat. The drawing is 2D: its lines have no height. Click Front to check; a flat drawing shows as a line. A solid model shows nothing. Solids made with a solid modeler are ACIS bodies, listed under Not drawn yet. Export the model as IFC or as 3D faces from the program that made it. The drawing is a tiny cluster in the middle. Something far away in model space, such as an old copy of a plan, makes the extents large. Zoom in with the wheel where you want to look. Next Work with layers and sheets Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"dwg-layers-sheets","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Work with layers and sheets","summary":"Turn drawing layers on and off, switch between model space and sheets, and understand how sheets show the model through their viewports.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["layers","layer on off","freeze","thaw","sheets","layouts","paper space","tabs","viewports","layer manager","la"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-layers","text":"Steps: layers","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"steps-sheets","text":"Steps: sheets","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-work-here","text":"How sheets work here","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClean up the view of a busy drawing by turning layers off, then look at the drawing&#039;s sheets one by one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-layers\"\u003ESteps: layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list on the left. Each layer has a checkbox, a color swatch and its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a layer to hide everything on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E to hide every layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick only the layers you want to see.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E to show everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers disappear and reappear at once, on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E and on every sheet. Layers that are off or frozen in the drawing itself start unticked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-sheets\"\u003ESteps: sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the row of tabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, then each sheet by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the sheet: the paper sits on a darker desk, and each viewport shows its part of the model at its own scale and angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom into a viewport to read detail. You are zooming the sheet, not the viewport, so the whole sheet moves together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E or double-click to see the whole sheet again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-work-here\"\u003EHow sheets work here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet (a layout, or paper space) holds a title block and viewports that look into model space. BimCraft draws each viewport clipped to its edge, at its own scale and twist, and hides the layers frozen in that viewport. Each sheet&#039;s first viewport is the sheet itself and is not drawn twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting layer changes to be saved.\u003C/strong\u003E Layer settings only affect the view, until you open another file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting per-viewport layer control.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list switches a layer everywhere. Layers frozen in one viewport by the drawing stay frozen there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for a layer search or layer states.\u003C/strong\u003E Not built yet. The list is in the drawing&#039;s order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to work inside a viewport.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to activate: BimCraft only shows the sheet. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E to look at model space directly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Clean up the view of a busy drawing by turning layers off, then look at the drawing's sheets one by one. Steps: layers Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Find the Layers list on the left. Each layer has a checkbox, a color swatch and its name. Untick a layer to hide everything on it. Click All off to hide every layer. Tick only the layers you want to see. Click All on to show everything again. What you should see Layers disappear and reappear at once, on Model and on every sheet. Layers that are off or frozen in the drawing itself start unticked. Steps: sheets Look at the row of tabs above the drawing: Model, then each sheet by name. Click a sheet tab. Look at the sheet: the paper sits on a darker desk, and each viewport shows its part of the model at its own scale and angle. Zoom into a viewport to read detail. You are zooming the sheet, not the viewport, so the whole sheet moves together. Click Fit or double-click to see the whole sheet again. How sheets work here A sheet (a layout, or paper space) holds a title block and viewports that look into model space. BimCraft draws each viewport clipped to its edge, at its own scale and twist, and hides the layers frozen in that viewport. Each sheet's first viewport is the sheet itself and is not drawn twice. Common mistakes Expecting layer changes to be saved. Layer settings only affect the view, until you open another file. Expecting per-viewport layer control. The Layers list switches a layer everywhere. Layers frozen in one viewport by the drawing stay frozen there. Looking for a layer search or layer states. Not built yet. The list is in the drawing's order. Expecting to work inside a viewport. There is nothing to activate: BimCraft only shows the sheet. Use Model to look at model space directly."},{"slug":"dwg-linked-files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find linked drawings and pictures","summary":"Show a drawing's linked drawings (xrefs) and title-block pictures, by opening its folder or adding the files by hand.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["xref","external reference","linked drawing","attach","overlay","image","picture","logo","title block","missing xref","etransmit","reference manager"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way-open-the-folder","text":"The easy way: open the folder","level":2},{"id":"the-other-way-add-files-by-hand","text":"The other way: add files by hand","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMake a drawing complete: show the drawings it links to (external references, or xrefs) and the pictures it uses, such as a logo in the title block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way-open-the-folder\"\u003EThe easy way: open the folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E and choose the folder that holds the drawing and its links, for example the whole \u003Ccode\u003ECad\u003C/code\u003E folder of a project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the folder report, scroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EImages\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft looks for each linked drawing by its name (&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E.dwg&quot;), in the same folder as the drawing first, then anywhere in the folder you opened. Pictures are found the same way, and also by a similar name: an eTransmit or Revit export often adds the project&#039;s name in front, so \u003Ccode\u003Elogo.png\u003C/code\u003E is found as \u003Ccode\u003ETower-logo-5425051.png\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-other-way-add-files-by-hand\"\u003EThe other way: add files by hand\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the drawing on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right, under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd linked drawings…\u003C/span\u003E. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EImages\u003C/strong\u003E, the button is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd pictures…\u003C/span\u003E; both open the same chooser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the linked \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E files and the picture files. You can choose several at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while BimCraft opens the drawing again with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach linked drawing and picture is listed with a label:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFound and drawn. The path under it says where it came from, or &quot;added by you&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShown, similar name\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture found by a similar name. Check it is the right one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot in the folder or the files you added. A missing picture shows as its empty frame.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETIFF cannot be shown in a browser\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe picture is a TIFF file, which browsers do not draw. Save it as PNG with the same name and add it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECould not be read\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file was found but could not be opened as a picture.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left shows &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E loaded&quot; for linked drawings. Linked drawings&#039; layers appear in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E as &quot;\u003Cem\u003Elink name\u003C/em\u003E|\u003Cem\u003Elayer\u003C/em\u003E&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening only the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;This drawing shows its own content only until its linked drawings are added. Opening the whole project folder finds them automatically.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder too small.\u003C/strong\u003E If the links live in a sibling folder, open the parent folder instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenamed links.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft matches linked drawings by name. A link renamed on disk is not found; add it by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinks of links.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft loads the drawings the open drawing links to. Drawings linked inside those are not looked for, so they usually do not show in this build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Make a drawing complete: show the drawings it links to (external references, or xrefs) and the pictures it uses, such as a logo in the title block. The easy way: open the folder Click Open a folder and choose the folder that holds the drawing and its links, for example the whole Cad folder of a project. In the folder report, scroll to CAD drawings. Click the drawing. Look at the Properties panel on the right, under Linked drawings and Images. BimCraft looks for each linked drawing by its name (\"name.dwg\"), in the same folder as the drawing first, then anywhere in the folder you opened. Pictures are found the same way, and also by a similar name: an eTransmit or Revit export often adds the project's name in front, so logo.png is found as Tower-logo-5425051.png. The other way: add files by hand Open the drawing on its own. On the right, under Linked drawings, click Add linked drawings…. Under Images, the button is Add pictures…; both open the same chooser. Choose the linked .dwg files and the picture files. You can choose several at once. Wait while BimCraft opens the drawing again with them. What you should see Each linked drawing and picture is listed with a label: Label Means Shown Found and drawn. The path under it says where it came from, or \"added by you\". Shown, similar name A picture found by a similar name. Check it is the right one. Not found Not in the folder or the files you added. A missing picture shows as its empty frame. TIFF cannot be shown in a browser The picture is a TIFF file, which browsers do not draw. Save it as PNG with the same name and add it. Could not be read The file was found but could not be opened as a picture. The Drawing panel on the left shows \"n of m loaded\" for linked drawings. Linked drawings' layers appear in Layers as \"link name|layer\". Common mistakes Opening only the drawing. \"This drawing shows its own content only until its linked drawings are added. Opening the whole project folder finds them automatically.\" Opening a folder too small. If the links live in a sibling folder, open the parent folder instead. Renamed links. BimCraft matches linked drawings by name. A link renamed on disk is not found; add it by hand. Links of links. BimCraft loads the drawings the open drawing links to. Drawings linked inside those are not looked for, so they usually do not show in this build."},{"slug":"print-view","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a drawing in print view","summary":"Switch a DWG or DXF to print view to see it black on white with its line weights and without the layers that never print.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["print","plot","preview","plot preview","line weights","lineweight","monochrome","black and white","defpoints","no plot"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee how a sheet will look on paper before you print it from the program that made it: black on white, line weights at their true width, and without layers that are set not to print.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab above the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E at the end of the tabs, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom in on a detail to compare heavy and light lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E again, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to go back to the normal colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhite paper on a gray desk, every line and text in black, whatever the theme.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELine weights: on a sheet each weight is drawn at its real width on paper, so a 0.5 mm line is twice as thick as a 0.25 mm line at any zoom. A line is never drawn thinner than its screen weight.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers set not to print, and the DEFPOINTS layer, are hidden.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E button stays pressed. Print view stays on when you switch sheets or open another drawing, until you turn it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, print view uses screen weights only (thin lines one pixel, heavier ones a little thicker), because model space has no paper size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting it to print.\u003C/strong\u003E Print view is a preview. Printing and PDF export are not built in BimCraft yet; print from the program that made the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting plot styles.\u003C/strong\u003E Pen tables and plot style colors are not read. Everything is plain black.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPressing P while typing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while the equipment schedule is showing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See how a sheet will look on paper before you print it from the program that made it: black on white, line weights at their true width, and without layers that are set not to print. Steps Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Click a sheet tab above the drawing. Click Print view at the end of the tabs, or press P. Zoom in on a detail to compare heavy and light lines. Click Print view again, or press P, to go back to the normal colors. What you should see White paper on a gray desk, every line and text in black, whatever the theme. Line weights: on a sheet each weight is drawn at its real width on paper, so a 0.5 mm line is twice as thick as a 0.25 mm line at any zoom. A line is never drawn thinner than its screen weight. Layers set not to print, and the DEFPOINTS layer, are hidden. The Print view button stays pressed. Print view stays on when you switch sheets or open another drawing, until you turn it off. In Model, print view uses screen weights only (thin lines one pixel, heavier ones a little thicker), because model space has no paper size. Common mistakes Expecting it to print. Print view is a preview. Printing and PDF export are not built in BimCraft yet; print from the program that made the drawing. Expecting plot styles. Pen tables and plot style colors are not read. Everything is plain black. Pressing P while typing. F and P do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while the equipment schedule is showing."},{"slug":"equipment-schedule","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Build an equipment schedule and export CSV","summary":"Turn the item tags in a foodservice drawing into an equipment schedule, filter it, jump to each item on its sheet, and download it as CSV.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["equipment","schedule","foodservice","kitchen","item tags","kcl","attributes","bom","bill of materials","csv","spreadsheet","export","data extraction"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"how-the-schedule-is-built","text":"How the schedule is built","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead the equipment list out of a kitchen or foodservice drawing, check it, find each item on the plan, and download it as a spreadsheet file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works on drawings whose equipment tags carry their data as block attributes, as kitchen-design libraries such as KCL do: each tag holds the item number (\u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E), quantity, category, manufacturer, model, electrical and plumbing data, and remarks. Other drawings have no \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the drawing, ideally from its project folder so its linked drawings load too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab above the drawing. Its label shows how many items are on sheets, for example &quot;Equipment (88)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the schedule: \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EQty\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEquipment\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EManufacturer\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPlumbing\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick what to list in the menu above the table: \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery sheet\u003C/strong\u003E, one sheet, \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on any sheet\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003EEverything tagged in the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E. Each choice shows its count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the filter box (&quot;Filter: category, maker, model…&quot;) to narrow the list, for example \u003Ccode\u003Erefrigerator\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E208\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an item&#039;s sheet name in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the drawing: BimCraft switches to that sheet, zooms in on the item and draws a ring round it. The status line says &quot;Item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E, \u003Cem\u003Ecategory\u003C/em\u003E is ringed on \u003Cem\u003Esheet\u003C/em\u003E.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Edrawing name\u003C/em\u003E equipment.csv&quot; (with the sheet&#039;s name in brackets when you listed one sheet) in your downloads folder. It opens in Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice or Google Sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Downloaded \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E (\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E items).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV has these columns: Item, Qty, Category, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing, Remarks, Electrical remarks, Plumbing remarks, Sheets, Placed (how many tags). It downloads exactly the rows the table shows, after the menu and the filter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-schedule-is-built\"\u003EHow the schedule is built\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is read from the \u003Cstrong\u003Etags themselves\u003C/strong\u003E, not from the schedule table drawn on the sheet. The note above the table says how many tags were read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne row is one item number with one set of data. Its quantity is the \u003Cstrong\u003Esum\u003C/strong\u003E of its tags, the way a printed schedule counts it: shelving tagged &quot;5&quot; in one place and &quot;3&quot; in another is 8, shown as &quot;5 + 3&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E lists the sheets that show each item, worked out from what each sheet&#039;s viewports show, which is what prints. &quot;Model only&quot; means no sheet shows it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumber used twice\u003C/strong\u003E marks one item number on two different pieces of equipment. The row has an amber mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETags no sheet shows are kept apart under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on any sheet\u003C/strong\u003E. They are often left over from earlier plans or other jobs, and are never mixed into \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery sheet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical\u003C/strong\u003E shows amps, kW, HP, volts, phase, connection and plug as tagged. \u003Cstrong\u003EPlumbing\u003C/strong\u003E shows connection sizes: CW cold water, HW hot water, DD direct drain, ID indirect drain, and gas. Other tag fields are not shown yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing has no \u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E tags, or its equipment is drawn without data. There is nothing to read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounts differ from the printed schedule.\u003C/strong\u003E A printed schedule may list spare numbers or use shorter names than the tags. Trust the tags for what is placed; check differences with the designer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening the CSV shows odd characters.\u003C/strong\u003E The file is UTF-8. Open it with your spreadsheet&#039;s import command and choose UTF-8 if the program asks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA cell starts with an apostrophe.\u003C/strong\u003E Values that begin with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E get a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E They work on the drawing, not on the schedule. Click a sheet tab first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Read the equipment list out of a kitchen or foodservice drawing, check it, find each item on the plan, and download it as a spreadsheet file. This works on drawings whose equipment tags carry their data as block attributes, as kitchen-design libraries such as KCL do: each tag holds the item number (KCL-ITEM), quantity, category, manufacturer, model, electrical and plumbing data, and remarks. Other drawings have no Equipment tab. Steps Open the drawing, ideally from its project folder so its linked drawings load too. Click the Equipment tab above the drawing. Its label shows how many items are on sheets, for example \"Equipment (88)\". Read the schedule: Item, Qty, Equipment, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing and Where. Pick what to list in the menu above the table: Every sheet, one sheet, Not on any sheet, or Everything tagged in the drawing. Each choice shows its count. Type in the filter box (\"Filter: category, maker, model…\") to narrow the list, for example refrigerator or 208. Click an item's sheet name in the Where column. Look at the drawing: BimCraft switches to that sheet, zooms in on the item and draws a ring round it. The status line says \"Item n, category is ringed on sheet.\" Click Equipment to go back to the schedule. Click Download CSV. What you should see A file named \"drawing name equipment.csv\" (with the sheet's name in brackets when you listed one sheet) in your downloads folder. It opens in Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice or Google Sheets. The status line: \"Downloaded file name (n items).\" The CSV has these columns: Item, Qty, Category, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing, Remarks, Electrical remarks, Plumbing remarks, Sheets, Placed (how many tags). It downloads exactly the rows the table shows, after the menu and the filter. How the schedule is built It is read from the tags themselves, not from the schedule table drawn on the sheet. The note above the table says how many tags were read. One row is one item number with one set of data. Its quantity is the sum of its tags, the way a printed schedule counts it: shelving tagged \"5\" in one place and \"3\" in another is 8, shown as \"5 + 3\". Where lists the sheets that show each item, worked out from what each sheet's viewports show, which is what prints. \"Model only\" means no sheet shows it. Number used twice marks one item number on two different pieces of equipment. The row has an amber mark. Tags no sheet shows are kept apart under Not on any sheet. They are often left over from earlier plans or other jobs, and are never mixed into Every sheet. Electrical shows amps, kW, HP, volts, phase, connection and plug as tagged. Plumbing shows connection sizes: CW cold water, HW hot water, DD direct drain, ID indirect drain, and gas. Other tag fields are not shown yet. Common mistakes No Equipment tab. The drawing has no KCL-ITEM tags, or its equipment is drawn without data. There is nothing to read. Counts differ from the printed schedule. A printed schedule may list spare numbers or use shorter names than the tags. Trust the tags for what is placed; check differences with the designer. Opening the CSV shows odd characters. The file is UTF-8. Open it with your spreadsheet's import command and choose UTF-8 if the program asks. A cell starts with an apostrophe. Values that begin with =, +, - or @ get a leading ' so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas. F and P do nothing. They work on the drawing, not on the schedule. Click a sheet tab first."},{"slug":"place-from-library","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Place equipment and furniture from the library","summary":"Find an item in LibraryCraft, a file or the open drawing, place it on a storey of an IFC model with a ghost preview, turn it, snap it to a wall, and save.","section":"tasks","order":150,"keywords":["move","rotate","turn","drag","library","librarycraft","place","insert","equipment","furniture","block","family","item","catalog","content","kcl","csv","gltf","obj","rfa","dwg","ifc type"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"move-or-turn-a-placed-item","text":"Move or turn a placed item","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"items-in-your-language","text":"Items in your language","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut a library item into an IFC model: a sink, a work table, a chair. BimCraft writes it as a proper IFC element with a type, so every copy of the same item shares one set of geometry, and its data, item number and license travel with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E on the right, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a source:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibraryCraft\u003C/strong\u003E: the CadCraft library on this site. Public items need no sign-in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThis file\u003C/strong\u003E: the blocks of an open DWG or DXF drawing. A dynamic block with visibility states is offered once per state, named &quot;Sink: Double bowl&quot;, each drawn with only that state&#039;s objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFiles…\u003C/strong\u003E: a DWG, DXF, IFC, glTF/GLB, OBJ, RFA or CSV file on your computer. It is read in this tab; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther feeds\u003C/strong\u003E: another library that speaks the same feed format. Add its address once; it is kept in this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESearch or pick a category, then click a card. The card shows a plan preview, the name, maker and model, the license and the version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the storey in the placing bar. Storeys above it are hidden while you place, and the view turns to the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the model: a see-through box shows where the item goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E) to put its back against the nearest wall, facing the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place it. Or type the position in \u003Cstrong\u003EX, Y (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1200, 3400\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA light or anything else hung from above: set \u003Cstrong\u003EMount\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EAt the ceiling\u003C/strong\u003E. The item&#039;s top then sits at the underside of the ceiling over the spot (or at the level&#039;s height where there is no ceiling). With a ceiling plan on the plan view, \u003Cstrong\u003EMount\u003C/strong\u003E starts on \u003Cstrong\u003EAt the ceiling\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnswer the one question: the \u003Cstrong\u003Eitem number\u003C/strong\u003E (the next free number is filled in), the quantity and a note. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace more, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-or-turn-a-placed-item\"\u003EMove or turn a placed item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, pick the item in the plan. A square grip shows at its insertion point and a round grip beside it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the item, or its square grip, to move it. It snaps like the Wall tool (wall ends and faces); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps it square to where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the round grip to turn it in 15° steps. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 1° steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E (also in the 3D view), type \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/strong\u003E, or click \u003Cstrong\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach move is one undo step and travels in Studio Live. The item keeps its item number, its library data and its height above the level. Not built yet: dragging it in the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe item in the model, picked, with its properties on the right: a \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box with its license and attribution, and the sets \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Placement\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_LibraryItem\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Equipment\u003C/code\u003E and, for items from tagged drawings, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_SourceAttributes\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Placed: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E (item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E)&quot; in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Placed \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E as item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn item with no 3D body (a 2D symbol) is placed with a stand-in box; you give its height when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMore of the same: pick a placed item and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo start every placement with wall snap on, turn on \u003Cstrong\u003EStart with wall snap on\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELengths are converted to the model&#039;s own units, whatever they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEntourage\u003C/strong\u003E (trees, shrubs, people, cars) is in the library too: search for &quot;tree&quot;, &quot;person&quot; or &quot;car&quot;. These are drawn by CadCraft (CC0) and keep their colors when placed (a trunk brown, leaves green, a car&#039;s windows see-through), in BimCraft and in other BIM programs. They show in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/render\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/render\"\u003Erendered pictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn an IFC2X3 file the body is written as a faceted solid; in IFC4 as a triangulated face set. Both open in other IFC tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing (DWG or DXF) cannot be saved here, so items are placed into IFC models only. Use \u003Cstrong\u003EThis file\u003C/strong\u003E on a drawing to see what its blocks would become.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"items-in-your-language\"\u003EItems in your language\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn item can carry its name, description and type in other languages. When it has them in the language BimCraft is set to (Settings › General › Language), the item&#039;s page shows those, with the name its maker gave it underneath, and \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace\u003C/strong\u003E writes the name in your language into the model, so the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E schedule lists it that way. The item stays the same item: checking for updates and swapping go by the item itself, never by its name. Items without a translation keep their own words.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E is missing.\u003C/strong\u003E No IFC model is open. Open one first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe ghost does not follow the pointer.\u003C/strong\u003E The pointer is off the model&#039;s area, or the storey plane is out of view. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E says there are no walls.\u003C/strong\u003E The storey has no walls in the IFC (some exports put walls on another storey). Pick that storey, or type X and Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA file&#039;s items are the wrong size.\u003C/strong\u003E The file has no units, or says the wrong ones. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles…\u003C/span\u003E choose the units and read it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlacing the same item twice shares one IFC type, so a model with forty chairs stores the chair once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put a library item into an IFC model: a sink, a work table, a chair. BimCraft writes it as a proper IFC element with a type, so every copy of the same item shares one set of geometry, and its data, item number and license travel with it. Steps Open an IFC model. Click the Library tab beside Properties on the right, or Library in the toolbar above the model. Pick a source: LibraryCraft: the CadCraft library on this site. Public items need no sign-in. This file: the blocks of an open DWG or DXF drawing. A dynamic block with visibility states is offered once per state, named \"Sink: Double bowl\", each drawn with only that state's objects. Files…: a DWG, DXF, IFC, glTF/GLB, OBJ, RFA or CSV file on your computer. It is read in this tab; nothing is uploaded. Other feeds: another library that speaks the same feed format. Add its address once; it is kept in this browser. Search or pick a category, then click a card. The card shows a plan preview, the name, maker and model, the license and the version. Click Place in the model. Pick the storey in the placing bar. Storeys above it are hidden while you place, and the view turns to the top. Move the pointer over the model: a see-through box shows where the item goes. Press R to turn it 90°. Press Tab (or Wall snap) to put its back against the nearest wall, facing the room. Click to place it. Or type the position in X, Y (mm), for example 1200, 3400, and press Enter. A light or anything else hung from above: set Mount to At the ceiling. The item's top then sits at the underside of the ceiling over the spot (or at the level's height where there is no ceiling). With a ceiling plan on the plan view, Mount starts on At the ceiling. Answer the one question: the item number (the next free number is filled in), the quantity and a note. Press Enter. Place more, or press Esc to stop. Click Save as IFC to download the edited copy. Move or turn a placed item In Draw, pick the item in the plan. A square grip shows at its insertion point and a round grip beside it. Drag the item, or its square grip, to move it. It snaps like the Wall tool (wall ends and faces); Shift keeps it square to where it was. Drag the round grip to turn it in 15° steps. Hold Shift for 1° steps. Press R to turn it 90°. In Properties (also in the 3D view), type X, Y and Rotation (°), or click Turn 90°. Each move is one undo step and travels in Studio Live. The item keeps its item number, its library data and its height above the level. Not built yet: dragging it in the 3D view. What you should see The item in the model, picked, with its properties on the right: a Library item box with its license and attribution, and the sets CadCraft_Placement, CadCraft_LibraryItem, CadCraft_Equipment and, for items from tagged drawings, CadCraft_SourceAttributes. \"Placed: item (item n)\" in the change list. Ctrl+Z undoes it. The status line: \"Placed item as item n.\" Good to know An item with no 3D body (a 2D symbol) is placed with a stand-in box; you give its height when asked. More of the same: pick a placed item and use Copy and Paste, or Ctrl+D. See Copy, paste and duplicate library items. To start every placement with wall snap on, turn on Start with wall snap on in BimCraft settings. Lengths are converted to the model's own units, whatever they are. Entourage (trees, shrubs, people, cars) is in the library too: search for \"tree\", \"person\" or \"car\". These are drawn by CadCraft (CC0) and keep their colors when placed (a trunk brown, leaves green, a car's windows see-through), in BimCraft and in other BIM programs. They show in rendered pictures. In an IFC2X3 file the body is written as a faceted solid; in IFC4 as a triangulated face set. Both open in other IFC tools. A drawing (DWG or DXF) cannot be saved here, so items are placed into IFC models only. Use This file on a drawing to see what its blocks would become. Items in your language An item can carry its name, description and type in other languages. When it has them in the language BimCraft is set to (Settings › General › Language), the item's page shows those, with the name its maker gave it underneath, and Place writes the name in your language into the model, so the Equipment schedule lists it that way. The item stays the same item: checking for updates and swapping go by the item itself, never by its name. Items without a translation keep their own words. Common mistakes Place in the model is missing. No IFC model is open. Open one first. The ghost does not follow the pointer. The pointer is off the model's area, or the storey plane is out of view. Click Top. Wall snap says there are no walls. The storey has no walls in the IFC (some exports put walls on another storey). Pick that storey, or type X and Y. A file's items are the wrong size. The file has no units, or says the wrong ones. In Files… choose the units and read it again. Placing the same item twice shares one IFC type, so a model with forty chairs stores the chair once."},{"slug":"swap-and-update-library-items","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Swap a placed item, and update items from the library","summary":"Replace one placed library item with another while keeping its place and item number, and check LibraryCraft for newer versions of everything in the model.","section":"tasks","order":151,"keywords":["swap","replace","substitute","update","newer version","check for updates","library","withdrawn","license change","item number"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"swap-one-item","text":"Swap one item","level":2},{"id":"check-for-updates","text":"Check for updates","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange your mind about an item without moving it again, and keep the library items in a model current.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"swap-one-item\"\u003ESwap one item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a placed library item in the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap item…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the new item and click its card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap … to this\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe element keeps its place, turn, storey, item number, quantity and notes. Its type, shape and item data change to the new item. Items placed the same way share one type, as when you place them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the new item is a different kind of thing, for example a sink in place of a table, the element&#039;s IFC class changes to match (here from furnishing element to sanitary terminal), so other software schedules it correctly. It stays the same element, with the same GlobalId, so links to it still work. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings the old class back with the old item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-for-updates\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdates\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/span\u003E in a library item&#039;s box).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the list: each LibraryCraft item in the model with its version in the model, the latest version and a status: \u003Cstrong\u003EUp to date\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENewer version\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E. It also says what changed (geometry, data, license or a fix).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E on a row, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate all\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Updated \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E placements. Places, item numbers, quantities and notes are kept.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Updated: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E&quot; lines in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes each one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly items that came from LibraryCraft can be checked. Items from files carry no library id.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen a license changed, the copy you already placed keeps the license it came with until you update it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/strong\u003E item is not offered for new placements. One withdrawn for copyright reasons cannot be downloaded at all; replace it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap item…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChecking sends only the item ids and versions, never your model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Change your mind about an item without moving it again, and keep the library items in a model current. Swap one item Click a placed library item in the model. In Properties, click Swap item… in the Library item box. The Library tab opens. Find the new item and click its card. Click Swap … to this. The element keeps its place, turn, storey, item number, quantity and notes. Its type, shape and item data change to the new item. Items placed the same way share one type, as when you place them. When the new item is a different kind of thing, for example a sink in place of a table, the element's IFC class changes to match (here from furnishing element to sanitary terminal), so other software schedules it correctly. It stays the same element, with the same GlobalId, so links to it still work. Ctrl+Z brings the old class back with the old item. Check for updates Click Updates in the toolbar (or Check for updates in a library item's box). Read the list: each LibraryCraft item in the model with its version in the model, the latest version and a status: Up to date, Newer version, Withdrawn or Not found. It also says what changed (geometry, data, license or a fix). Click Update on a row, or Update all. What you should see \"Updated n placements. Places, item numbers, quantities and notes are kept.\" \"Updated: item\" lines in the change list. Ctrl+Z undoes each one. Good to know Only items that came from LibraryCraft can be checked. Items from files carry no library id. When a license changed, the copy you already placed keeps the license it came with until you update it. A Withdrawn item is not offered for new placements. One withdrawn for copyright reasons cannot be downloaded at all; replace it with Swap item…. Checking sends only the item ids and versions, never your model."},{"slug":"library-credits","scope":"bimcraft","title":"List library credits and sign in to the library","summary":"See every library item used in a model with its license and attribution, copy the credits or download them with the schedule, and sign in to see your team's items.","section":"tasks","order":152,"keywords":["credits","attribution","license","cc by","cc0","copyright","team library","sign in","connect","token","equipment schedule","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"library-credits","text":"Library credits","level":2},{"id":"the-equipment-schedule-of-a-model","text":"The equipment schedule of a model","level":2},{"id":"sign-in-to-the-library","text":"Sign in to the library","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive credit where a license asks for it, and reach your team&#039;s own library items.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"library-credits\"\u003ELibrary credits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECredits\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the list: each item used, its version, license, attribution and the item numbers it is placed as.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy credits\u003C/span\u003E to copy the attribution lines, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV with the schedule\u003C/span\u003E for a spreadsheet of every placement with its credit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe license and attribution also show in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E when you pick a placed item, and they are saved in the IFC (\u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_LibraryItem\u003C/code\u003E), so they travel with the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-equipment-schedule-of-a-model\"\u003EThe equipment schedule of a model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The schedule is built from the placed library items: item number, quantity, name, maker, model, electrical and plumbing data, grouped by storey. It downloads as the same CSV a drawing&#039;s schedule does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sign-in-to-the-library\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab, pick \u003Cstrong\u003ELibraryCraft\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/span\u003E. A small window opens on this site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESign in if asked, then click \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect\u003C/strong\u003E. The window hands BimCraft a library key and you can close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Signed in to the library&quot; at the top of the LibraryCraft list. Your team&#039;s items appear beside the public ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EItems you place are recorded as used, so the library can tell you when they change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe key is kept in this tab only and ends when you close it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForget sign-in\u003C/span\u003E drops it sooner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour account&#039;s security page lists the key as &quot;BimCraft library (browser)&quot;; revoke it there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing about your model is sent: only which library items you placed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Give credit where a license asks for it, and reach your team's own library items. Library credits Open the model. Click Credits in the toolbar. Read the list: each item used, its version, license, attribution and the item numbers it is placed as. Click Copy credits to copy the attribution lines, or CSV with the schedule for a spreadsheet of every placement with its credit. The license and attribution also show in Properties when you pick a placed item, and they are saved in the IFC (CadCraft_LibraryItem), so they travel with the file. The equipment schedule of a model Click Equipment in the toolbar. The schedule is built from the placed library items: item number, quantity, name, maker, model, electrical and plumbing data, grouped by storey. It downloads as the same CSV a drawing's schedule does. Sign in to the library In the Library tab, pick LibraryCraft. Click Sign in to the library. A small window opens on this site. Sign in if asked, then click Connect. The window hands BimCraft a library key and you can close it. What you should see \"Signed in to the library\" at the top of the LibraryCraft list. Your team's items appear beside the public ones. Items you place are recorded as used, so the library can tell you when they change. Good to know The key is kept in this tab only and ends when you close it. Forget sign-in drops it sooner. Your account's security page lists the key as \"BimCraft library (browser)\"; revoke it there. Nothing about your model is sent: only which library items you placed."},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate library items","summary":"Copy a placed library item and paste it where you click, in place beside the original, or into another model; copy any other element's properties as text; undo and redo.","section":"tasks","order":155,"keywords":["copy","paste","duplicate","clipboard","paste in place","cut","delete","select all","undo","redo","edit menu","more","library item","placement","repeat","array"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"into-another-model-or-another-tab","text":"Into another model or another tab","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut more copies of an item you already placed: a second work table, a row of sinks. This topic is about \u003Cstrong\u003Eplaced library items\u003C/strong\u003E, the elements BimCraft wrote into the model: one of them can be pasted into any model, even in another tab. Walls, doors and every other element are copied, cut, pasted and deleted the same way within their own model: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe commands are in the toolbar above the model: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, a menu with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E. A command that cannot act right now is greyed out; point at it to read why, or click it and the status line says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model with a library item in it, or place one first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the item in the 3D view. Its properties show a \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The placing bar opens and a see-through box follows the pointer, turned like the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to snap its back to a wall, or type X and Y in the placing bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the copy goes. Each click places another copy; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited copy of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes with no clicking, at the same X and Y and turned the same way:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInto the same model, on the same storey\u003C/strong\u003E: two items in exactly one spot would be no use, so the copy goes right beside the original, one item width plus 100 mm along the item&#039;s own width. Paste in place again from the new copy to make a row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnto another storey or into another model\u003C/strong\u003E: exactly the same X, Y and turn. BimCraft uses the storey with the same name; if there is none, the one at the nearest height. The status line says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the picked item and starts pasting it straight away: one step instead of two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"into-another-model-or-another-tab\"\u003EInto another model or another tab\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat you copy is kept in this browser, so you can open another IFC model, or BimCraft in another tab, and paste there. The other model needs the library item itself: BimCraft has it when the item was placed, swapped in or updated in this tab. An item placed in an earlier session can be pasted again in its own model only; in another model, place it from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe copy in the model, picked, with the same library item, license, equipment data, quantity and note as the original, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Enext free item number\u003C/strong\u003E. Change the number in Properties, under \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Placement\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Placed: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E (item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E)&quot; in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies share the original&#039;s type, so all of them use one set of geometry, as placing the same item twice does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E cover every change: values you edited, placements, pastes, swaps and updates. Redo puts a placement back at the same place; a new change clears what could be redone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 2D view of a drawing there is nothing to select, so there is nothing to copy. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E to change the drawing, when it shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E says the elements came from another model.\u003C/strong\u003E The last thing you copied was a building element, or several elements: those paste into their own model only. Pick one placed library item and copy it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E says the copy came from another model without its library item.\u003C/strong\u003E Place the item from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab once in this tab, then copy and paste it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies page text instead.\u003C/strong\u003E Text was selected on the page. Click the model first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard#edit\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"edit\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put more copies of an item you already placed: a second work table, a row of sinks. This topic is about placed library items, the elements BimCraft wrote into the model: one of them can be pasted into any model, even in another tab. Walls, doors and every other element are copied, cut, pasted and deleted the same way within their own model: see Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements. The commands are in the toolbar above the model: Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and More, a menu with Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none. A command that cannot act right now is greyed out; point at it to read why, or click it and the status line says why. Steps Open an IFC model with a library item in it, or place one first. See Place equipment and furniture from the library. Click the item in the 3D view. Its properties show a Library item box. Click Copy or press Ctrl+C. Click Paste or press Ctrl+V. The placing bar opens and a see-through box follows the pointer, turned like the original. Press R to turn it 90°, Tab to snap its back to a wall, or type X and Y in the placing bar. Click where the copy goes. Each click places another copy; press Esc to stop. Click Save as IFC to download the edited copy of the model. Paste in place More, Paste in place or Ctrl+Shift+V pastes with no clicking, at the same X and Y and turned the same way: Into the same model, on the same storey: two items in exactly one spot would be no use, so the copy goes right beside the original, one item width plus 100 mm along the item's own width. Paste in place again from the new copy to make a row. Onto another storey or into another model: exactly the same X, Y and turn. BimCraft uses the storey with the same name; if there is none, the one at the nearest height. The status line says which. Duplicate More, Duplicate or Ctrl+D copies the picked item and starts pasting it straight away: one step instead of two. Into another model or another tab What you copy is kept in this browser, so you can open another IFC model, or BimCraft in another tab, and paste there. The other model needs the library item itself: BimCraft has it when the item was placed, swapped in or updated in this tab. An item placed in an earlier session can be pasted again in its own model only; in another model, place it from the Library tab. What you should see The copy in the model, picked, with the same library item, license, equipment data, quantity and note as the original, and the next free item number. Change the number in Properties, under CadCraft_Placement. \"Placed: item (item n)\" in the change list. Ctrl+Z takes it back; Ctrl+Y puts it back again. Good to know Copies share the original's type, so all of them use one set of geometry, as placing the same item twice does. Undo and Redo cover every change: values you edited, placements, pastes, swaps and updates. Redo puts a placement back at the same place; a new change clears what could be redone. In the 2D view of a drawing there is nothing to select, so there is nothing to copy. Use Edit in CadCraft to change the drawing, when it shows. Common mistakes Paste says the elements came from another model. The last thing you copied was a building element, or several elements: those paste into their own model only. Pick one placed library item and copy it. Paste says the copy came from another model without its library item. Place the item from the Library tab once in this tab, then copy and paste it. Ctrl+C copies page text instead. Text was selected on the page. Click the model first. Related Keys and mouse BimCraft settings"},{"slug":"edit-elements","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements","summary":"Pick walls, doors, furniture or anything else in an IFC model; delete them, cut them, or copy and paste them where you click, in place, or on another storey.","section":"tasks","order":156,"keywords":["delete","remove","erase","cut","copy","paste","duplicate","move","select all","pick all","building element","wall","door","window","stair","opening","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-goes-with-an-element","text":"What goes with an element","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange what is in an IFC model: take out elements you do not want, copy a wall or a set of furniture to another place, or move elements by cutting and pasting them. Every change is one step you can undo, and saving writes a new file, so your original stays as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick what you want to change: click an element, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to pick more, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E) to pick every element shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E. The elements go, and you can paste them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo paste, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. A see-through box the size of what you copied follows the pointer on the storey chosen in the placing bar. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. Each click places another copy; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E): after a cut the elements go back exactly where they were; after a copy the copy goes right beside the originals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-goes-with-an-element\"\u003EWhat goes with an element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts:\u003C/strong\u003E a stair takes its flights, a curtain wall its panels, anything made of parts takes them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings:\u003C/strong\u003E a wall takes the openings cut in it. Deleting a door or window also takes the opening it filled, so the wall closes, as it does in an authoring program. Deleting a wall leaves its doors and windows where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EData:\u003C/strong\u003E a copy has the same type, materials, property sets and quantities as the original, and a new GlobalId. Changing a value on the copy later leaves the original alone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary items\u003C/strong\u003E keep their library data; a copy gets the next free item number, and a cut item pasted back keeps its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Deleted: …&quot; or &quot;Pasted: …&quot; in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the last change back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter a delete, the status line says how many parts and openings went too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPasted elements are picked, so you can paste in place again to make a row, or delete them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies use the same geometry as the original (IFC lets elements share it), so the file grows very little.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuilding elements paste into the model they were copied from, in the same tab. To use placed library items in another model, copy one item at a time: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStoreys, spaces, buildings and the site cannot be deleted or copied. Pick the elements in them instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy also puts the names and properties of what you copied on your clipboard as text, to paste into a spreadsheet or an e-mail.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E is greyed out.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing is picked, or only storeys or spaces are. Point at it to read why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaste says the elements came from another model.\u003C/strong\u003E You opened another model since you copied them. Open the first model again and copy them there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe pasted copy is on another floor.\u003C/strong\u003E The placing bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E decides where it goes; pick the storey before you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard#edit\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"edit\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Change what is in an IFC model: take out elements you do not want, copy a wall or a set of furniture to another place, or move elements by cutting and pasting them. Every change is one step you can undo, and saving writes a new file, so your original stays as it was. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Pick what you want to change: click an element, hold Ctrl or Shift and click to pick more, or press Ctrl+A (More, Select all) to pick every element shown. Then: Delete: press Delete, or More, Delete. Copy: press Ctrl+C or click Copy. Cut: press Ctrl+X, or More, Cut. The elements go, and you can paste them. To paste, press Ctrl+V or click Paste. A see-through box the size of what you copied follows the pointer on the storey chosen in the placing bar. Press R to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. Each click places another copy; press Esc to stop. Or press Ctrl+Shift+V (More, Paste in place): after a cut the elements go back exactly where they were; after a copy the copy goes right beside the originals. Click Save as IFC to download the edited model. What goes with an element Parts: a stair takes its flights, a curtain wall its panels, anything made of parts takes them all. Openings: a wall takes the openings cut in it. Deleting a door or window also takes the opening it filled, so the wall closes, as it does in an authoring program. Deleting a wall leaves its doors and windows where they were. Data: a copy has the same type, materials, property sets and quantities as the original, and a new GlobalId. Changing a value on the copy later leaves the original alone. Library items keep their library data; a copy gets the next free item number, and a cut item pasted back keeps its own. What you should see \"Deleted: …\" or \"Pasted: …\" in the Changes list. Ctrl+Z takes the last change back; Ctrl+Y puts it back again. After a delete, the status line says how many parts and openings went too. Pasted elements are picked, so you can paste in place again to make a row, or delete them. Good to know Copies use the same geometry as the original (IFC lets elements share it), so the file grows very little. Building elements paste into the model they were copied from, in the same tab. To use placed library items in another model, copy one item at a time: see Copy, paste and duplicate library items. Storeys, spaces, buildings and the site cannot be deleted or copied. Pick the elements in them instead. Copy also puts the names and properties of what you copied on your clipboard as text, to paste into a spreadsheet or an e-mail. Common mistakes Delete is greyed out. Nothing is picked, or only storeys or spaces are. Point at it to read why. Paste says the elements came from another model. You opened another model since you copied them. Open the first model again and copy them there. The pasted copy is on another floor. The placing bar's Storey decides where it goes; pick the storey before you click. Related Hide, isolate and see through elements Keys and mouse"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Work on one model together, live","summary":"What changes in BimCraft while you are in a CadCraft Studio live session: everyone's edits to the same IFC model, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":199,"keywords":["live","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","ifc","properties","rename","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-edits-at-once","text":"Everyone edits at once","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to an IFC model while several people edit it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the model itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-edits-at-once\"\u003EEveryone edits at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s model comes to everyone who joins. Rename elements, change their property values, place, swap or remove library items and elements as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them. When an edit changes what you see (an element placed or removed), BimCraft redraws the model and keeps your view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one element:\u003C/strong\u003E the later change to the same line of the model wins. A property set shared by several elements is copied for the element you edit, as always, so the others keep their values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn element someone just removed\u003C/strong\u003E can&#039;t be edited any more: your edit is taken back, and the status bar says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach person&#039;s new lines in the model get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. BimCraft opens in the browser and opens the host&#039;s model once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: Save as IFC if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the first model in the scene is shared. Opening another file during a session leaves the session in that window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to an IFC model while several people edit it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the model itself. Everyone edits at once The host's model comes to everyone who joins. Rename elements, change their property values, place, swap or remove library items and elements as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. When an edit changes what you see (an element placed or removed), BimCraft redraws the model and keeps your view. Two people change one element: the later change to the same line of the model wins. A property set shared by several elements is copied for the element you edit, as always, so the others keep their values. An element someone just removed can't be edited any more: your edit is taken back, and the status bar says why. Each person's new lines in the model get numbers of their own, so two people never make the same one. Undo undoes your own work Undo undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. BimCraft opens in the browser and opens the host's model once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: Save as IFC if you want to keep it. Only the first model in the scene is shared. Opening another file during a session leaves the session in that window."},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Coming from other BIM and drawing programs","summary":"How habits from desktop BIM authoring and DWG viewing map to BimCraft, and the differences that trip people up.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","desktop bim","family","parameters","project browser","properties palette","view cube","xref","layer manager","differences","compare"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-mapped","text":"Habits mapped","level":2},{"id":"from-viewing-dwg-files","text":"From viewing DWG files","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you know Revit, most of BimCraft will feel familiar in words and unfamiliar in scope. The single most important difference: \u003Cstrong\u003Ethis build reads and inspects models and edits IFC data. It does not author like Revit yet.\u003C/strong\u003E You cannot draw walls, place families, make views or sheets, or save a project file. What you can do is open almost any building file on any computer with a browser, see what is in it, check a whole project folder, and correct the data in an IFC model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-mapped\"\u003EHabits mapped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIf you are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening an \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E in Revit to see the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft opens the \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E as a file card: release, project information, links, preview. For the 3D model, export IFC and open that. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/read-rvt-file\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/read-rvt-file\"\u003ERead a project or family file\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChecking which release a file was saved in before opening it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe card says &quot;saved in Revit …&quot; at the top, without upgrading or touching the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Project Browser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo browser of views and sheets. An IFC model has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E lists; a drawing has sheet tabs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Properties palette\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, for the element you clicked. IFC property sets instead of parameter groups\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstance and type parameters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstance property sets, and sets marked &quot;(type)&quot;. A type change applies to every element of the type, as in Revit. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit Type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo dialog: click a value in a &quot;(type)&quot; set and type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelecting many elements, filters, Select All Instances\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne element at a time. Not built yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view cube and the navigation wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA view cube in the bottom-right corner, four buttons (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E) and free orbit. No navigation wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift and middle-drag to orbit, middle-drag to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-drag orbits, right-drag (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag) pans, wheel zooms. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBIM ribbon\u003C/span\u003E menu style switches to middle-drag pan and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the middle button to orbit. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFloor plans, sections, section boxes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane at a time (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E); no section box or 2D plans. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHide in view, isolate, visibility/graphics\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E on and off, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E. No per-element hiding yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC spaces, hidden at first. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave, Save As, synchronize with central\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E downloads a new IFC file. The original never changes. No central, no worksharing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on IFC edits until you save. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EManage Links\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the project folder: every link is checked and marked \u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAudit, or opening a file to see if it is corrupt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe folder report checks every copy and backup for being cut off, in seconds. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/check-folder-health\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/check-folder-health\"\u003ECheck a folder for damaged copies\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking the right file from a messy folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box suggests one, with reasons\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you mostly open models to check them, as in a model checking or IFC viewing program, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewer\u003C/span\u003E menu style: the viewing and checking tools come first and the building tools wait in one menu. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-viewing-dwg-files\"\u003EFrom viewing DWG files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open DWGs in AutoCAD or a DWG viewer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIf you are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel and layout tabs at the bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E and one per sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZOOM Extents, double-click the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe layer manager\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list: tick to show, untick to hide, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe xref manager\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E on the right, with \u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd linked drawings…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot preview\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: black on white with line weights. It does not print\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EData extraction of block attributes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab for tagged foodservice drawings, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure, draw, edit, save as\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot in BimCraft. The CadCraft drafting app draws and edits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt runs in the browser, and the browser is careful with your files.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft can only read files and folders you hand it, by choosing them or dragging them in. It cannot open a file by itself, and it cannot save over one. Every save is a download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E The words &quot;upload&quot; in some browsers&#039; folder dialogs are the browser&#039;s own wording. The files stay on your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC is the model format.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file opens as a card because its 3D content is undocumented. Treat IFC as the way models travel into BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdits are copy-on-write.\u003C/strong\u003E Changing one element&#039;s value never changes its neighbors, even when the exporter shared that value between them. Type properties are the deliberate exception.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo project, no views.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft shows one file at a time and remembers nothing about it after you close the tab, except the names of recent folders in Chrome and Edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMac keys.\u003C/strong\u003E Where Revit&#039;s Windows shortcuts use Ctrl, BimCraft&#039;s shortcuts use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: Command on a Mac.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour mouse habits.\u003C/strong\u003E Orbit is the left button, not Shift and the middle button, unless you pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBIM ribbon\u003C/span\u003E menu style or set the buttons yourself in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for what to use instead today.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you know Revit, most of BimCraft will feel familiar in words and unfamiliar in scope. The single most important difference: this build reads and inspects models and edits IFC data. It does not author like Revit yet. You cannot draw walls, place families, make views or sheets, or save a project file. What you can do is open almost any building file on any computer with a browser, see what is in it, check a whole project folder, and correct the data in an IFC model. Habits mapped If you are used to In BimCraft Opening an .rvt in Revit to see the model BimCraft opens the .rvt as a file card: release, project information, links, preview. For the 3D model, export IFC and open that. Read a project or family file Checking which release a file was saved in before opening it The card says \"saved in Revit …\" at the top, without upgrading or touching the file The Project Browser No browser of views and sheets. An IFC model has Storeys and Elements lists; a drawing has sheet tabs The Properties palette The Properties panel on the right, for the element you clicked. IFC property sets instead of parameter groups Instance and type parameters Instance property sets, and sets marked \"(type)\". A type change applies to every element of the type, as in Revit. Change a type property for every element of a type Edit Type No dialog: click a value in a \"(type)\" set and type Selecting many elements, filters, Select All Instances One element at a time. Not built yet The view cube and the navigation wheel A view cube in the bottom-right corner, four buttons (Fit, Top, Front, Side) and free orbit. No navigation wheel Shift and middle-drag to orbit, middle-drag to pan Left-drag orbits, right-drag (or Shift and drag) pans, wheel zooms. The BIM ribbon menu style switches to middle-drag pan and Shift with the middle button to orbit. Menus, keys and mouse Floor plans, sections, section boxes One Section plane at a time (X); no section box or 2D plans. Cut an IFC model with a section plane Hide in view, isolate, visibility/graphics Storeys on and off, Spaces and Edges. No per-element hiding yet Rooms IFC spaces, hidden at first. Click Spaces Save, Save As, synchronize with central Save as IFC downloads a new IFC file. The original never changes. No central, no worksharing Undo with Ctrl+Z Works on IFC edits until you save. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy Manage Links Open the project folder: every link is checked and marked In this folder, Same name found, Library file or Not in this folder. Open a whole project folder Audit, or opening a file to see if it is corrupt The folder report checks every copy and backup for being cut off, in seconds. Check a folder for damaged copies Picking the right file from a messy folder The Start here box suggests one, with reasons If you mostly open models to check them, as in a model checking or IFC viewing program, pick the Viewer menu style: the viewing and checking tools come first and the building tools wait in one menu. See Menus, keys and mouse. From viewing DWG files If you open DWGs in AutoCAD or a DWG viewer: If you are used to In BimCraft Model and layout tabs at the bottom Tabs above the drawing: Model and one per sheet ZOOM Extents, double-click the wheel Double-click, F or Fit Pan with the middle button Drag with any button The layer manager The Layers list: tick to show, untick to hide, All on and All off The xref manager Linked drawings on the right, with Shown or Not found, and Add linked drawings… Plot preview Print view or P: black on white with line weights. It does not print Data extraction of block attributes The Equipment tab for tagged foodservice drawings, with Download CSV Measure, draw, edit, save as Not in BimCraft. The CadCraft drafting app draws and edits Differences that trip people up It runs in the browser, and the browser is careful with your files. BimCraft can only read files and folders you hand it, by choosing them or dragging them in. It cannot open a file by itself, and it cannot save over one. Every save is a download. Nothing is uploaded. The words \"upload\" in some browsers' folder dialogs are the browser's own wording. The files stay on your computer. IFC is the model format. A project file opens as a card because its 3D content is undocumented. Treat IFC as the way models travel into BimCraft. Edits are copy-on-write. Changing one element's value never changes its neighbors, even when the exporter shared that value between them. Type properties are the deliberate exception. No project, no views. BimCraft shows one file at a time and remembers nothing about it after you close the tab, except the names of recent folders in Chrome and Edge. Mac keys. Where Revit's Windows shortcuts use Ctrl, BimCraft's shortcuts use Ctrl: Command on a Mac. Your mouse habits. Orbit is the left button, not Shift and the middle button, unless you pick the BIM ribbon menu style or set the buttons yourself in Menus, keys and mouse. See What is not built yet for what to use instead today."},{"slug":"two-bim-programs","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Working between two BIM programs","summary":"Send a model from one BIM program to another through IFC with BimCraft in the middle, keep its GlobalIds, property sets and classifications, set both programs up for the same properties, and see what came back.","section":"switching","order":40,"keywords":["ifc","round trip","interoperability","globalid","guid","property sets","psets","classifications","shared parameters","property mapping","translator","bcf","coordination","exchange"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-bimcraft-keeps","text":"What BimCraft keeps","level":2},{"id":"set-both-programs-up-for-the-same-properties","text":"Set both programs up for the same properties","level":2},{"id":"send-the-model-and-bring-it-back","text":"Send the model and bring it back","level":2},{"id":"read-the-report","text":"Read the report","level":2},{"id":"issues-travel-as-bcf","text":"Issues travel as BCF","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove a model between two BIM programs, for example from Revit to Archicad and back, through IFC, with BimCraft in the middle to check it, add to it and report what survived. Neither program&#039;s own file format is read: everything travels as IFC.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-bimcraft-keeps\"\u003EWhat BimCraft keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you change a model in BimCraft and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E, every line you did not change is written back exactly as it was. So:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGlobalIds\u003C/strong\u003E stay the same, and the other program can match each element to the one it sent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperty sets\u003C/strong\u003E and their values stay, apart from the values you changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClassifications\u003C/strong\u003E stay attached to their elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-both-programs-up-for-the-same-properties\"\u003ESet both programs up for the same properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the model in BimCraft (and the project bundle, if you have one: its property groups are added).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the left panel, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBetween two BIM programs\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EProperty mapping\u003C/strong\u003E, download what each program needs:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShared parameters\u003C/span\u003E: a shared parameter file. In Revit, load it with Manage › Shared Parameters and add the parameters to the categories that need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC export property sets\u003C/span\u003E: a user-defined property set file. In Revit&#039;s IFC export settings, choose it under user-defined property sets, so the exported IFC carries the same set and property names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMapping table (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E: every property with its IFC set and name, its type, and the matching names in each program. Archicad&#039;s mapping files are not a published format, so set up its IFC translator and property manager from this table by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMapping file\u003C/span\u003E: the whole mapping as one file, to keep with the project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStandard sets (Pset_ and Qto_) are left out of the shared parameter file: both programs write those from their own data.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-the-model-and-bring-it-back\"\u003ESend the model and bring it back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExport IFC from the first program with GlobalIds kept (Revit: &quot;Store the IFC GUID in an element parameter after export&quot; on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen it in BimCraft, check it (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel checks\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E), add issues (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E, BCF), change values if you need to, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the saved IFC in the second program, work on it, and export IFC from there with GlobalIds kept (Archicad&#039;s translator: &quot;Global Unique Identifiers: Keep existing&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBack in BimCraft, open the model that went out, then add the one that came back (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model\u003C/span\u003E under Models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBetween two BIM programs\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cstrong\u003EWent out\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECame back\u003C/strong\u003E, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake the report\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-report\"\u003ERead the report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElements\u003C/strong\u003E: how many came back with the same GlobalId, how many are missing, and how many are new. Elements with a new GlobalId show as one missing and one new: the other program did not keep it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC class changed\u003C/strong\u003E: elements that came back as another kind of element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperty values kept\u003C/strong\u003E: the share of values that came back unchanged. The list below shows the properties with the most changed or missing values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClassifications\u003C/strong\u003E: kept, changed (same system, another code) or missing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the report (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E gives every property with its counts. To see which elements moved or changed shape, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare versions\u003C/span\u003E on the same two models.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft runs neither program. Which settings carry a value through depends on each program&#039;s IFC settings: the report shows what actually came back, so try a setting, export again and compare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"issues-travel-as-bcf\"\u003EIssues travel as BCF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIssues you raise in BimCraft export as BCF (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E), which both programs can read with their BCF tools, so a question about an element reaches the person working in the other program with its viewpoint.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-bundle\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-bundle\"\u003EOpen a project bundle\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EFrom a project file to BimCraft through IFC\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/compare-versions\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/compare-versions\"\u003ECompare two versions of a model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Move a model between two BIM programs, for example from Revit to Archicad and back, through IFC, with BimCraft in the middle to check it, add to it and report what survived. Neither program's own file format is read: everything travels as IFC. What BimCraft keeps When you change a model in BimCraft and click Save as IFC, every line you did not change is written back exactly as it was. So: GlobalIds stay the same, and the other program can match each element to the one it sent. Property sets and their values stay, apart from the values you changed. Classifications stay attached to their elements. Set both programs up for the same properties Open the model in BimCraft (and the project bundle, if you have one: its property groups are added). In the left panel, open Between two BIM programs. Under Property mapping, download what each program needs: Shared parameters: a shared parameter file. In Revit, load it with Manage › Shared Parameters and add the parameters to the categories that need them. IFC export property sets: a user-defined property set file. In Revit's IFC export settings, choose it under user-defined property sets, so the exported IFC carries the same set and property names. Mapping table (CSV): every property with its IFC set and name, its type, and the matching names in each program. Archicad's mapping files are not a published format, so set up its IFC translator and property manager from this table by hand. Mapping file: the whole mapping as one file, to keep with the project. Standard sets (Pset_ and Qto_) are left out of the shared parameter file: both programs write those from their own data. Send the model and bring it back Export IFC from the first program with GlobalIds kept (Revit: \"Store the IFC GUID in an element parameter after export\" on). Open it in BimCraft, check it (Model checks, Clash check), add issues (Issues, BCF), change values if you need to, and Save as IFC. Open the saved IFC in the second program, work on it, and export IFC from there with GlobalIds kept (Archicad's translator: \"Global Unique Identifiers: Keep existing\"). Back in BimCraft, open the model that went out, then add the one that came back (Add a model under Models). In Between two BIM programs, choose Went out and Came back, and click Make the report. Read the report Elements: how many came back with the same GlobalId, how many are missing, and how many are new. Elements with a new GlobalId show as one missing and one new: the other program did not keep it. IFC class changed: elements that came back as another kind of element. Property values kept: the share of values that came back unchanged. The list below shows the properties with the most changed or missing values. Classifications: kept, changed (same system, another code) or missing. Download the report (CSV) gives every property with its counts. To see which elements moved or changed shape, use Compare versions on the same two models. BimCraft runs neither program. Which settings carry a value through depends on each program's IFC settings: the report shows what actually came back, so try a setting, export again and compare. Issues travel as BCF Issues you raise in BimCraft export as BCF (Issues), which both programs can read with their BCF tools, so a question about an element reaches the person working in the other program with its viewpoint. See also Open a project bundle, From a project file to BimCraft through IFC and Compare two versions of a model."},{"slug":"settings","scope":"bimcraft","title":"BimCraft settings","summary":"Choose whether rooms and spaces show, whether edges are drawn and which view a model opens in, and start placing with wall snap on.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","spaces","rooms","ifcspace","edges","lines","performance","large model","opening view","camera","top view","wall snap","snapping"],"headings":[{"id":"menus-keys-and-mouse","text":"Menus, keys and mouse","level":2},{"id":"when-a-model-opens","text":"When a model opens","level":2},{"id":"opening-view","text":"Opening view","level":2},{"id":"placing-library-items","text":"Placing library items","level":2},{"id":"section-box","text":"Section box","level":2},{"id":"location","text":"Location","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"your-name-on-issues","text":"Your name on issues","level":2},{"id":"assistants","text":"Assistants","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). BimCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset BimCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of the dialog puts them, and the shared ones, back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe menu style (BimCraft&#039;s own toolbar, a home-design menu bar, a BIM ribbon or a drafting command line), your own keys and menus, and what the wheel and mouse buttons do. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-model-opens\"\u003EWhen a model opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow rooms and spaces\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start): rooms and spaces are see-through boxes that can hide the walls behind them, so they start hidden. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E button above the model still turns them on and off for the open model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges on\u003C/span\u003E (the start) draws the edge lines that give the drafting look. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmall models only\u003C/span\u003E draws them only for models under 300,000 triangles, which keeps big models quicker to turn. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges off\u003C/span\u003E starts every model without them. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E button still switches them for the open model. Above 1.5 million triangles edges are always off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-view\"\u003EOpening view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera a model opens with: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E (the start, the whole model from a corner), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E. The buttons above the model change the view any time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA building turned in its site\u003C/strong\u003E: many models arrive with the building at an angle to the file&#039;s axes (on survey or shared coordinates). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESquare to the building\u003C/span\u003E (the start) shows such a building square: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E, the view cube&#039;s faces and every new section follow its walls, and the view cube&#039;s compass still points to the model&#039;s north. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs placed in the site\u003C/span\u003E keeps the file&#039;s axes. A building whose walls run along the axes looks the same either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-library-items\"\u003EPlacing library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStart with wall snap on\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start): when placing or pasting a library item starts, its back snaps to the nearest wall, facing the room. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E in the placing bar turns it off for the moment. A storey with no walls starts without it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-box\"\u003ESection box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoom round the pick\u003C/span\u003E is how much space \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection box round the pick\u003C/span\u003E leaves on every side of the picked elements, in millimetres: 300 to start, up to 5000. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"location\"\u003ELocation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELatitude and longitude in\u003C/span\u003E chooses the system \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E gives latitudes and longitudes in: WGS 84 (EPSG:4326, the start, as GPS and web maps use), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe file&#039;s own datum\u003C/span\u003E, or one of ETRS89, NAD83, GDA94, GDA2020, NZGD2000, JGD2011 and CGCS2000. The file&#039;s own map system and datum are always shown too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, face areas and locations in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat sits in the bottom-right corner of the 3D views (an IFC model, and a drawing switched to 3D): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y and Z) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. It changes at once. The 2D drawing view never shows one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the view cube, click a face, an edge or a corner to look from that side, drag the cube to orbit, drag the ring to turn round, and click the house for the starting view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-name-on-issues\"\u003EYour name on issues\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESigned in, issues carry your account&#039;s name and BimCraft asks for nothing: this part shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom your account (&lt;name&gt;)\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another name\u003C/span\u003E opens a box for a different name, which wins until you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse my account name\u003C/span\u003E. Signed out, the issue form asks for a name as before. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open model through CadCraft Studio: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E finds elements and reads their properties, \u003Cstrong\u003EChange the view\u003C/strong\u003E isolates, hides and shows them. \u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E lists its calls. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe other settings apply when a model opens or placing starts. A model already open keeps how it looks until you use the toolbar buttons or open it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe BimCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, BimCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). BimCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset BimCraft settings at the bottom of the dialog puts them, and the shared ones, back to the start. Menus, keys and mouse The menu style (BimCraft's own toolbar, a home-design menu bar, a BIM ribbon or a drafting command line), your own keys and menus, and what the wheel and mouse buttons do. See Menus, keys and mouse. When a model opens Show rooms and spaces (off at the start): rooms and spaces are see-through boxes that can hide the walls behind them, so they start hidden. The Spaces button above the model still turns them on and off for the open model. Edges: Edges on (the start) draws the edge lines that give the drafting look. Small models only draws them only for models under 300,000 triangles, which keeps big models quicker to turn. Edges off starts every model without them. The Edges button still switches them for the open model. Above 1.5 million triangles edges are always off. Opening view The camera a model opens with: Fit (the start, the whole model from a corner), Top, Front or Side. The buttons above the model change the view any time. A building turned in its site: many models arrive with the building at an angle to the file's axes (on survey or shared coordinates). Square to the building (the start) shows such a building square: Fit, Top, Front, Side, the view cube's faces and every new section follow its walls, and the view cube's compass still points to the model's north. As placed in the site keeps the file's axes. A building whose walls run along the axes looks the same either way. Placing library items Start with wall snap on (off at the start): when placing or pasting a library item starts, its back snaps to the nearest wall, facing the room. Tab or Wall snap in the placing bar turns it off for the moment. A storey with no walls starts without it. Section box Room round the pick is how much space Section box round the pick leaves on every side of the picked elements, in millimetres: 300 to start, up to 5000. See Cut an IFC model with a section plane. Location Latitude and longitude in chooses the system Measure › Location gives latitudes and longitudes in: WGS 84 (EPSG:4326, the start, as GPS and web maps use), The file's own datum, or one of ETRS89, NAD83, GDA94, GDA2020, NZGD2000, JGD2011 and CGCS2000. The file's own map system and datum are always shown too. See Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D. Corner indicator What sits in the bottom-right corner of the 3D views (an IFC model, and a drawing switched to 3D): View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y and Z) or None. It changes at once. The 2D drawing view never shows one. On the view cube, click a face, an edge or a corner to look from that side, drag the cube to orbit, drag the ring to turn round, and click the house for the starting view. Your name on issues Signed in, issues carry your account's name and BimCraft asks for nothing: this part shows From your account (\u003Cname\u003E). Use another name opens a box for a different name, which wins until you choose Use my account name. Signed out, the issue form asks for a name as before. See Issues and BCF files. Assistants Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open model through CadCraft Studio: Read finds elements and reads their properties, Change the view isolates, hides and shows them. Activity lists its calls. See Let an AI assistant work with your model. The other settings apply when a model opens or placing starts. A model already open keeps how it looks until you use the toolbar buttons or open it again. Related Copy, paste and duplicate library items Place equipment and furniture from the library The BimCraft screen Let an AI assistant work with your model"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between menus, and set what the wheel and each mouse button do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","menu bar","ribbon","command line","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","pan","orbit","two-letter keys","export","import","switching","familiar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-five-styles","text":"The five styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them, so you can keep the habits you have. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-five-styles\"\u003EThe five styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft&#039;s own toolbar and keys (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E wall, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E door, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E window, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E section, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit). The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome design\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA menu bar (File, Edit, Build, Library, 3D, CAD, Tools, View) over a row of building tools, the way residential home-design programs lay them out. The same single keys, plus \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command L\"\u003E⌘L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+L\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the library, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F9\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the 3D view, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the plan and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for both.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBIM ribbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs of large buttons (Architecture, Insert, Annotate, Modify, View, Output) with a File menu and quick buttons on the right. Two-letter keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a wall, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E door, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E window, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E section, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit. The middle button pans; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button orbit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA small ribbon and a command line, for people who type commands: WALL, DOOR, WINDOW, ROOF, U (undo), ZOOM, SECTION, PLOT, INSERT, HELP. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E runs, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on nothing repeats the last command, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewer\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor opening and checking models rather than drawing them: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E menus over a row of viewing tools (fit, top, front, side, section, measure, hide, isolate, see-through, show all, walk). The building tools wait in a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E menu at the end. The same keys as BimCraft&#039;s own style.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe menus and ribbons show once a model is open or you start a new building; before that, the panel on the left opens files and starts buildings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to BimCraft&#039;s own layout but not to the style you picked does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In BIM ribbon, Section is SC&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning, and a running tool keeps its own keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while placing, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while walking, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the door and window tools, numbers while you type a length.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a menu bar or a ribbon, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another menu or panel, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan, and what each button does in the 3D views.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Home design&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the plan and drawings with\u003C/strong\u003E: any of the left, middle and right buttons. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag pans too; a left drag pans only where a click would not pick or draw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D views\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps BimCraft&#039;s buttons (left orbits, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the button swaps orbit and pan. A click without dragging still picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at BimCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it (a command this version doesn&#039;t have, a key that doesn&#039;t work here) is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar, a ribbon or Drafting classic; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewer\u003C/span\u003E is BimCraft&#039;s alone and has no match elsewhere), with the changes you made in each app; an app without a matching style keeps its own. Choosing another style in one app changes only that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other BIM and drawing programs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"BimCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them, so you can keep the habits you have. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › BimCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The five styles Style What you get CadCraft BimCraft's own toolbar and keys (W wall, D door, N window, X section, F fit). The start. Home design A menu bar (File, Edit, Build, Library, 3D, CAD, Tools, View) over a row of building tools, the way residential home-design programs lay them out. The same single keys, plus Ctrl+L for the library, F9 for the 3D view, F10 for the plan and F11 for both. BIM ribbon Tabs of large buttons (Architecture, Insert, Annotate, Modify, View, Output) with a File menu and quick buttons on the right. Two-letter keys: W then A for a wall, D R door, W N window, S C section, Z F fit. The middle button pans; Shift and the middle button orbit. Drafting classic A small ribbon and a command line, for people who type commands: WALL, DOOR, WINDOW, ROOF, U (undo), ZOOM, SECTION, PLOT, INSERT, HELP. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line; Enter runs, Enter on nothing repeats the last command, Esc clears it. Viewer For opening and checking models rather than drawing them: File, View, Show, Check and Edit menus over a row of viewing tools (fit, top, front, side, section, measure, hide, isolate, see-through, show all, walk). The building tools wait in a Model menu at the end. The same keys as BimCraft's own style. The menus and ribbons show once a model is open or you start a new building; before that, the panel on the left opens files and starts buildings. A key that belongs to BimCraft's own layout but not to the style you picked does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In BIM ribbon, Section is SC\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning, and a running tool keeps its own keys: R and Tab while placing, W A S D while walking, H with the door and window tools, numbers while you type a length. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With a menu bar or a ribbon, Where it sits moves the command to another menu or panel, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan, and what each button does in the 3D views. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Home design\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the plan and drawings with: any of the left, middle and right buttons. Space and drag pans too; a left drag pans only where a click would not pick or draw. Drag in the 3D views: Each view's own keeps BimCraft's buttons (left orbits, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the button swaps orbit and pan. A click without dragging still picks. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or from a colleague. An imported file can only point at BimCraft's own commands: anything else in it (a command this version doesn't have, a key that doesn't work here) is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a menu bar, a ribbon or Drafting classic; Viewer is BimCraft's alone and has no match elsewhere), with the changes you made in each app; an app without a matching style keeps its own. Choosing another style in one app changes only that app. Related: Keyboard and mouse · BimCraft settings · Coming from other BIM and drawing programs"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Keys and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse, trackpad and touch action in BimCraft, grouped by what you are looking at.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","trackpad","touch","orbit","pan","zoom","hide","isolate","see-through","undo","redo","copy","paste","duplicate","save","fit","print view","escape","settings","draw","wall","door","window","plan"],"headings":[{"id":"everywhere","text":"Everywhere","level":2},{"id":"3d-view-ifc-models","text":"3D view (IFC models)","level":2},{"id":"edit","text":"Edit","level":2},{"id":"plan-view-draw","text":"Plan view (Draw)","level":2},{"id":"placing-and-pasting-library-items","text":"Placing and pasting library items","level":2},{"id":"editing-ifc-values","text":"Editing IFC values","level":2},{"id":"2d-drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"2D drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"drawings-in-3d","text":"Drawings in 3D","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft has few shortcuts. Most work is done with the mouse and the panels. Shortcuts written with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E for how keys are written in this help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everywhere\"\u003EEverywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle the screen modes: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Ctrl on every platform, including a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave full screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove between buttons, checkboxes and values. Every control can be reached from the keyboard\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view-ifc-models\"\u003E3D view (IFC models)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the whole model, three-quarter view (same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear the selection, or stop placing or pasting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the element under the pointer; click empty background to clear\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd an element to the pick, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHide the picked elements. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow only the picked elements (isolate)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow the picked elements, everything else see-through\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow every element again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThen \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or arrows walk, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E runs, drag looks round, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walk\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walk\"\u003EWalk through a model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure distances and face areas. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, face areas and locations in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (handy on a trackpad)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit; a tap picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E| \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E | Section plane on and off. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E | | \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift X\"\u003E⇧X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E | Section box round the picked elements, with the room set in Settings |\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no keys for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E; use the buttons.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit\"\u003EEdit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same commands are in the toolbar: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, which shows each key. They work in the 3D view of an IFC model, not while you type in a field. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo what Undo took back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the picked elements (their names and properties also go to the clipboard as text)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the picked elements: copy them, then delete them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste what you copied: it follows the pointer, click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: where a cut came from, or beside the originals after a copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: copy the picked elements and start pasting them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the picked elements, with their parts and openings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick every element shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen text is selected on the page, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies that text as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plan-view-draw\"\u003EPlan view (Draw)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese work while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E is on and you are not typing in a field. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/doors-and-windows\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDoor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDoor\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E: hinges at the other end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while drawing a wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep it at 0°, 90°, 180° or 270°\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDigits and the foot and inch marks (\u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart typing a length into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E box; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E uses it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, right-click or double-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd the wall you are drawing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the picked walls, doors and windows; the walls left join up again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the pointer over the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom about the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle or right button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, a plain drag pans too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a door or window with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove it along its wall, or onto another wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-and-pasting-library-items\"\u003EPlacing and pasting library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the item 90°\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall snap on and off: the item&#039;s back goes against the nearest wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EX, Y (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace at the typed position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop placing or pasting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-ifc-values\"\u003EEditing IFC values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the value you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel and put the old value back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick elsewhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the value you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last change. Only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo what Undo took back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as IFC. Only while you have unsaved changes; otherwise the browser&#039;s own save command opens\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo edit with the keyboard alone: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until a value is highlighted, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start typing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"2d-drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003E2D drawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the drawing or the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint view on and off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer. Two-finger scroll does not pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-in-3d\"\u003EDrawings in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D on and off (also on the 2D view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtents: the whole drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle or left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward what is under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing while you type in a field, while the equipment schedule is showing, or when you hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a file onto the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a folder onto the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it as a project folder, with no permission prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"BimCraft has few shortcuts. Most work is done with the mouse and the panels. Shortcuts written with Ctrl use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See Keys, mice and trackpads for how keys are written in this help. Everywhere Key What it does Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle the screen modes: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Ctrl on every platform, including a Mac Esc Leave full screen Ctrl+, Open Settings. See BimCraft settings Tab Move between buttons, checkboxes and values. Every control can be reached from the keyboard 3D view (IFC models) Key or action What it does F Fit the whole model, three-quarter view (same as Fit) Esc Clear the selection, or stop placing or pasting Click Pick the element under the pointer; click empty background to clear Ctrl+click or Shift+click Add an element to the pick, or take it out H Hide the picked elements. See Hide, isolate and see through elements I Show only the picked elements (isolate) T Show the picked elements, everything else see-through Shift+H Show every element again Walk Then W A S D or arrows walk, Shift runs, drag looks round, Esc stops. See Walk through a model M Measure distances and face areas. See Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D Drag with the left button Orbit Drag with the right button Pan Shift or Ctrl and drag with the left button Pan (handy on a trackpad) Scroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button Zoom Two-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad Zoom One finger (touch) Orbit; a tap picks Two fingers (touch) Pinch to zoom, move together to pan | X | Section plane on and off. See Cut an IFC model with a section plane | | Shift+X | Section box round the picked elements, with the room set in Settings | There are no keys for Top, Front and Side; use the buttons. Edit The same commands are in the toolbar: Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and the More menu, which shows each key. They work in the 3D view of an IFC model, not while you type in a field. See Copy, paste and duplicate library items. Key What it does Ctrl+Z Undo the last change Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo what Undo took back Ctrl+C Copy the picked elements (their names and properties also go to the clipboard as text) Ctrl+X Cut the picked elements: copy them, then delete them Ctrl+V Paste what you copied: it follows the pointer, click where it goes Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: where a cut came from, or beside the originals after a copy Ctrl+D Duplicate: copy the picked elements and start pasting them Delete Delete the picked elements, with their parts and openings. See Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Ctrl+A Pick every element shown Esc Select none When text is selected on the page, Ctrl+C copies that text as usual. Plan view (Draw) These work while Draw is on and you are not typing in a field. See Draw walls and Doors and windows. Key or action What it does V Select W Wall D Door N Window H With Door or Window: hinges at the other end Shift while drawing a wall Keep it at 0°, 90°, 180° or 270° Digits and the foot and inch marks (' and \") Start typing a length into the Length box; Enter uses it Enter, Esc, right-click or double-click End the wall you are drawing; Esc again goes back to Select Delete Delete the picked walls, doors and windows; the walls left join up again F with the pointer over the plan Fit the plan Scroll the wheel Zoom about the pointer Drag with the middle or right button, or Space and drag Pan; with Select on, a plain drag pans too Drag a door or window with Select Move it along its wall, or onto another wall Two fingers (touch) Pinch to zoom, move together to pan Placing and pasting library items Key What it does R Turn the item 90° Tab Wall snap on and off: the item's back goes against the nearest wall Enter in X, Y (mm) Place at the typed position Esc Stop placing or pasting Editing IFC values Key What it does Enter Keep the value you typed Esc Cancel and put the old value back Click elsewhere Keep the value you typed Ctrl+Z Undo the last change. Only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field Ctrl+Y Redo what Undo took back Ctrl+S Save as IFC. Only while you have unsaved changes; otherwise the browser's own save command opens To edit with the keyboard alone: press Tab until a value is highlighted, then press Enter to start typing. 2D drawings (DWG and DXF) Key or action What it does F Fit the drawing or the sheet P Print view on and off Double-click Fit Drag with any button Pan Scroll the wheel Zoom at the pointer Two-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad Zoom at the pointer. Two-finger scroll does not pan One finger (touch) Pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings Drawings in 3D Key or action What it does 3 3D on and off (also on the 2D view) F Extents: the whole drawing Drag with the middle or left button Orbit Drag with the right button, or Shift and drag Pan Scroll the wheel Zoom toward what is under the pointer Two-finger swipe on a trackpad Orbit; with Shift, pan Pinch on a trackpad Zoom One finger (touch) Orbit Two fingers (touch) Pinch to zoom, move together to pan F, P and 3 do nothing while you type in a field, while the equipment schedule is showing, or when you hold Ctrl or Alt. Files Action What it does Drag a file onto the page Open it Drag a folder onto the page Open it as a project folder, with no permission prompt"},{"slug":"file-formats","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What BimCraft reads from each format","summary":"Exactly what BimCraft reads from IFC, project and family files, DWG and DXF, and what it leaves alone.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["formats","ifc","ifc2x3","ifc4","rvt","rfa","rte","rft","dwg","dxf","dgn","ifczip","versions","compatibility","what is read"],"headings":[{"id":"ifc","text":"IFC","level":2},{"id":"project-and-family-files","text":"Project and family files","level":2},{"id":"pln-pla-tpl-and-bpn-project-files","text":".pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files","level":2},{"id":"dwg-and-dxf","text":"DWG and DXF","level":2},{"id":"in-a-folder","text":"In a folder","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"ifc\"\u003EIFC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometry\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery element with a shape, colored as the file says. Rooms and spaces are drawn see-through and hidden at first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStoreys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilding storeys in elevation order, and which elements sit on each. Elements on no storey are grouped as &quot;Not on a storey&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElement counts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements of each kind (walls, doors, slabs…), shown in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject facts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject name, long name and phase; schema; the exporting program, author and export date from the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElement data\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, type, object type, tag, description, GlobalId; every property set and quantity set on the element and on its type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEditable: name, object type, tag, description, and single property values. Saved as a new IFC file. Schemas IFC2X3, IFC4 and IFC4X3 open, read by BimCraft&#039;s own IFC engine; anything it cannot draw yet is listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. The model is moved to the origin when it opens, so a model placed far from it still shows in the middle of the screen. \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E do not open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"project-and-family-files\"\u003EProject and family files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E projects, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E project templates, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E families and \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E family templates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelease and build, worksharing, central model path, last saved path, user name, locale, document GUID\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s own fields (name, number, client, address, status…), without new-project defaults\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily name, category, release, last update, and its parameters by group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe list of linked models, CAD links, images and keynote tables the file points to\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPreview picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe thumbnail saved in the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot read: the 3D model, elements, views, sheets and schedules. They are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only a few tens of kilobytes from each file, so file size barely matters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pln-pla-tpl-and-bpn-project-files\"\u003E.pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot read yet: BimCraft says so when you open one. Export IFC for the model and DWG for the drawings; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpaces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel space and every sheet (layout), in tab order\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach sheet&#039;s viewports, with their scale, twist and frozen layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, color, on/off, frozen, plot flag and line weight\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solids, text and multiline text, blocks and attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, points, images\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawn where they are inserted, when their files are found\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawn from the picture files when found (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP; not TIFF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFoodservice item tags (\u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E attributes), for the equipment schedule\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything else is counted under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E, never guessed. DWG formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked (R13 and R14 files are accepted too, but none were in the test set; older DWGs do not open); the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel shows the format code (such as AC1032). DXF files of every version open, R12 included. Both are read by BimCraft&#039;s own readers, in this tab. Nothing is saved back to DWG or DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-a-folder\"\u003EIn a folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the report\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrouped with numbered backups, health-checked, card read, links checked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed; opens in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECAD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dgn\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed with the models that link them; DWG and DXF open in 2D, DGN does not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeftovers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.ac$\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.lck\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.slog\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHousekeeping folders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E, hidden folders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, not read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/folder-roles\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/folder-roles\"\u003EFolder report: roles, statuses and links\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"IFC Read Details Geometry Every element with a shape, colored as the file says. Rooms and spaces are drawn see-through and hidden at first Storeys Building storeys in elevation order, and which elements sit on each. Elements on no storey are grouped as \"Not on a storey\" Element counts How many elements of each kind (walls, doors, slabs…), shown in Elements Project facts Project name, long name and phase; schema; the exporting program, author and export date from the file header Element data Name, type, object type, tag, description, GlobalId; every property set and quantity set on the element and on its type Editable: name, object type, tag, description, and single property values. Saved as a new IFC file. Schemas IFC2X3, IFC4 and IFC4X3 open, read by BimCraft's own IFC engine; anything it cannot draw yet is listed under Not drawn yet in the File panel. The model is moved to the origin when it opens, so a model placed far from it still shows in the middle of the screen. .ifczip and .ifcxml do not open. Project and family files .rvt projects, .rte project templates, .rfa families and .rft family templates. Read Details File information Release and build, worksharing, central model path, last saved path, user name, locale, document GUID Project information The project's own fields (name, number, client, address, status…), without new-project defaults Family information Family name, category, release, last update, and its parameters by group Links The list of linked models, CAD links, images and keynote tables the file points to Preview picture The thumbnail saved in the file Not read: the 3D model, elements, views, sheets and schedules. They are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only a few tens of kilobytes from each file, so file size barely matters. .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files Not read yet: BimCraft says so when you open one. Export IFC for the model and DWG for the drawings; see Open .pln and .pla project files. DWG and DXF Read Details Spaces Model space and every sheet (layout), in tab order Viewports Each sheet's viewports, with their scale, twist and frozen layers Layers Name, color, on/off, frozen, plot flag and line weight Objects Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solids, text and multiline text, blocks and attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, points, images Linked drawings Drawn where they are inserted, when their files are found Images Drawn from the picture files when found (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP; not TIFF) Equipment tags Foodservice item tags (KCL-ITEM attributes), for the equipment schedule Anything else is counted under Not drawn yet, never guessed. DWG formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked (R13 and R14 files are accepted too, but none were in the test set; older DWGs do not open); the Drawing panel shows the format code (such as AC1032). DXF files of every version open, R12 included. Both are read by BimCraft's own readers, in this tab. Nothing is saved back to DWG or DXF. In a folder Kind Extensions In the report Models .rvt .rfa .rte .rft Grouped with numbered backups, health-checked, card read, links checked IFC .ifc Listed; opens in 3D CAD .dwg .dxf .dgn Listed with the models that link them; DWG and DXF open in 2D, DGN does not Leftovers .dwl .dwl2 .bak .err .log .tmp .sv$ .ac$ .lck .slog Listed under Set aside Housekeeping folders *_backup, *_log, Revit_temp, hidden folders Listed under Set aside, not read See Folder report: roles, statuses and links."},{"slug":"point-cloud-formats","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Point cloud formats: what opens, and what to export","summary":"Which laser scan files open in the 3D view, which are a scanner maker's own format, and what to export from the scanner's software so the scan opens here.","section":"reference","order":28,"keywords":["point cloud","laser scan","scan","formats","e57","las","laz","ply","pcd","ptx","pts","xyz","rcp","rcs","fls","lgs","tzf","zfs","export","can't open","not read"],"headings":[{"id":"what-opens-here","text":"What opens here","level":2},{"id":"files-that-only-the-scanner-s-software-reads","text":"Files that only the scanner's software reads","level":2},{"id":"why-these-files-are-not-read","text":"Why these files are not read","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-opens-here\"\u003EWhat opens here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen these with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E or by dropping them on the page (see the topic on showing a point cloud beside the model):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is read\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELAS, LAZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.las\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.laz\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELAS 1.0–1.4: points, colors, intensity, LiDAR classes, the coordinate system. LAZ up to 1 GB.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE57\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.e57\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery scan in the file, each placed by its pose; colors, or intensity when the file has no colors. Pictures in the file are left out (said). Up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export bigger scans as LAS or LAZ.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPLY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ply\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText and binary (both byte orders): the vertices&#039; x y z, colors and intensity. Faces are left out (said).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPCD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.pcd\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText and binary: x y z, packed or separate colors, intensity. Compressed PCD files are not read yet: save them as binary or text.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPTX\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ptx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne or more scans, each placed by its own transform; points the scanner did not measure are left out. Meters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPTS, XYZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.pts\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.xyz\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText: x y z, with intensity and colors when the columns are there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-only-the-scanner-s-software-reads\"\u003EFiles that only the scanner&#039;s software reads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome scan files are a scanner maker&#039;s own format, with no published description and no open reader. BimCraft can&#039;t read them, and opening one shows a card that says what it is and what to do. Export the scan from the maker&#039;s software as E57 or LAS (or PTX, PTS), then open the exported file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExport it from\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rcp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap: open the project and export it. The project only points to its scans, so copying the \u003Ccode\u003E.rcp\u003C/code\u003E alone gives no points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rcs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap: open the project the scan belongs to and export it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFARO scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.fls\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFARO SCENE.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeica scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.lgs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeica Cyclone REGISTER 360.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrimble scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.tzf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrimble RealWorks or Trimble Business Center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ+F scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.zfs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ+F LaserControl.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the export asks for a format, LAS or LAZ keeps everything BimCraft shows (colors, intensity, classes and the coordinate system) in the smallest file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-these-files-are-not-read\"\u003EWhy these files are not read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThose formats are not published, and the only way to read them is each maker&#039;s own software library, under its own terms. BimCraft only reads formats it can read with its own code on your computer, so a scan never has to be sent anywhere to open.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What opens here Open these with Open or by dropping them on the page (see the topic on showing a point cloud beside the model): Format Extension What is read LAS, LAZ .las, .laz LAS 1.0–1.4: points, colors, intensity, LiDAR classes, the coordinate system. LAZ up to 1 GB. E57 .e57 Every scan in the file, each placed by its pose; colors, or intensity when the file has no colors. Pictures in the file are left out (said). Up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export bigger scans as LAS or LAZ. PLY .ply Text and binary (both byte orders): the vertices' x y z, colors and intensity. Faces are left out (said). PCD .pcd Text and binary: x y z, packed or separate colors, intensity. Compressed PCD files are not read yet: save them as binary or text. PTX .ptx One or more scans, each placed by its own transform; points the scanner did not measure are left out. Meters. PTS, XYZ .pts, .xyz Text: x y z, with intensity and colors when the columns are there. Files that only the scanner's software reads Some scan files are a scanner maker's own format, with no published description and no open reader. BimCraft can't read them, and opening one shows a card that says what it is and what to do. Export the scan from the maker's software as E57 or LAS (or PTX, PTS), then open the exported file. File Extension Export it from ReCap project .rcp ReCap: open the project and export it. The project only points to its scans, so copying the .rcp alone gives no points. ReCap scan .rcs ReCap: open the project the scan belongs to and export it. FARO scan .fls FARO SCENE. Leica scan .lgs Leica Cyclone REGISTER 360. Trimble scan .tzf Trimble RealWorks or Trimble Business Center. Z+F scan .zfs Z+F LaserControl. When the export asks for a format, LAS or LAZ keeps everything BimCraft shows (colors, intensity, classes and the coordinate system) in the smallest file. Why these files are not read Those formats are not published, and the only way to read them is each maker's own software library, under its own terms. BimCraft only reads formats it can read with its own code on your computer, so a scan never has to be sent anywhere to open."},{"slug":"properties-panel","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Properties panel fields","summary":"What every field in the Properties panel and the File, Drawing and Folder panels means, and which ones you can change.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["properties","fields","attributes","globalid","guid","copy","copy value","copy all","csv","clipboard","serial number","tag","object type","line","express id","property set","pset","quantities","schema","file panel"],"headings":[{"id":"an-ifc-element","text":"An IFC element","level":2},{"id":"copy-a-value","text":"Copy a value","level":2},{"id":"the-file-panel-ifc","text":"The File panel (IFC)","level":2},{"id":"the-file-panel-project-and-family-files","text":"The File panel (project and family files)","level":2},{"id":"the-drawing-panel-dwg-and-dxf","text":"The Drawing panel (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"the-folder-panel","text":"The Folder panel","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"an-ifc-element\"\u003EAn IFC element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows the element&#039;s kind without the &quot;Ifc&quot; prefix (for example &quot;Door&quot;, &quot;WallStandardCase&quot;, &quot;FurnishingElement&quot;) and its name, or &quot;Unnamed&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEditable\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s name, often the family and type name from the exporter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name of the element&#039;s type, when it has one\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA free-text type description some exporters fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s tag or mark; many exporters write the element ID here\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescription\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFree text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGlobalId\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s permanent 22-character IFC identifier, the same in every export of the same element\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s line number (\u003Ccode\u003E#1234\u003C/code\u003E) in this IFC file. It changes between exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow come the \u003Cstrong\u003Eproperty sets\u003C/strong\u003E, sorted by name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach set opens and closes when you click its name. The first three are open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA set marked &quot;(type)&quot; belongs to the element&#039;s type: &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuantity sets (lengths, areas, volumes, counts, weights) are read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELists and enumerated values are shown, read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYes/No values show as \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers are rounded to four decimals for display, in the file&#039;s own units, without a unit symbol.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn empty value shows as \u003Cem\u003Eempty\u003C/em\u003E. An edited value is bold with a blue bar beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-a-value\"\u003ECopy a value\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery value in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E has a small copy button beside it: click it to put that value on the clipboard exactly as shown, for example a GlobalId, a serial number or any property set value. Copying never starts an edit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy all\u003C/span\u003E copies every value of the element as text, set by set, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy as CSV\u003C/span\u003E copies them as rows of group, property and value to paste into a spreadsheet; empty values are kept so every field has a row. This works the same for an element of another open model; when more than one model is open, the CSV starts with a Model column naming its file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file-panel-ifc\"\u003EThe File panel (IFC)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile, Size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file you opened\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESchema\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC version, such as IFC2X3 or IFC4\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s name and long name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhase\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project phase, if the exporter wrote one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExported from\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exporting program, from the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuthor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe author in the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExported\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe date and time in the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements with a shape were drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many triangles their shapes make; a measure of how heavy the model is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file-panel-project-and-family-files\"\u003EThe File panel (project and family files)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject, Project template, Family or Family template\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe release that last saved the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exact build of that release\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow much of the file BimCraft read, out of its size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-drawing-panel-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EThe Drawing panel (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormat\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe release the drawing was saved as, with its format code, such as AC1032\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnits\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s insertion units, when set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEntities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many objects were read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many sheets (layouts), or None\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many of its linked drawings were found and loaded\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many tagged equipment items are on sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-folder-panel\"\u003EThe Folder panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFolder, Files, Size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe folder you opened, how many files it holds and their total size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files, each with its backups counted once\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECAD drawings, IFC models\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many of each\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProblems\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many model copies are incomplete or unreadable, or None\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"An IFC element The top of the Properties panel shows the element's kind without the \"Ifc\" prefix (for example \"Door\", \"WallStandardCase\", \"FurnishingElement\") and its name, or \"Unnamed\". Field Meaning Editable Name The element's name, often the family and type name from the exporter Yes Type The name of the element's type, when it has one No Object type A free-text type description some exporters fill Yes Tag The element's tag or mark; many exporters write the element ID here Yes Description Free text Yes GlobalId The element's permanent 22-character IFC identifier, the same in every export of the same element No Line The element's line number (#1234) in this IFC file. It changes between exports No Below come the property sets, sorted by name: Each set opens and closes when you click its name. The first three are open. A set marked \"(type)\" belongs to the element's type: \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Quantity sets (lengths, areas, volumes, counts, weights) are read-only. Lists and enumerated values are shown, read-only. Yes/No values show as Yes or No. Numbers are rounded to four decimals for display, in the file's own units, without a unit symbol. An empty value shows as empty. An edited value is bold with a blue bar beside it. Copy a value Every value in Properties has a small copy button beside it: click it to put that value on the clipboard exactly as shown, for example a GlobalId, a serial number or any property set value. Copying never starts an edit. At the top, Copy all copies every value of the element as text, set by set, and Copy as CSV copies them as rows of group, property and value to paste into a spreadsheet; empty values are kept so every field has a row. This works the same for an element of another open model; when more than one model is open, the CSV starts with a Model column naming its file. The File panel (IFC) Field Meaning File, Size The file you opened Schema The IFC version, such as IFC2X3 or IFC4 Project The project's name and long name Phase The project phase, if the exporter wrote one Exported from The exporting program, from the file header Author The author in the file header Exported The date and time in the file header Elements How many elements with a shape were drawn Triangles How many triangles their shapes make; a measure of how heavy the model is The File panel (project and family files) Field Meaning Kind Project, Project template, Family or Family template Saved in The release that last saved the file Build The exact build of that release Read How much of the file BimCraft read, out of its size The Drawing panel (DWG and DXF) Field Meaning Format The release the drawing was saved as, with its format code, such as AC1032 Units The drawing's insertion units, when set Entities How many objects were read Layers How many layers Sheets How many sheets (layouts), or None Linked drawings How many of its linked drawings were found and loaded Equipment How many tagged equipment items are on sheets The Folder panel Field Meaning Folder, Files, Size The folder you opened, how many files it holds and their total size Models Project and family files, each with its backups counted once CAD drawings, IFC models How many of each Problems How many model copies are incomplete or unreadable, or None"},{"slug":"folder-roles","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Folder report: roles, statuses and links","summary":"Every label in the folder report, what it means, and how the Start here suggestion is chosen.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["folder report","roles","status","complete","incomplete","unreadable","linked","background","starter","central","family","backups only","start here","link status"],"headings":[{"id":"copy-status","text":"Copy status","level":2},{"id":"backups","text":"Backups","level":2},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles","level":2},{"id":"how-start-here-is-chosen","text":"How Start here is chosen","level":2},{"id":"link-status","text":"Link status","level":2},{"id":"set-aside","text":"Set aside","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-status\"\u003ECopy status\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery copy of a model, main file and numbered backups alike, gets a header check.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file&#039;s header and index fit inside the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is shorter than its own index says: it was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced, or it is a cloud file that is still &quot;online only&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnreadable\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a valid compound file, or could not be read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E column a model shows \u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E, an amber &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E incomplete&quot; when at least one copy is bad but another is sound, or red \u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E when no copy is sound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"backups\"\u003EBackups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFiles named \u003Ccode\u003EName.0001.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.0002.rvt\u003C/code\u003E and so on are numbered backups of \u003Ccode\u003EName.rvt\u003C/code\u003E. They are grouped under their model, newest first, in \u003Cstrong\u003EAll … copies\u003C/strong\u003E. The card is read from the main file when it is complete, else from the newest complete backup (&quot;newest complete&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"roles\"\u003ERoles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShown in italics under a model&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERole\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked by \u003Cem\u003Eother model\u003C/em\u003E: background\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnother model in the folder links this one, so it is most likely someone else&#039;s background, such as a consultant&#039;s model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarter or untitled file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA name like Blank, Template, Default, Untitled or Project1: a starting point, or a file never properly named\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECentral model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA workshared central model. Work happens in a local copy, not here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily (a part)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E file: a component, not the project\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackups only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe main file is gone and only numbered backups remain\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-start-here-is-chosen\"\u003EHow Start here is chosen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft applies the reasoning an experienced user would:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECandidates are readable project files (\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E) that are not linked by another model and not starters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECentrals and backup-only models count only when nothing better exists.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOf the candidates, the one saved most recently wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box lists its reasons, and warns when a file that looks like the obvious choice is damaged, when other models are background, and when the pick is a central model. It is a best guess; check with whoever last worked on the project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-status\"\u003ELink status\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFound at the saved relative path, or at the end of the saved full path inside the folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot at its path, but a file with the same name is in the folder. Check it is the right one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot found anywhere in the folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ELinks found\u003C/strong\u003E column in \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E shows how many of a model&#039;s links were found, out of those expected in the folder (library files are not counted).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-aside\"\u003ESet aside\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevit housekeeping folders (not read)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E folders, plus hidden folders and \u003Ccode\u003E__MACOSX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock, log and backup leftovers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ac$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.lck\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.slog\u003C/code\u003E files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything else (the first 40 are named)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Copy status Every copy of a model, main file and numbered backups alike, gets a header check. Label Meaning Complete The file's header and index fit inside the file Incomplete The file is shorter than its own index says: it was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced, or it is a cloud file that is still \"online only\" Unreadable The file is not a valid compound file, or could not be read In the Status column a model shows Complete, an amber \"n incomplete\" when at least one copy is bad but another is sound, or red Incomplete when no copy is sound. Backups Files named Name.0001.rvt, Name.0002.rvt and so on are numbered backups of Name.rvt. They are grouped under their model, newest first, in All … copies. The card is read from the main file when it is complete, else from the newest complete backup (\"newest complete\"). Roles Shown in italics under a model's name. Role Meaning Linked by other model: background Another model in the folder links this one, so it is most likely someone else's background, such as a consultant's model Starter or untitled file A name like Blank, Template, Default, Untitled or Project1: a starting point, or a file never properly named Central model A workshared central model. Work happens in a local copy, not here Family (a part) An .rfa or .rft file: a component, not the project Backups only The main file is gone and only numbered backups remain How Start here is chosen BimCraft applies the reasoning an experienced user would: Candidates are readable project files (.rvt, .rte) that are not linked by another model and not starters. Centrals and backup-only models count only when nothing better exists. Of the candidates, the one saved most recently wins. The box lists its reasons, and warns when a file that looks like the obvious choice is damaged, when other models are background, and when the pick is a central model. It is a best guess; check with whoever last worked on the project. Link status Label Meaning In this folder Found at the saved relative path, or at the end of the saved full path inside the folder Same name found Not at its path, but a file with the same name is in the folder. Check it is the right one Library file A keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer Not in this folder Not found anywhere in the folder The Links found column in Models shows how many of a model's links were found, out of those expected in the folder (library files are not counted). Set aside Line Meaning Revit housekeeping folders (not read) *_backup, *_log and Revit_temp folders, plus hidden folders and __MACOSX Lock, log and backup leftovers .dwl, .dwl2, .bak, .err, .log, .tmp, .sv$, .ac$, .lck and .slog files Other files Everything else (the first 40 are named)"},{"slug":"platforms","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Browsers, devices and memory","summary":"What differs in BimCraft between Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux, with a mouse, a trackpad or touch, and how big a file it can take.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["browser","chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","phone","touch","trackpad","memory","large files","webgl","performance"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-a-folder","text":"Opening a folder","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"mouse-trackpad-and-touch","text":"Mouse, trackpad and touch","level":2},{"id":"large-files-and-memory","text":"Large files and memory","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft runs in current versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The 3D view needs WebGL, which every current browser has. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E for what the apps need in general.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-a-folder\"\u003EOpening a folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E does this\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDragging a folder in\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser&#039;s folder picker, then &quot;Let site view files?&quot;: click \u003Cstrong\u003EView files\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, for folders picked or dragged\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe system&#039;s folder chooser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe system&#039;s folder chooser, with an \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E button (nothing is uploaded)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBehind the scenes: Chrome and Edge use the File System Access picker and can remember the folder. Firefox and Safari use the older folder chooser, which hands over every file in the folder at once and cannot be remembered. A dragged folder is read entry by entry in every browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrave and some other Chromium browsers turn the folder picker off. BimCraft then uses the system&#039;s folder chooser instead, and the browser may ask &quot;Upload \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E files to this site?&quot;. Nothing is uploaded; the files only go to the tab. Dragging the folder in avoids the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShortcuts written \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E are Command on a Mac and Ctrl on Windows and Linux.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (screen modes) is Control on every platform, including a Mac.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFile dialogs and the downloads folder are your system&#039;s own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-trackpad-and-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInput\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E3D view\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E2D drawing\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-drag orbits, right-drag pans, wheel zooms, click picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny drag pans, wheel zooms, double-click fits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick-drag orbits; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick-drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger orbits, two fingers pinch and pan, tap picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger pans; no pinch zoom yet; tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (or Control-click). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETablets and phones can open single files and look at them, but the screen is small, many mobile browsers cannot choose folders, and large models may not fit in their memory. A computer works best.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"large-files-and-memory\"\u003ELarge files and memory\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything BimCraft reads lives in the browser tab&#039;s memory. How much a tab can use depends on the browser and your computer, more than on BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow much is read\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to expect\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole file, plus the 3D geometry built from it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow large depends on your computer and browser; a model of several hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Over 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 40 KB to 100 KB, whatever the size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEven very large central models open in a moment, also from a network drive\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVery large drawings show &quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFolders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file list, plus a few kilobytes per model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to 20,000 files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a tab runs out of memory, the browser shows its own error page (&quot;Aw, Snap!&quot; in Chrome, for example). Close other tabs, reload BimCraft and try again, or use a computer with more memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"BimCraft runs in current versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The 3D view needs WebGL, which every current browser has. See Browsers and computers for what the apps need in general. Opening a folder Browser Open a folder does this Dragging a folder in Recent folders Chrome, Edge The browser's folder picker, then \"Let site view files?\": click View files Opens with no prompt Yes, for folders picked or dragged Firefox The system's folder chooser Opens with no prompt No Safari The system's folder chooser, with an Upload button (nothing is uploaded) Opens with no prompt No Behind the scenes: Chrome and Edge use the File System Access picker and can remember the folder. Firefox and Safari use the older folder chooser, which hands over every file in the folder at once and cannot be remembered. A dragged folder is read entry by entry in every browser. Brave and some other Chromium browsers turn the folder picker off. BimCraft then uses the system's folder chooser instead, and the browser may ask \"Upload n files to this site?\". Nothing is uploaded; the files only go to the tab. Dragging the folder in avoids the question. Mac, Windows and Linux Shortcuts written Ctrl+S and Ctrl+Z are Command on a Mac and Ctrl on Windows and Linux. Ctrl+Shift+F (screen modes) is Control on every platform, including a Mac. File dialogs and the downloads folder are your system's own. Mouse, trackpad and touch Input 3D view 2D drawing Mouse Left-drag orbits, right-drag pans, wheel zooms, click picks Any drag pans, wheel zooms, double-click fits Trackpad Click-drag orbits; Shift or Ctrl and drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms Click-drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms Touch One finger orbits, two fingers pinch and pan, tap picks One finger pans; no pinch zoom yet; tap Fit On a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (or Control-click). See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and phones can open single files and look at them, but the screen is small, many mobile browsers cannot choose folders, and large models may not fit in their memory. A computer works best. Large files and memory Everything BimCraft reads lives in the browser tab's memory. How much a tab can use depends on the browser and your computer, more than on BimCraft. File How much is read What to expect IFC The whole file, plus the 3D geometry built from it How large depends on your computer and browser; a model of several hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Over 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off Project and family files About 40 KB to 100 KB, whatever the size Even very large central models open in a moment, also from a network drive DWG and DXF The whole file Very large drawings show \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" Folders The file list, plus a few kilobytes per model Up to 20,000 files If a tab runs out of memory, the browser shows its own error page (\"Aw, Snap!\" in Chrome, for example). Close other tabs, reload BimCraft and try again, or use a computer with more memory. Related Online and offline Troubleshooting"},{"slug":"touch-screens","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Use BimCraft on a touch screen or tablet","summary":"Orbit, zoom and pick in the 3D model, pan and pinch a drawing, pick several elements, walk, and place items with your fingers.","section":"reference","order":52,"keywords":["touch","tablet","ipad","android","finger","pinch","long press","tap","orbit","walk","gestures","stylus","pen"],"headings":[{"id":"the-3d-model","text":"The 3D model","level":2},{"id":"drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"Drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"walk","text":"Walk","level":2},{"id":"placing-library-items","text":"Placing library items","level":2},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft works with fingers as well as with a mouse. The first time you touch the screen, buttons, tabs and list rows grow to finger size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-model\"\u003EThe 3D model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch two fingers apart or together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag two fingers together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick an element\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd an element to the pick, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress and hold on it for half a second (the same as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pinch or a two-finger drag never picks anything, even when a finger lifts over an element.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDrawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch; the point between your fingers stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow the whole sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap, or tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E view, the model&#039;s gestures apply: one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, a tap picks, a long press adds to the pick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk\"\u003EWalk\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E, then tap the floor where you want to stand. A row of arrow buttons appears in the walk bar:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold an arrow to walk forward, back, or step to the side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGravity\u003C/span\u003E off, the double arrows rise and sink.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag one finger on the model to look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-library-items\"\u003EPlacing library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap where the item goes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/span\u003E on the placing bar turns it (the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E key on a keyboard), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E snaps its back to the nearest wall, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop placing\u003C/span\u003E ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the first point, then the second. Points snap to corners and edges near your tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a keyboard attached to a tablet, every shortcut works as on a computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard shortcuts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"BimCraft works with fingers as well as with a mouse. The first time you touch the screen, buttons, tabs and list rows grow to finger size. The 3D model To Do this Orbit Drag one finger Zoom Pinch two fingers apart or together Pan Drag two fingers together Pick an element Tap it Add an element to the pick, or take it out Press and hold on it for half a second (the same as Ctrl+click) Pick nothing Tap empty space A pinch or a two-finger drag never picks anything, even when a finger lifts over an element. Drawings (DWG and DXF) To Do this Pan Drag one finger Zoom Pinch; the point between your fingers stays put Show the whole sheet Double-tap, or tap Fit In a drawing's 3D view, the model's gestures apply: one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, a tap picks, a long press adds to the pick. Walk Tap Walk, then tap the floor where you want to stand. A row of arrow buttons appears in the walk bar: Hold an arrow to walk forward, back, or step to the side. With Gravity off, the double arrows rise and sink. Drag one finger on the model to look round. Tap Done to stop. Placing library items Tap where the item goes. Turn 90° on the placing bar turns it (the R key on a keyboard), Wall snap snaps its back to the nearest wall, and Stop placing ends. Measure Tap the first point, then the second. Points snap to corners and edges near your tap. With a keyboard attached to a tablet, every shortcut works as on a computer. See Keyboard shortcuts."},{"slug":"online-offline","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Online and offline","summary":"What BimCraft loads from the site, how Save for offline makes IFC models and DWG drawings open with no connection, and why nothing you open is ever uploaded.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["offline","online","internet","connection","no network","airplane","site visit","save for offline","offline ready","upload","privacy","wasm","webassembly","ifc engine","dwg","cloud"],"headings":[{"id":"what-bimcraft-loads-from-the-site","text":"What BimCraft loads from the site","level":2},{"id":"save-bimcraft-for-offline-use","text":"Save BimCraft for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-the-button-says","text":"What the button says","level":2},{"id":"what-never-needs-a-connection","text":"What never needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-available-yet","text":"What is not available yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft reads your files inside the browser tab. Opening, inspecting, editing and saving never send your files anywhere, online or offline. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E explains how offline copies work in every app; this page covers what is particular to BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-bimcraft-loads-from-the-site\"\u003EWhat BimCraft loads from the site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhen it loads without an offline copy\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESize\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app itself, with the 3D viewer and the IFC engine&#039;s code\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen you open BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral megabytes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC engine&#039;s WebAssembly part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first IFC file you open in the tab: &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 1.6 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDWG and DXF drawings, project and family files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-bimcraft-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave BimCraft for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen BimCraft while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWatch the button count up: &quot;Saving 40 of 212&quot;, for example. BimCraft&#039;s copy is about 11 MB, because it includes the IFC engine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, IFC models, DWG and DXF drawings, project files, folders and this help all open with no connection. You do not need to open an IFC or a DWG first to &quot;warm up&quot; the readers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo update or remove the copy, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-button-says\"\u003EWhat the button says\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo offline copy in this browser yet. Click to save one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Saving \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving; keep the tab open until it finishes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved. Hover to see when; click for the menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download. Check your connection and click to try again. The last good copy, if any, is kept\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis window cannot keep an offline copy (a private window or a managed computer). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, every kind of file opens as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it does not, only what this tab has already loaded works: a reader that never loaded in this tab cannot start, and opening that kind of file shows an error from the browser in the status line. Do not reload the tab until you are online again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-never-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat never needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce BimCraft is open in the tab, these work offline even without a saved copy:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReading project and family files and checking folders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPicking elements, reading properties and editing IFC values in a model that is already open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E: both are downloads made in the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-available-yet\"\u003EWhat is not available yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECloud saving.\u003C/strong\u003E Files are saved to your computer only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"BimCraft reads your files inside the browser tab. Opening, inspecting, editing and saving never send your files anywhere, online or offline. Working offline explains how offline copies work in every app; this page covers what is particular to BimCraft. What BimCraft loads from the site Part When it loads without an offline copy Size The app itself, with the 3D viewer and the IFC engine's code When you open BimCraft Several megabytes The IFC engine's WebAssembly part The first IFC file you open in the tab: \"Starting the IFC engine…\" About 1.6 MB DWG and DXF drawings, project and family files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. Save BimCraft for offline use Open BimCraft while you are online. Click Save for offline in the app bar at the top. Watch the button count up: \"Saving 40 of 212\", for example. BimCraft's copy is about 11 MB, because it includes the IFC engine. Wait for Offline ready. Once it says Offline ready, IFC models, DWG and DXF drawings, project files, folders and this help all open with no connection. You do not need to open an IFC or a DWG first to \"warm up\" the readers. To update or remove the copy, click Offline ready and choose Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy. What the button says Button Means Save for offline No offline copy in this browser yet. Click to save one \"Saving n of m\" Saving; keep the tab open until it finishes Offline ready Saved. Hover to see when; click for the menu Could not save Some files did not download. Check your connection and click to try again. The last good copy, if any, is kept Offline not available This window cannot keep an offline copy (a private window or a managed computer). See Working offline You are offline. If the button says Offline ready, every kind of file opens as usual. If it does not, only what this tab has already loaded works: a reader that never loaded in this tab cannot start, and opening that kind of file shows an error from the browser in the status line. Do not reload the tab until you are online again. What never needs a connection Once BimCraft is open in the tab, these work offline even without a saved copy: Reading project and family files and checking folders. Picking elements, reading properties and editing IFC values in a model that is already open. Save as IFC and Download CSV: both are downloads made in the tab. What is not available yet Cloud saving. Files are saved to your computer only. See Where your files go."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Messages BimCraft shows, what causes them, and what to do, for opening files, folders, 3D models, drawings and edits.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","cannot open","failed","incomplete","corrupt","slow","crash","blank","missing","message","help"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"opening-folders","text":"Opening folders","level":2},{"id":"the-ifc-engine-loads-the-first-time","text":"The IFC engine loads the first time","level":2},{"id":"saving-for-offline-use","text":"Saving for offline use","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"editing-ifc","text":"Editing IFC","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMessages appear in the status line at the bottom of the stage, in red when something failed. Find the message below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is not an IFC, RVT, RFA, DWG or DXF file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft did not recognize the file. Check the extension and that the file is not empty. PDFs, images and office files do not open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;.ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E (also for \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E) Unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E (it is an ordinary zip holding an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E) or export plain IFC.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E A \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E file. Export IFC or DWG from it and open that: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This IFC file could not be read.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The IFC file is damaged or not really IFC. Open it in a text editor: a real IFC file starts with \u003Ccode\u003EISO-10303-21;\u003C/code\u003E. Export it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This file is incomplete: it is … MB, but its own index points to data at least … MB in. …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The project or family file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is &quot;online only&quot; looks the same. A file opened on its own still opens, with this message at the top of the card and &quot;Showing what was recovered; copy the original again for the full model.&quot;: the card shows only the parts whose every byte is in the file and lists the rest under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the file\u003C/strong\u003E. Make the file available offline in your cloud drive, or copy the original again, and reopen it. In a folder, BimCraft shows the newest complete backup instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… The file&#039;s own list of contents is in the missing part too, so nothing in it can be opened.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file was cut off so early that not even its list of contents is there. Nothing can be recovered from this copy. Copy the original again and reopen it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This compound file has no BasicFileInfo stream, so it is not a Revit file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file is a compound file of another kind (old office documents use the same container). Check you picked the right file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Still working on the last one…&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft is still busy with the previous file or folder. Wait for the status line to finish, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file chooser greys out my file.\u003C/strong\u003E The chooser only offers \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E. Drag other files onto the page instead, and BimCraft will tell you whether it can read them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-folders\"\u003EOpening folders\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You picked a folder too high up, such as a whole drive. Pick one project folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The browser did not give access to \u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E. Choose it again with Open a folder.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You clicked \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E on a recent folder and the browser&#039;s permission was refused or has lapsed. Choose the folder again, or drag it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe browser says it will &quot;upload&quot; files.\u003C/strong\u003E That is the browser&#039;s own wording for handing files to a page. BimCraft does not send them anywhere. Drag the folder in to avoid the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happens on a phone or tablet.\u003C/strong\u003E Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders. Open single files, or use a computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-ifc-engine-loads-the-first-time\"\u003EThe IFC engine loads the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe status line stays on &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;, or shows an error such as &quot;Failed to fetch&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The IFC engine could not be downloaded. You may be offline, or a firewall or content blocker is stopping it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the app bar&#039;s button does not say \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, the IFC engine was never saved in this browser. Reconnect, open the file again, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E so it works offline next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReload the page and try again. On a work network, ask your IT team to allow the site&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.wasm\u003C/code\u003E files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-for-offline-use\"\u003ESaving for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Some files did not download, usually because the connection dropped. Check your connection and click the button again. Any earlier offline copy is kept until a new one is complete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E This window cannot keep an offline copy, for example a private window or a managed work computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button stays on &quot;Saving \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E&quot; for a while.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft&#039;s copy is about 11 MB, most of it the IFC engine. Keep the tab open until it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft opened offline but looks out of date.\u003C/strong\u003E Your saved copy is from an earlier visit. When you are online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe model is not where I expected, or it is tiny.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E. Fit always frames everything in the model, including storeys you have unticked and stray elements far from the building, so the building can look small. Zoom in from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking does not pick anything.\u003C/strong\u003E Hold the mouse still while you click: a small drag orbits. Elements on unticked storeys cannot be picked. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E on, the see-through spaces are picked before what is inside them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E button is greyed out: &quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The model has more than 1.5 million triangles. Edges are left off to save memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe tab turns slow, white, or shows the browser&#039;s crash page.\u003C/strong\u003E The model is too large for the memory the browser gives the tab. Close other tabs and reload. Export a smaller IFC: one building, one discipline or fewer storeys. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe 3D view stays blank.\u003C/strong\u003E WebGL may be turned off or blocked. In the browser&#039;s settings, turn hardware acceleration (or graphics acceleration) on and restart the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-ifc\"\u003EEditing IFC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Use a number.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The value is numeric. Type a plain number without units, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2.4\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Use a whole number.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The value is a count or integer. Remove the decimals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Only single-value properties can be edited.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Lists, enumerations and quantities are read-only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens the browser&#039;s &quot;Save page&quot; window.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no unsaved changes, so BimCraft left the key to the browser. Close the window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E It works only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field. After a save, there is nothing left to undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list still shows edits after I discarded them and opened a drawing or a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E When you answer the &quot;Discard them?&quot; question with OK and then open something other than an IFC model, the old edits are kept in memory and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E would still save them. To really start fresh, open the original IFC again, or reload the page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy edit changed more elements than I picked.\u003C/strong\u003E You changed a property in a set marked &quot;(type)&quot;. Type properties apply to every element of the type. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet (…).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Save the drawing from the program that made it as an older DWG (2018 or earlier) or as DXF, and try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing has nothing to show.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing is empty, or holds only things BimCraft does not draw yet (see \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E on the right).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft stopped drawing to stay responsive. Turn off layers you do not need, or open the sheets you need from a smaller drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts of a sheet are missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Linked drawings may be missing: look under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E on the right. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003EFind linked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E. Layers may also be off: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA picture shows as an empty frame.\u003C/strong\u003E Its file was not found, or it is a TIFF (&quot;TIFF cannot be shown in a browser&quot;). Add a PNG or JPEG copy with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd pictures…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling on my trackpad zooms instead of moving the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E That is how drawings work in BimCraft. Click and drag to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E: the thing you are looking for may not exist in this build.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Messages appear in the status line at the bottom of the stage, in red when something failed. Find the message below. Opening files \"name is not an IFC, RVT, RFA, DWG or DXF file.\" BimCraft did not recognize the file. Check the extension and that the file is not empty. PDFs, images and office files do not open. \".ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.\" (also for .ifcxml) Unzip the .ifczip (it is an ordinary zip holding an .ifc) or export plain IFC. \"… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.\" A .pln, .pla, .tpl or .bpn file. Export IFC or DWG from it and open that: see Open .pln and .pla project files. \"This IFC file could not be read.\" The IFC file is damaged or not really IFC. Open it in a text editor: a real IFC file starts with ISO-10303-21;. Export it again. \"This file is incomplete: it is … MB, but its own index points to data at least … MB in. …\" The project or family file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is \"online only\" looks the same. A file opened on its own still opens, with this message at the top of the card and \"Showing what was recovered; copy the original again for the full model.\": the card shows only the parts whose every byte is in the file and lists the rest under Not in the file. Make the file available offline in your cloud drive, or copy the original again, and reopen it. In a folder, BimCraft shows the newest complete backup instead. \"… The file's own list of contents is in the missing part too, so nothing in it can be opened.\" The file was cut off so early that not even its list of contents is there. Nothing can be recovered from this copy. Copy the original again and reopen it. \"This compound file has no BasicFileInfo stream, so it is not a Revit file.\" The file is a compound file of another kind (old office documents use the same container). Check you picked the right file. \"Still working on the last one…\" BimCraft is still busy with the previous file or folder. Wait for the status line to finish, then try again. The file chooser greys out my file. The chooser only offers .ifc, .rvt, .rfa, .rte, .rft, .dwg and .dxf. Drag other files onto the page instead, and BimCraft will tell you whether it can read them. Opening folders \"This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.\" You picked a folder too high up, such as a whole drive. Pick one project folder. \"The browser did not give access to name. Choose it again with Open a folder.\" You clicked Reopen on a recent folder and the browser's permission was refused or has lapsed. Choose the folder again, or drag it in. The browser says it will \"upload\" files. That is the browser's own wording for handing files to a page. BimCraft does not send them anywhere. Drag the folder in to avoid the question. Nothing happens on a phone or tablet. Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders. Open single files, or use a computer. The IFC engine loads the first time The status line stays on \"Starting the IFC engine…\", or shows an error such as \"Failed to fetch\". The IFC engine could not be downloaded. You may be offline, or a firewall or content blocker is stopping it. You are offline. If the app bar's button does not say Offline ready, the IFC engine was never saved in this browser. Reconnect, open the file again, then click Save for offline so it works offline next time. See Online and offline. Reload the page and try again. On a work network, ask your IT team to allow the site's .wasm files. Saving for offline use The button says Could not save. Some files did not download, usually because the connection dropped. Check your connection and click the button again. Any earlier offline copy is kept until a new one is complete. The button says Offline not available. This window cannot keep an offline copy, for example a private window or a managed work computer. See Working offline. The button stays on \"Saving n of m\" for a while. BimCraft's copy is about 11 MB, most of it the IFC engine. Keep the tab open until it says Offline ready. BimCraft opened offline but looks out of date. Your saved copy is from an earlier visit. When you are online, click Offline ready and choose Save again (get the newest version). 3D view The model is not where I expected, or it is tiny. Press F or click Fit. Fit always frames everything in the model, including storeys you have unticked and stray elements far from the building, so the building can look small. Zoom in from there. Clicking does not pick anything. Hold the mouse still while you click: a small drag orbits. Elements on unticked storeys cannot be picked. With Spaces on, the see-through spaces are picked before what is inside them. The Edges button is greyed out: \"Edges are off for very large models\". The model has more than 1.5 million triangles. Edges are left off to save memory. The tab turns slow, white, or shows the browser's crash page. The model is too large for the memory the browser gives the tab. Close other tabs and reload. Export a smaller IFC: one building, one discipline or fewer storeys. See Browsers, devices and memory. The 3D view stays blank. WebGL may be turned off or blocked. In the browser's settings, turn hardware acceleration (or graphics acceleration) on and restart the browser. Editing IFC \"Use a number.\" The value is numeric. Type a plain number without units, such as 2.4. \"Use a whole number.\" The value is a count or integer. Remove the decimals. \"Only single-value properties can be edited.\" Lists, enumerations and quantities are read-only. Ctrl+S opens the browser's \"Save page\" window. There are no unsaved changes, so BimCraft left the key to the browser. Close the window. Ctrl+Z does nothing. It works only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field. After a save, there is nothing left to undo. The Changes list still shows edits after I discarded them and opened a drawing or a folder. When you answer the \"Discard them?\" question with OK and then open something other than an IFC model, the old edits are kept in memory and Save as IFC would still save them. To really start fresh, open the original IFC again, or reload the page. My edit changed more elements than I picked. You changed a property in a set marked \"(type)\". Type properties apply to every element of the type. Click Undo. See Change a type property for every element of a type. Drawings \"name could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet (…).\" Save the drawing from the program that made it as an older DWG (2018 or earlier) or as DXF, and try again. \"This drawing has nothing to show.\" or \"name is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.\" The drawing is empty, or holds only things BimCraft does not draw yet (see Not drawn yet on the right). \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" BimCraft stopped drawing to stay responsive. Turn off layers you do not need, or open the sheets you need from a smaller drawing. Parts of a sheet are missing. Linked drawings may be missing: look under Linked drawings on the right. See Find linked drawings and pictures. Layers may also be off: click All on. A picture shows as an empty frame. Its file was not found, or it is a TIFF (\"TIFF cannot be shown in a browser\"). Add a PNG or JPEG copy with Add pictures…. Scrolling on my trackpad zooms instead of moving the drawing. That is how drawings work in BimCraft. Click and drag to pan. Still stuck Read What is not built yet: the thing you are looking for may not exist in this build."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"What this BimCraft build does not do yet, and what to use instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","limits","limitations","not supported","roadmap","authoring","modeling","sections","measure","clash","bcf","cloud","desktop","export"],"headings":[{"id":"modeling-and-authoring","text":"Modeling and authoring","level":2},{"id":"viewing-models","text":"Viewing models","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"files-and-platforms","text":"Files and platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft today is a reader, checker and IFC data editor that has started to draw buildings: walls, doors and windows, rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/doors-and-windows\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/doors-and-windows\"\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/floors-and-roofs\"\u003ERooms, floors, ceilings and roofs\u003C/a\u003E). This is the honest list of what it does not do yet, with what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modeling-and-authoring\"\u003EModeling and authoring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing beams; columns of steel sections\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse your authoring program, then open its IFC export here. Rectangular and round columns are placed here (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/grids-and-columns\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/grids-and-columns\"\u003EGrids and columns\u003C/a\u003E); straight, L, U, winder and spiral stairs and railings are drawn here (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/stairs-and-railings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/stairs-and-railings\"\u003EStairs and railings\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA window in a dormer&#039;s front; dormers on roof planes drawn by hand; curved roofs; a rendered roof covering texture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof planes drawn by their corners, and a pitch for each wall: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/roofs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/roofs\"\u003ERoofs: a pitch for each wall, gables, sheds and roof planes\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging walls, doors or windows that another program made\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program; the drawing tools change only what they drew\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn curved walls: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim / extend\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner\u003C/span\u003E, framing, automatic plan dimensions, snapping along the arc (it snaps to its ends and the middle of its arc)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the wall straight for those, or use straight segments. Curved walls are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/draw-walls\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/draw-walls\"\u003EDraw walls\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDragging a placed library item in the 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag it in the plan, or type X, Y and Rotation in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopying drawn walls, doors and windows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw them again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an element&#039;s shape or size (other than walls, doors and windows drawn here, and elements placed from a family)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program. Delete, copy, cut and paste work here: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeleting storeys, spaces, buildings or the site\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the elements in them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPasting building elements into another model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste within one model; placed library items paste into any model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdding new properties or property sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill in existing values here, or add the parameter in your authoring program and export again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing quantities, lists, enumerations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving project files of other programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft saves IFC (and its own family files: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/families\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/families\"\u003EFamilies and their sizes\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamilies: one family inside another, arrays of solids, a material as a parameter, a list of sizes read from a spreadsheet, a placed element showing other parts than its size, coarse / fine detail levels, a family&#039;s own plan lines (a new door or window family draws the plan symbol of its starter; furniture is drawn by its cut), families in the library\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamilies with parameters, formulas, Yes / No parameters, boxes, extrusions, revolves, sweeps and Booleans are made here: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/family-editor\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/family-editor\"\u003EEdit a family\u003C/a\u003E. Make one family per variant, or duplicate one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing other programs&#039; types as families; turning a library item into a family\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther programs&#039; types are listed, grouped by a guess, and their elements selected. Library items are placed as they are\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakers&#039; fittings, valves and equipment (air handlers, fans, pumps, diffusers, grilles); pressure losses through fittings and balancing; sprinklers; cable trays; sizing drains by fixture units; dragging a run&#039;s corners\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe and duct runs with generic standard fittings, systems, plans, schedules and the takeoff are drawn here: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pipes-and-ducts\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pipes-and-ducts\"\u003EPipes and ducts\u003C/a\u003E. Bring makers&#039; items from your MEP program as IFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructural design code checks; second-order (P-Δ), buckling or dynamic analysis; walls and slabs in the analysis; loads made from areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab gives forces, reactions and deflections of beams, columns and braces by first-order analysis; an engineer checks them: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/frame-analysis\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/frame-analysis\"\u003EAnalyse the structural frame\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"viewing-models\"\u003EViewing models\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D from project files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC and open it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInterior elevations in the generated drawing set (from a room&#039;s Properties and on your own sheets they are built); notes and your own dimensions on elevations and sections (level marks, heights, the width and door / window tags are drawn)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing set made from the model: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/sheets\"\u003EElevations, sections and sheets\u003C/a\u003E. For any IFC model: one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane, or a section box; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECallout views, detail views, drafting views and legends; drafting lines and hatch drawn in a view; cropping an elevation or section; view templates for elevations, sections, ceiling plans and 3D views; ceiling plans in the generated drawing set; a whole set of your own sheets in one PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlans and ceiling plans have view ranges, a crop region, a scale and an underlay (plans also templates), and your own sheets take plans, ceiling plans, elevations, sections and schedules, each saved as a PDF: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-sheets\"\u003EViews, view ranges and your own sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving a placed light up to the ceiling afterwards; sloped or stepped ceilings; ceiling patterns other than the five grids; a light&#039;s own symbol in the ceiling plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace it with \u003Cstrong\u003EMount: At the ceiling\u003C/strong\u003E; the ceiling plan draws a fixture from its own shape: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESharing search sets with others, sets inside the IFC file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESets stay in this browser; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasuring angles, or along a path of several points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure each part with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances, face areas and locations in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures in the live 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPainted materials show their color in the view and their texture in rendered pictures: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/render\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/render\"\u003ERender a picture of the model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClearance (soft) clashes, clash groups, saving clash results\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E and make issues of the ones that matter: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/clash-check\"\u003ECheck for clashes\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving several models as one set, or editing two models at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit one model at a time; each saves its own file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssigning issues, due dates, labels, several viewpoints per issue, editing an issue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne view per issue; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnzip or export plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers for a model without a map conversion, or in a drawing&#039;s views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the model with its coordinates: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/map-layers\"\u003EA map on the ground under the model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE57 scans over 6 million points or 512 MB; scanner makers&#039; own files (RCP, RCS, FLS, LGS, TZF, ZFS); point clouds in a drawing&#039;s 3D view; saving a cloud&#039;s place with the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the scan as LAS or LAZ and open it beside an IFC model: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/point-cloud-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/point-cloud-formats\"\u003EPoint cloud formats\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/point-clouds\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/point-clouds\"\u003EShow a point cloud beside the model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E project files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC and DWG from them, or a project bundle: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-bundle\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-bundle\"\u003EOpen a project bundle\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E list to select by kind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a kind&#039;s group in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping a comment or a title on an issue in VR, voice notes, moving a section plane by hand in VR, a section box in VR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace the issue in VR, then add its words in the Issues tab; set a section plane or box in the 3D view before you go in: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/vr-review\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/vr-review\"\u003EReview the model in a VR headset\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeeing people at the desk in VR (only people in VR show), recording a VR walk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk them to join in VR, or follow along in the 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERendering a recorded walkthrough as a video\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlay it in the 3D view: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/views-and-walkthroughs\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/views-and-walkthroughs\"\u003ESave views and walkthroughs\u003C/a\u003E; ModelCraft renders scenes to video\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDaylight simulation (daylight at points, climate-based daylight, glare, sun hours), roof windows in the daylight factor, checks against BS EN 17037 or green building schemes; heating, cooling or energy use\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe average daylight factor of each room, an early-design estimate: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/daylight-factor\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/daylight-factor\"\u003EEstimate the daylight in each room\u003C/a\u003E. Use a simulation program for compliance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESensor readings from a file or a live feed, documents (manuals, certificates) attached to an asset, a COBie spreadsheet, alerts when maintenance is due\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsset data and a maintenance record in the IFC, with what is overdue or due listed first and a CSV: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assets-and-maintenance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assets-and-maintenance\"\u003EKeep asset data and a maintenance record in the model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlug-ins or a live link to rendering programs; FBX or Datasmith files; one object per element, saved views and walks in the .glb\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a .glb for rendering programs that read glTF, or the IFC for those that run inside another design program: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-for-rendering\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-for-rendering\"\u003ESend the model to a rendering program\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing, measuring or saving DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe CadCraft drafting app (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E on the 2D view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrinting or exporting PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint from the program that made the drawing; check it first with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot styles and pen tables\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E shows plain black with line weights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG files older than R13, and binary DXF (BimCraft says so when you open one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the drawing again as a newer DWG, or as ASCII DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawings linked inside linked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen them directly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETIFF pictures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a PNG or JPEG copy with the same name and add it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDGN drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed in folder reports only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView the drawing in the program that made it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EACIS solids, bodies, regions and surfaces, and subdivision meshes in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC or 3D faces from the program that made the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures, multileaders and sheets in the 3D view of a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab and the sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking objects in a drawing&#039;s 2D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E and click the object there: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment schedules from drawings without \u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment schedule as XLSX or PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E and open it in a spreadsheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-platforms\"\u003EFiles and platforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave copies on your computer or office server. Cloud saving is not available yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemembering folders in Firefox and Safari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose the folder again, or drag it in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo after saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the original file again to start over\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFilling in the missing part of a cut-off project file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the original again; in a folder, BimCraft reads the newest complete backup\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReopening the files you had open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnsaved changes are offered back as &quot;Recover your work?&quot; when you return; open the files again from the recent list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"BimCraft today is a reader, checker and IFC data editor that has started to draw buildings: walls, doors and windows, rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs (see Draw walls, Doors and windows and Rooms, floors, ceilings and roofs). This is the honest list of what it does not do yet, with what to do instead. Modeling and authoring Not yet Instead, today Drawing beams; columns of steel sections Use your authoring program, then open its IFC export here. Rectangular and round columns are placed here (see Grids and columns); straight, L, U, winder and spiral stairs and railings are drawn here (see Stairs and railings) A window in a dormer's front; dormers on roof planes drawn by hand; curved roofs; a rendered roof covering texture Roof planes drawn by their corners, and a pitch for each wall: see Roofs: a pitch for each wall, gables, sheds and roof planes Changing walls, doors or windows that another program made Your authoring program; the drawing tools change only what they drew On curved walls: Trim / extend and Corner, framing, automatic plan dimensions, snapping along the arc (it snaps to its ends and the middle of its arc) Draw the wall straight for those, or use straight segments. Curved walls are in Draw walls Dragging a placed library item in the 3D view Drag it in the plan, or type X, Y and Rotation in Properties: see Place equipment and furniture from the library Copying drawn walls, doors and windows Draw them again Changing an element's shape or size (other than walls, doors and windows drawn here, and elements placed from a family) Your authoring program. Delete, copy, cut and paste work here: see Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Deleting storeys, spaces, buildings or the site Delete the elements in them Pasting building elements into another model Copy and paste within one model; placed library items paste into any model Adding new properties or property sets Fill in existing values here, or add the parameter in your authoring program and export again Editing quantities, lists, enumerations Your authoring program Saving project files of other programs BimCraft saves IFC (and its own family files: see Families and their sizes) Families: one family inside another, arrays of solids, a material as a parameter, a list of sizes read from a spreadsheet, a placed element showing other parts than its size, coarse / fine detail levels, a family's own plan lines (a new door or window family draws the plan symbol of its starter; furniture is drawn by its cut), families in the library Families with parameters, formulas, Yes / No parameters, boxes, extrusions, revolves, sweeps and Booleans are made here: see Edit a family. Make one family per variant, or duplicate one Editing other programs' types as families; turning a library item into a family Other programs' types are listed, grouped by a guess, and their elements selected. Library items are placed as they are Makers' fittings, valves and equipment (air handlers, fans, pumps, diffusers, grilles); pressure losses through fittings and balancing; sprinklers; cable trays; sizing drains by fixture units; dragging a run's corners Pipe and duct runs with generic standard fittings, systems, plans, schedules and the takeoff are drawn here: see Pipes and ducts. Bring makers' items from your MEP program as IFC Structural design code checks; second-order (P-Δ), buckling or dynamic analysis; walls and slabs in the analysis; loads made from areas The Analysis tab gives forces, reactions and deflections of beams, columns and braces by first-order analysis; an engineer checks them: see Analyse the structural frame Viewing models Not yet Instead, today 3D from project files Export IFC and open it. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC Interior elevations in the generated drawing set (from a room's Properties and on your own sheets they are built); notes and your own dimensions on elevations and sections (level marks, heights, the width and door / window tags are drawn) The drawing set made from the model: see Elevations, sections and sheets. For any IFC model: one Section plane, or a section box; see Cut an IFC model with a section plane Callout views, detail views, drafting views and legends; drafting lines and hatch drawn in a view; cropping an elevation or section; view templates for elevations, sections, ceiling plans and 3D views; ceiling plans in the generated drawing set; a whole set of your own sheets in one PDF Plans and ceiling plans have view ranges, a crop region, a scale and an underlay (plans also templates), and your own sheets take plans, ceiling plans, elevations, sections and schedules, each saved as a PDF: see Views, view ranges and your own sheets Moving a placed light up to the ceiling afterwards; sloped or stepped ceilings; ceiling patterns other than the five grids; a light's own symbol in the ceiling plan Place it with Mount: At the ceiling; the ceiling plan draws a fixture from its own shape: see Place equipment and furniture from the library Sharing search sets with others, sets inside the IFC file Sets stay in this browser; see Find elements by class, name or property Measuring angles, or along a path of several points Measure each part with Measure: see Measure distances, face areas and locations in 3D Textures in the live 3D view Painted materials show their color in the view and their texture in rendered pictures: see Render a picture of the model Clearance (soft) clashes, clash groups, saving clash results Run Clash check and make issues of the ones that matter: see Check for clashes Saving several models as one set, or editing two models at once Edit one model at a time; each saves its own file Assigning issues, due dates, labels, several viewpoints per issue, editing an issue One view per issue; see Issues and BCF files .ifczip and .ifcxml Unzip or export plain .ifc Map layers for a model without a map conversion, or in a drawing's views Export the model with its coordinates: see A map on the ground under the model E57 scans over 6 million points or 512 MB; scanner makers' own files (RCP, RCS, FLS, LGS, TZF, ZFS); point clouds in a drawing's 3D view; saving a cloud's place with the model Export the scan as LAS or LAZ and open it beside an IFC model: see Point cloud formats and Show a point cloud beside the model Opening .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files Export IFC and DWG from them, or a project bundle: see Open .pln and .pla project files and Open a project bundle Opening the Elements list to select by kind Click a kind's group in the Model tree: see Find your way with the model tree Typing a comment or a title on an issue in VR, voice notes, moving a section plane by hand in VR, a section box in VR Place the issue in VR, then add its words in the Issues tab; set a section plane or box in the 3D view before you go in: see Review the model in a VR headset Seeing people at the desk in VR (only people in VR show), recording a VR walk Ask them to join in VR, or follow along in the 3D view Rendering a recorded walkthrough as a video Play it in the 3D view: see Save views and walkthroughs; ModelCraft renders scenes to video Daylight simulation (daylight at points, climate-based daylight, glare, sun hours), roof windows in the daylight factor, checks against BS EN 17037 or green building schemes; heating, cooling or energy use The average daylight factor of each room, an early-design estimate: see Estimate the daylight in each room. Use a simulation program for compliance Sensor readings from a file or a live feed, documents (manuals, certificates) attached to an asset, a COBie spreadsheet, alerts when maintenance is due Asset data and a maintenance record in the IFC, with what is overdue or due listed first and a CSV: see Keep asset data and a maintenance record in the model Plug-ins or a live link to rendering programs; FBX or Datasmith files; one object per element, saved views and walks in the .glb Save a .glb for rendering programs that read glTF, or the IFC for those that run inside another design program: see Send the model to a rendering program Drawings Not yet Instead, today Editing, measuring or saving DWG and DXF The CadCraft drafting app (Edit in CadCraft on the 2D view) Printing or exporting PDF Print from the program that made the drawing; check it first with Print view Plot styles and pen tables Print view shows plain black with line weights DWG files older than R13, and binary DXF (BimCraft says so when you open one) Save the drawing again as a newer DWG, or as ASCII DXF Drawings linked inside linked drawings Open them directly TIFF pictures Save a PNG or JPEG copy with the same name and add it DGN drawings Listed in folder reports only Objects listed under Not drawn yet View the drawing in the program that made it ACIS solids, bodies, regions and surfaces, and subdivision meshes in 3D Export IFC or 3D faces from the program that made the drawing Pictures, multileaders and sheets in the 3D view of a drawing See them on the Model tab and the sheets Picking objects in a drawing's 2D view Switch to 3D and click the object there: see See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D Equipment schedules from drawings without KCL-ITEM tags Not available Equipment schedule as XLSX or PDF Download CSV and open it in a spreadsheet Files and platforms Not yet Instead, today Cloud saving Save copies on your computer or office server. Cloud saving is not available yet Remembering folders in Firefox and Safari Choose the folder again, or drag it in Undo after saving Open the original file again to start over Filling in the missing part of a cut-off project file Copy the original again; in a folder, BimCraft reads the newest complete backup Reopening the files you had open Unsaved changes are offered back as \"Recover your work?\" when you return; open the files again from the recent list"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone."},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app."},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
